          "textEn": "57\n\nMeaning: The ultimate truth is both knower and knowable, and there is no qualitative difference between them. Therefore,\nBrahma, the Supreme Soul, and God are qualitatively one. This is what the inquisitive, unqualified seekers of the Upanishads\nexperience as Brahma, as the womb of gold, or as the Supreme Soul, and as the devotees experience as God.\nIn that form. In other words, the form of God, that is, the Supreme Being, is the ultimate truth.\nThe supreme soul is a partial form of God, and the formless Brahman is the same for God.\nIt is as radiant as the sun's rays are to the Sun God. Sometimes, the ignorant and curious of both the above-mentioned ideologies\nargue in favor of their respective experiences, but those who are the real seers of the ultimate truth know very well that these three characteristics of the one ultimate truth appear different from different\nperspectives.\nAs it is said in the first verse of the first chapter of Bhagwat, the supreme truth is supremely complete,\nis omniscient and is completely free from the delusion of relativity. In this relative world, the knower is different from the known,\nbut in the ultimate truth, the knower and the known are one and the same. In the relative world, the knower is a living soul or supreme power, while the knowable is an inert substance or inactive power. Therefore, here is the demon of Para and\nApara Shakti, but in the absolute world, the knower and the knowable are both of the same Para Shakti. The Supreme Sovereign has three kinds of powers. There is no difference between power and might,\nbut there is a difference in the quality of powers. The supreme world and the souls are one\nmade from the supreme power, but this physical world is the inferior power. The soul, by being in contact with the lower power,\nbecomes deluded and thinks that it is related to the lower power.\nTherefore,\nin the physical world, there is a sense of relativity. But in the supreme, there is no difference between the knower and the known, and\nevery object is complete (supreme). That which is in the mouth, O sages, is knowledge and detachment.\nBeholding the self in the self, with devotion, with what is heard. 12)."
