          "textEn": "64\nThey have to live in animal wombs, due to which they have no knowledge of the ultimate truth, God. These\ndemons, according to God's will, are gradually purified in various countries by the grace of their liberated servants, here-\nin consciousness. Such devotees of God are the inner associates of God, and when\nthey come to save society from the danger of becoming godless, then they are called the powerful\nincarnations of God, their sons, their servants, or their associates. But none of them falsely\ncall themselves God. This is a condemnation declared by demons, and even the demonic followers of such demons consider these imposters to be God or their incarnation. In authentic scriptures, definite information is found about the incarnation of God. Until it is confirmed by authentic scriptures,\none should not consider anyone as God or an incarnation of God. In reality, those devotees who are eager to go to the abode of God, they should consider the servants of God\nas God.\nSuch servants of God are called great souls or pilgrimages, and they give teachings according to specific country-\ntime. Servants of God inspire people to become devotees of God. They can never tolerate that someone would call them God. Although Shri Chaitanya Mahaprabhu\nwas directly God according to the instructions of authentic scriptures, he remained like a devotee. Those who\nknew them as God, they used to call them here, but then they would close their ears with their hands\nand start chanting the name of Lord Vishnu. They strongly opposed calling themselves God,\nalthough they were undoubtedly God himself. God warns us from those cunning people\nTo act in such a way that they make themselves God |\n\nThe servants of God come to spread divine consciousness. Intelligent people should\nthat they cooperate with them in every way. God is more pleased by the service of his servant than by his direct service.\nGod is very pleased when he sees that his devotees are being properly respected, because they all\nendure all kinds of suffering for the service of God, therefore they are very dear to him. The Bhagavad-gita (18.69)"
