          "textEn": "79\nThey have an intense desire to attain salvation from the physical world. All the gods, even among the gods,\nEven the best Brahma, cannot grant salvation to anyone. Hiranyakashipu performed rigorous penance to become immortal,\nbut his revered god Brahma could not please him by granting such a boon. Therefore,\nother than Vishnu, no other liberator or provider of liberation is called God. All the gods also\nlike other beings in the physical creation, are destroyed at the time of the destruction of this physical creation.\nWhen\nthey themselves cannot attain liberation, then how can they grant liberation to their devotees? These\ngods can only provide some momentary benefits to those who worship them, not ultimate benefits.\n\nThis is why those who are desirous of liberation abandon the worship of gods, although they do not show disrespect to any of them.\nThey worship the gods of the Rajas and Tamas qualities.\nThose who are born of fathers, kings, and other noble beings, and those who are born of the wealth of the gods. 27. Meaning of the word:\nRajah, the quality of passion; Tamah, the quality of ignorance; Parakrutayah, of the nature of others; Sam-sheela:--Of the same category; they worship;\nVa-Nishraya only; Pitra--Pitrganah; Bhuta--other beings; Prajesh-Aadin—Prajapati etc.; Shriya--prosperity; Aishwarya--wealth and power; Praja--offspring, descendants; Ipsavah--they desire this,\nThose who are Rajoguni and Tamoguni worship the ancestors, other beings, and those deities\nwho are in charge of the activities related to creation, because they are inspired by the desire to take advantage of women, wealth, power, and\noffspring.\nMeaning: If a person is serious about returning to the abode of God, then he has no need to worship any\ncategory of deities. It is clearly stated in the Bhagavad Gita (7.20, 23) that those who are crazy after material pleasures,\nthey go to various deities for their momentary gain, but such gains are for the ignorant. We should never\nmake the desire for material enjoyment strong. Physical enjoyment should be adopted only as much as necessary for life's minimum needs, not less, not more"
