          "textEn": "90\n\nChapter three\n\nThe source of all incarnations: Krishna\nSuta said:\nIn the house, the form of the Lord, the great among the gods. Sambhutam shodashakalmadau lokasrikshaya. 1>. Meaning of the word:\nSuta said: The son said; Jagruha - accepted; Paurusham - complete in the form of a man; Rupam - form; Bhagavan - God;\nBhagavan; Mattah - the Adi Dhih - the material world, including its parts; Sambhrutam - originated in this way; Ghodasha Kalam - sixteen main\nprinciples; Adau - in the beginning; Loka - the world; Sisrukshaya - with the intention of creating;.\n\nSutji said: In the beginning of creation, God first expanded himself in the form of the cosmic being,\nand revealed all the material for creation. Thus,\nfirst, sixteen elements of physical action were produced. All this was done to create the physical universe. Meaning: The Bhagavad Gita says that Lord Krishna, in the expansion of his quien, follows these material\nuniverses. Therefore, this masculine form is the confirmation of that very principle.\nThe original God\nVasudeva or Krishna Bhagwan, who is famous as the son of King Vasudeva or King Nanda, is full of all\nWealth, powers, fame, beauty, knowledge, and detachment. A part of their glories\nappears as the formless Brahma, then a part in the form of the Supreme Soul. Of that same Lord Shri Krishna,\nthis male form is the original Supreme Soul form. In the physical creation, there are three male forms, and\nthis form, which is called the causal manifestation of Vishnu, is the first of the three. There are other forms,\nGarbhodakshayi Vishnu and Kshirodakshayi Vishnu, about whom we will learn respectively. In\nCaranodakshayi Vishnu, from the navels, countless universes are created, and in each universe,\nGod enters in the form of Garbhodakshayi Vishnu. In the Bhagavad Gita, it is also mentioned that the physical world is created for a definite period\nand then destroyed. This creation and destruction bound beings or eternally bound beings"
