ि गर्भोदकशायी विष्णु के पूर्ण",
          "textEn": "96\n\nThis is the treasure of the manifested forms, the imperishable seed.\nBy whose part the gods, demons, and other beings are created. 5>. Shabdartham\nEtat-this (form); Nana-many; Avataranam-of the incarnations; Nidhanam-source; Bijam-seed; Avyayam-indestructible; Yasya-whose; Ansh-part; Anshena-from a part; Srujyante-being produced; Deva-deities; Tiryak-tilted; Pashu-animal; Nar-human; Adayah-etc..\nThis form (the second manifestation of the male) is the source of various incarnations within the universe and\nis the imperishable seed. From these particles and parts, gods, humans, etc., various living beings\nare produced. Meaning: After creating countless universes in the great principle, this man, from each of these universes,\nentered as the second person--Garbhodakshayi Vishnu. There, when he\nsaw that there was only darkness and emptiness in the universe, and there was no place for rest,\nhe filled half the universe with the water of his sweat, and he himself lay down in that\nwater.\nThis is called the water-bearing aquifer. Then from his navel, the lotus-like sprout emerged.\nAnd on the petals of that flower, the chief sculptor of the universe, Brahma, was born. Brahma became the creator of the universe.\nAnd God, in the form of Vishnu, took the responsibility of nurturing this universe upon himself. Brahma originated from the Rajoguna of nature, and Vishnu became the lord of the Sattvaguna. Vishnu, being beyond all qualities, always remains separate from physical influences. Its explanation has already been done. From Brahma emerged the daityas (Shiva's side) – the heaviest of the gunas, Tamas. They, by the will of God, are complete.\nThey destroy creation. Therefore, these three--Brahma, Vishnu, and Shiva--are incarnations of Vishnu himself.\nFrom Brahma, Daksha, Marichi, Manu, and many other deities descended, who created living beings in the universe. In the Vedas, the praise of this Garbhodakshayi Vishnu is done in the form of stotras of Garbhaswati,\nwhose beginning is with the description of the Lord with thousands of heads, etc.\nGarbhodakshayi\nVishnu is the lord of the universe, and although he appears to be sleeping within this universe,\nHe is always divine. It has already been explained. Vishnu, who is the complete Vishnu, who is the embodiment of Garbhodakshayi Vishnu"
