          "textEn": "07\n\nMeaning of the word:\nSur-- Atheist; Asuranam-- and Nastiko; Udadhim-- in the sea; Manshantam-- while churning; Mandrachalam--\nMandrachal mountain; Dre-- held; Kamtha-- tortoise; Rupena-- in the form of; Pishche-- on the back; Ekadashi-- eleventh;\nVibhuh-- great..\n\nThe eleventh incarnation of God took place in the form of a tortoise, whose back became the base for the Brahmanda's\nastronomical and atheistic, being used as a churning rod by the mountains of Mandarachal\nfor. Meaning: Once, both theists and atheists were engaged in extracting nectar from the ocean,\nso that they could become immortal by drinking all the nectar they obtained. At that time, the Mandarachala mountain was used like a churning rod,\nand in the water of the ocean, the avatar of God, the tortoise, made the mountain a base.\nDhanvantaram, the twelfth, the thirteenth, and also the eleventh.\nHe who deludes others, deludes himself. 17). Shabdartham.\nDhanvantaram.-- The incarnation of the Lord named Dhanvantari; Dvadashamam-- Twelfth; Trayodashamam-- Thirteenth; Eva-- Certainly; Cha--\nAnd; Apayayat--given for drinking; Suran--to the gods; Aniyan--to others; Mohinya--by Sundari; Mohayan--by\nMohate ho; Stri--as a woman..\n\nIn the twelfth incarnation, Lord Dhanvantari appeared, and in the thirteenth incarnation, he\nAttracted the atheists with the enchanting beauty of a woman and provided nectar to the gods to drink. The fourteenth, Narasimha, with his arms outstretched, is seated on a demon.\nWith his hands clasped together, he is like a crow. 18. Shabdartham\nChaturdasham- fourteenth; Nar-Siham-- an incarnation of God with a human and a lion body; Bibhrath-- descended; Daitya-Indram-- the king of the demons; Oorjitam-- invigorated; Dadar-- torn; Karjeah- with nails; Oorau- lap; Eerakam- to the waist; Kat-cut; Kat-cut- cut; Yatha- as; Jatha- as."
