          "textEn": "34\nIt is necessary to not despise the stories and histories of the Puranas by calling them fictional.\nWhat was the benefit to the sages like Vyasa from keeping fictional stories in their literature? In Shrimad Bhagavat, various histories of different worlds have been depicted with selected historical facts.\nHas been done. Therefore, all spiritualists accept it as a Mahapurana. The uniqueness of these histories\nis that they are all related to the pastimes of God in different times and environments. Srila Shukadeva Goswami is the foremost among the self-realized individuals, and he received this subject from his father, Vyasadeva. Srila Vyasadeva is a great scholar. He, considering the subject of Shrimad Bhagavat\nas extremely important, first gave it to his great son, Srila Shukadeva Goswami. It has been compared to malai curry. Vedic literature is like the milk of knowledge,\nIt is an ocean.\nJust as the most delicious element of butter milk is, similarly this\nis also Shrisaddughavat, because it contains extremely delicious,\npromotional and authentic descriptions of the activities of God and his devotees. But if the Bhagavat is heard by atheists and business readers,\nwho do business of Bhagavat for the common people, then there is no benefit\nfrom it. Srila Shukadeva Goswami was given the Bhagavat for this reason, because he did not want to do the business of Bhagavat. He did not want to run his family with this business. Therefore, Shrimad Bhagavat should be accepted by such a representative of Shukdev, who is in the Sanyas Ashram\nand who does not have the burden of the family. Milk is truly excellent and nutritious, but when\na snake touches it with its mouth, it no longer remains nutritious; instead, it becomes the cause of death.\nSimilarly, those who are not firmly engaged in the Vaishnava tradition should neither engage in the business of Bhagwat,\nnor should they become the cause of the spiritual death of many listeners. In the Bh