          "textEn": "245\nis for. In contrast, Narad Muni explains the second method of restraining the senses, which is\nthe divine loving devotion to Lord Mukunda. They say based on their experience that God's\ndevotional service is more practical and\nmore effective than the artificial method of controlling the senses. Serving Lord Mukunda makes the senses divinely engaged. In this way, there is no opportunity left for them to engage in sensory gratification. The senses need some work.\n. Artificially stopping them is not stopping at all, because as soon as there is an opportunity for indulgence,\nthe snake-like senses must take advantage of it. In history, there are many such\nexamples--such as Vishwamitra Muni's fascination with Menaka's beauty. Similarly, beautiful,\nDressed in a garment, Maya came to enchant Thakur Haridas at midnight, but she could not trap this\ndevotee in her net.\nThe sentiment is that without devotional service to God, neither the yoga method nor dry contemplation\ncan ever be successful. The best way to attain self-realization is through desire-driven actions.\nWithout being clouded by dry contemplation, one should have pure devotion to God. Such pure devotional service is divine, and the methods of yoga and knowledge are secondary to such a method. When divine\nwith devotion, any other secondary method is added, then it does not remain divine,\nbut is called mixed devotion. The author of Shrimad Bhagavat, Srila Vyasadeva, will later, in turn,\nexpand upon the various methods of these divine realizations.\nAll this is spoken by me, O sinless one, who was asked by you.\nThe secret of my birth and actions, O self-pleaser. 36. Meaning of the word:\nSarvam-- all; Tat-- that; Idam-- this; Akhyatam-- told; Yat-- that which; Pusth-- asked; Aham-- I; Tvaya-- by you; Anadh-- innocent; Janma-- birth; Karma-- work; Rahasyam- mystery; Me- mine; Bhavat- yours; Cha-- and;\nAtma- personal, self; Toshnam- satisfaction"
