          "textEn": "247\n\nAmantya Veena Ranayan Yayau Yahchiko Muni. 37). Meaning:\nSoota said--Soot Goswami said; Evam--in this way; Samvabhashya--by addressing; Bhagavan--Almighty;\nNarada--Narada Muni; Vasavi--Vasavi (Satyavati); Sutam--son; Aamchya--by calling; Veenam--Veena;\nVadayan--playing; Ranayan--doing; Yayo--went; Yadvichitra--by choice; Muni--Muni. Suta Goswami said: Thus, addressing Vyasadeva, Narad Muni bid him farewell, and while playing his Veena, he went to wander according to his free will. Meaning: Every creature desires complete independence, because this is its divine nature. And this freedom can only be achieved through divine service of God.\nDistracted by external power,\neach person considers himself independent, but in reality, he is bound by the laws of nature.\nWhen a bound soul cannot freely roam in this world, then what to say about his\ngoing from one world to another? But a person like Narad, who is completely liberated,\nwho is constantly engaged in the praise of God, is not only free to wander on the earth,\nbut is also free to wander in any part of the universe, as well as in any part of Vaikuntha. We can only imagine their spread and infinity.\nWhich is just like the freedom of God. There is no reason for their wandering, nor any responsibility.\nAnd no one can stop them from wandering freely. Similarly, the divine method of devotional service\nis also independent. It is possible that despite all the actions, it may not develop in any person.\nIt may not be able to. Similarly, association with devotees is also independent.\nIt is possible that someone lucky may get this,\nor someone may not get it even after thousands of attempts. Therefore, in all aspects of devotion,\nfreedom is the main pillar. There is no devotion without freedom. Offering to God\nThe meaning of freedom is not that the devotee is dependent in every way. Through the transparent medium of the Guru,\nto go into the refuge of God is to attain complete independence in life."
