          "textEn": "269\n\nWill be happy. But Duryodhana condemned this heinous act, and he was not at all pleased. Meaning: In Shrimad Bhagavat, the divine stories of Lord Shri Krishna, the war of Kurukshetra,\nbegins with the end, where God himself has spoken about himself in the Bhagavad Gita. Therefore, the Bhagavad Gita and the Srimad Bhagavatam are both divine stories of the Lord. The Gita is a story of Krishna.\nBecause it is said by God, and the Bhagwat is also a story of Krishna, because it is a story said about Krishna. Lord Shri Chaitanya Mahaprabhu wanted that every person should know about these two\nKrishna stories. Lord Krishna Chaitanya, in the guise of a devotee of Lord Krishna,\nis the direct Krishna, therefore the intentions of Lord Krishna and Shri Krishna Chaitanya Mahaprabhu are the same.\nIt was the wish of Lord Chaitanya that all those who have taken birth in India should understand these Krishna stories\nseriously and, after having a complete realization, spread this divine message in every part of the world. From this, desired peace and prosperity can be attained in the troubled world.\nMother, the death of children,\nSilently, in the terrible heat. Tadarudrappakalakkulakshi\nTan santvayannaah kiritamali. 15>. Meaning of the word:\nMother--Mother; Shishunamu--innocent; Death--murder; Sons--sons; Nishamy--hearing;\nParitapyamana--grieving; Tada--At that time; Arudat--started crying; Vashya-kal-akul-akshi--with tears in the eyes;\nTam--to her; Sanvathayan--giving patience; Aah--said; Kiritamali--Arjuna..\n\nThe mother of the five sons of the Pandavas, Draupadi, hearing the killing of her sons, with tears,\nwith eyes full of tears, began to lament. In this terrible loss, while reassuring him, Arjun\nSaid to him in this way."
