          "textEn": "336. They are like sandalwood that grows on the Malaya mountain. Trees grow everywhere,\nbut the sandalwood tree grows especially in the Malay Peninsula, therefore sandalwood\nand the name of the Malay Peninsula are interconnected. Therefore, the conclusion that emerges is that God is always unborn like the sun, yet he appears in the same way as the sun rises in the eastern horizon. Just as the sun never remains only as the eastern horizon, similarly,\nGod is not the son of anyone, but He is the father (janak) of all things. Having approached Vasudeva, the supreme, for the sake of the gods,\nFor the protection and slaying of the demons. 33). Shabdartham\nApare--Other people; Vasudevasya--of Vasudeva; Devakyam--of Devaki; Yachitah--once prayed; Abhyagatah--approached;\nBirth taken; Ajah--Unborn; Tvam--You; Asya- of this; Kashemaaya--for welfare; Vadhaya--to kill;\nCha--and; Suradwisham--of the gods who are jealous.\n.\n\nOthers say that since Vasudeva and Devaki both prayed for you,\ntherefore you are born as their son. Undoubtedly, you are unborn, yet you accept birth to\ndo good to the gods and to kill the demons who are FEIT to them. Meaning: It is also said that to attain God in the form of a son, Vasudeva and\nDevaki performed rigorous penance in their previous birth as Sutapa and Prishni. As a result of this,\nGod appeared to them in the form of their sons. It has already been declared in the Bhagavad Gita\nthat God appears to do welfare of all the people of the world and to destroy the demons or materialistic atheists. The burden is not on the earth, but on the sky. He who is filled with the burden of teeth, is the self-controlled one. 34)."
