          "textEn": "353\nIn the eighteen-day war of Kurukshetra, sixty-four crore people were killed and several lakhs disappeared. In a sense, it was the greatest battle in world history in the last five thousand years. To get Maharaj Yudhishthir the throne, this collective killing was extremely saddened.\nTherefore, sages like Vyas and Lord Krishna themselves wanted to convince him with the evidence of histories that this war was justified, because its cause was justified. But\nMaharaj Yudhishthir was not satisfied, although the great men of that time gave him advice. Here\nKrishna is said to have performed superhuman deeds, but in this matter, neither Vyasa nor Krishna\nwere able to console King Yudhishthir.\nSo, does this mean that they could not prove to be superhuman doers? No, not at all. ©. Its discussion is that God, in the form of God,\nbeing situated in the hearts of both King Yudhishthira and Vyas, performed even more superhuman deeds,\nbecause God had such a desire off. As the Supreme Being of King Yudhishthira, he did not allow the king\nto be assured by the words of Vyas and himself and others, because he wanted the king\nto receive instruction from the dying Bhishmadev, who was another great devotee of God. God wanted\nthat in the final moments of his physical existence, the great warrior Bhishmadev should have a direct vision of him\nand at that time, see his grandsons, King Yudhishthira, etc., seated on the throne, and in this way he\nshould attain peaceful death. Bhishmadev was not at all pleased because of fighting against the Pandavas,\nbecause all the Pandavas were his fatherless grandsons.\nBut Kshatriya people are very strict.\nTherefore, they had to take Duryodhana's side, because it was through Duryodhana that they\nwere being supported. In addition to this, Lord Kau also had this wish that King Yudhishthira\nshould receive solace from Bhishmadev's words, so that the world could see that Bhishmadev was the greatest in knowledge, here\neven