          "textEn": "355\n\nKing Yudhishthira said: Alas, I am the most sinful man! Just look at my heart,\nWhich is full of ignorance! This body, which is ultimately for the sake of benevolence, has killed countless\nAkshauhini armies. Meaning: 21,870 chariots, 21,870 elephants, 1,09.650, infantry, and 65, 600 horsemen\nThe formation is called Akshauhini. In the battle of Kurukshetra, many Akshauhini armies were killed oft. Maharaj,\nYudhishthir, being the best righteous king in the world, takes the responsibility of killing so many creatures\nupon himself, because this war was fought to make him the king. This body is ultimately for the sake of benevolence. As long as life remains in the body, then this\nis for the sake of benevolence, and after death, this is eaten by dogs and jackals or by insects. He was saddened by the great massacre for this mortal body.\nBaladvijasuhanmitrapitrubhartrugurudrahaha.\nNa me syanirayanmoaksho hapi varshayutayutahitaha. 49>. Meaning of the word:\nBoy--a child; dvi-j-two times, one who takes; Suhrut-auspicious-minded; Mitra-friend; Pita-paternal uncle; Bhrata-brother;\nGuru-teacher; Daruhah-one who kills; Na-never; Me-mine; Syat-maybe; Nirayat-from hell; Moksha-liberation;\nHi-certainly; Api-even though; Varsh-year; Ayuta-lakhs; Ayuta-joined together..\n\nI have killed many children, Brahmins, auspicious-minded people, friends, uncles, gurus, and\nbrothers. Even if I live for millions of years, I will never be able to escape the hell that I will meet\nbecause of all these sins. Meaning: Whenever there is a war, many innocent creatures--such as children, Brahmins, and others--\nwhose killing is considered the most sinful, they are also killed. These are all innocent creatures and\nIn all circumstances, their slaughter is forbidden by the scriptures. ©. Maharaj Yudhishthira knew about this massacre.\nSimilarly, in both groups, there were friends, uncles and teachers, and they all died"
