।",
          "textEn": "3\n\nHadistham Pujayamas Mayayopattavigraham. 10. Shabdartham\nKrishnam--to Lord Krishna; Cha--also; Tat--that; Prabhava-gyaha--knowers of the effect (Bhishma); Asinam--sitting; Jagat-Ishwaram--Lord of the Universe; Hadi-Stham--sitting in the heart; Pujayam Aas--to worship; Mayaya--inner;\nBy Shakti; Upatta--manifested; Vigraham-Swaroopako..\n\nEven though Lord Krishna resides in the hearts of everyone, he reveals his divine form with his inner power.\nSuch a God was sitting in front of Bhishmadev. And\nsince Bhishmadeva was well aware of his glory, he ritually worshipped him. Meaning: The manifestation of God's almighty power is through His presence in every place. They are always present in their eternal abode, Golok Vrindavan, yet they reside in the hearts of every person,\neven within every invisible atom.\nWhen they, in this physical world,\nexpress their eternal divine form, they do so with their inner power. Outer being\nShakti or Maya has no concern with this eternal form. All these things were known to Shri Bhishmadev.\nOff, therefore, he worshiped them accordingly. Those who are devoted to the sons of Pandu,\nare deluded by the intoxication of love for the abode of the ascetics, and with their eyes. 11). Meaning of the word:\nPandu- Maharaja Yudhisthira and his brothers' deceased father's; sons, sons; Upasinaan-- sitting peacefully nearby;\nPrashraya- Abhibhuta- with love; Sankhataan- gathered; Abhyachta- congratulated; Anuraga- with love; Ashrai- by tears;\nAnandashru- by tears; Andi- by being blind; Chakshusha- by eyes..\n\nNearby, all the sons of Maharaja Pandu were sitting peacefully, and were overwhelmed by the love of their dying grandfather. Seeing this, Bhishmadev gave them hearty congratulations. In their eyes, there were tears of joy,\nbecause they were immersed in love and affection.\nNote: When Maharaja Pandu died, all of his sons were young children. Therefore,\nIt was natural that he was raised in the pampering of the elders of the royal family, especially Bhishmadev. Later, when the Pandavas grew up, they were deceived by the cunning Duryodhana and his group."
