          "textEn": "40\nHow would one have gotten the opportunity to give the teachings of the Bhagavad Gita? The Bhagavad Gita was recited for the salvation of the bound souls of the world, so that they would not consider the body as false and the soul,\nand be able to re-establish the eternal relationship between God and the soul. The discourse of self-knowledge by God\nwas mainly for the benefit of all the people in all parts of the universe. One who is not self-controlled, who is not committed to his promise,\nHe is like a chariot driver who is driven by the wheel.\nHe is like a chariot driver who is not steady,\nHe is like a hunter who is driven by the arrow. 27. Shabdartham\nSwa-nigamam--own integrity; Apahay--to nullify; Mat-pratimam--my vow; Ritam--real;\nAdhi--more; Kartum--to do; Avplutah--descending; Rath-sthah--from the chariot; Dhrut--accepted; Rath--chariot;\nRatha--chariot; Charanah--wheel, disc; Abhyayat--moving quickly; Chaladguh--stepping on the earth; Harih--lion; Iva--like;\nSadrishah--similar; Hanthum--to kill; Ibham--elephant; Gata--leaving on one side; Uttaraih--shawl, garment to wear\nWhile fulfilling his desire and breaking his promise, he came down from the chariot,\nHe lifted its wheel and ran quickly towards me, just as a lion runs to kill an elephant. His overcoat also fell into the road. Meaning: Although the Kurukshetra war was fought on military principles, at the same time,\nthere was also a playful spirit, as if it were a war between two friends. Duryodhana criticized Bhishmadev\nand accused him of hesitating to kill Arjuna because of paternal affection. A Kshatriya cannot bear the insult to the principle of war. Therefore, Bhishmadev\npledged that the next day he would kill those five Pandavas with his special weapon. From this,\nDuryodhana was satisfied, and he took those arrows with him, which he would give at the time of the next day's battle. But Arjun, by using a trick, obtained those arrows from Duryodhana, and Bhishmadev understood this.\nIt didn't take long to understand that this is the trick of Lord Krishna.\nTherefore, Bhishmadev vowed that the next\nday Krishna would have to lift the asr, otherwise his friend Arjun would be killed. The next day"
