          "textEn": "427\n\nThereafter, Maharaj Yudhishthira immediately went to his capital,\nHastinapur, with Lord Shri Krishna, and there he met his uncle Dhritarashtra and his aunt Gandhari.\nDhaadhas SET.\n\nMeaning: Dhritarashtra and Gandhari were the parents of Duryodhana and the other hundred brothers, and in relation, they\nwere the uncles and aunts of Maharaj Yudhishthira. After the battle of Kurukshetra, losing all his sons and grandsons,\nthis glorious couple was under the protection of Maharaj Yudhishthir. They were deeply afflicted by the immense loss of so much,\nand were living a life like ascetics. Dhritarashtra's uncle, Bhishmadev's\ndeath was a second deep shock to these kings and queens, therefore they needed consolation from Maharaj Yudhishthir.\nThat was the need. Maharaj Yudhishthir was aware of his duty, therefore he, along with Krishna,\nimmediately reached that place and consoled the grief-stricken Dhritarashtra with kind words,\nand from the side of God.\nAlthough Gandhari was living as an obedient wife and a compassionate mother, she\nwas also a powerful ascetic of. It is also said that due to her husband's blindness,\nshe voluntarily kept her eyes closed. It is the wife's duty to follow her husband 100%\nand Gandhari was so devoted to her husband that she kept following him in his constant blindness. Therefore, she was a great ascetic through her actions. In addition to this, she must have been deeply shocked by the collective killing of her hundred\nsons and grandsons, which is difficult for any woman\nto bear. But she, like an ascetic, endured all this. Even though she was a woman,\nGandhari's character was no less than Bhishmadev in any way. These two are well-known characters in the Mahabharata.\nThey are. Pitra chaanumato raja vasudevanumoditah. I am the lord of the Chakar kingdom, the all-pervading, righteous father. 49."
