          "textEn": "29\n\nRajyo hi paramo dharma: Swadharmasthapanam.\nShasato anyan yathashakramanapadyutpathanihi. 16. Shabdartham\nRajnah--of the king; Hi--certainly; Parah--superior; Dharmah--duty; Swa-dharma-sthah--steadfast in one's prescribed duty; Anugamyah--obedient; Anupalayanam--always surrendering; Shasthah--while governing; Anyan--to others; Yathah--as;\nShastramu--according to the scriptures; Anapadi--without danger; Utpatan--a misguided person; Iha--in a way..\n\nIt is the supreme duty of the ruler to provide protection to law-abiding individuals in all ways,\nand to punish those who, on ordinary days, when there is no emergency, are ruined by the ordinances of the scriptures. Meaning: The scriptures mention Adadu-Dharma, which means the duty of the time of extraordinary events. It is said that in an unusual, terrible situation, Rishi Vishwamitra had to eat dog meat.\nIt was said.\nIn an emergency, a person may be allowed to eat the meat of any animal,\nbut it does not mean that regular slaughterhouses should be run for those who eat meat,\nand this system should be encouraged by the state. Only for taste, in normal times, no one\nshould try to survive by eating meat. If someone does that, then the king or\nthe administrator should punish him for this indecent pleasure. Engaged in various actions, for different types of people, there are prescribed commands of the scriptures, and one who\nfollows them is called self-righteous, meaning one who fulfills his prescribed duties.\nIn the Bhagavad Gita (18.48), it is advised that a person should not abandon his prescribed actions (Swadharma),\neven if they are not entirely flawless. Such self-righteousness can be violated in times of emergency,\nif circumstances compel it, but in normal days, it should not be violated.\nThe ruler should see that such self-righteousness, no matter what the circumstances, should not be changed by those who follow it, and he should provide all kinds of protection to the protector of self-righteousness. The violator should be punished according to the scriptures, and it is the duty of the king that\nhe sees that every person strictly follows the prescribed self-righteousness in the scriptures."
