          "textEn": "327\n\nThe sages say:\nSoot, the soul, is gentle, eternal, and full of glory.\nYou are the one who inspires us; we are not immortal like Krishna. 11>. Meaning of the word:\nThe sages said; the sage said; the living being said; for many years; gentle; profound;\nForever; clearly; in glory; because you; you say beautifully;\nOf Lord Krishna; of mortals; of mortal beings; of immortality; the imperishability of life, the eternity; certainly; to you;\nThe sages said: O gentle Suta Goswami! May you live for many years and attain eternal fame,\nbecause you are telling about the activities of Lord Shri Krishna in an excellent manner. For mortal beings like us, this is like nectar. Meaning: When we hear about the divine qualities and activities of God, then we should always\nremember what God himself has said about himself in the Bhagavad Gita (4.9).\nWe should remember that.\nAll their actions, even when they act in human society, are still divine.\nBecause they are all inspired by the spiritual power of God, which is different from their physical power. Such actions are called divyam. This means that\nthey do not act like ordinary beings, remaining under the control of physical power, and do not take birth\nare. Neither is their body material like that of ordinary creatures, nor is it changeable. Whether from God or from any authentic source, the person who understands this fact, he does not take birth again after leaving this present\nbody. Such an enlightened being is allowed to enter the abode of God,\nwhere he engages in the divine, loving service of God.\nTherefore, in the form in which the Bhagavad Gita\nand the Srimad Bhagavatam contain the divine activities of God, the more we listen about them,\nthe more we come to know about their divine nature, and the more we keep moving forward on the path of the divine abode."
