आत्म-संयमी है,",
          "textEn": "340. Therefore, who else can be more powerful than Mukunda (Lord Krishna), who\ncan be called the complete Supreme Personality of Godhead in reality? Lakshmiji, Brahmaji, and Shivji are not independent.\nThey are powerful only in the expansion of God. They all remain engaged in the loving divine service of God, and all beings are like them. There are four sects of devotees who worship God,\nAmong which Brahma-Sampradaya, Rudra-Sampradaya, and Shri-Sampradaya are prominent, which come directly from Brahma, Shiva, and Lakshmi\nrespectively. In addition to these three, there is a fourth sect, the Kumarasamhita, which comes from Sanatkumara. These four roots are still engaged in the divine service of God, and they all declare that\nLord Krishna or Mukunda is the complete Supreme God, and no other person is equal to him, nor\ngreater than him.\nWhere the unattached are always patient,\nAnd are free from the delusion of the body and other things,\nThey go to the end of the supreme assembly,\nIn whom there is no violence, forbearance, and self-control. 22). Meaning of the word:\nWhere-- towards whom; Anurakta-attached; Sahasa- suddenly; Ev- certainly; Dheera- patient; Vyapohya- leaving aside; Deha- gross body and subtle mind; Adi- related to; Sankham- attachment; Udham- engaged; Vrajanti- go; Tat- that; Param- supreme; Hansyam- the highest state of accomplishment; Antyam- beyond that; Yasmin- in which; Ahimsa- non-violence;\nUpasham-And detachment; self-righteousness is the result of conduct.\n\nThe self-controlled man, attached to the Supreme Lord Shri Krishna, along with the gross body and subtle mind,\ncan suddenly abandon the world, which is overflowing with material attachment, and go out to attain the supreme accomplishment of life's renunciation,\nas a result of which non-violence\nand detachment arise.\n\nMeaning: Only a self-controlled person can gradually become attached to the Supreme Personality of Godhead.\nSelf-restraint means not being too engaged in sensual indulgence. And those\nwho are not self-controlled, they become engrossed in sensual pleasures. Dry philosophical contemplation is the subtlety of the mind.\nIt is sensory enjoyment. The senses lead man to the path of darkness. Those who are self-controlled,"
