          "textEn": "342\n\nI am the one who is asked, the knower of the self,\nThe knower of the self, who is beyond the sky, who is here.\nHe who is beyond the sky, who is equal to the self,\nAnd also equal to the path of Vishnu, who is in doubt. 23). Meaning of the word:\nAham-I; Hi-Certainly; Prusthah-Asked by you; Aryamanah-Like the sun; Bhavadhih-By you;\nAchakshya-Describe; Atma-Knowledgeable; Jaho-Until; Atra-At this point; Yavan-Until; TH:-Aka;\nPatanti-Fly; Atma-Sam-As far as possible; Patachintan-Those who contemplate; Patachit-Likewise; Vishnu-\nKnowledge of Vishnu; Vipashchit-Even through scholars..\n\nOh, sages as powerful as the sun, I will try to describe the divine\nleelas of Vishnu according to my knowledge. Just as birds fly as far as they can in the sky,\nSimilarly, learned devotees describe the pastimes of God\naccording to their own experiences.\n\nMeaning: The ultimate, complete truth is infinite.\nNo living being can know the infinite with their limited capacity. God is formless, with form, and is the eternal. From his formless side, he is the omnipresent Brahman, so\nFrom his inner form, he resides in the hearts of all beings in the form of the Supreme Soul, and in his\nmost concrete form, he becomes the subject of their divine, loving service by the fortunate devotees. In these various forms, only great scholars and devotees can partially estimate the pastimes of God.\nTherefore, Srila Suta Goswami, according to his experience, has taken upon himself the task of describing the pastimes of Lord Kau.\nIt is appropriate. In reality, only God himself\ncan describe himself, and his learned devotees can also describe him as much as\nfor whom he bestows power.\nOnce, filled with enthusiasm, wandering in the forest for hunting,\nFollowing the deer, tired, hungry, thirsty, and very thirsty. 24). Having entered the reservoir, he went to the hermitage.\nHe saw the sage seated there, peaceful, with eyes closed. 25."
