          "textEn": "343\n\nMeaning of the word:\nOnce--Once; Dhanush--bow and arrow; Udyamya--to hold with determination; Vicharan--wandering; Mrigayam--for hunting; Vane-in the forest; Mriganu--deer; Anugatah--chasing; Shrantah--tired --Tired; hungry; thirsty; --being; Bhrusham--excessively; Jal-aasayam--to the water's abode, the pond; Achakshanah--searching; Pravivesha--entered; Tam--that famous; Ashramam--the hermitage of the sage; Dadarsha- saw; Munim- to the sage; Asinam- a seat; --being; Shantam- peaceful; Mileet- closed; Lochanam- eyes..\n\nOnce, Maharaj Parikshit, while hunting with a bow and arrow in the forest, while chasing deer,\nbecame extremely tired and felt excessive hunger and thirst. While searching for a reservoir,\nhe entered the hermitage of the famous Shamik Rishi, where he closed his eyes, sat quietly,\nand saw the sage.\nMeaning: God is so merciful to his pure devotees that at the right time, he calls such devotees to himself and creates an auspicious situation for the devotee. Maharaj Parikshit was a pure devotee of God,\ntherefore, there was no reason for him to be extremely tired, hungry, and thirsty, because\na devotee of God is never distracted by such physical needs. But by the will of God,\nsuch a devotee may also appear tired and thirsty from worldly activities, to create a favorable situation for detachment. Before a person goes to the abode of God, he has to renounce all worldly\nattachment to relationships. Therefore, when a devotee becomes more engrossed in worldly matters,\nGod creates such a situation that indifference arises in him. God never forgets his pure devotee, even if he is busy in so-called worldliness.\nWhy not?\nSometimes they create strange situations, and the devotee is forced to renounce all worldly attachments. The devotee understands the sign of God, but others\nconsider it adverse and disappointing. Maharaj Parikshit was to become the cause of the inauguration of Shrimad Bhagwat by Lord Shri Krishna, just as his great-grandfather Arjun became the medium for the Bhagavad Gita. If, by the will of God, Arjuna did not develop attachment towards his family, then\nthe Bhagavad Gita sermon would not have been delivered by God for the benefit of all. Similarly, if at this time Parikshit\nMaharaj oh, had not been hungry and thirsty, then by Srila Shukadeva Goswami, the chief officer of the Bhagavat"
