          "textEn": "346\nThe guest enemy Bhima had to fight Jarasandha, yet he was given a grand welcome. At night, they\ntalked to each other as friends and guests, and during the day, they wrestled, risking their lives. This was the rule of hospitality. In the rules of hospitality, it is ordered that if a poor person has\nnothing to give to the guest, he should give a mat to sit on, a glass of water to drink, and say some sweet words. Therefore, in the hospitality of a guest, whether he is a friend or an enemy,\nno expense is incurred. This is a matter of etiquette. When Maharaj Parikshit entered the door of Shami Rishi, he did not expect the royal treatment by the Rishi,\nbecause he knew that sages are not rich in material terms. But they never thought that they would not get a seat to sit, a glass of water, and some sweets.\nThey would not even get a promise. They were neither ordinary guests nor enemies of the sage, therefore the king was very surprised by this\ndry reception of the sage.\nIn fact, when the king was extremely thirsty,\nit was appropriate for him to be angry. It was not unnatural for the king to be angry in such a critical situation,\nbut since the king himself was no less than a great saint, therefore his anger and then\nto take action was surprising. Therefore, it should be accepted that God, with supreme will,\nIt was bound to happen. The king was a great devotee of God and the sage was likewise. But it was God's wish that such circumstances arise that the king, becoming detached from his family and government\nduties, would be completely self-surrendered at the lotus feet of Lord Krishna. Sometimes, merciful God creates such difficult circumstances for his pure devotees,\nso that they can pull them from the swamp of the material world. But from above, all the\nsituations seem extremely disappointing to the devotees.\nDevotees of God always live in the protection of God,\nand in any condition, whether there is disappointment or success, God 