          "textEn": "354\n\nMeaning: Thus, the misuse of Brahmin power began, and gradually in Kali Yuga,\nall Brahmins became devoid of both their divine power and culture. The Brahmin boy considered Maharaj Parikshit\nas a low person of the lineage, but in reality, the Brahmin boy himself was like that,\nbecause because of him, the Brahmin caste became as weak as a snake with its venom broken. As long as a snake has fangs, it is terrifying; otherwise, it is only frightening for children. Kali first conquered the Brahmin boy and gradually conquered other castes. Thus, in this era, all the scientific system of society has taken the form of a corrupt caste system,\nwhich is being eradicated by other caste people who are under the control of Kali Yuga. Man should see the root cause of the evil and without knowing the scientific importance of this system,\ndo not try to disrespect it.\nTAS HH Balo Gale Sarpakalavaram |\nPitaram Vikshya Dushkarta Muktakantho Rurod Haa. 38). Shabdartham\nTatah-- After that; Abhyetya— Entering; Ashramam— In the ashram; Balah— Child; Gale sarpa— Snake in the throat; Kalevaram— Body;\nPitaram— To the father; Vikshya— Having seen; Dukha-aarta:— In a distressed state; Mukta-kanthah— Loudly; Ruroda-chillaaya; Ha--\nThe past indicative word I.\n\nTatah, after the child returned to the ashram, he saw a snake around his father's neck.\nDue to the anxiety, he shouted loudly. Meaning: That boy was not happy because he had made a big mistake and he\nwanted to lighten his mind by crying. Therefore, after entering the ashram, when he saw his father's condition,\nhe shouted loudly so that he could get relief. But it was too late. The father\nexpressed regret over the whole incident. Having heard the story of the son of Brahma,\nHe rose, with his eyes on the elephant, and saw the corpse of the dead. 39)."
