          "textEn": "36]\n\nBy the sin committed by the self, by the self, by the self, by the self,\nHe who is the sinner, the sinner, the sinner, the sinner, the sinner, the sinner,\nShould be forgiven. 47). Shabdartham\nApapeshu-- one who is free from all sins; Swa-bhutyeshu-- one who is under the protection of the Lord; Balen-- by a child; Apakta- immature; Buddhina-- with intelligence; Paapam-- a sin-filled deed; Krutam-- done; Tat- Bhagavan-- therefore, the Lord; Sarva-\nAtma- Sarvavyapak; Kshantu- for forgiveness; Arhati-- are worthy..\n\nThen the sage prayed to the omnipresent God to forgive his immature and unintelligent son,\nwho had committed the great sin of cursing such a person who was free from all sins\nand was dependent and worthy of being protected in every way. Meaning: each person is responsible for their own holy or sinful deeds.\nRishi Shamik\nfell to know beforehand that his son had committed a great sin by cursing Maharaj Parikshit,\nbecause he was a holy ruler and, being a great devotee, was free from all sins, therefore he\nwas worthy of protection by Brahmins. When a crime is committed against a devotee of God, it is extremely difficult to get rid of its consequences. Due to being in the main position of the social system, it is the duty of Brahmins\nto provide security to their dependents, not to curse them. Such occasions have come when a Brahmin\nhas given a fierce curse to his under-Kshatriya or Vaishya, but there was no such thing with Maharaj Parikshit,\nas it has been said. The foolish boy, being a Brahmin's son, did this out of mere pride.\nTherefore, he was subject to punishment by divine law. God never forgives such a person,\nwho insults his pure devotee. Therefore, by cursing the king, the foolish Bhangi not only committed sin,\nbut also committed the greatest crime.\nTherefore, the sage could see that only the Supreme Personality of Godhead\ncan save their son from this sin. Therefore, they directly\nprayed to God to ask for forgiveness, because only God can erase such a thing that is impossible to change. The sage, in the name of that foolish boy, asked for forgiveness, in which there was not even a slight development of intellect. Here, a question can be raised that since it was God's will that Maharaj Parikshit in that\ndifficult situation, so that he could be saved from this world, then why a Brahmin boy"
