          "textEn": "37]\nHe does. Therefore, when they were assured of death on a fixed date, they became firm in the divine love devotion of Lord Krishna by complete\nfasting, passing by the banks of the divine Yamuna River, the capital of Hastinapur (now in the Delhi province). Ganga and Yamuna both are\nimmortal (divine) rivers and Yamuna is still more sacred for the following reasons. Or, mixed with the lotus-like tulasi,\nWith the Krishna-like, exceedingly abundant water of the eyes,\nHe who is afraid of the world,\nShould not serve the one who is dying. 6>. Shabdartham\nYa--that river; Vai--always; Lasat-flowing; Shri-Tulsi - from the Tulsi leaf; Vimishra-mixed; Krishna-Angri-Lord;\nShrikrishna's lotus feet; Renu--dust; Abhyadhik--auspicious; Ambu-water; Netri--carrying; Punati--purifies;\nLokan-to the worlds; Ubhayatra-both above and below or inside and outside; Sa-Ishan-including Shivji; Kah-who else; Tam-that river;\nNa--that river; Na Nahi; Serve, worship; Marishyamanah-one who is always dying \\\nThis river (Ganga, on whose banks the king sat in penance) holds very auspicious water,\nin which the dust and basil leaves remain mixed with the lotus feet of the Lord. Therefore, this water\npurifies all three worlds from within and out, and also purifies Shivji and other deities. Therefore, one whose death is certain should take refuge in this river. Meaning: As soon as Maharaj Parikshit received the news of his death within seven days, he immediately retired from household life and went to the sacred banks of the Yamuna River. Generally, it is said that\nthe king took refuge on the banks of the Ganges River, but according to Srila Jiva Goswami, the king took refuge on the banks of the Yamuna-\nriver. From a geographical point of view, Srila Jiva Goswami's statement seems more accurate.\nMaharaj Parikshit used to reside in his capital Hastinapur, which is located near present-day Delhi,\nand the Yamuna River flows near this city.\nIt is natural that the king 