          "textEn": "375\n\nDvāipayano Bhagavannārādśa. 10>. Atri to Narada--all these are names of different sages who came here from different parts of the universe..\n\nFrom various parts of the universe, great sages came there--namely Atri, Chyavan, Sharadvan,\nArishtanemi, Bhrigu, Vasistha, Parashara, Vishwamitra, fg, Parashurama, Utthya, Tandrapradama, Idhmavahu,\nMedhatithi, Devala, Arshena, Bharadwaja, Gautam, Pippalada, Metreya, Ora, Kavasha, Kumbhayoni,\nDavayapayan and the great sage Narada.\n\nMeaning: Chyavan--He was a great sage and was one of the sons of YHA. His birth was premature,\nwhen his pregnant mother was abducted. Chyavana was one of the six sons of his father. Bhrigu, when Brahmaji was performing a great Yagya (sacrifice) with Varuna, then Maharishi Bhrigu was born from the fire of the Yagya.\nHe was produced. They were great sages and their beloved wife was Puloma. They, like Durvasa, Narada, and others,\ncould wander in the sky and they used to go wandering in all the planets of the universe.\nKurukshetra.\nBefore the war, they tried to stop the war. Once he gave the education of astronomy to Bharadwaja Muni,\nand he is the author of the great astrology Brihat Bhrigu Samhita. He explained how\nair, fire, water, and earth originate from the sky. He explained how air in the stomach\nworks and organizes the intestines. As a great philosopher, he established the living being's\ntruth with logic (Mahabharata). He was a great dance scholar. They, a long time ago,\npresented the theory of development. He was among the scientific proponents of the caste system. They\nMade Kshatriya king Vitahavya a Brahmin.\n\nVasistha, see Shrimad Bhagwat (1.9.6).\n\nParashara-These were the grandson of Vasistha Muni and the father of Vyasadeva.  These were the sons of Maharishi Shakti and their\nmother's name was Adrushyati. When they were in their mother's womb, their mother was only twelve years old.\nAnd in the mother's womb, they learned the Vedas.\nHis father was killed by a demon named Kalmashapad A.\nTherefore, to take revenge for this, he wanted to destroy the entire world, but his\ngrandfather Vashistha stopped him. Then they performed the demon-slaying yajna, but Maharishi Pulatsya stopped them. Attracted by Satyavati, they attained Vyasadeva.  Later, Satyavati became the queen of King Shantanu"
