          "textEn": "385. He approved of it by saying 'Sadhu-Sadhu'. Such sages always want the welfare of common people,\nand when they see a great person like Maharaj Parikshit progressing on the path of devotion,\nthen there is no limit to their joy, and they give all the blessings that they can. Devotion to God is so auspicious that all the gods and sages, even God himself, are pleased with the devotee.\nTherefore, every object appears auspicious to the devotee. All inauspicious things are removed from the path of a progressive devotee. Meeting the sages at the time of death was certainly auspicious for Maharaj Parikshit, and in this way, he was blessed by the so-called curse of the Brahmin boy.\nThis is the image of the best of kings.\n\nIn the happiness of Krishna, he is always devoted.\n\nThis is the seat, adorned with a royal crown.\n\nHe always offers sacrifices to the half-divine Lord. 20>. Meaning of the word:\n\nN--not; Va--in this way; Idam--this; Rajarshi--a king; Varya--chief; Chitram--astonishing; Bhavatsu--you all; Krishnam--Lord Krishna; Samanutreshu--to those who remain steadfast on that path; Ye--who; Adhyasanam--seated on the throne; Raj-\nKirit-Kiritamukutam; Jushtam--decorated; Sadyah--quickly; Jahuha--renounced; Bhagavat--Lord; Par-Kamah--desirous of attaining;\nThe one who follows the tradition of the sages.\n\n(The sages said:) Oh Lord, the chief of the Pandava dynasty, who follows the tradition of the sages,\nThe chief of the Rajarshis! It is not at all surprising that you are abandoning your throne, which is adorned with the crowns of many kings, to attain the eternal presence of God. Meaning: Foolish politicians who are entrusted with administrative positions think that the temporary\npositions they hold are the highest achievement of life.\nTherefore, they remain attached to those positions until the last moments of their lives.\nWhere do they pay attention to this, that after attaining liberation, among the associates of God in the divine abode,\nto attain a position is the supreme objective of life. Human birth is given for this purpose. God has\nIn the Bhagavad Gita, he has assured us many times that returning to the eternal Bhagavad Gandham is the highest achievement. While praying to lord Narasimha, TER Maharaj said, O Lord! I am extremely afraid of Jeevan's materialistic\nstyle. I am not at all afraid of your terrifying form of Nrusimha Deva."
