्त भी। अतः महर्षियों ने ठीक ही\nअपने-अपने स्थानों पर अन्तिम क्षण तक रहने का निश्चय किया।",
          "textEn": "387\n\nMeaning of the word:\nSarve--all; Vayam--we; Tavat--until; Iha--in this place; Asmahi--will stop; Ath--thereafter; Kalevaram--body;\nYavat--until; Asau--king; Vihaya--renouncing; Lokam--people; Param--supreme) Virajaskam--completely free from worldly impurities;\nVishokam--completely free from all kinds of sorrow; Yasyati--returns; Ayam--this; Bhagavat--devotee; Pradhanahi --\nWe all will wait here until the chief devotee of God, Maharaj,\nParikshit returns to the supreme abode, which is completely free from all worldly impurities and all kinds of sorrows. Meaning: The physical creation, which is compared to the clouds of the sky, beyond its limits,\nis the supreme sky, that is, the spiritual sky, which is filled with planets called Vaikuntha.\nSuch Vaikuntha planets also\nare known by different names, such as Purushottalmala, Achyutlala, Trivikramlala, Hashikeshalala,\nKeshavala, Aniruddha Lala, Madhavala, Pradyumna Lala, Sankarshan Lala, Shridhar Lala, Vasudeva Lala,\nAyodhya Lala, Dwarakal Lala, and many other millions of spiritual worlds where God is the presiding deity. All creatures there are liberated souls and their bodies are spiritual, just like God. There is no physical\nsin, every object is spiritual. Therefore, there is nothing to grieve over there. These people\nare full of divine bliss. There is neither birth, nor old age, nor death, nor disease. In all the above-mentioned Vaikuntha worlds,\nthere is a supreme abode, which is called Goloka Vrindavan, which is the abode of Lord Shri Krishna and his special associates. For Maharaj Parikshit, this specific abode was predetermined, and all the sages gathered there\ncould see it beforehand.\nThey were all talking to each other about the great departure of the great king.\nAnd they wanted to see him until the last moment, because they would not get to see such a great devotee of God again.\nThey will not get to see. When a great devotee of God passes away, there is nothing to mourn in it,\nbecause the devotee's entry into the abode of God is predetermined. But the sad thing is that such a\ngreat devotee becomes invisible from our sight. Therefore, it is a matter of sorrow. Just as\nGod rarely appears from these eyes of ours, similarly, great devotees too. Therefore, the sages rightly\ndecided to stay in their respective places until the last moment."
