          "textEn": "[Chapter One]\nThe first step of the experience of the divine\nSalutation to the Lord\nOm, salutations to Lord Vasudeva. O--Hail to thee, O Lord; TH: -- My reverence to thee; O God of gods,-- O Lord of Loves, O Krishna, son of Vasudeva. Oh, all-pervading Lord, I respectfully bow to you. \n\nMeaning: Vasudevaya means * Krishna, the son of Vasudeva. Since by chanting the name of Vasudeva Krishna, a person can attain all the auspicious fruits of charity and penance, therefore, Om Namo Bhagavate Vasudevaya.\nBy chanting this mantra, the creator, speaker, or any reader of Srimad Bhagavatam, the blissful Supreme God,\nrespectfully bows to Krishna. In the first why of Shrimad Bhagwat, there is a description of the laws of creation,\nTherefore, the first Skandha can be called 'Srishti'.\n\nSimilarly, in the second why, 'The subsequent visible world of creation' is described. In this why, the description of various worlds\nis done as different parts of the immense body of God.\nFor this reason, this\nsecond wheel can be called \"the visible world\" ©. In the second book, there are ten chapters, and in these ten\nchapters, there is an account of the purpose and various characteristics of the goal of Shrimad Bhagavat. In the first chapter,\nthere is a description of the glory of Kirtan, and it indicates such a method by which a newly initiated devotee\ncan meditate on the universal form of God. In its first verse, Shukdev Goswami Maharaj\nanswers those questions of Parikshit in which he had asked about the duty of a dying man. Maharaj Parikshit was feeling happy welcoming Shukdev Goswami, and he was proud that\nhe is a descendant of Arjun, the close friend of Krishna. He was personally very humble and gentle,\nBut he expressed happiness that Krishna was very pleased with his grandfather, Pandu's sons, and especially\nwith his grandfather Arjun. Because Lord Krishna was from the family of Parikshit"
