          "textEn": "\n. Do the trees that follow others now give alms in charity?\nDo they not give alms? Will rivers not provide water to the thirsty now that they have dried up? 2 Will now\nthe caves of the mountains be closed? Or does the almighty God not protect all those souls who have completely surrendered? So then, why do the learned sages flatter them, who have become intoxicated by the wealth of their eternal earnings? Meaning: Renunciation is never for begging alms or for living by depending on others by becoming a parasite.\nAccording to the dictionary, a parasite is one who, without contributing in any way to society,\nlives only at the expense of society. The monastic order is for making important contributions to society,\nnot for depending on the earnings of a householder.\nIn contrast, by authentic ascetics,\ntaking alms from householders is an opportunity given by the saints for the benefit of the giver. In the Sanatan-dhars tradition,\nit is the duty of a householder to give alms to a monk, and the scriptures command that they should treat monks like children of their own family and provide food, etc., without asking. Therefore, hypocritical\nrenunciants should not take undue advantage of the demonic nature of devout householders. Of the Sanyas Ashram,\nIt is the first duty of every individual to provide some literary work for the benefit of mankind\nso that they can attain the direction of self-realization.\nSrila Sanatan, Srila Roop, and\nduring the retirement of other Goswamis of Vrindavan, the foremost duty among other duties was the mutual scholarly debate (Goshti) in the Sevakunja of Vrindavan.\nAt this place, the establishment of Shri Radha Damodar Temple by\nShrila Jiva Goswami and the Samadhis of Shrila Roop Goswami and Shrila Jiva Goswami"
