          "textEn": "73\nArchana Kaun Di Vidhi is not for them. Yavanna jayet paravaremsmin\nVishveshvare drashtari bhakti-yoga. At that time, the form of the embodied being,\nIn the course of action, one should remember. 14). Meaning of the word\n\nYavat- Until; Na- Not; Jayet-- Develops; Par-- Divine; Aure-- Worldly; Asminu-- In this form; Vishw-Ishwar--\nLord of all the worlds; Drashtari- To the seer; Bhakti-Yoga-- Devotional service, devotion; Tavat-- Until; Sthavih-- Gross\nMaterialistic; Purushasya— Of the Supreme Being; Rupam— Universal Form; Kriya-Avasane— At the completion of one's duty; Prayat-\nCarefully; Smaret- Remember;.\n\nUntil a materialistic person develops love and devotion to God, the seer of both the divine and material worlds,\nhe should remember or meditate on the immense form of God after fulfilling his duties.\nMeaning: God is the seer of all the worlds, whether it is the physical world or the spiritual. In other words,\nGod is the ultimate enjoyer of all the worlds, as it is confirmed in the Bhagavad Gita (5.29). Spiritual\nThe world is the inner power of their inner being, while the physical world is the outer power of their outer being. All beings\nare also their stationary power, and they can live in the physical world or the spiritual world according to their interest.\nCan. This physical world is not a suitable abode for souls, because all beings\nare inseparable from God in a spiritual sense, but in the physical world, all beings are bound by the laws of the physical world.\nSince all beings are part of Him, therefore God wants them all to live with Him in the spiritual world. In the physical world, all the Vedas and scriptures are available to enlighten beings,\nthrough which they can be specially called back to the abode of God.\nUnfortunately, even after enduring the three types of suffering in a life bound by worldly attachments, one remains not very serious about going to the abode of God. This is because of his way of life, which has become complicated due to sins and virtues. From among the living beings, some beings, who perform virtuous deeds, begin to restore their forgotten relationship with God,\nbut they do not understand the tangible form of God. The real purpose of life"
