          "textEn": "]07\nThey have already mentioned in the previous verse that, in addition to the grand method of devotion, there is no auspicious way to attain salvation.\nThere is no other way. By devotees, various methods such as listening, chanting, remembrance, serving the feet, worship, bowing, servitude, friendship,\nand self-dedication, the practice of Bhakti Yoga is done. These are the nine authentic methods.\nA devoted devotee can obtain the desired result through all, some, or only one of these methods. But among these,\nthe first method, that is, the method of listening, is the most important in Bhakti Yoga. Without listening with sufficient and appropriate methods, no progress can be made through the practice of other methods, and only for listening,\nthe entire Vedic literature is filled, which has been compiled by the powerful incarnation of God like Vyasadeva. Since it has been ensured that God is the Supreme Being of every object, therefore their\nlistening and chanting should be everywhere and always. This is a human’s unique duty.\nWhen a man\nstops listening about the omnipresent God, then he becomes accustomed to listening to garbage broadcast by man-made instruments. No instrument is bad, because through the instrument, man can listen to God's\nsubject, but by being used for hidden purposes, the instrument is bringing about an accelerated decline in the level of human civilization. Here it is said that it is essential for a human to listen, because\nBhagavad Gita and Srimad Bhagavat, such texts are written with this objective. Besides humans, no other\nliving being can listen to such Vedic texts. If human society listens to Vedic literature,\nthen it will not be able to hear the impure words uttered by those impure people who are lowering the entire\nsociety's level. Listening is done through the Positive Kirtan. The person who listens from a proper source\nbecomes assured about the omnipresent God, and then he is encouraged to sing the praises of God.\nAll the great teachers, such as Ramanuja, Madhva, Chaitanya, Saraswati,\nThakur, or in other countries, Muhammad, Jesus, and others, have always and everywhere praised God through kirtan.\nThey have sung the praises of God. Since God is omnipresent, His praise is necessary everywhere and always. There should be no restrictions of country and time in praising God. This is Sanatan Dharma or\nBhagwat Dharma is called. The meaning of Sanatan is eternal, always and WIAA. The meaning of Bhagwat is God"
