          "textEn": "8\nWithout taking acceptance, one cannot obtain any fruit from the Gods. Therefore, a broad-minded person can see that even for material gains, the ultimate authority (power) is God alone. In such a situation, a person with broad vision should worship God directly, even if\nhe is desiring material pleasures or liberation. Whether it is SHH or Sakam or\nMokshakam, everyone should worship God with eagerness. This means that without any kind of mixture of action\nand knowledge, Bhakti Yoga can be fully applied. For example,\nthe rays of the sun without impurity are very intense. Similarly, the indefinite of Shravan, Kirtan, etc.,\ncan be accomplished by all, being independent of the internal purpose of Bhakti Yoga. Etavaneva yajatamiha ni shreyaso dayah.\nThe devotion that is always with the Lord, that is always with the Bhagavata. 11>. Meaning of the word:\nThese are different worshippers; Eva- certainly; Yajatam- while worshiping; Iha- in this life; Nishreyas- the highest boon; Udayah-\nDevelopment; Bhagwati- Supreme God; Achalah- unwavering; Bhavah- attraction; Yat- that; Bhagwat- of the pure devotee of God;\nwg: association AL\nVarious types of people who worship many gods, the pure devotee of God\ncan attain the supremely fulfilling boon only through association, which is in the form of unwavering attraction to God. Meaning: In this physical creation, from the first deity Brahma to a tiny ant, all beings of various species\nare subject to the laws of nature or the outward power of God. The soul, in its pure state,\nremains aware that it is a part of God, but when the soul, due to the desire to assert dominance over material power,\nfalls into the material world, it becomes bound by the three qualities of nature, and\nit struggles for more and more gain in life.\nThis life-struggle, being controlled by material pleasures,\nbeing a witness to running after the web of illusion. All the plans for material enjoyment, whether they are accomplished through the worship of various\ndeities, as described in the previous verses of this chapter, or\nwithout the help of God or deity, the modern progress of scientific knowledge is merely an illusion, because"
