          "textEn": "4]\nIf one can do it, then he should go to the temple of someone else, where all the above-mentioned tasks are regularly\ncompleted. By going to the temple of a devotee and seeing the adorned form of God,\nspiritual inspiration will arise in the worldly mind. Humans should go to pilgrimage sites like Vrindavan, where\nthere are arrangements for such temples and worship of Shri Vigraha. In ancient times, all wealthy individuals, such as kings and wealthy merchants, used to construct such mandirs (temples) according to the instructions of skilled devotees like Shadgoswamis.\nAnd it is the duty of the common people to take the benefit of these mandirs and festivals celebrated in pilgrimage sites\nby following in the footsteps of great devotees (Anubraj). Humans should not,\ntake the feeling of traveling and sightseeing to these holy pilgrimage sites and temples, But consider them immortalized by the divine plays of God,\nand guide them under the guidance of a person who knows the science of Isha. This is called Anubraj.\nAnu also means to follow closely. Therefore, it would be best to follow the Guru's orders even in going to see the wid and\npilgrimage sites. He who does not travel by this method,\nis like that inert tree, which God has cursed to remain inert. Of man\nThe misuse of the tendency to move around is in seeing the scene or traveling to places for tourism.\nIn traveling. The proper use of such a tendency of travel is that one should visit the pilgrimage sites established by great teachers,\nand one should not be misled by the atheistic propaganda of those wealthy people, who\nhave no knowledge of spiritual subjects.\nThe living being is the dust of the feet of the Lord.\nHe who does not know the feet of Shri Vishnu,\nis like a man with a lotus in his mouth.\nHe who does not know the fragrance of the lotus. 23). Meaning of the word:\nJeevan-- while living; shav:-- corpse; bhagwat-angirēnūm-pure- dust of the feet of the devotee; na-- never;\nJatu-- at any time; wel:-- at all; abhilābhet-- especially obtained; yah- he who; tu- but; shri- of śrī; āishvarya- of opulence; viṣṇu-padyāh-of the lotus feet of Vishnu; manu-jah- born of Manu (human); tulasī- of Tulasi; śrasan- while breathing; shav-- corpse; shavā- dead body; yah- he who; tu- but; na- not; veda- not even; gandham- fragrance; gandh- of fragrance. The person who has never held the dust of the feet of a pure devotee of God on his forehead"
