          "textEn": "96\nChapter five\nBecause of all causes\nNarada said\nO Gods, salutations to you, ancestors of the past and present. Tad vijanihi yajnajanamatmata-tatvanirdarshanam. 9>. Meaning of the word.\nNarad said --Shri Narad said; Deva--of all the gods; Deva--god; Namah--Salutations to you; Astu--is; Bhuta--\nBhavana--the father of all beings; Purva-ja--first born; Tat Vijanihi--tell that knowledge; Yat Gnanam--that knowledge;\nAtma-tatva--divine; Nidarshanam--instructs in the form of poison..\nShri Narad Muni asked Brahmaji: O chief of the gods, O foremost being, O soul of the first birth, I\nbow down to you with reverence. Please tell me that divine knowledge, which leads a man to the truth of the soul\nand the Supreme Soul. Meaning: The completeness of the traditional system is also being confirmed. In the previous chapter, this establishment\nhappened that the original being Brahmaji directly received knowledge from God, and he gave that knowledge to the next disciple\nNarad.\nNarada requested for knowledge, so Brahma gave knowledge. Therefore, to ask for divine knowledge from the right person and to obtain it in the right way is the tradition.\nIt is a rule. This method is recommended in the Bhagavad Gita (4.2). The curious disciple should go to a worthy guru to\nobtain divine knowledge through dedication, humble curiosity, and service. This\nkind of humble curiosity and service-acquired knowledge is more effective than the knowledge obtained in return.\nIt is more effective. Brahma and Narada, the guru of the Ka Parampra, never ask for dollars and cents (rupees and paise). An honest disciple, by pleasing him with his prestigious service, has to attain the knowledge of the relationship between the soul and the Supreme Soul, and\nof the nature. Whatever is the form, whatever is the base, from whom this creation is, O Lord.\nWhatever is established, whatever is supreme, whatever is to be obtained, speak the essence of that from the essence. 2>. Lexico