          "textEn": "25\nSa esha bhagavalliddrastriabhiratairadho kshajah.\nSvalakshyat-gatirbrahman sarveshanam mama chaishvarah. 20>. Meaning of the word:\nSah--He; Eshah--This; Bhagavan--God; Linghaih--From the characteristics; Tribhih--Three; Etaih--From all of these; Adhokshajah--The supreme seer;\nBrahma; Su-Alakshit--Invisible; Gatih--Movement; Brahman-He Narada; Sarvesham--Of each; Mama-Mine; Cha--Also; Ishwarah--\nThe controller.\nO Brahmin Narada, the supreme seer, the supreme Brahman, beyond the perception of the senses of living beings,\nis the controller of all, including me. Meaning: In the Bhagavad Gita (7.24-25), God has clearly declared that a non-differentialist\nwho gives more importance to the divine rays of the Brahman-light form of God and concludes\nthat the ultimate truth is actually non-differential, and when necessary, reveals its form, such\na non-differentialist is less intelligent than a qualified one, no matter how much he\nengages in the study of Vedanta.\nThe fact is that such a non-differentialist remains covered by the aforementioned qualities, therefore he\nremains unable to reach God. God is not accessible to every individual, because they\nremain covered by their yogmaya power. But from this, it should not be concluded that\nGod was originally unmanifest and now appeared in human form. This delusion of God's formlessness\nis due to the veil of illusion of God's yoga. And when a bound soul goes to the refuge of God,\nit can be removed only by supreme desire. The devotees of God, who remain beyond the above three qualities,\nin devotion, with their loving gaze, can see the all-blissful divine form of them. Kalam karma swabhavam cha mayesho mayaya swaya.\nThe self, having obtained it by chance, adorns the feet. 29). Meaning of the word\nKaalam-- Eternal time; Karma-- destiny of the individual; Swabhavam-- nature; Cha-- also; Maya-- power; Ishaan-- controller; Mayayaa-- by power; Swayam-- by oneself; Atman-- (of) oneself; Yachchhaya-