ययः ॥ ९८ ॥\nशब्दार्थं",
          "textEn": "249\nSelf-dhishnya--Radiation, light; Pratapan--In detail; Prana--Life; Bahi--Outside; Cha--Also; Pratapati--Illuminates;\nAso--Sun; Evam--Like this; Virajamu--Virat Swaroopah; Pratapan--In detail; Tapati--Provides life; Antar--From within; Bahi--From outside; Pumanu--The Supreme Being.The sun expands its radiation to distribute light inside and outside. In this\nway, God, expanding His immense form, nurtures every object in this creation from within\nand from outside. Meaning: Here, a clear explanation has been given of the immense form of God, or of His formless nature, which is called Brahmajyoti, and its comparison has been made with the distribution of sunlight. Sun-\nlight can spread throughout the entire universe, but the solar realm or Surya Narayan, which is its source, is the same\nsuch light is the root of it. Similarly, Lord Krishna is the basis of the formless nature of Brahmajyoti or God. This is confirmed in the Bhagavad Gita (14.27).\nTherefore, the immense form of God is a secondary concept of his\nunique form, but the original form is the two-handed one, playing his eternal flute,\nthe form of Shyamasundar. Seventy-five percent of the distributed light of God is expressed in the spiritual sky (Tripat\nVibhuti), and the remaining twenty-five percent illuminates the overall expansion of the physical universes. Its discussion and mention are found in the Bhagavad Gita (10.42). In this way, of God's\nlight, seventy-five percent expansion is called their inner power, and the remaining twenty-five percent\nis called outer power, §. All beings are residents of spiritual and physical expanses, they are called their\ntatstha power and they get the freedom that whether they live in external power or internal power\nin. Those who live within the spiritual expanse of God are called liberated souls, and those who\nlive in external expansion are bound souls.\nWe can estimate the number of inhabitants of these two expanses,\nand easily reach this conclusion that the number of souls is much greater than the number of bound souls.\nCompared to the number. That which is beyond the fear of death, that which is the essence of immortality, that which is the food of the dead.\nThat which is the greatness, then that is the difficulty of the Supreme Being. 98. Shabdharam"
