          "textEn": "273\nThat is, they are also fathers of fathers. A young man takes advice from his experienced father to perform his duty, therefore the father is the best advisor. But Brahma is the father of all fathers. He is the father of Manu, the father of all mankind. Therefore, the people of this small\nworld should kindly accept Brahma's command, and it would be good if they, instead of measuring the powers of God,\nsurrender themselves at His lotus feet. His powers are\nInfinite, as is attested by the Vedas-- Parasyasti vividhai vashyakrutveva shrüyate swabhāvika jñānabalakriyāccha.\n(TYR Upanishad 6.8). They are greater than all and other people, even the best among all living beings,\nEven Brahmaji accepts that the best for us would be to take refuge in them.\nTherefore, those who are ignorant, they claim to be all-powerful, but how much is their reach?\nHow much is their reach? They cannot measure even the length and breadth of a small universe. The so-called\nPhysicist says that it will take him forty thousand years to reach the highest realm of the universe,\nif he makes this journey by spacecraft. This is also an imaginary ideal, because no person\ncan live for forty thousand years. In addition to this, when the astronaut returns,\nthen, as it has become a fashion among today's fascinated scientists, as the best astronaut,\nthere will be no one to welcome him. A scientist who did not believe in God, was very eager to make plans for his\nphysical life, therefore he opened a very big hospital to save living people. But within six months of opening the hospital,\nhe himself died.\nTherefore, man should not waste his human life in the name of economic progress and the development of scientific knowledge by repeatedly changing bodies in eighty-four lakh species.\nDo not waste it in vain for the sake of artificial needs by increasing artificial necessities for a fabricated materialistic life. Instead of life,\nTo solve all the sorrows, take refuge in the feet of God. In the Bhagavad Gita, the Lord\nThis is the order of Krishna, and in the Shrimad Bhagavat, this is also the order of Brahmaji, the supreme father of all beings.\nIs."
