          "textEn": "274\nThe person who denies the self-surrender (sharanagati) prescribed by the Bhagavad Gita and Shripadudhavagvat,\nand thus rejects all valid scriptures, must be compelled to surrender to the laws of nature. According to its natural state,\na living being is not independent. He must surrender himself to God or Nature. Nature also\nis not independent from God, because God himself has called nature 'Mama Maya,' meaning 'My illusion.'\n(In Bhagavad Gita 7.14) and 'Se bhitra Prakritirashtadha,' meaning 'Eightfold'\nMy different power, thus addressing. Therefore, nature is also under the control of God, as he has claimed-- Mayadhyakshena Prakriti: Suyatechcharam-- In my supervision, this\nnature works, and in this way, all things are in motion.\nIn comparison to living beings, 98 (para)\ndue to the power, whether one surrenders to God, or to nature, hl. By surrendering to God, man becomes happy and liberated, but by surrendering to nature, the living being\nsuffers only sorrow. Therefore, the end of all sorrows means surrendering to God,\nbecause this process of surrendering is itself liberation from all material sufferings,\nthe experience of all happiness, and the letter of all welfare, O. Therefore, only by surrendering oneself to God can one attain freedom, happiness, and good fortune,\nbecause they are completely independent, completely full of happiness, and completely full of well-being. Such liberation and happiness\nare also boundless, and their comparison is not even with the sky, although they are even greater than the sky.\nWe\nunderstand this vastness in our current state by comparing it to the sky. We are unable to measure the sky, but the happiness and liberation that is attained from the presence of God is greater than the sky\nin comparison. This spiritual bliss is so vast that even God himself cannot measure it,\nThere is no question of others. It is said in the scriptures, 'Brahmasukhyam Tvanantam'-- spiritual bliss is boundless. 