          "textEn": "299\nAfter the age of 299, a woman, abandoning the company of women, enters the Vanaprastha Ashram and practices living without a woman.\n©. When the practice is completed, the Vanaprasthi takes Sanyas and separates himself from women,\neven from his married wife. It appears from the study of the complete plan of being separated from a woman that Khe is an obstacle in the path of self-realization, and God\nappeared only to give the education of being separated from Khe in the form of Narayan. The ascetics of the steadfast celibate\nBeing envious of life, the gods send the soldiers of Kama Deva with the intention of breaking his vow. But none of them worked in front of God, because when the divine beautiful women saw that their\ncountless beautiful women could appear from Yogmaya and they do not need to be externally attracted.\nIt is a common saying that a confectioner never tempts with sweets. A confectioner who always makes sweets\ndoes not have any desire to eat them; similarly, God, with the power of his heart, can create countless\ndivine beautiful women, therefore they are not even slightly attracted to the six beautiful women of the material world.\nThey are not attracted. He who does not know this, foolishly blames that Lord Krishna in the Raas Leela of Vrindavan\nwith women or with his sixteen thousand wives in Dwarka, he enjoyed. Those who burn desires, not with anger,\nBurning anger, it is not tolerable.\nHe who enters the inner fire,\nHow can his mind be pleased again? 7/. Meaning of the word:\nWork-desire; Dahanti- they punish; Krittinah- great leaders; Nanu- but; Rosh- with anger; Chitvan- with anger;\nRosham- anger; Dahantam- burned; Ut- although; Te- they; Na- not; Dahanti- they subdue; Ahahyam- unbearable; Sah- he;\nAyam-- that; Yat-- why; Antaram-- inside; Alam-- still; Pravishan-- going inside; Bibhiti-- is afraid;\nHTH: -- by desire; Katham- how; Nu- in fact; Punah-- again; Asya- his; Manah- mind; Shrayet- taking refuge; He \\. A great man like Shiva, by defeating desire (Kama) with his averted gaze, can\ndestroy it, but he himself cannot be freed from the excessive influence of his anger. Such anger\nnever enters their (God's) heart, because they are above it. Then how can desire take refuge in their mind?"
