          "textEn": "300\n\nMeaning: When Shivji was engrossed in rigorous penance, Kamdev released his Kama-bana (weapon of love) on him.\nShiv looked at Kamdev with an intensely angry gaze, and his body was immediately reduced to ashes. Shiva, despite being so powerful, was not free from the effects of anger. But in the conduct of Lord Vishnu,\nnever was any incident of anger found. In contrast, when Bhrigu Muni deliberately\nstrike his chest with his feet, instead of being angry at the Muni, the Lord asked for forgiveness by saying\nthat his chest is very hard, maybe the Muni's feet were hurt more. The Bhrigu-pada mark on the chest of the Lord symbolizes his tolerance.\nTherefore, when God is not affected by any\nkind of anger, then how can there be any place for less powerful desire than anger? When work or desire is not fulfilled, anger arises; therefore, when anger\nis absent, how can desire arise? God is called Aptakam, because\nHe himself fulfills his desires. They do not need anyone's help to fulfill their desires. God is infinite, so is his desires. Besides God, all\nliving beings are finite in every way, therefore, how can those who are finite satisfy the desires of the infinite?\nHow can they? The conclusion that came out is that in the complete Purushottam Bhagwan, there is neither lust, nor anger, and if ever in them\nIf one even has a glimpse of them, then it should be considered a supreme blessing (vow).\nThose who are wise, praise the lotus-like feet of the kings,\nEven the young ones, humbly surrender to the penance in the forest.\nTherefore, they praise the divine movement, the joyful ones,\nThe divine sages praise from the top of the mountain. 8 ”. Meaning of the word\n\nViddhah- Bidha kar; Sapali- stepmother; Udit- said; Patribhih- by them; Anti- in front; Rajah- of the king;\nBalah- child; Api- although; San- while; Upaghatah- having received; Tapase- rigorous penance; Vanani- in the forest;\nTasme- therefore; Adad- in return; Shruv- having hea