          "textEn": "307\nHaving become, he granted them the world, which all the sages of the upper and lower worlds worship. Meaning: When Prince Dhruva, the son of Maharaj Uttanpad and a great devotee, was five years old, he\nwas sitting in his father's lap, but his stepmother did not like her stepson being treated in this way by the king. He pulled them out saying that since she\nis not born from his womb, therefore she has no right to sit in the king's lap. That little boy\nwas extremely humiliated by this behavior of his stepmother. His father did not make any objection,\nbecause he was very attached to his second wife. After this incident, Prince\nDhruva went to his mother and complained. Even her own mother could not take any steps against this disgraceful behavior,\nthat's why she started crying. The boy asked his mother how he could\nsit on his father's throne. The poor mother said that only God could be her helper.\nCould be.\nThe boy asked where God would be found, so the mother said that people say that God\nis sometimes seen by sages in dense forests. The young prince decided that he would go to the forest and perform rigorous penance to fulfill his goal. Prince Dhruva, according to the orders of his guru Narad, who was specially appointed by God for this task, performed rigorous penance. Narada initiated him into chanting the eight-syllable mantra. This is the mantra-- Om Namo Bhagavate Vasudevaya. Lord Vasudeva himself, in the form of a four-armed Lord named Prishigarbha,\nhaving incarnated, granted the prince a special abode above the seven constellations. After achieving success in his\npenance, Prince Dhruva had a vision of God. Thus,\nall their wishes were fulfilled and they were satisfied. The world that Rajukmar Dhuv Maharaj got, that is, by the will of Lord Vasudeva, in the material sky,\nis the immovable Vaikuntha world.\nOf this world, even though in the physical world, at the time of destruction,\nthere will be no destruction, rather it will remain immovable in its place. Because this is the ever-destroying Vaikuntha"
