          "textEn": "38\nWill do. Such a purification process will generate faith in the expected devotee, and it will take him up to the divine\ndelicious state of devotion. This will generate pure attachment towards devotion to God, and\nbefore bringing it to the state of divine love, it will take it to the point of Aahvad.\n\nTwo sub-divisions of such knowledge related to devotion can be made--initial knowledge related to the nature of devotion\nand secondary knowledge of its adherence. In the Bhagwat, there is a description of Shri Bhagwan, his beauty,\nhis glory, fame, prestige, attraction, and divine qualities that attract towards oneself in exchange for love.\nThere is a description of that. There is an innate attraction in the soul to have devotion and love for God. Due to the influence of physical\ncoordination, this attraction is artificially covered, and Shrimad Bhagwat is helpful in removing this artificial\ncovering in the right way. Therefore, there is a special mention in this verse that\nSrimad Bhagavat is like a lamp of divine knowledge.\nIn devotion, these two sub-divisions of divine knowledge manifest in those individuals\nwho have taken refuge in Vasudeva, as it is said in the Bhagavad Gita (7.19)--\nIn the lotus feet of Vasudeva, the great men who are completely dedicated are extremely rare.\n\nThe cycle is also beyond the directions, in the ten directions, the radiance of the self.\nIn the cycles of time, the Manu dynasty's heroes are adorned. Among the wicked, he showed his own fame in the kingdom.\nIn truth, he established three-fold fame and character. 20>. Meaning of the word\n\nChakram-- the Sudarshan Chakra of Lord; Cha-- also; Dikshu-- in all directions; Avihitam-- without any obstruction; Dashasu-- ten;\nDishasu-- directions; Swa-tejah-- of one's own valor; Manvantheshu-- in the various incarnations of Manu; Manu-vansha-dharah-- as the descendants of Manu;\nBibharti- rules; Dushteshu- on the wicked; Rajasu- on the kind of kings; Dhamam-