          "textEn": "323\nYasma adadudadhirudha-bhayanka-vepo\nMarga sapadyaripuram haravad didhakshoḥ.\nDure suhṇmāthit-rāśa-sūṇa-dṛṣṭyaya\nTatapyamāna-makaroraga-nakra-chakraḥ. 24). Meaning of the word:\n\nYasam- to whom; Adat- provided; Udadhih- the Indian Ocean; Udad- fearful; Ank- disguise; Koppate- with a frightened body;\nMargam- path; Sapadi- immediately; Ari- city of enemies; Har- like Hari (Lord); Didhaksho- to burn;\nUdyat- rising; Dure- at a distance; Su- close- close friend; Mathit- afflicted; Rosh- in anger; Su- red- red; Dashtaya- with such a sight;\nTatapyaman- blazing; Makar- crocodile; Urag- snake; Nak- gharial; Chakrah- circle, Gola.\n\nSri Ramachandra, saddened by the separation from his beloved consort (Sita), cast his gaze with burning red eyes like Har (Shiva, who wanted to destroy the kingdom of heaven) on the city of his enemy Ravana.\nThe immense ocean gave way to him, trembling with fear, because his family\nAll the aquatic members, such as crocodiles, snakes, and gharials, were being burned by the blood-red eyes of God.\nThey were being burned by the fire of wrath. Meaning: Even in God, there is a feeling like other sensitive beings, because they are the main and\nprimary person and the ultimate source of all other living beings ©. They are eternal, are prominent, that is, all other\neternal souls are prominent. These are dependent on many subordinate, eternal, and constant things, therefore qualitatively\nIn form, these two are the same. Because of this uniformity, all the emotional experiences are in both these eternities,\nbut there remains a difference in quantity in the greatness of the main eternity and the subordinate eternity. When Ramchandraji\nbecame angry and started showing red, red burning eyes, the entire ocean became so heated by that power\nthat all its aquatic creatures started burning, and the actual ocean began to tremble with fear. He gave them a simple way to reach the enemy's\ncity.\nNirvikarvadis may object to this 