          "textEn": "332\nMan gets freedom from worldly suffering. Here, it is clearly mentioned that the residents of Vrindavan, day\nAll day, they used to work hard and, tired, they used to sleep peacefully at night. Therefore, they rarely got much time to worship or\nfor spiritual activities on) but in reality, they were immersed in the best\nspiritual works. His every action was spiritual, because he was connected to Shri Krishna's\nrelationships. Their works were centered around Shri Krishna, as a result of which all their\nso-called material works were saturated with spiritual power. This is the benefit of the Bhakti Yoga method. Man should do every work for the sake of God, and in this way, all his actions will be saturated with the thoughts of Krishna, which is the highest form of Samadhi in spiritual realization.\nGopa-makha pratihate Vraja-viplavaya\nDeva abhivarshati pan, krupaya rirakshuha.\nDhartochilinndhamiva sapta-dinani sapta-\nvarsho mahishramadhayakare salilam. 22). Shabdarth--By the cowherds; Makhe- in offering sacrifices to the king of heaven; Pratihate-- when obstacles are removed; Vraj- Viplavay--\nTo devastate the land of Vraja, the abode of Shri Krishna; Deve- by the king of heaven; Avivarshati- by rain;\nPashun- animals; Kripya- by unselfish grace; Rirakshuh- desiring to save them; Dharta- possessed;\nUchchili- lifted like a roof; Iva- like; Sapta- seven days; Sapta- years, though they were seven years old; Mahadhramu- Govardhan Parvat; Anagha- without tiredness; Ek-kare--in one hand; salil-in play. When the cowherds of Vrindavan stopped offering to Indra, the king of heaven, on the orders of Shri Krishna,\nthen for seven consecutive days, there was torrential rain, and it seemed as if all\nThe land of Braj will be washed away. Then, at the age of only seven, Shri Krishna, out of his selfless grace, lifted Govardhan Parvat with one hand on the residents of Braj. They did this only to protect the animals from the rain. Meaning: Children often play with marbles (kurkure). It was when Shri Krishna was just seven years old that\nhe grabbed the huge Govardhan mountain located in Vrindavan and lifted it like a umbrella, and\nbecause the residents of Vraja refused to offer sacrifices to Indra, he continuously held it for seven days to protect the animals and\nthe residents of Vraja from Indra's wrath."
