          "textEn": "344\nVidur, Shrutadev, etc., are also known. Meaning: As said above, all the devotees of God who have existed in the past or present,\nand all the devotees who will exist in the future, they are aware of the various powers of God, along with their names,\nqualities, pastimes, associates, personalities, etc., and the powers of the soul. How do they know about it? Certainly, this does not happen through imagination or with the limited help of the senses. Through limited sense organs,\nwe are not able to properly know the physical powers of God that appear before our eyes,\nwhether they are physical senses or instruments like physical microscopes and telescopes. For example,\nBeyond the calculation of scientists, there are countless planets (lokas), but these are only the form of God's physical power.\nWhat can a scientist expect to know about the spiritual power of God through such physical efforts? “If knowledge cannot progress from dozens of imaginations like “if” and “maybe,”\ninstead, such imagination will lead to the denial of the existence of God, and\nonly disappointment will be achieved, and the matter will be dismissed. Therefore, a wise man does not contemplate subjects beyond the limits of his small brain, but rather he learns to surrender himself to God,\nbecause only he can lead him to true knowledge. There is a clear mention in the Upanishads\nthat merely by doing hard work and exerting pressure on the brain, one can never know the Supreme Personality of Godhead,\nnor can He be known through imagination and verbal tricks. Only he knows God,\nwho is surrendered to Him. Here, among the living beings, the supreme, Brahmaji, has accepted this truth.\nTherefore,\none should stop wasting energy in following the path of experimental knowledge. Man should\nbe devoted to God and, accepting the authenticity of the authorized men described here, gain knowledge. Because God is infinite, by the grace of Yogmaya, He helps the surrendered to know Himself\nin a sequential manner."
