          "textEn": "358\nAgun is said, so it does not mean that they are without qualities, but rather that they do not have physical qualities like Satva,\nRajo, or Tamo Guna, which are found in bound souls. Because they are beyond all material\nconcepts, they are called Agun. I wish to know this truth, the knower of truth, the best.\nThe story of Hari's wonderful power, the people are amazed. 2>. Meaning of the word\n'Etat- This; Veditum-- to know; Ichhami-- I wish; Tattvam- Truth; Tattvavidam- Of the knowers of truth; Var- Hey\nThe best; Hareh- Of God; Adduut- Of the mighty; Kathah- Story; Lok- All the worlds; Su-\nHG: - Auspicious, welfare-causing L\nThe king said: I am eager to know §. Stories related to the God endowed with wonderful powers\nare surely auspicious for all the beings of the world. Meaning: Shrimad Bhagwat is full of descriptions of the Lord's pastimes and is auspicious for the beings residing in every realm.\nHe who considers it related to a particular sect,\nHe certainly remains in delusion. Shrimad Bhagavat is a very dear branch for all the devotees of God,\nbut it is also beneficial for the non-devotees, because according to it, even the non-devotees who are caught in the cycle of material power\nare freed from the clutches if they listen to the story of Ramadbhagavat from the appropriate source of the disciple tradition\nwith faith and devotion. Tell me, O great one, how I am in my own self. Krishna, investing, without accumulation, without desiring the body. 3. Meaning of the word\nKathayasva-- Please tell me; Mahabhag-- Oh, most fortunate one; Yatha-- As; Aham-- I; Akhil-Atmani- Supreme Soul\nTo; Krishno- Lord Shri Krishna; Nivasya-- By establishing; Nishaddham-- Free from worldly qualities; Manah-- By abandoning the mind; Tyaakshye-- By ceasing the mind\nTo be able to renounce; Kalevaram-- Body L\nOh, most fortunate Shukadeva Goswami, please continue to narrate the Srimad Bhagavatam to me\nSo that I can fix my mind on the Supreme Soul, Lord Shri Krishna, and thus on worldly qualities\nHaving become completely free, abandon this body of yours."
