          "textEn": "380\n\nMeaning: A guru is always ready to impart knowledge to his disciple, especially when\nthe disciple is very eager. It is imperative for the progressive disciple to keep up the momentum. King Parikshit was a unique disciple, because he was very eager. If someone is not very eager for self-realization,\nthen one should not go to the guru just to show discipleship. Maharaj Parikshit is not only eager to know about what he has asked, but also eager to know about what\nhe could not ask. In reality, it is not possible for a human to ask every single thing from a guru, but an authentic guru can illuminate it in every way for the benefit of the disciple.\nHere, you are the proof, the supreme being, as the self-existent.\nOthers also perform, those who were before the former. 25. Shabdartham\n\nAtra--in this context; Pramanam--evidence; Hi--certainly; Bhavan--you; Parameshi--Brahma, the primeval being of the universe; Yatha--as; Jatha--as; Atmabhoo-h – directly from God; Apara- -others; Cha- only; Anutisthanti- only; Anukaran- for imitation;\nPurvesham- according to the previous; Purv-ja- suggested by the previous philosopher; Krutam- done; Kriyaman- in the process of doing;\nPurvesham- according to the previous; Purva- -the previous; Jatha- as; Atmabhoo-h – directly from God; Apara- -others; Cha- only; Anukaran- for imitation;\nPurvesham- according to the previous; Purva-ja- suggested by the previous philosopher; Krutam- done; Kriyaman- in the process of doing;\nPurvesham- according to.\n\nOh, great sage, you are equal to the original creator Brahma. Other people only follow tradition.\nJust as previous philosophers used to do. Meaning: Here, this argument can be made that Shukdev Goswami is not the only authority on complete spiritual knowledge-- many other sages and their followers are also authorities. Of Vyasadeva,\nContemporary or even before him, there have been many great sages, such as Gautam, Kanad, Jaimini, Kapil, and\nAshtavakra, and all of them have presented their respective philosophical paths.\nPatanjali is also one of them. Thus, these six sages, like modern philosophers and thinkers, have their own\nideas. The difference between the philosophical paths of Srisukdev Goswami and the aforementioned six sages is that while the six sages describe facts according to their contemplation, in Shrimad Bhagwat, Shukdev\nGoswami presents the knowledge received directly from Brahma, who is called Atmabho, meaning those who\nare born from the almighty God and are educated by Him."
