          "textEn": "38]\n\nThe descent of divine knowledge from God comes directly. By the grace of God, the first living being in the universe,\nBrahmaji attained knowledge; from Brahma, Narad received knowledge, and from Narad, Vyasa received knowledge. Shukdev Goswami\nreceived this divine knowledge directly from his father, Vyasadeva. Thus, the knowledge obtained through the disciple lineage\nis complete. Until a disciple receives knowledge through tradition, he cannot become a complete guru.\nUnless he becomes one. This is the secret to attaining divine knowledge. Even though the aforementioned six sages are great thinkers,\ntheir knowledge gained through contemplation is not complete. In presenting philosophical opinions, no one, even if a knower,\nhowever complete, such knowledge is never complete, because it is the product of an incomplete brain.\nSuch sages also have disciple traditions, but these are not authentic, because they\nare not obtained from the independent, complete Supreme Personality of Godhead, Narayana.\nNo one can be independent except Narayana,\nTherefore, no one's knowledge is complete, because everyone's knowledge is dependent on the fluctuating mind. The mind is a substance, therefore, the knowledge presented by the materialist can never be divine and complete.\nCannot be. A worldly philosopher, due to being incomplete himself, maintains differences with other philosophers, and a worldly\nphilosopher, because he has no own principles, that philosopher can never be called. Maharaj,\nIntelligent men like Parikshit do not believe in these thinkers, no matter how great they may be. They only listen from an officer like Shukdev Goswami, who is inseparable from Shri Bhagavan through tradition and method,\nas it is said with special emphasis in the Bhagavad Gita.\nThey do not attain Brahman by drinking the nectar of the un-drunk.\nThey drink the un-drunk, that which is poured out in the ocean of speech. 26). Meaning of the word:\n\nN--Never; Me--mine; Asavah--~ life; Parayanti--can come out; Brahmanu--Oh learned Brahmin; Anashnat--due to fasting; Ami--that which is not; Pibatah--due to drinking; Achyuta--not falling; Piyusham--nectar; Tat--that which is yours; Vakya-abdhi--\nFrom the ocean of speech; Vinirasrutam--coming out;\nOh learned Brahmin, due to drinking the nectar of the story of Lord Kau, which is flowing from the ocean of your speech,\nI do not feel any kind of weakness due to fasting."
