          "textEn": "399\nA human being, by obtaining this divine sound from a suitable source, accepting it as truth, without any hesitation,\nhas to follow it as an order. The secret of success is that sound should be received from an authentic guru, that is,\nfrom a suitable source. In worldly artificial sounds, there is no power; therefore, in the so-called divine sound obtained from an unreliable person, there is no power. One should have the ability to know such divine power,\nand one should be able to receive this divine sound through the Guru, either through their own discretion or by fate.\nUnderstand that the path to their liberation has been paved. But the disciple should be ready to follow the Guru's orders, just as Brahmaji followed the orders of his authentic Guru, the direct God. The disciple's only duty is to obey the orders of the authentic guru, and\nto follow the orders of the authentic guru with complete faith is the secret to success.\nBrahmaji gained control over both types of his senses, the sensory organs of action and the sensory organs of knowledge,\nbecause their use was to be in obeying the orders of God. Therefore, the meaning of controlling the senses is\nto engage them in the divine service of God. God's order is revealed through an authentic guru to the disciple-\ntradition, therefore, following the order of an authentic guru means truly\ncontrolling the senses. With complete faith and devotion, by doing such penance, Brahmaji could become so powerful\nthat he became the destroyer of the universe. It is because of attaining such power that they are called the best among ascetics.\nTo Him, the Supreme Lord, who is self-existent,\nI offer that which is supreme, which is beyond all else.\nThat which is free from distress, delusion, and suffering,\nThat which is established in one's own vision. 9>. Meaning of the word:\nTasmai-to him; Swa-lokam--own abode; Bhagavan--Bhagavan; Sabhagitah--pleased by Brahma's penance; San-sandarshayam--\nAs- revealed; Param- supreme; Na-- not; Yat- whose; Param- even greater than that; Vyapt- completely absorbed;\nSad- suffering- the five types of material heat; Vimoh- without attachment; Sadhvasam- fear of the world; Swa- created- by oneself; Vaddhi- increased;\nBy them; Purusha- by men; Adhishtutam- worshiped; I-\nShri Bhagavan, being exceedingly pleased with Brahmaji's penance, in all the worlds Shreshth"
