          "textEn": "474\n\nMay you be blessed. Hey Brahma, you can ask me for whatever you want, because I am the giver of all boons.\nYou should know that the ultimate boon that is obtained as a result of all penance is\nmy darshan (vision). Meaning: The ultimate realization of God is to know Him and to have a vision of Him. The realization of the formless Brahman and the omnipresent Supreme Soul is not the ultimate realization. When a person realizes God,\nhe no longer needs to struggle for such penance.\nIs. The second stage of life is to worship God to please Him. In other words, this\ncan be said that one who has seen God has attained complete perfection, because\neverything is included in that supreme perfection. But the nirvikar (without attributes) and chhadayogi (one who practices chhadayoga) cannot attain this state. Maneeshitanubhavahayam mama loakavalokanam.\nYadupashrutya rahasi chakartha paramam tapah. 22). Meaning of the word:\n\nManisit-- adorned; Anubhava-- vision; Ayam-- this; Mama-- mine; Lok-- abode; Avalokanam-- observing with real experience;\nYat-- because; Upashrutya-- having heard; Rahasi-- in supreme penance; Having completed the Chakartha; Paramam-- supreme; Tapah-- penance;\n\nThe highest perfection is adornment, the direct vision of my abode, and this vision has been possible for you according to my order,\nbecause of your humble inclination towards rigorous penance. Meaning: The ultimate achievement of life, the state of being, is to know God through the fruit of God's grace, through real vision. Its attainment can be possible for every person who is devout and\ndesires devotional service according to the standard accepted by authentic teachers. For example,\nBhagavat Gita, all the great Acharyas, such as Shankar, Ramanuj, Madhva, Chaitanya, Vishwanath, Baldev, Siddhanta,\nSaraswati, and many others, is an authentic Vedic text accepted by them.\nIn the same Bhagavad Gita, God\nShri Krishna says that keep my meditation, become my devotee, worship me, and always in front of me\neT | By doing this, undoubtedly, a man's journey to the abode of God is certain. In other places also"
