          "textEn": "433\nRithe--without essence; Artham--essence; Yat--that; Pratyeyet--appears; Na--not; Pratyeyet--appears; Cha--and; Atmani--in the self; Tat--that; Vidyat--should know; Atmanah--myself; Mayamu--illusion; Yatha--as; Abhasah--reflection;\nYatha--as; Tamah--darkness;\nO Brahma, whatever appears to be full of essence, if it is not related to me, then there is no\nreality in it. Know this as my illusion, consider this as such a reflection that appears in darkness\nand disappears. Meaning: In the previous verse, it has been concluded that any state of the universe,\nregarding the cause, is--its origin, preservation, development, the mutual actions of various powers, its destruction, and\ndisappearance--are all connected to the existence of God. Therefore, whenever this fundamental relationship with God\nis forgotten, and objects are considered true without being associated with God, then\nthis concept should be understood as the result of God's illusion.\nBecause without God, no object can exist, therefore Maya is also a power of God. The appropriate conclusion of relating each object to God is called ** Yogamaya**, and the deluded\nperception (vichched) of separating from God is called God Ka Daivika Maya or ^“Mahamaya” (the great illusion). Both types of Maya\nhave a relationship with God, because without their relationship, no object can exist. Thus, the false notion of separation from God is not false but illusory.\n\nTo understand one thing as another is called illusion. For example, considering a rope as a snake\nis an illusion, but the rope is not false (mithya). The rope is not false in the presence of a delusional person. Yes, it is a confusion to think of it as a serpent. Therefore, regarding the physical existence, the erroneous perception of God\nbeing freed from power is an illusion, but it is not false.\nThis tired perception is the reflection (shadow) of truth in the darkness of ignorance. Any object that does not seem to be produced by \"my power,\"\nthat is Maya. To consider the soul formless or the concept of God being formless is also an illusion. In the Bhagavad Gita (2.12), the Lord, while standing on the battlefield, says that Arjuna, standing before Arjuna,\nthe warrior and the Lord himself, were also present before this, they are also present in the battlefield of Kurukshetra, and\neven in the future, even after the destruction of the present body and after getting rid of worldly bondage, in this form"
