          "textEn": "460\nis based, and the meaning of liberation from old age is that the senses should be directed towards beholding the beauty of God, listening to His\nglory, and performing actions for Him. In this way, Brahmaji again created the works of the universe. The lord of the world, the lord of righteousness, is the one who is always in the rules and regulations.\n\nHe is the one who is always in the service of the good, desiring the welfare of the people. 40>. Meaning of the word\nPraja-patih--ancestors of all living beings; Dharma-patih--father of Dharma; Ekada--once; Niyaman--regulations; Yaman--\nRules of restraint; Bhadram-- welfare; Prajanaam--of living beings; Anvichhan--by wish; Atishthat--being situated; Swa-Artha--self-interest;\nKamyaya--desiring TCL\nOnce, the ancestor of living beings and the father of Dharma, Brahmaji, in the welfare of all living beings,\nunderstanding his own interest, followed the rules of Yama and Niyama according to the rules. Meaning: no one can occupy a high position without observing the Yama-nitrs.\nIndriya Trupti's unrestrained life is animal life, and Brahmaji taught his descendants to have restraint on the senses for the sake of higher duties. They were desiring the welfare of all as servants of God.\nAnd whoever desires the welfare of their family and generations has to lead a moral and religious life.\nOne has to live. The highest moral life is to become a devotee of God, because in a pure devotee, all the qualities of God\nappear. On the contrary, one who is not devoted to God, no matter how much he may be in worldliness,\ncannot be said to be endowed with any excellent quality.\nBrahma and many individuals from his disciple lineage who\nare pure devotees of God, until they themselves do not practice such behavior, they do not give any kind of advice to their dependents.\n\nThen Narada, the beloved, is devoted to giving alms.\n\nHe is engaged in service, with purity, with compassion, and with restraint. 49>.