          "textEn": "463\nIt is the declaration of the Vedas (Shadhhaitashatar Upanishad 6.33) —\n\nYasya deve para bhakti yatha deve tatha gurau. By his own words, these meanings are illuminated by the great soul. \"For whom unwavering devotion is towards God and the Guru, divine knowledge manifests itself.\nFor them.\"Such is the relationship between a disciple and his guru that is eternal. The one who is a disciple today will become a guru tomorrow. And until a disciple strictly follows the orders of his guru, he cannot become an authentic and\nvalid guru. Brahmaji, as a disciple, attained true knowledge from God and gave it to\nhis beloved disciple Narad. Similarly, Narad, in the form of a guru, transferred this knowledge to Vyasa,\nand it continued to happen in the future as well. Therefore, the so-called formal guru and disciple Brahma and\nNarad or Narad and Vyasa cannot be the embodiment.\nThe relationship between Brahma and Narada was a\nreality (truth), but the relationship between a fraud and the one who is deceived is a so-called formality. Here,\nit is clearly mentioned that Narada was not only polite, obedient, and obedient, but he was also self-controlled. He, who is not self-controlled, especially in a lustful life, can neither be a disciple, nor a guru. A human\nshould receive disciplinary education to control speech, anger, tongue, mind, stomach, and senses.\nShould receive. He who gains control over the aforementioned specific senses is called a Goswami. A Goswami\nWithout being, no disciple can become, nor a guru. Without self-control of the senses, the so-called guru is indeed\na deceiver (thug), and his disciple is deprived. We should not consider Brahmaji as dead, as we have experienced this world. They are the greatest\ngrandfather and are still alive. Narada is still there.\nIn the Bhagavad Gita, the residents of Brahmaloka\nmention the age ©. These small earth dwellers can hardly calculate the duration of one day of Brahma. Therefore, this 