          "textEn": "465\nMeaning of the word:\nNarad:--Narada Muni; Praah--gave advice; Munay- to the sage; Saraswatyah--of the Saraswati river; Tatte- on the bank; Nrip- O King;\nDhyayate- is meditated upon; Braham- the ultimate truth; Param- supreme; Parameshwar; Vyasay- to Srila Vyasadeva; Amit- endless; Tejase-\nTo the mighty..\n\nHey Rajan, in that same tradition, Narad Muni imparted the teachings of Shrimad Bhagavat to the infinitely powerful one\nVyasadeva, who, situated in devotion on the banks of the Saraswati River, was meditating on the ultimate truth, the complete Supreme Personality of Godhead. Meaning: In the fifth chapter of the first book of Shrimad Bhagavat, Narad gave the following advice to Vyasadeva--\n\nThen, O great one, you are free from all faults.\n\nYou are pure, truthful, and steadfast.\n\nYou are free from all bonds of action,\n\nYou are remembered in Samadhi, and you are not considered. The great and glorious Lord Jesus Christ! Your name and fame are universal, and you are spotless.\nYour character and unwavering vision are situated in the ultimate truth. I ask you to meditate on the incomparable divine plays of God.\nI ask you to do so.\nIn this way, in the Brahmasampradaya Kau Shishya-parampara, the practice of yoga and meditation is not neglected. But because of being devotees, devotees do not meditate on the formless Brahman. They are as if indicated here,\nThey meditate on the Supreme Brahman (FF WHY). The realization of Brahma begins with the light of Brahma.\nAnd by meditating more, one becomes aware of the Supreme Soul. Further progress leads to the realization of God. Shrinarad Muni, being the guru of Vyasadeva, was aware of Vyasadeva's position,\ntherefore he certified that Shrila Vyasadeva is fully devoted to the Lord's pastimes\nwith complete devotion. Narada gave a sermon on the divine plays of the Lord. Of the formless Brahman,\nthere is no play, but the plays of the with attributes Brahman are many, and all these plays are divine.\nAnd in these, there is not even a touch of material qualities. If the plays of the Supreme Being were material, then Narada would have"
