 या निष्कृष्ट शरीर ग्रहण करके इस संसार के जन्म-मरण के आवागमन में HEA |\nइसी में मानव जीवन की बुद्धिमत्ता है और मनुष्य को ऐसे ही जीवन के कार्यकलापों कौ आकांक्षा",
          "textEn": "47]\nThe solution is to return to the abode of God, where there is no rebirth like in the material world. Due to forgetting their relationship with the lord of Vaikuntha, the bound souls,\nunaware of this simple fact, try to make plans to live a permanent life in this material world. Due to being fascinated by external power, they become busy in various types of economic and religious development works,\nand they forget that their purpose is to return to the abode of God. Due to the influence of Maya, this forgetfulness becomes so strong that bound souls do not want to return to the abode of God.\nDue to sensual pleasures, they repeatedly get trapped in the cycle of birth and death, and they spoil human life, in which they get opportunities to return to the abode of God.\nIn various eras and\nin the ages, the authoritative branches that Manu created are called Sadharm. They are to show the right path to humans.\nTherefore, humans should use these\nscriptures for their own benefit and to make the end times successful. Creation is not false, but it is a temporary, temporary existence, and to provide an opportunity for bound souls to return to the abode of God,\nto go to God, the desire to go and the actions taken in that direction, only then does the path of karma become righteous. Those who follow such a regular path,\nGod provides unwilling protection to those devotees out of unintentional grace, but those who are non-devotees, they, being caught in the bondage of desires,\nBy getting trapped in the bonds of actions, put themselves in the risk of activities. In this context, the word 'Sadharm'\nis important. 'Sadharm' means to do one's duty to return to the abode of God, and in this way,\nto become a pure devotee is a sacred task. All other people pretend to be pure, but in reality, they are not\nlike that.\nTherefore, God has given the teaching in the Bhagavad Gita that a man should abandon all so-called\nreligious activities and engage in devotional service to God, so that he can be freed from all the sufferings of this material\nworld's dreadful life. To remain in righteousness, to work\nis the right direction of life. The purpose of man should be to go to the abode of God, not to temporarily\nAccepting an excellent or inferior body for life, in the cycle of birth and death of this world, HEA.\nIn this lies the intelligence of human life, and man should have such aspirations for the activities of life"
