          "textEn": "480) (v: God). The Shrimad Bhagavatam is indicative of the architect of the universe. Man should not be amazed by the mechanical structure of a motor or dynamo, but rather praise the engineer who made such a\namazing work machine. This is the only difference between a devotee and an un-devotee. Devotees always\npraise the Lord, the director of the physical laws. In the Bhagavad Gita (9.10), regarding the subject of material nature,\nGod's instruction is as follows--\n\nFrom the Maya (illusion) is created this moving and non-moving world.\n\nFrom the cause, O son of Kunti, this world is pervaded. *'The physical nature, complete with physical laws, is one of my various powers, therefore it is neither\nindependent nor purposeless. Because I am divinely omnipotent, therefore, by casting my gaze upon nature,\nthe laws of nature are functioning in such a wonderful way.\nTherefore, the action-reaction of physical laws\nkeeps happening, and this world is repeatedly created, sustained, and destroyed, O!\n\nBut those who are ignorant are amazed by the structure of the human body and the physical laws within this universe,\nand foolishly deny the existence of God by assuming that\nphysical laws are independent and have no supernatural control over them. The answer to such foolishness\nis found in the following words in the Bhagavad Gita (9.11)--\n\nThey do not know my other aspect, O foolish man.\n\nThey do not know my supreme being, O great one. “My foolish (stupid) people mock me when I incarnate in human form. They do not know the divine\nnature of God.' 'Foolish people consider God's divine body to be like their own body, therefore they\nare unable to think about the immense regulatory power of that God, who is not visible in the operation of physical laws.\nBut when God descends with his virtuous power, then it is visible to the eyes of ordinary people.\nLord Krishna appeared in his original form and he performed many wonderful deeds.\nAnd the relation of Bhagavad Gita is with such deeds and knowledge. Even then, the foolish people, Lord Krishna"
