          "textEn": "502\nIn the Bhagavad Gita (4.14), God says §—\nThey do not tremble, nor do they desire the fruits of action. Thus, he who does not know the fruits of actions, does not suffer the consequences. God is never bound by actions that he performs through his various incarnations,\nnor does he have the desire to achieve success through desire-driven actions. God is full of various powers of wealth, strength, fame,\nbeauty, knowledge, and sacrifice, therefore, like a bound soul, he does not need to work hard. Therefore, such a wise man who can differentiate between the divine works of God and\nthe works of bound souls, he also does not get bound in the fruits of karma. Brahma, Vishnu, and Shiva,\nin the form of, are the conductors of the three Gunas of nature. From Vishnu, Brahma, and from Brahma, Shiva are born. Sometimes, Brahma is a separate part of Vishnu, and sometimes, Brahma is Vishnu himself.\nIn this way, Brahma\nproduces all the species of the universe, which means that God creates the entire creation\neither creates it himself or through his appointed helper. The subjects, the gods, the sages, the ancestors, and the cows,\nThe accomplished charanas, Gandharvas, the Vidyadhars, and the Guhyakas. 37). Kinnarapsaraso Nagan Sarpaan Kimpurushannaran |\nMatru Rakshah-Pipaan Shra Preta-Bhuta-Vinayakan | 38. Kushmando-vetala-yatudhaan grhanaapi |\nKhagaanmrigaan pashun gruthaan girinrupa sariripaan | 39). Two kinds, four kinds, these others, water, land, and sky.\nWell-skilled, unskilled, mixed, actions, gone, these two. 40>. Meaning of words\nPraja-pati- Brahma and their sons, such as Daksha etc.; Manu- Vaivasvata Manu like the chiefs who occur from time to time; Devan-\nsuch as Indra, Chandra and Varuna; Rishis- such as Bhrigu and Vasistha; Pitra- Ganas- inhabitants of the world of ancestors; Prithak- separately;\nThe inhabitants of Siddha-Siddha Loka; Charan-- inhabitants of Charan Loka; Gandharvan-- inhabitants of Gandharva Loka; Vidyadhara- inhab