          "textEn": "508\nThen, during Brahma's night, without any obstruction, it is destroyed again. After the night of Brahma,\nonce again, it reappears in the form of creation, and once again, it goes through the cycle of nurturing and destruction. The\nfoolish, bound soul who considers this transient world to be permanent should learn this intelligently that why such\ncreations and destructions occur. The material world's desire-driven, God's material factors,\nare very eager to produce large industries, large houses, large empires, and other\nbig things from the physical substances and power provided. The bound soul, by originating from these means and valuable\npowers, fulfills its desire, but without the desire, it has to leave all these\nobjects and repeatedly enter the other aspect of life to originate in the same way.\nIt has to enter.\nTo such foolish, bound beings who waste their power in this transient world, God\nInforms them to be hopeful that there is another nature that is repeatedly created or destroyed\nWithout being eternal, and the bound being can understand what to do and how to make good use of power. Instead of destroying one's power in the substance that is destroyed in due course by supreme desire, the bound soul should engage this power in devotion to God, so that he can go to other\neternal nature where there is neither birth, nor death, nor creation, nor destruction--rather, there is full of knowledge\nand unlimited bliss, a life full of joy. Thus, the creation and destruction of this perishable world is only to awaken bound beings\nwho are attached to momentary things. Its purpose\nis to provide the bound soul with the opportunity for self-realization, not the sensual gratification that is the root goal of all motivated\nemployees. Thus it is said, the Lord, the Supreme Being.\nThey alone, by their own intellect, are able to see the supreme. 44). Meaning of the word\nIttham--in these forms; Bhavena--the thought of creation and destruct