          "textEn": "Uaa\n\nNature is also one of the powers of God, and it can only function by the order of God. When God casts his divine gaze upon nature, only then can it act, just as a mother conceives only through the contact of a father. Although an ignorant person thinks that a mother gives birth to a son,\nan experienced person knows that the father is the one who gives birth to the son. Therefore, physical nature, only when in contact with the Supreme Father, gives birth to inert and conscious substances, not independently.\nNo. Considering nature itself as the cause of creation, sustenance, and destruction is like arguing that the fleshy-\npods hanging around the goat's neck are its breasts.\"Srila Krishnadas Kaviraj Goswami in Sricharitamrita, explains Ajagalastan Tark in this way (as mentioned by Krishnakripamurti Shri Shrimad\nBhaktisiddhanta Saraswati Goswami Maharaj) “Prakriti is called the primary cause in the form of material nature,\nand Maya is called the efficient cause.\nBut, since this is inert matter, therefore this\nis not the indirect cause of creation.\" Kaviraj Goswami says it this way--\nTherefore, Krishna is the cause of the world. Nature-- The cause of this chaos. (Chaitanyachari Adi 5.61)\n\nSince Vishnu, the sustainer of causes, is a part of Shri Krishna, therefore, he alone provides the electrical power for the substance to become dynamic. The example of providing electricity (electrification) is appropriate. A piece of iron\nis certainly not fire, but when it is heated to redness, it acquires the property of fire due to its burning\ncapability. The substance is compared to a piece of iron, and this\nVishnu becomes electrified or red-hot from the consciousness or thought of Vishnu or from the reasoning. Thus,\nthrough electrification, the power of matter is manifested in various functions. Therefore, inert matter is neither\ncapable, nor the cause of the visible world.\nIn Bhagavat (3.28.40), Shri Kapila Deva says--\n\nYatholuyukad