          "textEn": "572\n\nThe original fire, its flame, its embers, and its smoke--all are one, because\nfire, even being fire, is different from flame, flame is different from spark, and smoke is different from smoke. In all of these,\nthat is, in the flames, sparks, and smoke, even if the power of fire exists, all these separate powers\nare separate from each other. The visible world is like smoke, because when smoke goes into the sky,\nit takes on many forms, which resemble many known and unknown forms. Chinigariyis are like living beings and the whirlwinds are like material nature (Prada). A person should know\nthat each of these shows its effect only because they have the power of the original fire\nof qualities. Therefore, all these are the physical nature, the visible world, and the living being, God (fire) <uh>\nare different powers. Therefore, those who consider nature as the root cause of the visible world (according to Samkhya philosophy,\nthe cause of creation is nature), they are not correct in their conclusion.\nOf physical nature,\ndoes not exist separately without God. Therefore, not revealing God as the cause of all causes\nis the argument of Ajagalstan's justice--even if the goat's udders appear milky, it is foolish to try to extract milk from them. This is the Brahma Kalpa, with one choice, illustrating the one choice. Vidhi: Sarvam Sarga: Prakrita-Vaikhruta. 46). Shabdartham\n\nAyam - This action of creation and destruction; Tu - but; Brahmanah - of Brahma; Kalpa - one day of Brahma; Sa-vikalpa -\nIncluding the duration of the universes; Udaharitah - as an example; Vidhih - rule; Sadharanah - common; Yatra - in which;\nSargah - creation; Prakrita - about nature; Vaikruthah - usage, expenditure..\n\nHere, in summary, this process of creation and destruction, described, is in the form of rules for one day (kalpa) of Brahma. This is also the regulator and mastermind of the creation - Nature which is discarded. Meaning: There are three types of creation: the great epoch, the alternation, and the epoch.\nIn the great era, God, the great essence,\nand along with the sixteen types of creative elements and all the powers of the means, takes the form of the first person incarnation of Vishnu, the cause-and-effect.\nThe number of creative causes is eleven, and the parts are five"
