          "textEn": "4\nShri-Shuka said -- Shri Shukadeva Goswami said; Yada-- when; Tu- but; Raja-- King Dhritarashtra; Swa- sons-- his own sons;\nAshadhun- dishonest; Pushnan- nourishing; Na- never; Dharmena- on the right path; Vinashtadrishti- whose inner vision is lost; Bhratuh- of his brother; Yavishthasya- younger, sons; Sutan- to his sons; Vibandhun- orphaned; Praveshya- to enter;\nLaksha- of lakhs; Bhavane- in the house; Dadah- set fire to..\n\nShri Shukdev Goswami said: King Dhritarashtra, having been driven by his unholy desires to raise his dishonest sons,\nhad lost his inner vision and had set fire to the house of lac to burn his fatherless\nnephews, the Pandavas. Meaning: Dhritarashtra was born blind, but his blindness in doing unholy deeds in support of his dishonest sons\nwas much greater than the physical defect of his eyesight. Due to the physical deficiency of sight, someone's\nspiritual progress does not stop.\nBut when someone, even while being physically healthy, is blind in a spiritual sense,\nthen that blindness is terribly obstructive in the path of human life.\n\nWhen the gods and goddesses\nare adorned with hair,\nthe king does not touch them, nor does the nurse touch them.\nTEE: Kuchakunku Mani. 7/. Meaning of the word\nYada-- when; Sabhayaam-- in the assembly; Kuru-deva-devyaiha-- like gods, Yudhishthira's wife Draupadi's; Kesha-abhimarshamu-- the insult of catching her hair; Suta-karma-- the work done by his son; Rein-nindyaha; Na-- river; Varyaam-aas-- refused;\nNripaaha-- king; Sushaayaaha-to his son-in-law; Swasaraiha-from her tears; Harantyaha-washed; Kuchakunku mani-to his\nchest's kumkum..\n\n( This ) king did not refuse his son Dushasana for the despicable act of pulling the hair of Devatulya Raja Yudhishthira's wife Draupadi, although his chest was\nwashed up to the kumkum with his tears.\nHe who is righteous in his actions,\nHe who is dependent on truth,\nHe who is not asked, is given at the right time,\nHe who is born of the dark, is the enemy of the dark. 8. Meaning of the word:\nDyut--by playing dice; Tu-but; Adharmen--by cunning; Jitasya--defeated; Sadhoh-of a saintly person; Satya-Avalamnasya-of truth;\nHe who has taken refuge in truth; Vanam-forest; Gatasya-of the one who has gone; Na-never; Yachat-when asked;\nAdat-given; Samayen-by the time; Daayam-appropriate share; Tamah-Jushanah-one who is deluded by attachment; A-as much as; Ajat-\nEnemy-of one who has no enemy."
