          "textEn": "0\nwere looking towards. But Duryodhana was so foolish that he dared to insult Vidur. This was the result of his maternal uncle Shakuni and his friend Karna's bad company, which always provoked him in his misdeeds. Where the sound of anklets is heard,\nThe son of a maidservant is nourished by the streams.\nIn that place, the lord of the world resides,\nAnd the ancient cremation grounds are quickly cleansed. 95 Meaning of the word.\n\nWho-- Who; Enam-- This; Atra- these; Upanjuhaav-- Called; Jihyam- crooked; arm: -of the arm; Sutam- son; Yat- of whom;\nOf whom; Balina- from the support; Ev- certainly; Pushtah- grown; Tasmin- to him; Pratipah- enmity; Parakritya- the benefit of the enemy;\nAaste- is situated; Nirvasatyam- take out; Ashu- immediately; Purat- from the palace; Shwasanah- let only his breath go.“. He is so cunning that against those on whose strength he has grown, he spies in the interest of the enemy. Immediately take him out of this palace.\nTake him out and let him walk only with his breath.\n\"\n\nMeaning: While getting married, Kshatriya kings used to take many other young women with the married princess. This young woman of the king - his concubine - was called a maidservant. These maids, by the association of the king, produce sons. Such sons were called maidservant sons. They had no right to the throne,\nbut they received maintenance and other facilities equal to those of the princes. Vidur was just like that.\nHe was the son of a maidservant, therefore he was not counted among the Kshatriyas. King Dhritarashtra had great affection for his young\nmaidservant's son brother, and Vidur was his great friend and philosopher advisor. Duryodhana knew very well that Vidur was a great soul and a well-wisher, but unfortunately, he used harsh words that hurt his\ninnocent uncle. Duryodhana not only mocked Vidur's\nbirth, but also called him ungrateful, because he seemed to be a supporter of Yudhishthira,\nwhom Duryodhana considered his enemy.\nHe wanted that Vidur should be immediately removed from the palace, and\nall his property should be confiscated. If it were possible, he would have made him lie down until\nhe had nothing left but breath. He accused that he was a spy of the Pandavas"
