          "textEn": "3\nThousands of divine forms of God are situated. Meaning: Vidur was undoubtedly a virtuous soul of great merit, otherwise he would not have been born in the Kaurava dynasty.\nTo be born in a high family, to be prosperous, to be a great scholar, and to be very handsome in body--all these are obtained as a result of past\nvirtuous deeds. But to receive God's grace and to remain engaged in His divine love and devotion, such sacred objects are not enough. Vidur considered himself less pure, therefore,\nHe decided to travel to all the great pilgrimage sites of the world\nto gain more merit and reach closer to God. At that time, although Lord Krishna was physically present in this world,\nVidur could have immediately reached him, but he did not do so because he was not completely free from sins. Until one is completely free from the effects of sin,\nno human being can be 100% devoted to God.\nVidur had this in mind that\ndue to the company of diplomats Dhritarashtra and Duryodhana, his merit has been exhausted, therefore he is immediately\nnot worthy of associating with God. In the Bhagavad Gita (7.28), its confirmation is in the following verse--\n\nYeshanam tvantagatam papa jananam punyakarmanam.\n\nTe FEHR HT bhajante mam tadadhrutaah. Sinful demon-like people like Kansa and Jarasandha cannot even imagine that Krishna is the complete Supreme Personality of Godhead.\nThat is, the ultimate truth. Only those who are pure devotees, who follow the principles of religious life prescribed in the scriptures,\nare able to engage themselves in Karma Yoga, then in Gnana Yoga, and after that,\ncan understand pure consciousness through pure meditation. When the consciousness of God arises, man can take the benefit of the association of pure devotees.\nIn this great age, one can attain the association of God. In this life,\none can attain the association of God.\nThese pilgrimage sites are for the purpose of absolving the sins of pilgrims, and for the convenience of those who attain pure life and God-\nrealization, these pilgrimage sites are spread throughout the universe. But,\na human being should not be satisfied only by going to pilgrimage sites and performing his prescribed duties."
