          "textEn": "6\nHe who is always engaged in the sight of the tourist,\nAlways eager, always sleeping, and always free. Unseen, in the wild attire,\nThe vows are adorned with green ornaments. 19. Shabdartham.\nGaam-- on earth; Paritran-- one who travels; Medhad-pure; Vivikta-vrithi-- one who is free to live; Sada-- always; Apluth-prakrita-- one who is purely free; Sadan-prakrita-- one who is purely free; Avadhuta-prakrita-- one who is like an Avadhuta; Vrataani-vrata;\nChere- completed; Haritoshani- pleasing to God; Vrata-vrata;\nChere- accomplished; Haritoshani- pleasing to God;\nVrata-vrata;\nChere- completed; Haritoshani- pleasing to God;\nVrata-vrata;\nChere- completed; Haritoshani- pleasing to God;\nVrata-vrata;\nChere- completed; Haritoshani- pleasing to God;\nVrata-vrata;\nChere- completed; Haritoshani- pleasing.\nIn this way, while circling the Earth, they performed actions to please Lord Hari.\nThey did. Their nature is pure and independent off. They kept getting constantly purified by bathing in holy places,\neven though they were in the guise of an Avadhuta - neither were their hair neatly combed, nor did they have a bed to lie on.\nIn this way, they remained unseen by all their relatives.\nMeaning: The foremost duty (kritakarya) of a pilgrim is to please Lord Hari. While undertaking a pilgrimage, one should not worry about the pleasure of society. He does not need to depend on social formalities or\nhabits or attire. He should always be absorbed in works that please God. In this way, the pilgrim, purified by thought and action, through the method of pilgrimage,\ncan have a vision of God.\nThus, the Bharatavarsha is like a year.\nAs long as the light of the sun is there.\nIn that age, the earth is a single wheel,\nPartha is unconquered by the one who has conquered the earth. 20>. Meaning of the word:\nIttham--in this way; Vrajan--wandering; Bharatam--India; Ev--only; Varsham--land; Kalen--at the right time;\nYavat--as long as; Gatavan--gone; Prabha