          "textEn": "30\n\nwas. Meaning: Shivji took Arjun's strength test through a fight over a pig hunt. He confronted Arjuna in the guise of a hunter. Arjun kept covering them with arrows until they were satisfied with Arjun's war. He gave Arjun the Pashupati Asra.\nAnd gave blessings. Here, Vidur is asking about the well-being of this great warrior. Yama, the one who protects the sons, Pritha,\nArmed with arrows, like a bee guarding flowers,\nLike a deer in the sunrise, like a swan in the water,\nLike a parrot on a banyan tree. 39. Meaning of the word\n\nYamo--twins (Nakula and Sahadeva); Utstit--what; Tanayau--son; Prithayaa:--of Pritha; Pathahi:--by the sons of Pritha; Vrutau--\nprotected; Pakshambhi:--by the eyelids; Akshini:--of the eyes; Iva:--like; Remate:--playing carelessly; Udday:--by snatching;\nMridhe:--in battle; Swar-riktham:--own property; Parat:--from the enemy Duryodhana; Supanau:--Vishnu's vehicle Garuda; Iva:--like; Vanchi-\nVaktraatu:--from Indra's mouth\nAre the twin brothers living under the protection of their brothers? Just as the eye\nis always protected by the eyelid, similarly, they are protected by Pritha Putra, who, from the hands of their enemy\nDuryodhana, has snatched away their rightful empire, just as Garuda\nsnatched the nectar from the mouth of the drummer Indra. Meaning: The king of heaven, Indra, holds a bow in his hand and is extremely powerful, but\nGaruda, the vehicle of Lord Vishnu, snatched the nectar from his mouth. Similarly, Duryodhana was as powerful as the king of heaven.\nYet, the Prithaputra Pandavas were successful in snatching their empire from Duryodhana. Garuda and Partha both are devotees of ASH Bhagavad, that is why they are able to face such strong enemies.\nAre able.\n\nVidur's curiosity was about the two youngest brothers of the Pandavas, Nakula and Sahadeva.\nThese\nwere twin brothers, the sons of Madri, who was the sister-in-law of the other Pandavas. When Madri departed with her husband, Maharaj Pandu