          "textEn": "34\nSee or recognized. In this way, they were able to enjoy the freedom of life without being bound to perform many duties of the world. When Lord Shri Krishna was present in this mortal world in his eternal blissful, dark-complexioned form,\nthose who were not pure devotees of God could neither recognize him nor understand his glory. They do not know, O Mother, that they are always deluded by the body (of the embodied being)--they are always deluded by those who are always deluded by the devotees, but the devotees can always see them because of their pure devotion to God.\n\nNanam nripanam trimadotpathanam\nMahi muhushchalayatam chamubhih.\nVadhatpranjhihisho-\nApyupekshatagam bhagavan kurunam. 43). Meaning of the word:\nNunam - without; Nripam - of kings; Tri - three; Mad - from false pride; Utpatanam - from false pride; Mithya - from false pride; Gavah - of cows; Muhah - of the earth; Muhah - of the earth; Muhuh - of the earth;\nConstantly; Chalayatam - agitating; Chamuhibhih - with the movement of soldiers; Vadhat - by the act of killing; Prapanna - surrendered; Aarti-\nJihirshay--willing to remove the sorrow of the afflicted; Isha--of God; Api--despite; Upaisat--waited;\nAgham--crime; Bhagavan--God; Kurunam--of the Kurus..\n\nEven while being (Krishna), and with the desire to always remove the sorrow of the afflicted,\neven then, he kept saving himself from killing the Kurus, although he was seeing that those people had committed all\ntypes of sins, and he was also seeing that, other kings, being subject to three types of false pride,\nbeing under their strong military activities, are constantly disturbing the earth. Meaning: As it is declared in the Bhagavad Gita, God appears to fulfill the purpose of killing the wicked in this mortal world\nand protecting the afflicted devotees. Despite this purpose, Lord Krishna endured the insult of Draupadi by the Kauravas and the injustice being done to the Pandavas.\nAlong with this, he continued to 