          "textEn": "50\nThey drowned in the wide separation. In the first chapter, we have already discussed the sorrow of Arjun and Yudhishthira,\nfor whom Krishna's disappearance remained extremely unbearable until the end of their lives. The Yadavas were partially aware of the Lord. They are also glorious, because they had the opportunity to associate with God as the head of their family, and those people also did the\nintimate service of God. Yadavs and other devotees of God are different from those who wrongly\nassumed them to be ordinary people through assessment. Such people are definitely deluded by Maya. They are hell-hunters and jealous of God. Maya works very powerfully on them,\nbecause such people, despite high worldly education, are without faith and are contaminated by a mentality of atheism.\nThose people are always eager to establish that Krishna was an ordinary man,\nwho, as a result of committing sins of killing the demonic kings of the earth, that is, the sons of Dhritarashtra and Jarasandha,\nwas killed by a hunter. They do not believe in these statements of the Bhagavad Gita that God remains unaffected by the fruits of actions--Na maa karmani limpanti. According to the atheistic\nviewpoint, the entire family of Lord Krishna, the Yadav dynasty, was destroyed by the sins committed by Krishna, the killing of the sons of Dhritarashtra,\ndue to which they were cursed by the Brahmins and destroyed. These accusations\ndo not touch the hearts of devotees of God, because they keep knowing the reality. Regarding God,\ntheir intellect is never disturbed. But those who are disturbed by the statements of demons\nare also condemned.\nIn this verse, Uddhava wants to say this.\nThose who are thirsty, who are satisfied with the heat of penance,\nBeginning and ending, the beginning and ending, is the reflection of the self, the eye of the world. 11. Meaning of the word:\n\nDisplay--by showing; unfulfilled--without doing; penance--austerity; unfulfilled--by completing the penance; of the people--of the people;\nAcquisition--by taking; disappearance--disappearing; completed--completed; He--who; But--but; Self-illusion--one's own form;\nPublic view--in public view. Lord Shri Krishna, who had revealed his eternal form before everyone's eyes on earth,\nhad removed his form from the sight of those people and disappeared, those who did not perform the desired penance"
