एँ सामान्य रूप से गलती\nसे समझती हैं । यह कथन उन श्रद्धाविहीन अभक्तो के इस मिथ्या प्रचार को झुठला देता है कि भगवान्‌",
          "textEn": "5\n\nBecause of their inability to do so, they were unable to see them in their true form. Meaning: In this verse, the word Avitrutta-hasham is very important. In the physical world,\nthe bound souls are engaged in satisfying their senses in various ways, but they have been\nunsuccessful in doing so, because it is impossible to be satisfied by such efforts. The example of a fish on land is extremely\nsuitable. If someone takes a fish out of the water and puts it on land, it cannot remain happy,\nno matter how much joy is given to it. The soul can remain happy only in the presence of the supreme being, God,\nnot elsewhere.\nBy the immense and unconditional grace of God, to them,\nIn the spiritual world, in the Brahmajyoti Mandal, in countless Vaikunthalokas, and in that divine world, for the infinite joy of the beings,\nThere is an infinite arrangement.\n\nGod himself comes to display those divine plays, which are especially\nperformed in Vrindavan, Mathura, and Dwarka. He appears to attract the bound souls to the eternal abode of the Lord.\nBut due to the lack of sufficient merit, the audience is not attracted to such plays of God.\nIt is said in the Bhagavad Gita that those who have completely crossed the path of sin,\nOnly they can engage themselves in the divine love and devotion of God. The purpose of the entire Vedic ritual is\nto put every individual on the path of virtue. Following the strict rules prescribed for all castes\nwill lead a person to truth, self-control, control of the senses, One can attain qualities like patience, etc., and can rise to the level of pure devotion to God. Only through such divine vision can material desires of a human being\nbe completely satisfied.\n\nWhen God was present, those who could fulfill their material desires to see Him in His true form\ncould return to His abode with Him. But those who do not see God in his true form,\nremain bound by material desires and cannot return to the abode of God. When God's sight\npassed beyond, they did so in their original eternal form, as it is said in this verse. They undertook a physical journey.\nThey did not leave their body, as the liberated souls generally mistakenly\nunderstand. This statement refutes the false propaganda of those faithless devotees that God"
