          "textEn": "54\n\nLooking at it, it was considered that they are the ultimate skillful creations of Brahma, the creator of humans. Meaning: When Lord Krishna was present in this world, there was no object to compare his physical form.\nThe most beautiful object in the physical world could be compared to a blue lotus or the full moon in the sky, but even lotuses and the moon were defeated in front of Krishna's physical beauty, which was confirmed by the most beautiful creatures in the universe, namely the gods. The gods thought that, like them, Lord Krishna was also created by Brahma. Whereas the fact is\nthat Brahma's creation was done by Lord Krishna. Creating the divine beauty of God was not in Brahma's power. No one is a destroyer of Krishna; rather, they are the destroyers of everyone.\nIn the Bhagavad Gita (10.8), it is said-- I am the origin of all, and everything else proceeds from Me.\n\nThose who are attached to My pastimes,\nbeing delighted, behave as if they have attained Me.\nThe women of Vraj, engaged in playful activities,\nare absorbed in their thoughts and actions. 94. Meaning of the word\n\nYasya- whose; Anurag-- attachment; Plut-- increased; Haas-- laughter; Raas-- joke, humor; Leela-- plays; Avalok--\nThought; Pratilabdha-- obtained; Manaah-- distressed; Vraja- women; Hagabhi: with eyes; Anuprvruttah: followed; Dhiih- by intellect; Avatasthuh: sat quietly; Kil- without doubt; Kritya- Seshaah: the work of the house..\n\nAfter the play of laughter, jokes, and sightseeing, the maidens of Braj become distressed upon Krishna's departure.\nThey follow him with their eyes, therefore they sit with a dull mind\nand are unable to complete their household chores.\n\nMeaning: In Vrindavan, in their childhood, Lord Krishna was known for teasing all the other young women with divine\nlove.\nKrishna's love for them was so profound that that joy\nhas no comparison, and the Vrajas were so devoted to them that their devotion was greater than even the affec