          "textEn": "58\nIn the above, many contradictory statements are found. Nothing is objectionable. When we\nunderstand God as God and do not consider Him as our equal, full of imperfections,\nthen everything becomes possible. Dwelling in the mind, remembering this.\nWhen I bow to the feet of the ancestors.\nTo those who are not afraid of Tata, Kansa, and others,\nMay they be pleased, O uncreated, and accomplished. 17). Meaning of the word\n\nDunoti-- causes me pain; Chetah-- heart; Smaratah-- while thinking; Mama- mine; Etat- this; Yat- as much as; Aah- said\nPadau- of the feet; Abhivandya- worshipping; Pitro- of the parents; Tat- hey father; Amba- hey mother; Kansat- from Kansa; Uru-\nGreat; Shankitanaam- fearful of; Prasidatam- be pleased; Nah- ours; Akrut- not completed; Nishkriti- your\nDuty to serve..\n\nLord Krishna asked his parents for forgiveness for their inability to serve at his feet, because due to the terrible fear of Kansa, they remained away from home\ndue to Kansa's terrible fear.\nHe said, \"Oh mother, oh father, forgive our inability.' '\nThis whole conduct of God pains my heart. Meaning: It seems that Krishna and Balram were both extremely afraid of Kansa, that's why\nthey had to hide themselves. But if Krishna and Balram are the complete Supreme God, then\nhow is it possible that they were afraid of Kansa? Are there any contradictions in such statements? Vasudeva\nDue to his deep love for Krishna, he wanted to provide them protection. He never thought\nthat Krishna is God and he himself can protect himself. He considered Krishna as his son. Because Vasudeva was a great devotee, therefore he did not want to think that Krishna, like his other sons,\nshould be killed. From a moral point of view, Vasudev was committed to handing over Krishna to Kansa, because\nhe had promised to give all his children to him. But due to immense love for Krishna, he\nbroke his promise.\nLord was very pleased with Vasudeva's divine attitude. They did not want to