भक्तों द्वारा यह पूजनीय है। इस उपदेश को चैतन्य",
          "textEn": "69\nIn a way, they used to wander in the gardens covered with dense trees and filled with the sound of chirping birds on the banks of the Yamuna River. Meaning: Nand Maharaj was the Bhumidhar (landowner) of Kansa, but because of his caste, being a Vaishya, he had thousands of\ncows. The duty of Vaishyas is to provide security to the cows, just as Kshatriyas have to provide security to humans. Because God was a child, therefore, along with other cowherds,\nhe was entrusted with the responsibility of grazing the calves. This Gwalbal was a sage and a yogi in his previous birth, and\nafter many such holy births, he attained the association of God, so that he could play with them at the same level. Such cowherds never cared to know who Krishna was, but\nthey used to play with him as very close and dear friends. They loved God so much that\nEven at night, they would think about when it would be dawn, and they would meet God and together go to graze the forest.\nGo.\nAll the forests along the banks of the Yamuna were beautiful gardens, which were filled with mango, jackfruit, apple, guava, orange, grapes,\njujube, palm fruit trees, and many other plants and fragrant flowers. Since this forest was on the banks of the Yamuna,\nit is natural that here there would be ducks, cranes, and peacocks on the branches of trees. These\nAll trees, birds, and animals were sacred beings, who were in the divine abode of Vrindavan to give joy to God and his eternal\ncompanions, the cowherds. God, along with his companions, playing like a small child, killed the demons Agasur, Bakasur, Pralambasur,\nand the Gadhabasur Asuras. Although they appeared in Vrindavan as a child, but\nIn reality, they were like covered flames of fire. A spark of fire, even when mixed with fuel, can create a terrible\nfire; similarly, God, from his childhood in the house of Nand Maharaj,\nstarting from that time, killed all these great demons.\nThe land of Vrindavan, the playground of Lord's childhood.\nThe land still exists, and whoever goes to these places, they find the same divine bliss, although\nLord is not visible to our imperfect eyes. Shri Chaitanya Mahaprabhu described this land of God as inseparable from God, therefore it is worshipable by devotees. This teaching, Chaitanya"
