          "textEn": "70\nThe followers of Mahaprabhu, called Gaudiya Vaishnavas, believe especially. Because this land is inseparable from God,\ntherefore, devotees like Uddhav and Vidur visited these places 5000 years ago\nso that they could establish direct contact with the visible or invisible God. Even today, thousands of devotees of God\ntravel in these holy places of Vrindavan, and in this way, they prepare to go to Bhagavad Dham.\nare. Like a Kumari, watching the actions of others, and observing the play of the Brajas.\nLike a child, smiling, watching the lion's play. 28>. Meaning of the word:\n\nKaumari--suitable for childhood; Darshayant--showing; Chestham--activity; Parakshaniyam--worth seeing, worthy of viewing;\nVraja-okasam--by the residents of Vrindavan; Rudan--shouting; Iva--like; Hasan--laughing; Mugdh--astonished; Bal-\nSingh--lion cub; Avalokanah--looking at; Uchitarah--like; Hetu--reason; Hetu-Hetu--reason; Hetu-Hetu--reason; Hetu-Hetu--reason; Hetu-Hetu--reason; Hetu-Hetu--reason; Hetu-Hetu--reason; Hetu-Hetu--reason; Hetu-Hetu--reason; Hetu-Hetu--reason; Hetu-Hetu--reason; Hetu-Hetu--reason; Hetu-\nThey, like a baby, sometimes cried and sometimes laughed, and while doing so, they appeared like lion cubs. Meaning: If a person wants to enjoy the childhood pastimes of God, then he must follow the footprints of Nand,\nUpanand, and other ancestral residents of Vrindavan. A child can insist on getting any\nobject and can cry as much as he wants to get it, due to which all\nneighbors' peace can be disturbed, but as soon as he gets the desired object, that child immediately starts laughing.\nIs. Crying and laughing like this is pleasing to parents and the elders of the family. In the same way, God, at the same time, cries and laughs, and immerses his devotees, parents, in divine bliss.\nThese events are only enjoyed by the Brajwasis, such as Nand Maharaj, not by the non-dualistic worshippers of Brahma or\nSupreme Soul. Sometimes, when demons attacked Krishna in the forest,\nthey appeared astonished, but they cast a maternal lion's eye on them and\nkilled them; even their childhood companions were astonished, and when they returned home, they told the whole\nstory to their parents, so that all the people praised the virtues of their Krishna."
