          "textEn": "94\nDaiva--divine; Adhineshu--being controlled; Kameshu--in sensual pleasures; Daiva-adhinah--subject to divine power;\nSwayam--self; Pumanu--living being; Kaha--who; Vishrambhet--can have faith; Yogena--through yoga; Yogeshwaramu--Lord\nIn; Anubratah--while serving\n\nEvery living being is controlled by a divine power, and thus its sensual pleasures\nare also under the control of that divine power. Therefore, in the divine activities of Krishna,\nonly he can place his faith who has become a devotee of God by performing devotional service.\nBe. Meaning: As it is said in the Bhagavad Gita, no one can understand the divine birth and actions of God. This fact has been confirmed here-- Only an enlightened person through devotion to God can understand the difference between the actions of God and the actions of other people controlled by supernatural power.\nIn this\nphysical universe, the sensory indulgence of all visible animals, humans, and gods is controlled by a\nsupernatural power, which is called nature or divine illusion. No person is free to attain sensual pleasures,\nand in this world, every person is after sensual pleasures.\nHe remains after. Such people who are under the control of supernatural power themselves cannot believe\nthat in matters of sensual indulgence, Lord Krishna is beyond their control\nis not. They do not understand that God’s senses are divine.\nIn the Brahma-Samhita, the senses of God are described as omnipotent--that is, they can perform the functions of one sense with another.\nWhich is not believed by one who has limited senses that God can eat with his hearing\ndivine power and can have intercourse just by seeing. A controlled being in his\nbonded life cannot even dream of such sensory functions. But from the works of devotion,\nHe can understand that God and His works are always divine. As the Bhagavad Gita (18.55) says--Bhagavan kahiye hai--Bhaktiya mamabhijanaati yavan yashchasmi tatvatah--If a person is not a pure devotee of God, then he cannot know even a part of God's actions. The complete, perhaps, the playful, the delightful, the sons of the gods.\nThe sages, the sages, the lords of the gods, the knowers of the Lord's opinion. 24)."
