          "textEn": "00\nChapter four\nVidur's going to Maitreya\nUddhava said\nThen, having eaten and drunk the Varuni,\nHe, having tasted the torn knowledge, grieved over the broken words. 9>. Meaning of the word.\n\nUddhava said—Uddhava said; Atha—thereafter; Te- they (Yadavas); Tat- by the Brahmins; Anumohita- having given permission;\nBhuktva- having eaten; Pitva- having drunk; Cha- and; Varunim- wine; Taya- to him; Vibhrashit- having become devoid of knowledge;\nDuruktaih- by harsh words; Marma- inside the heart; Paspushuh- having touched..\n\nAfter that, all of them (the Vrishni and Bhoj dynasty) ate the prasad (blessed food) with the permission of the Brahmins,\nand also drank the liquor made of rice. By drinking, they all became devoid of senses, and being devoid of knowledge, they began to say each other harsh, piercing words. Meaning: When Brahmins and Vaishnavas are well-fed during a festival, the guest\neats the leftover food when permission is granted.\nTherefore, the Vrishni and Bhoj dynasties\ntook formal permission from the Brahmins and then ate the prepared food. Kshatriyas\nhave the freedom to drink alcohol on certain occasions, therefore all of them drank light alcohol made from rice.\nDid. Thus, from such drinking, they became so senseless and unconscious that they forgot their mutual relationships.\nAnd they began to use such bitter words that they began to hurt each other's hearts. Intoxicated drinking is so harmful that even a very cultured family is affected by addiction, and\nin a state of intoxication, one can forget oneself. §. From the Vrishni and Bhoj dynasties, in this way, one's own\nself is not expected to forget. But by the will of God, it happened, and they became bitter towards each other. Tesham mairayadoshen vishamikrutascha.\nNimlochati ravaasivadrenunamiv mardanam. 2). Meaning of the word\n\nTesham--of them; Mairay--of intoxication; Doshen--from fault; Vishamikrut--unbalanced; Chetasam--whose mind; Nimlochati--settles; Ravau - Sun; Asit--happens; Venunam--of the wind; Iva- similar; Mardanam--destruction."
