          "textEn": "03\nIt was not possible. Adraksamekamasinam vichinvandayitam patim. Shri Niketam Saraswatyam kritetaketamaketanam. 6>. Meaning of the word\n\nAdraksham- seen; Ekam- alone; Asinam- sitting; Vichinvan- contemplating deeply; Dayitam- protector;\nPatim- to the master; Shri- to the abode; Saraswatyam- on the bank of Saraswati; Krit- to the Ketam- taken shelter;\nHaving taken; Akeetanam- situated without shelter..\n\nIn this way, while chasing them, I saw my patron and master (Lord Krishna)\ntaking refuge on the banks of the Saraswati River and sitting alone, contemplating deeply, although they are the refuge of the goddess\nLakshmi. Meaning: those who become monks usually take refuge under a tree. Uddhav saw\nGod taking refuge in that situation as helpless people do. Because they are the master of every\nobject, therefore their refuge is in every place and every place is in their refuge.\nComplete\nPhysical and spiritual vast universe is sustained by them, therefore they are the refuge of every\nthing. Therefore, taking refuge in them, becoming homeless like ascetics, was not at all surprising. Shyamavatam, the one without darkness, the one with serene, youthful, and beautiful eyes.\nKnown by the two, the four, and also by Pitaka Kaushambara. 7>. Meaning of the word\n\nShayam-Avadatam-- Beautiful from the color of the night; Virajam-- Made of pure Satoguna; Prashant- Peaceful; Arun- Red red; Lochanam--\nEyes; Dorbhih-- By the arms; Chaturbhi: - Four; Viditam- Having known; Pitam- Yellow; Kaush- Silk; Ambaren- From the cloth;\nChattha,\n\nThe body of God is dark, but He is very beautiful, full of eternal bliss. Their eyes\nare always calm and they are as red as the rising sun in the morning. I immediately recognized them by their four\nhands, various symbols, and yellow silk clothes, that they are God. Vām uravaradhishṭhitya dakṣināṅkṛṣṭāprisarōruham |"
