          "textEn": "05\nThey were mutual friends and well-wishers. Fortunately, Maitreya reached the place where Lord Krishna was resting. Meeting God is no ordinary occurrence. Maitreya was a great sage and a scholar,\nbut he was not a pure devotee of God, therefore, at that time, his meeting with God must have happened due to some unknown good deeds,\nthat is, unknown devotion. Pure devotees always remain engaged in pure devotional service, therefore, their meeting with God is natural. But when such people meet God who are not at that appropriate level,\nthen this meeting happens due to the invisible fate of accidental devotion. Tasyanuraktasya Muner Mukundah\nPramodabhavatakandharasya.\nO Ashvatthama, with a smile of love,\nHe said, pausing in contemplation. 10>. Meaning of the word\n\nTasya--his; Anuraktasya--of the one who is attached; Munih--of the sage (Metreya); Mukundah--the god who grants salvation; Pramoda-bhava--\nWith a happy expression; Anat--lower; Kandharasya-of the shoulders; Ashwrnvatah--while listening in this way; Mam--to me; Anuraga-haas--with a kind smile;\nWith a kind smile; Sikhriya--looking at me in a special way; Vishra-mayan--letting me complete my rest; Uvacha--said\n\nMetreya Muni was very attached to him and he was listening with a happy expression. Giving me time to rest, God smiled and spoke to me in this way with special\nthought. Meaning: Although Uddhava and Metreya were both great souls, but Lord's attention was more on Uddhava.\nBecause they were spotless, pure devotees. A knowledge-devotee, that is, one whose devotion is mixed from the perspective of monotheism,\nis not a pure devotee. Although Maitreya was a devotee, his devotion was mixed.\nGod\nGives back to his devotees based on divine love, not based on knowledge or desire-driven actions. In the divine love and devotion of God, there is no place for monotheism, knowledge, or desire-driven actions. The gopis of Vrindavan were neither scholars nor yogis. They had a love for 