          "textEn": "06\nShri Bhagavan said:\nI grant to those desires of yours, which are in your mind.\nI grant to those who are not satisfied.\nIn the past, I created the universe,\nMay you be satisfied with me. 11). Meaning of the word\n\nShri-Bhagavan said-- God said; Veda- I know; Aham- I am; Antar- Inside; Manasi- In the mind; Ipitam- Desired; Te- You;\nDamami- I give; Yat- That which is; Tat- That; Durvapam- Extremely difficult to attain; Anya- By others; Satre- In the Yagya;\nPura- In ancient times; Vishwa-Srijamu- Of those who created this universe; Vasunam- Of the Vasus; Mat- Achiever- Kamen- My\nWith the desire to attain harmony; Vaso- O Vasu; Tvaya- By you; Ishta- The ultimate goal of life L\n\nHey Vasu, I know from within your mind what you desired to attain in ancient times,\nwhen the Vasus and gods who expanded the activities of the universe performed sacrifices.\nYou especially desired to have my company. Of | It is rare to get it for other people, but I am giving it to you. Meaning: Uddhava is one of the constant companions of Lord and Uddhava's Swansha was one of the eight Vasus in ancient times. The eight Vasus and the gods of heaven, who are responsible for the system of the world's actions,\nin ancient times, with the desire to fulfill the ultimate goals of their respective lives, performed a Yagya (sacrifice). At that time, a part of Uddhava, in the form of a Vasu, expressed a desire to become a companion of God. God knew this, because He remains in the heart of every living being as the Supreme Soul or Parachaitana.\nHe remains present. In the heart of each, there is a representation of the subconscious, which provides memory to the partial\nconsciousness of every living being. As a form of partial consciousness, the being forgets the events of his past life,\nbut the subconscious reminds him that according to the past practice of knowledge, how\nto perform actions.\nIn the Bhagavad Gita, this fact has been confirmed in many ways-- 'yatha mam prapadante' (as they bow to me)\n'Tadvadhai va bhajapyaham' (I am worshiped by them) (Bhagavad Gita 4.11) and 'sarvasya chaaham hadi fn mattah ssatitnimapohane cha' (I am the beginning of all that exists). (Bhagavad Gita 15.15)\n\nEvery living being has the freedom to desire according to their choice, but the fulfillment of this desire is done by God. Every person is free to think or desire, but the fulfillment of his desires is the ultimate desire"
