          "textEn": "5\nThey keep committing sins at the feet of God and keep making their life miserable. Here,\nIt is a matter of special attention that Uddhav read the Chatushloki Bhagwat directly from God, who said it\nFirst to Brahmaji, and this time, in the form of Prarama Sthitam, explained self-knowledge in a more concise manner. Having learned such self-knowledge of love, Uddhava is extremely saddened by the feelings of separation from God.\nUntil Uddhava is awakened to the state, the real meaning of these four\nessential verses of Srimad Bhagavat cannot be understood. This state of continuous separation like Uddhava\nwas also manifested by Shri Chaitanya Mahaprabhu. One should not engage in unauthorized activities to distort the meaning of Shrimad Bhagwat,\nand should not put oneself on the deadly path of crime.\nI will go to see him, O Lord, and depart from the forest where he is.\nI will go to his hermitage, O Badarayashramam Mandalam. 21. Meaning of the word\n\nSaha aham--like this, myself; tat--his; darshan--vision; aahad--of joy; viyog--separation; aarti-yutah--from sorrow\nAfflicted; Prabho--Oh Lord; Gamishye--I will go; Dayitam--in this way, the advice was given; tasya--his; Badarikashram--Himalayas situated\nBadarikashram; Mandalam--association,\n\nHey Vidur, now I am going to Badarikashram situated in the Himalayas to reduce the lack of joy in meeting them, as\nHe ordered me. Meaning: A pure devotee of God at the level of Uddhava, simultaneously experiences both separation and union,\nconstantly engages in the company of God. A pure devotee never deviates from the divine service of God even for a moment.\nHe does not deviate.\nServing God is the main task of a pure devotee. Uddhava's separation from God was unbearable.\nTherefore, to follow God's command, he set out for Badrikashram, because\nGod's command and God are inseparable. As long as a man remains diligent in obeying God's command,\nhe does not suffer from true separation from Him. Where Narayana is the God, and Naras is the sage. Soft, intense, and long penance, in the heat of the world. 22)."
