          "textEn": "[6\nMeaning of the word\nWhere-where; Narayana--Lord; Deva--From incarnation; Nara--Human; Cha--Also; Bhagavan--Lord; Rishi--Sage; Mridu--\nMeaning of the word\nMeaning of the word\nMild-meaningful, intense-meaningful, long-meaningful, austerity-meaningful, doing; long-time-meaningful; Tepate--doing; Lok-bhavanau--Merit of all beings..\nThere, in Badrikashram, as Nar and Narayan in their incarnation, the Lord\nhas been performing great penance for the welfare of all harmonious beings since time immemorial. Meaning: Badrikashram, located in the Himalayas, is the abode of Narayan Rishis and a\ngreat pilgrimage site for Hindus. Even now, millions of holy Hindu people go to offer their respects to Lord Narayan, the incarnation of God. It seems that even five thousand years ago, a holy person like Uddhav used to go to this sacred place, and even at that time, it was known by the name of a very ancient place.\nFor ordinary people,\nit is extremely difficult to reach this special pilgrimage site, because this difficult place\nis located in the Himalayas, which is mostly covered in snow throughout the year. Only in the few months of summer,\npeople can go to this place by enduring great\npersonal inconvenience. On this earth, there are four abodes, that is, the kingdom of God,\nwhich are symbols of the spiritual sky realms, including Brahmajyoti and Vaikunthaloka. These are the four Dhams--Badrikashram, Rameshwar, Jagannath Puri and Dwarka. Devout Hindu spiritual\nTo attain the perfection of realization, following the footsteps of devotees like Uddhav, even now all these\ngo to holy places.\nSreesukha said:\nHaving heard of this war, the wise one, overcome by sorrow,\nand the one who is afflicted by the destruction of knowledge,\nand the one who is afflicted by sorrow. 23). Meaning of the word\nShri-Shukah Uvacha -- Shri Shukagoswami said; Iti-- thus; Uddhavaat-- from Uddhava; Upakarnya - having heard; Suhadaam- friend;\nAnd relatives; Dusshaham- unbearable; Vadham- destruction; Gyanen- by divine knowledge; Ashamayat- giving solace to oneself; Kshatta-\nVidur- the wise; Shalokam- separation; Utpatitam- having been born; Buddah- the learned..\nSrishukdev Goswami said: Upon hearing about the destruction of his friends and relatives from Uddhava,\nthe learned Vidur, with the power of his divine knowledge, pacified his unbearable grief. Meaning: Vidur was told that as a result of the Kurukshetra war, along with the destruction of his friends and\nrelatives, the destruction of the Yadu dynasty and the departure of God has already happened. From all of these"
