          "textEn": "23\nare forms, and when they appear within the sight of living beings, as Lord Krishna actually appeared,\nthen all these forms merge into them. In addition to these unbroken forms, there is their universal form, which\nthey revealed to Arjuna at the battlefield of Kurukshetra. In this verse, the word 'Sphitam' is also used, which indicates that he abandoned his Virat form, not his original eternal\nform, because there is no possibility of his Sachchidananda form changing. Of God,\ndevotees immediately become aware of this simple knowledge, but those who are non-devotees and do not perform any devotion to God,\ndo not understand this simple fact, or knowingly create a dispute to deny the eternity of God's divine\nbody. This is due to that defect, which is called the tendency to deceive living beings.\nEven from practical experience, it is seen till today that the worship of the divine form of God\nis done by devotees in various temples, and all the devotees of God experience it as reality\nthat the form of the deity in the temple is inseparable from the form of God. Of the inner power of God,\nThe description of this inconceivable work is given in the Bhagavad Gita (7.25)--Naamah Prakashah Sarvasya\nYogamayasamasavrutah. God has the right that He should not appear before everyone. It is said in the Padma Purana-- Therefore, Shri Krishna is not known by the senses. The name and form of God cannot be experienced by the physical senses. But when they become visible to worldly people, they assume a cosmic form.\nThey assume it. This is an additional physical demonstration of the form and is validated by the logic of the subject and its adjectives. In grammar, when the subject of an adjective is separated, then its\nby which the specified subject does not change.\nSimilarly, when God abandons his universal form, then his\neternal form does not change, although there is no difference between him and one of his countless forms. In the fifth Skandha, it will be seen how in various realms, even now, the worship of God\nis performed in various forms, and on this earth also, in various temples, how their worship is done.\nIs. Srila Jeeva Goswami and Srila Vishwanath Chakrabarti Thakur, in their commentaries, of Vedic literature"
