          "textEn": "27\nGave advice to Uddhava. It was God's will that Uddhav complete his mission and spread the knowledge that\nwas not even given in the Bhagavad Gita. Those who are attached to the words of the Vedas,\nthey too can know from this verse that God is the destroyer of all Vedic knowledge. Those individuals\nwho, even after studying the pages of the Vedas, are unable to understand the Supreme Personality of Godhead,\ncan take refuge in a devotee like Uddhava with the intention of progressing in their knowledge of the subject.\nThe Brahma Samhita states that it is extremely difficult to understand God through the Vedas, but he can be easily understood by a pure devotee like Uddhava.\nGod, by bestowing grace upon the sages residing in Badrikashram,\nauthorized Uddhava to speak on his behalf. Without acquiring such authority,\nno person can understand devotion to God nor can he preach it.\nWhen God was present on this earth, he also traveled to the sky to bring Parijat from heaven,\nand performed many extraordinary acts such as bringing his guru (Sanatan Muni's son) back from Yamlok. Uddhav was definitely told about the conditions of life in other worlds.\nAnd all the sages were eager to know about those worlds in the same way as\nwe are eager to know about the planets of space. Uddhav was specially appointed to take this message to Badrikashram\nnot only for the sages of that pilgrimage site, but also for the Nar-Narayan Archavigrahs. Such a message would have been more mysterious than the knowledge described in the Vedas.\nGod undoubtedly is the source of all knowledge, and the messages sent through Uddhava to Nar-Narayan and other\nsages were also parts of Vedic knowledge, but they were more secret and could only be sent or understood through a pure devotee like Uddhava. Because such secret knowledge was only known to\nGod and Uddhava, therefore it is said that Uddhava himself was an incarnation of God. Like Uddhav, every being can become a divine me