          "textEn": "28\nIs, not by other means. The Bhagavad Gita or Srimad Bhagavat cannot be understood without the help of such trustworthy devotees of God. According to Srila Vishwanath Chakravarti Thakur, that secret message must have\nbeen related to knowing about the mystery of God's departure and the destruction of his lineage one hundred years after his manifestation in this world. Every person must have been very eager to know the secret of the destruction of the Yadava dynasty,\nand that message must have been told to Uddhava by God, and Nar Narayan\nand other pure devotees must have been sent to Badrikashram for information.\nVidur, having fought, became the Supreme Soul of Krishna.\nThe actions of the body, which are engaged in play, are also praised. 33). Meaning of the word:\n\nVidurah- Vidur; Api- also; Uddhavaat- from the source of Uddhava; Shrutva- having heard; Krishnasya- of Lord Krishna; Param- of the Supreme Soul; Kridya- for play in mortal world; Upatta- accepted extraordinarily; Dehasya- of the body; Karmani- of actions;\nDivya Karma- divine deeds; Shaladhitani- most praiseworthy; Cha- also..\n\nVidur told Uddhav about the appearance and disappearance of the Supreme Being, that is, Lord Krishna, in this mortal world,\nwhich the sages wanted to know with great interest. Meaning: The subject of the manifestation and disappearance of the Supreme Being, that is, Lord Krishna, remains a mystery to the great sages. In this verse, the word WEE: is important. Ordinary beings are usually called the soul,\nbut Lord Krishna is never an ordinary being, because he is the Supreme Soul.\nEven then,\ntheir appearance as humans and their disappearance from this mortal world are a subject of research for those researchers\nwho conduct research with great dedication. Such subjects are definitely more and more\ninteresting, because researchers have to find the divine abode of God, in which God enters the sound of ending the pastimes of the mortal world. But even the great sages have no information that\nBeyond this physical sky, there is a spiritual sky where Shri Krishna, along with his companions, eternally resides.\nAlthough, along with that, they repeatedly display their mortal world's pastimes in all the universes. This is confirmed in the Brahmasamhita (5.37)-- Goloka eva nivas-\ntyakhalatmabhutah-- God, with his inconceivable power, lives in his eternal abode, Goloka, yet"
