          "textEn": "39\nFor the complete development of the human body, the cow is the most important animal. The body can be nourished by any kind of food, but for the development of the finer cells of the human brain,\ncow's milk is especially necessary, so that man can understand the intricacies of divine\nknowledge. What is expected from a civilized man is that he should live on fruits, vegetables,\nfood, sugar, and milk-containing edible substances. Bulls help in the agricultural process of producing food grains, etc. In a way, the bull is the father of mankind, while the cow is the mother.\nBecause it provides milk to human society. Therefore, it is expected from any civilized man\nthat he provides all kinds of protection to bulls and cows. The gods or spirits that live among the higher folk are far superior to humans. Because\ntheir life situations are better organized, therefore they live in more luxury than humans.\nDespite all this, they are all devotees of the Lord. God, civilized humans, cows,\nand gods, who are directly responsible for regular life for the sake of self-realization,\ndescend in various forms to provide protection, such as Matsya, Kurma, Varaha, and Nrusimha form. The entire system of physical creation is planned so that bound lives can have opportunities for self-realization. Anyone who takes advantage of this arrangement is called a deity or a civilized man. Cow dung\nis for the purpose of helping to maintain such a high level of life. The divine plays of the Lord are meant to protect the twice-born, the cows, and the gods. Humans have a tendency to listen to good stories, therefore, for the satisfaction of the tastes of a developed soul,\nthere are numerous books, magazines, and newspapers in the market. But once read,\nthe joy of such literature becomes stale, and people lose interest in reading such literature repeatedly.\nIt remains.\nIn fact, newspapers are read for less than an hour, and after that,\nthey are thrown in the trash.