          "textEn": "52.\n\nMeaning: Mandavya Muni was a great sage (See Bhagwat 1.13.1) and Vidur in his previous birth was\nthe controller Yamraj, who takes charge of the souls after their death. In this physical world,\nliving beings have three states-- birth, sustenance, and death. Once death is over,\nYamraj, as the appointed\ncontroller, punished Mandavya Muni for his childhood misdeeds and ordered him\nto be pierced with a spear. For this unjust punishment, Mandavya, angered at Yamraj,\ncursed him to become a Shudra. In this way, Yamraj took birth from the womb of Vichitravirya's concubine,\nfrom the semen of Vichitravirya's brother, Vyasadeva. Vyasadeva was born from the womb of Satyavati, the son of Bhishmadeva's\nfather, Shantanu. This mysterious history of Vidur was known to Maitreya Muni, because he was a contemporary friend of Vyasadeva.\nDespite being born from the womb of his wife, Vidur, being a great devotee of God,\nreceived the inheritance of the highest talent, because his parents were great and his\nrelationship was great. Being born into such a great lineage is beneficial for attaining a devotional life. Vidur got this opportunity because of the greatness of his previous birth. The Lord, the Supreme Being, is eternally contemplated, and is always followed. Whose teaching of knowledge is the cause of the destruction of the foolish. 29). Meaning of the word:\n\nBhavan-- You yourself; Bhagavata-- Of the Lord; Nityam-- Eternal; Sammatah-- Beloved; Sa-anugasya-- One among the associates; H-are;\nYasya- Whose; Gyan-- Knowledge; Upadesha- For instruction; Ma-To me; Adishat- Thus initiated; Bhagavan-- The Lord;\nVrajant- While going to their abode..\n\nYou are one of the eternal companions of God, for whom God goes back to his abode,\nTime has left me with teachings. Meaning: After death, the great controller of life, Yamraj, decides the fate of the next life of living beings.\nHe is certainly the most reliably reliable of God's representatives. Such trusted positions\nare given to those great devotees of God, who are like the eternal companions of God in Vaikuntha. Since Vidur was one of them, therefore, while returning to Vaikuntha, he left the teachings for Vidur to Lord Maitreya. Generally, the companions of Lord Vaikuntha do not come into the material world. But sometimes"
