          "textEn": "58\nThe use of 'ka' has been done here. The great element is complete consciousness, because a part of it is manifested in every intellectual\nform. The great element is directly connected to the supreme consciousness of the supreme being, yet it appears in the form of\nmatter. The great principle or the reflection of pure consciousness is the germ-place of all creation. This is a slightly mixed pure essence from Rajoguna, therefore, mobility arises from here.\nSo, the soul is partially conscious of the divine vision.\nThe self is transformed, the self is the universe, through the desire of the world. 28>. Meaning of the word\n\nSah-mahat tatva; Api-bhi; Ansh-purush swansh; Gun-mainly tamoguna; Kaal-period of time; Atma-complete consciousness;\nBhagwat-of God; Drishti gochar: -limitation of sight; Atmanam-of many different forms; Vyakarot-differentiated; Atma-\nAgarah-of the universe; Asya-this; Sisrukshaya-produces false ego.\nThis Mahat element is mainly Tamoguna and gives rise to the false ego. This\nis the essence of God, which is full of the consciousness of creative principles and is endowed with the time of fruition.\nIs. Meaning: The great principle is the medium between the pure soul and the material world. This substance and\nis the meeting point of the soul, from where the false ego of the living being arises. All beings are parts of God.\nBeing bound by the pressure of false ego, the souls, being parts of the complete Supreme Being, God,\nstill claim to be the enjoyers of the material nature. This false ego is the binding\nforce of the material world. God repeatedly provides opportunities for the deluded souls to be freed from this false ego.\nBecause of this, the material creation happens over time.\nThey provide all the facilities to the enlightened ones to improve the actions of their false ego, but they do not interfere with their slight freedom to be a part of God.\nThey do not disrupt their slight freedom.\nMahattatvadvikarvanaadahantatvam vyajayat.\nKaryakaranakartatma bhuteendriyamanomayah.\nVaikarikastajascha tamaschchetyaham tridha. 29)."
