          "textEn": "63\n\nAfter that, God cast his gaze upon the water produced by electricity, and then in it, eternal time\nand the mixture of external energy occurred. In this way, it transformed into the earth, which is mainly composed of the quality of smell. Meaning: From the descriptions of physical elements in the above verses, it becomes clear that in all states,\nwith other combinations and changes, there is a need for the vision of God. In every transformation,\nthe vision of God alone provides the ultimate completeness. They work like a painter, who\nmixes different colors and transforms them into a special color. § When one element meets another element,\nits qualities increase. For example, the sky is the cause of air. Sound in the sky\nis only one quality, but due to the interaction of the sky and the vision of God, eternal time\nand the external nature combine to produce air, in which two qualities are found--sound and touch.\nSimilarly, when air is generated, the inner interaction of the sky and air, the time, and the outer power of God, due to the touch, generates electricity. And with air and sky, the interaction of electricity\nmixes the inner energy and the external power, and the vision of God on them, to produce water. In the final state of the sky, only one quality remains--that is sound; in air, two qualities remain—\nsound and touch; in electricity, there are three qualities—sound, touch, and form; and in water, there are four qualities—\nsound, touch, form, and taste. In the final stage of physical development, the earth is fruitful, which\nis endowed with all five qualities, namely sound, touch, form, taste, and smell. Although they are different substances\nare different mixtures, but such mixtures do not form spontaneously, just as the mixture of colors does not form spontaneously without the touch of a living painter.\nThis automatic system is actually activated by the touch of God's vision. In all physical changes, only living consciousness is decisive. This fact is mentioned in the Bhagavad Gita (9.10) as follows--\n\nBy the mind, the nature is created, the moving and the non-moving.\n\nBy the cause, O son of Kunti, the world is transformed. The conclusion is that even though physical elements may act in a surprising way in the eyes of ordinary people,"
