          "textEn": "G77\nFor the sake of the world's birth, stability, and order,\nHe is the lotus-like feet of the incarnate one. If you grant me refuge in you,\nI will remember you and fear no one. 43). Meaning of the word\n\nVishchasya- of the cosmic universe; Janma- creation; Sthiti- preservation; Sanyam- meaning- even for dissolution; Krit- held or accepted; Avatarasya- of the incarnation; Pad- lotus- feet; Te- yours; Brajem- go to refuge; Sarve- all of us;\nSharanam- refuge; Yat- which; Isha- O Lord; Smrutam- memory; Prayachchhatati- by giving; Abhayam- courage; Swa- of oneself; Punasam-\nDevotees HTL\n\nOh Lord, you incarnate for the creation, preservation, and destruction of the cosmic universe, therefore\nWe all take refuge in your lotus feet, Because these steps are for your devotees\nAlways providing memory and courage § |\n\nMeaning: Virat Jagat Ka Srishti, Palan Aur Pralay ke Liye Brahma, Vishnu aur Mahe Shwar (Shiv) teen\nAvatar hain. These are the controller or master of the three Gunas of material nature, which are the cause of the visible world.\nVishnu is the lord of Sattva Guna, Brahma of Rajo Guna and Maheshwara of Tamo Guna. According to the qualities of nature,\nThere are different types of devotees. ⏎. The virtuous worship Lord Vishnu, the Rajasic worship Brahma,\nand the Tamasic worship Shiva. These three are incarnations of Lord Krishna,\nbecause Krishna is the complete Supreme Personality of Godhead. The deities directly receive the instruction of the lotus feet of God, not of various incarnations. But in the physical world, the worship of Vishnu avatars is directly done by the gods. It is known from various scriptures that the gods go to Vishnu in the Ksheer Sagar and\nWhenever there is any difficulty in the administration of Vish's affairs, they request for their problems.\nAlthough Brahma and Shiva are incarnations of God, they worship Vishnu, and\nthus they are counted among the gods, not as God. Those who worship Vishnu\nare called deities, and those who do not are called Asuras. Vishnu always takes the side of the gods.\nBut Brahma and Shiva sometimes take the side of the Asuras. It is not that by taking interest in them\nthey become like them, but sometimes to gain control over the Asuras, they do something like that."
