          "textEn": "75\nYathaanjasanviiyurkunthadhishnyaam. 46). Meaning of the word\nPaneen--by drinking; Te-your; Dev--O Lord; Katha--Stories; Sudhayah--Of nectar; Pravriddh--Extremely enlightened; Bhaktya- devotion;\nBy; Vishad-Ashayah- filled with deep thoughts; Ye-which; Vairagya-Saram- the entire essence of detachment; Pratilabhya- obtainable; Bodham- intellect; Yatha- in the manner; Anjasa- immediately; Anviyuha- obtain; Akuntha- unfulfilled; Dhishnya- the abode of Vaikuntha;\nVaikuntha- the abode of Vishnu in the sky..\nOh Lord, those who, due to their serious mindset, attain the state of enlightened devotional service,\nunderstand the complete meaning of detachment and knowledge, and by drinking the nectar of your stories,\nobtain the Vaikunthaloka in the spiritual sky.\nMeaning: The difference between the non-differentialist mental thinkers and the pure devotees of God is that\nthe non-differentialists pass through each state with the difficult knowledge of the Supreme Being, while\nDevotees enter the abode of all bliss from the very beginning of their efforts. The devotee only needs to listen to the devotional\nactivities, which are as simple as any task in normal life. He works in a very simple way, while the mental thinker has to go through\nthe complexities, in which some facts remain, and some are merely for maintaining a unique state. Despite strenuous efforts to attain complete knowledge, the non-differentiated God\nis able to merge into the non-differentiated identity of Brahman light. Even the enemies of God are easily attained by them upon their death. But the devotees attain the supreme state of knowledge and detachment\nand go to the Vaikuntha worlds, which are the worlds of the spiritual sky. The non-differentialist only\nobtains the sky; he does not attain any real divine bliss, whereas the devotee attains those worlds\nwhere real spiritual life takes place.\nThe devotee, endowed with a serious mindset,\nshakes off all achievements like dust and accepts only devotion, which is divine\nconsequence. Therefore, the self-realized yogi,\nHaving conquered strength, the mighty one.\nThey call you the wise man,\nFor they do not find rest in service. 47)."
