          "textEn": "86\nHe who is entered in the form of the best of the saffron-colored ones, that group. He awakens the dormant karma by connecting the different. 3. Shabdartham\n\nSaha--He; Anupvishta - entering like this; Bhagavan - God; Cheshtha-rupena - in the form of one's efforts;\nTam - them; Ganam - all beings in which deities are included; Bhinnam - separate-separate; Sanyojayam - to connect; Aas - engaged in doing;\nSuptamu - asleep; Karma - action; Prabodhayam - awakening; Prabuddha - making enlightened..\n\nIn this way, when God entered within the elements with his power, all beings became enlightened.\nBecoming enlightened, they engaged in various tasks in the same way that a person wakes up from sleep and\nengages in their work. Meaning: Every living being remains unconscious after the dissolution of creation, and God enters into them with physical power. These individual beings are bound souls that have been going on since before, but in every physical\ncreation, they are given the opportunity to free themselves and become independent souls.\nTo take advantage of Vedic knowledge and to find out what their relationship is with God,\nhow they can become free, and what is the ultimate benefit in such liberation, an opportunity is provided\nOh. By properly studying the Vedas, a person becomes aware of their position, and in this way, while performing divine devotion to God, they gradually attain Vaikuntha. In the material world, individual souls remain engaged in various tasks according to their\nextinct incomplete desires. After the disintegration of a particular body,\nthe individual soul forgets everything, but the all-compassionate God, who is in the form of the witness Supreme Soul,\nis situated in the heart of every person, awakens him and reminds him of his past desires.\nHe keeps giving. In this way, he starts acting accordingly in his next life. This eight\nguidance is called fate, and any discerning person can understand that from this, nature\nremains bound by its three qualities, O.\nImmediately after the partial or complete rhythm of creation, some ignorant philosophers\nby mistake consider the unconscious dormant state of life to be the ultimate state. After the partial merger of the physical body, the being remains only\nconscious for a few months, and after the complete merger of the physical creation, it remains unconscious for millions of years"
