          "textEn": "88\nParena--by God; Vishta--entering in this way; Swasmin-self; Matraya-by self; Vishch-created--of the created; Tatva-element; TOT:--surely; Chukshobh--became transformed; Anyonvam--mutually; Asadhya-by attaining; Yasmin-in which; Lokah-worlds; Char-immobile and moving; Jad-inert and conscious;\nJyohi, as God entered into all the elements of Vishcha in his self-formed form, in that way, he became the immense\nform in which all the worlds and all the inert and conscious creations are situated. Meaning: All the elements of the vast creation are substances, and until God enters them in his own form,\nthey have no ability to increase in size. This means that\nuntil there is a spiritual touch, the substance cannot decrease or increase. The substance is the result of the soul,\nand it increases only through the touch of the soul. This entire vast world has not taken this vast form by itself, as is the misconception of the ignorant.\nAs long as the soul resides within the substance,\nthe substance can increase as needed, but without the soul, the substance stops growing.\nIt stops. For example, as long as spiritual consciousness remains within the body of a living being, the body continues to grow to the expected\nsize, but the dead physical body in which spiritual consciousness does not remain, stops growing. In the Bhagavad Gita (Chapter II), importance has been given not to the body but to the spiritual consciousness. The entire vast body grew in the same way that we experience in our small bodies.\nBut man should not mistakenly think that the individual subtle soul is the cause of the cosmic manifestation of the universe. The universe is called the cosmic form because God\nis within it in his own form. Hiranyamayah sa purushah sahasraparivatsara.\nThe mind is filled with all good things. 6>. Meaning of the word:\n\nHiranyamayah--Vishnu, who assumes a cosmic form, the destroyer of the womb; Sahasra--thousand; Aka--one; Prakash--light; Vaarsha--year