          "textEn": "90\nIn the state of pure consciousness, only one function occurs. The individual soul's consciousness becomes one with the supreme consciousness at that time.\nIt becomes one when there is complete coordination between these two. The Advaita believes that consciousness is only one, while the Sattvatas, that is, the believers, believe\nthat although consciousness is undoubtedly one, they are one only by their coordination. Individual consciousness is\nadvised to be united with the supreme consciousness, as Bhagavad Gita (18.66) has\npreached--Serve me and abandon all other religions. Individual consciousness (Arjun) is advised to live with the supreme consciousness\nand thus maintain its true purity. The attempt to stop the activities of consciousness\nis foolish, but by connecting with God, they can be purified.\nThis consciousness, according to the amount of purification, is divided into three qualities of self-recognition--Adhyatmik, that is, self-recognition with the body and mind, Adhibhautik or physical\nself-recognition with products, and Adhidaivik or self-recognition as a servant of God. In these\nthree, Adhidaivik self-recognition, while following the will of God, is the beginning of the purification of consciousness. This is the self-part of the essence of all beings, the Supreme Soul.\nThe first incarnation is where the entire world is seen. 8. Meaning of the word\n\nEshah- Yahah; Hi- without doubt; Ashhesh- without limit; Satvanam- of living beings; Atma- soul; Anshah- part; Param- of the supreme soul; Atha- from; Adya- first; Avatarah- incarnation; Yatra- in which; Asau- that; Sau- all; Bhutagramah- appropriate creations; Vibhavyate- is manifested; Phalati- blossoms\nBlooms\n\nThe universal form of God is the first incarnation and the essence of the supreme soul. They are the souls of countless beings,\nand the entire creation (Bhutagrama) is sustained within them, which blossoms and flourishes in this way.\nMeaning: God expands in 