          "textEn": "9\nIt remains fixed only on the universal form. Sadhyatmah, Sadhidaivaah, Sadhibhutah, thus in three ways. The tenfold Prana is one, and also the heart. 9>. Meaning of the word:\n\nSa-Adhyatmah - body and all senses including mind; Sa-Adhidevah - and the controller of the senses; Cha - and; Sa-\nAdhibhutah - present subject; Iti - thus; Tridha - three; Virat- Viratah; Prana- life force; Dashan-vidha- ten types; Ekadha- only one; Hridayena- consciousness power; Cha- also..\n\nThe cosmic form is presented in this sense by three, ten, and one, that they (God) are the body\nand mind and senses. They are the dynamic power of all movements through ten types of life force.\nAnd they are the heart where life force is created.\nMeaning: The Bhagavad Gita (7.4-5) states that the eight elements—Earth, water, fire, air,\nsky, mind, intellect, and false ego—are products of the subordinate power of God, while the subordinate power\nthat appears to be consumed is essentially related to the supreme power, that is, the inner power of God. All eight sub-powerful forces function in a gross and subtle manner, while the supreme power\nacts as an intermediate central life force--this is experienced in the human body. The gross elements--Earth, etc.--create the external gross body and are similar to the upper garment (kurta).\nWhile the subtle mind and false ego function in the inner garment (baniyan) of the body.\nThe movements of the body are first generated from the heart, and all the functions of the body are made possible by the senses.\nWhich receives energy from ten types of air inside the body. The description of ten types of air\nis as follows-- The main air that enters through the nose while breathing is called Prana. The air that exits through the anus is called Apan. The air that properly digests food inside the stomach,\nand which sometimes comes out as burps, is called 'samana'. The air that goes through the throat,\nand which causes suffocation when stopped, is called Udan Vayu. The air that circulates throughout the entire body is called Vyan Vayu. There is another subtle element other than these five elements. The air that helps in the opening of the mouth towards the eyes is called Nag Vayu. Which is air hunger"
