          "textEn": "202\nare; those who are situated in Rajoguna are placed in human society, and those who are situated in Tamoguna, they\nfind a place in the society of animals or ghosts. There is no contradiction in this conclusion. Countless beings\nare spread in the various realms of the universe, and in this way, they remain situated according to their respective\nqualities, within the physical nature-qualities. The face is the beginning of the Brahma Purusha, the Kuru is the middle, and the outward-facing one is the Guru. 30>. Meaning of the word\n\nFrom the mouth; originated; Brahmin--Vedic knowledge; of the man; Kuru-udrah--the chief of the Kuru dynasty;\nYah--who; Because of; from the inclination; of the castes; chief; appeared;\nBrahmin became called Brahmin; Guru--respected teacher or guru,\n\nThese chiefs of the Kurus, from the mouth of the Vishcha, Vishcha, the Vedic knowledge was revealed.\nThose who\nare inclined towards this Vedic knowledge are called Brahmins, and they are the natural teachers and gurus of all the castes of society. Meaning: As confirmed in the Bhagavad Gita (4.13), the four varnas of human society have evolved in the order of the body of the cosmic form. This is the physical division: mouth, arm, waist and leg. Those who are situated in the mouth,\nare called Brahmins, those who are situated in the arms,\nare called Kshatriyas, those who are situated in the waist,\nare called Vaishyas, and those who are situated in the feet,\nare called Shudras. Every person is situated in the universal form of God\nin their body. Therefore, a caste should not be considered inferior in the form of four varnas,\nbecause it is situated in a particular part of the body. We do not practice any real discrimination in our bodies, in our hands or feet,\nin our behavior towards ourselves. The body's organs are important, although\namong these, the mouth is the most important organ.\nIf other organs are cut off from the body,\nthen a human can live, but if his mouth is cut off, then he cannot live. Therefore, this most important part of God's body is called the abode of those Brahmins who are inclined towards Vedic knowledge. One who, instead of being inclined towards Vedic knowledge, is inclined towards worldly matters,\nis not called a Brahmin, even if he is from a Brahmin lineage or a Brahmin"
