          "textEn": "203\nWhy was he not born from his father? A Brahmin is not eligible to become a Brahmin just by being born from a Brahmin father.\nHe does not become a Brahmin. The main qualification of a Brahmin is his inclination towards Vedic knowledge. They reside in the mouth of the Veda Bhagavan.\nAnd therefore, whoever is inclined towards Vedic knowledge, he is definitely a Brahmin. This inclination towards Vedic knowledge is not limited to any particular caste or community. Any\nfamily and any person from any part of the world can be inclined towards Vedic knowledge\nand by this, he will become worthy of being a real Brahmin. A real Brahmin is by nature a teacher or a Guru. Until someone has Vedic knowledge,\nno person can become a guru.\nThe complete knowledge of the Vedas is to know God, and this Vedic knowledge\nis the end, that is, Vedanta. The person who has attained the formless Brahman and who has no knowledge of the Supreme Personality of Godhead,\neven if he becomes a Brahmin, he cannot become a guru. Padma\nIt is said in the Puranas--\n\nShabatkarmanipuno Vipro Mantratantravisharada:\n\nAvaiṣṇavao TE Syad Vaṣṇavaḥ ATE Gurah. A non-discriminatory, worthy Brahmin can become one, but he cannot become a guru until\nhe attains the position of a Vaishnava or the position of a devotee of God. In the modern era, the supreme authority of Vedic knowledge,\nShri Chaitanya Mahaprabhu has said--\n\nWhether it is forgiveness, whether it is trust, how can one be pure? Yēda Kṣṇatattvavetṛ; 95 “Guru” Haya. Any person can be a Brahmin, Shudra, or a Sanyasi, but if he is proficient in Krishna Vidya,\nthen he is worthy of being a Guru.\n(Chaitanya-Charitamrita, Middle 8. 128) Then the guru's worthiness is not to be a worthy Brahmin,\nbut to be proficient in Krishna Vidya. Anyone who is expert in Vedic knowledge is a Brahmin. And only that Brahmin who is pure Vaishnav\nand knows all the complexities of Krishna Vidya can become a guru."
