          "textEn": "22\nOne-end-odd; profit- profit; speech: -by discourse; Nu Punsam--after the Supreme Being; Sushlok--holy; Het: -\nActivity; virtue-advocacy--advocacy of virtues; Aahu--it is said; Shruti--of the ear; Cha--also; Vidvadhih--by the wise;\nUpaakrutayam--in this way edited; Katha-sudhayam--in such divine message-like nectar; Upasampraayamu--fulfilling the original purpose\nby being close, 5\n\nThe supremely accomplished benefit of humanity is to engage in the discussion of the activities and glory of the purifier.\nIt is to be engaged. Such activities have been beautifully recorded by great scholars and sages,\nthat the real purpose of the ear is fulfilled by staying close to them. Meaning: The nirvikar (without attributes) are extremely frightened by listening to the activities of God, because they\nthink that the happiness obtained from the divine position of Brahma is the ultimate goal of life. They think that every single\nwork, even if it is God, is worldly.\nBut in this verse, the feeling of happiness\nthat is expressed is different, because it describes the actions of the Supreme Being, whose qualities are divine. The word 'Gunavadam' is important because the qualities of God and his activities and his divine plays are the subjects of discussion for devotees. A sage like Maitreya certainly does not have any interest in discussing anything with worldly qualities. Even then, they say that the supremely perfected state of divine realization\nis to discuss the activities of God. Therefore, Srila Jeeva Goswami comes to this conclusion\nthat the stories related to the divine activities of God are far beyond the divine experience of liberation. These divine works of God have been recorded by the great sages in such a way that by listening to those stories,\na person attains self-realization and the ears and tongue are also properly utilized.\nShrimad Bhagavat is such a great book, and by merely listening to its subject matter,\none attains the highest state of perfection in life. The glory of the self-existent, the son, the poet, and so on. At the end of a year, with the yoga of meditation. 38. Meaning of the word\n\nAtmanah--Self; Paratma Kaun; Avasitah--Known; Vatsa--Son; Mahima--Glory; Kavina--By the poet Brahma; Adina--At the beginning; Sansvara--Divine year; Sahasraante--At the end of a thousand years; Dhiya--By intellect; Yoga-Vipakshaya--Through mature meditation.Oh son, the first poet Brahma, after a thousand divine years of maturing meditation, only this much\nknows that the glory of God is inconceivable."
