          "textEn": "29\nIt has been shown to be inseparable from them. Divine knowledge, strength, opulence, power, beauty, and sacrifice—all these opulences\nare inseparable from God. When they themselves display such opulences in this world, then they have no connection with the qualities of material nature. The word 'Chinmatrattva' guarantees that the activities of God\nare always divine, even if they are not displayed in the physical world. Their activities are themselves equal to the complete\nPurushottam, otherwise, free devotees like Shukdev Goswami would not be attracted by them. Vidur asked\nhow can the activities of God be similar to the qualities of physical nature, as sometimes\nis sometimes misjudged by the ignorant? The perishability of physical qualities is due to the difference between the physical body and the soul.\nThe activities of a bound soul are displayed through the qualities of material nature.\nTherefore, they appear distorted in appearance. But the body of God and God himself\nare one, and when the activities of God are displayed, they are certainly, in every way,\ninseparable from God. The conclusion is that those who consider the activities of God to be material\nare certainly deluded. Playful activity is for the beginner, and desire is for the wanderer.\nFor the self-content, and for the one who is satisfied, and for the one who is free from others. 3. Meaning of the word\n\nKaridayam-- in the matter of playing; Udyamah-- IE; Arbhasya-- of children; Kama-- desire; Chikriodisha-- desire to play;\nAnyathi-- with other children; Atmatushtasya-- for one who is content; Cha-- also; Katham-- for what; Nivruttasya--\nDetached; Sada-- always; Anyatha-- otherwise\n\nChildren are eager to play with other children or in various games,\nbecause they are encouraged by desire.\nBut there is no possibility of such a desire in God,\nbecause He is self-satisfied and always detached from everything. Meaning: Since the complete Supreme Personality of God