          "textEn": "224\nCan be won by Maya, but at the same time, they also believe that they are bound. This\nis contrary to all logic. Meaning: Sometimes it feels that God, who is 100% spiritual, cannot be the cause of that\nMaya power that veils the knowledge of the individual soul. But,\nin reality, there is no doubt that Maya or external power is also a part of God. When Vyasadeva\nhad a vision of God, he saw God with his external power, which covers the pure knowledge of individual\nliving beings. Why does the external power act in this way? On this, as follows,\ncan be considered, as the great commentators like Vishwanath Chakraborty Thakur and Srila Jiva Goswami have analyzed.\nAlthough the physical illusion is separate from the spiritual power,\nyet this God is one of many powers, and in this way, the physical qualities of nature are certainly,\nthe qualities of God himself. Power and the mighty God are not separate. Although such power remains united with God,\nthey are never subdued by it. Although all beings are also parts of God,\nthey are defeated by material power. The inconceivable yoga of the Lord, which is described in the Bhagavad Gita (9.5), is not properly understood by the Koppamandu philosophers. They\nIn support of this principle that Narayan (God himself) becomes Daridranarayan, they propose\nThat material power defeats God.\nBut Srila Jiva Goswami and Srila Vishwanath\nChakravarti Thakur presents a very beautiful example in its explanation. He says that although\nthe sun gives light everywhere, but clouds, darkness, and snowfall are all different parts of the sun. Without the sun,\nthere is no possibility of the sky being covered by clouds or darkness, nor can there be snowfall on the earth. Although life is sustained by the sun, darkness and snowfall produced by the sun\nalso disrupt life. But it is also a fact that the sun itself is never defeated by darkness,\nclouds, or snowfall. The Sun is far away from such disturbances. Those who are ignorant, they\nsay that the sun is covered by clouds or darkness. Similarly, the Supreme Being, God, is always unaffected by the influence of material\npower, although it is one of His powers. (Parasyashaktirvividhaiva)"
