          "textEn": "253\nMeaning: God is sometimes called Uttamshloka, which means ** whose worship is done by devotees with chosen words.\"The devotee who is completely immersed in devotion to God with affection and love,\nfrom his mouth, a stream of such chosen words flows. There are many such examples in which even a small\nchild, who was a great devotee of God, praised the Lord's divine plays in the chosen\nwords. In other words, without sweet affection and love, no person can praise God in an excellent manner. Thus spoke the Lord, by him who is devoted to the cessation of worldly existence.\nSanatkumaraya, who is always in front,\nWho is steadfast in his vow. 7/. Meaning of the word\nSaid--was said; Kil- certainly; Etat-this; Bhagavattamen-by the Lord; Nivritti-detachment; Dharm-\nAbhiratai-for the one who observes this religious vow; Tena-by him; Sanat-kumarayi-to Sanatkumara; Sah-him;\nHe; Cha-also; Aah-said; Pristah-upon being asked; Sankhyayanayi-to the sage named Sankhyayan; Agya-Oh Vidur; Dhrit-\nVrataayi-to the one who observes the vow..\nIn this way, Lord Sankarshana told the meaning of Shrimad Bhagavat to those Maharishi Sanatkumar.\nWho had already taken a vow of detachment. A. Sanatkumar also narrated Shrimad Bhagavat in his turn, in the same form in which he had heard it from Sankarshana.\nWhen asked by Sankarshana, he narrated Shrimad Bhagavat in the same form in which he had heard it. Meaning: this is the method of the traditional system. Although the well-known Sanatkumar was in the perfected state of life,\nstill he heard the message of Shrimad Bhagavat from Lord Sankarshana.\nSimilarly, Sankhyayan,\nwhen asked by the sage, Sanatkumar narrated the message that he had heard from Lord Sankarshana. In other words, until he hears from a proper officer, he cannot become an advisor. Therefore, among the nine things in Bhakti Yoga, two things--shravan (listening) and kirtan (singing)--are the most important. Properly,\nWithout listening, no person can propagate the message of Vedic knowledge. Sankhyayanah parahamshyamchyo"
