          "textEn": "260\nTries to find the cause of individual existence and the entire vast universe, and in this way he\naims to find the ultimate cause. If his effort with penance and perseverance is properly completed, then he will surely receive the credit of success. Having seen that, the fire of the navel-\nThe water from the nadis is poured out.\nThe navel is gone, the fire of the navel-\nThe navel is pierced, the navel is pierced. 19. Meaning of the word\nSah--He (Brahma); Ittham--In this way; Udarikshya-by looking inside; Tat-That; Aabaj--Lotus; Nal-Stalk; Nadibhih—through the veins;\nThrough the tube; Antar-Inside the water; Avivesha--entered; Na--Not; Arvag-through the throat; Gatah--having gone; Tat-That;\nNa--That; Avindata--Understood; Ajah--Having understood; Ajah--Having understood; Ajah--Having understood; Ajah--Having understood;\nSelf-existent..\nThinking this way, Brahmaji entered the water through the holes (pores) of the lotus stem. But even after entering the navel and going near the navel of Vishnu, they could not find the root.\nMeaning: Man can reach God through his own efforts, but without God's grace, he cannot reach the ultimate point. This understanding about God is possible only through devotion,\nas it is confirmed in the Bhagavad Gita (18.55)-- 'Bhaktya mamabhijñānāti yāvanā yāsmi\ntattvatah.' He who knows the self-manifested form beyond darkness,\nHe who is the one who is beyond the great three-eyed one.\nHe who is the fear of the bodies,\nHe who is the destroyer of the one who is afraid of the sun. 20>. Meaning of the word\nTamasi apara--due to the ignorant efforts of searching; Vidur--Oh Vidur; Atma-sargam--the cause of one's own creation; Vichinvat--\nThinking; Abhut--became; Su-mahan--extremely great; Tri-nemi- the time of Tri-nemi; Yah- who; Deha-bhām--of the embodied;\nBhayam--fear; Eirayanah--while crossing; Parikshinoti--reducing one hundred years; Ayu--age; Ajasya--of the unborn;\nHeti- the eternal cycle of time.\nOh Vidur, while searching in this way about one's own existence, Brahma in his prime"
