          "textEn": "265\nI have been marked. With a smiling face,\nWith a lotus-like face, adorned with earrings,\nWith a radiant glow, the orb of light,\nThey accept the sun's rays. 27. Meaning of the word\nMukhen- from the sign of the mouth; Lok-arti-Har-- one who removes the suffering of devotees; Smiten-- by laughter; Parisfurt- sparkling;\ndoing; Kundal- with earrings; Manditen- with a smile; Shonayiten- accepting; Adhar- of one's lips; Bimb-\nreflection; Bhasa- rays; Pratyarhanantam- while giving; Su-nasen- with one's beautiful nose; Su-pva- and beautiful\nwith the lips..\nThey also accepted the service of the devotees and relieved their suffering with their beautiful laughter. The reflection of his face adorned with earrings was extremely captivating, because it was shimmering with the rays of his lips,\nand with the beauty of his nose and eyebrows. Meaning: God is greatly pleased with His devotion. ©. In various fields of spiritual activities, there are many spiritualists, but the devotional service to God is unique.\nDevotees\nIn return for their service, they do not ask anything from God. Even the most desired liberation provided by God,\nthey refuse. In this way, God becomes indebted to the devotees.\nAnd they can try to become IHW by serving the devotees with their ever-seductive smile. Devotees are always pleased and lively with the smiling face of God. Devotees,\nseeing devotees so lively, God is even more pleased. In this way, such service and acceptance,\nfrom such exchange, a continuous divine competition continues between God and devotees.\nKadambakizhalkpishdvasasa\nWeg mekhalya nitambe |\nHaren chanantadhane natva |\nShrivatsavakchahsthalavallabhena. 28>. Meaning of the word:\nKadamb-kinchalk-- the saffron dust of the Kadamba flower; Pishankha-rangin paridhan; Vasasa-- from clothes; Su-alank tam-- well-decorated;\nWell-dressed; Mekhala-- with a kartani; Nitamba-- on the waist; Harena-- from a necklace; Cha-- also; Anant-- extremely; Dhanen- valuable; Vatsa--\nOh Vidura; Srivatsa-- of divine mark; Vakshah-sthal-- on the chest; Vallabhena-- extremely beautiful \\."
