          "textEn": "284\n\nremain. You are eternally auspicious, but you are unavailable to the non-devotees. Note: The administrator appointed for physical matters is also a devotee of God, the deities of Devaloka. But along with this, they have desires for material enjoyment and sensory gratification. God is so merciful\nthat He provides them with all kinds of material pleasures more than they desire, but they\nare not satisfied with them, because they are not pure devotees. God does not want that any of his countless sons\n(living beings) remain in the material world with three types of heat, experiencing continuously the physical\nsufferings of birth, death, old age, and disease. The gods of heaven and many devotees of this world also want to enjoy material pleasures by remaining devotees of God in the material world.\nThey take the risk of falling to a lower position in life\nand do so, which makes God unhappy with them. The pure devotees are neither attracted nor repelled by material objects.\nThey\nlink their desires to God's will and do not act for themselves. Arjuna is the best example of this. Arjun did not want to fight the war out of family affection and emotions.\nBut after listening to Shrimad Bhagavad Gita, he accepted to fight for the will of God.\nHe accepted. Therefore, God is exceedingly pleased with pure devotees, because they, for their sense gratification,\ndo not act, but act according to the will of God alone. In the form of God, they are always present in everyone's\nheart and provide everyone with the opportunity to benefit from good advice. Therefore, every person should, taking advantage of this opportunity, with full attention, only with their divine\nlove and devotion, do it. But the non-devotees are neither like the gods nor like the pure devotees, but they are averse to making a divine\nrelationship with God. They have rebelled against God; therefore, they constantly have to bear the consequences of their actions.\nIn the Bhagavad Gi