          "textEn": "293\nMeaning of the word\n\nTiryaku--other than human animal; Manushya--human etc.; Vibudha-adishu--gods etc.; Jiva-yonishu--in different life forms;\nAtma--soul; Ichchhaya--by wish; Atma-krita--self-created; Setu--responsibility; Parikshaya--by desire to keep; Yah--who; Reme--while performing divine play; Nirasta--without being more affected; Vishayah--material impurity; Api--certainly;\nAvaruddha--unobstructed; Deha--divine body; Tasmai--to that; Namah--my salutations; Bhagwate--to God; Purushottamaya--etc.\nLord.Oh Lord, you voluntarily appear in various life forms,\nin non-human animals, and in deities, to complete your divine plays. You are not even slightly affected by physical impurities.\nYou are not. You come only to fulfill the responsibilities of your principles of religion, therefore, O\nGod, I bow to you to reveal such various forms. Meaning: The incarnations of God in different species are all divine.\nThey, Krishna, Ram, etc., appear in human form in their\nincarnations, but they are not humans. The person who makes the mistake of understanding them as an ordinary human\nis certainly ignorant, as confirmed in the Bhagavad Gita (9.11)--Avajananti Maa Mudha \"FH Tanusashritamu. This principle also applies when they incarnate as pigs or\nfish. These are the divine forms of God, which appear only under the limitations of their personal desires and divine plays. They have to manifest such divine forms only to encourage their devotees. Whenever they need to uplift their devotees and keep their\nFASTA, then their various incarnations appear.\n\nEven though he is pleased by knowledge, with half-hearted actions,\nHe sleeps, and the world travels in the stomach.\nIn the inner self, the wise, the good, the pleasant,\nThe Bhima, the beautiful, the virtuous, the happiness of the people, describing. 20. Meaning of the word\n\nYah- who, that; Avidyaya- from ignorance; Anupahata- without being affected; Api- also; Dash-half- five; Vritta-\nIntera