          "textEn": "307\nIt is not the form. Brahma praised the eternal form of God in that way, and God approved of Brahma's\nspiritual conception. In the Bhagavad Gita, God has condemned the physical concept of Krishna's body, which arises from the fact that he appears like a human being. God can appear in one of his numerous spiritual forms, but they are neither\nmade of physical objects nor is there any difference between their body and soul.\nThis is the method of contemplating the spiritual form of God. For your medicine, the soul is shown to me, beyond.\nWith the stream of the Nal, the root of Pushkar is being searched. 37). Shabdartham.\n\nTubhyam- to you; Mat- to me; Vichitrikāyam- upon your efforts to know; Atma- soul; Me- mine; Darshitah- shown; Abahih- from within; Nalen- from the stem; Salile- in the water; Moolam- root; Pushkarasya- of the lotus;\nVichinvat- while contemplating.\n.\n\nWhen you were thinking about whether there is any source of the lotus-stem of your birth or not, and you had entered inside that stem, then you could not find out anything. But at that time, I had revealed my true form from within. Meaning: God can be experienced not through mental contemplation, nor with the help of physical senses, but\nonly by His selfless grace. Physical senses cannot reach the divine knowledge of God. They can be known only through humble devotion, then they appear before the devotee. Only through divine love can a man know God, Not by any other\nway. God cannot be seen with physical eyes, but he can be seen from within by open divine eyes with the sacred love\nof the divine.\nUntil the impure covering of the material world,\nthe spiritual eyes of man remain closed, he cannot see God. But when this impurity (dust) is removed by devotion,\nthen man can certainly have a vision of God. Brahma, through his personal efforts, failed to see the origin of Kamalnal, but when God\nwas pleased with his penance and devotion, he revealed himself from within without any external effort"
