          "textEn": "36\n\nThen Brahma saw that the lotus on which they were standing and the water in which the lotus grew\nwere trembling due to strong, fierce wind. Meaning: The physical world is called delusive, because it is the place of forgetfulness of the divine service of God. In this way, in this physical world, a person engaged in devotion to God may sometimes be greatly disturbed by adverse circumstances.\nThe power of Maya and the devotee--a declaration of war has been made between these two sides--\nremains, and sometimes the weaker side of the devotee comes into the conflict of the strong Mayaic power.\nBut by the unintentional grace of God, Brahmaji was very strong, and the power of Maya could not make him their prey.\nAlthough this became the cause of his worry when he was able to falter in his position.\nI\nBy penance, by austerity, by knowledge, and by self-control.\nThe strength of the accumulated knowledge, I, with arrogance, did not accept the wind. 6>. Meaning of the word:\n\nTapasya-- through penance; Hi-- certainly; Edhmanen-- increasing; Vidya-- through divine knowledge; Cha-- also; Atma- Atma; Sthitaya--\nAtmasthah; Vivruddha-- mature; Vijnan-- practical knowledge; Balah-- strength; Nyapat-- drank; Vayum-- air; Saha-- with.\nAmbhasa-- with water. Meaning: The struggle of Brahmaji for existence presents a personal example of that continuous war, which continues in this physical world, in living beings and in the enchanting power of Maya.\nFrom Brahma till today,\nall living beings have been struggling with the forces of material nature. Advancing in science and divine experience,\ndue to knowledge, man can try to gain control over that physical power that remains active against our efforts, and in the modern era, advanced physical scientific knowledge and penance have\nplayed a wonderful role in controlling physical powers. But such control over physical power can only be successful when a person surrenders to God and follows His commands with divine love and devotion."
