          "textEn": "38\nEighty-three--Up to here; of the living--Of the living beings; of the inhabited--Of the inhabited places; institution--Differentiation:--Different situations of residence;\nComplete--Completely full; of the religion--Of the religion; He--Indeed; of the unmeasured--Of the unmeasured; result--Mature state;\nSupreme--The supreme person in the universe; That--That..\n\nIn mature divine knowledge, due to the unconditional devotion to God, Brahmaji is the supreme in the universe.\nHe is the man. Therefore, they created fourteen worlds for the dwelling places of various types of creatures. Note: TEER is the home of all organism properties. In the physical world, bound souls give a glimpse of those qualities\nto a fraction, that is why sometimes they are called reflections. These reflective beings\nhave inherited the various proportions of their original qualities as parts of God\nand according to the acceptance of these qualities, they appear as different life forms\nand according to Brahma's plan, they find places in different worlds.\nBrahmaji is the abode of the three worlds--Patala Loka,\nthat is, Adholoka, Bhur Loka, that is, Madhya Loka, and Swargaloka, that is, Uchchatara Loka--. From these,\neven higher realms, such as the realms of the great, the realms of penance, the realms of truth, and the realms of Brahman, are not merged into the water of destruction. The reason for this is the unprovoked, devotional service to God performed by the residents of those worlds.\nAnd the existence of these residents remains until the end of the two-purpose period when they are generally freed from the cycle of birth and death in the physical world.\nVidura said:\nAs the form of Hari is of many forms, so are the wonderful actions.\nWe describe the characteristics of time and the nature of Brahman, O Lord. 10>. Meaning of the word.\n\nVidur said-- Vidur said; Yatha-- as; Attha-- you have said; Bahu-rupaasya-- of various forms; Hareh-- of God; Adduh-ta-- strange; Karmanah-- of the actor; Kaalah– time; Aakhyam– named; Lakshanam– characteristic; Brahman– O wise one\nBrahman; Yatha– as this is; Varnay– please describe; Nah– to us; Prabho– O Lord.\n\nVidur asked Maitreya: O Lord, O supreme scholar, please describe the eternal time\nWhich is the other form of the wonderful actor, the Supreme Being. What are the characteristics of eternal time? Please,\ntell us in detail. Meaning: This entire universe, from the smallest particles to the vast universe, contains countless strange and wondrous beings"
