्विज्ञान तथा गणित के",
          "textEn": "39\nIt is manifest and all these are under the control of God's time form. The regulatory period is specific to various organisms.\nAccording to that, it has different extensions. There is a time for atomic rhythm and also for world rhythm.\nThere is a separate time. It is a period of rhythm of the human body, and also a period of rhythm of the world body. Growth, development and consequent action are all time-dependent. Vidur wanted to know in detail about various physical\nmanifestations and the times of their dissolution. Maitreya Ubacha\nGunavyatikaraakaro nirvishesho pratishthitah.\nPurushastadupadanamaatmanam leelayasrijat. 11. Meaning of the word\n\nMaitreya said--Maitreya said; Guna-vyatikara--mutual actions of the qualities of nature; Aakaraha--source; Nirvishistha--unlimited; Purushaha--of the Supreme Being; Tat--that; Upadanam--cause; Atmanaha--of the physical creation;\nLila--by the plays; Asrijat--created.\n.\n\nMetreya said: The eternal time itself is the original source of the mutual actions of the three qualities of nature. This is immutable and unlimited, and in the plays of physical creation, it works as a cause of God. Meaning: The Nirvishesh Kaal Tatva (the principle of non-differentiated time) is the background of the physical world as an object (Nimitta) of God. This is a component of the help given to the physical nature. No one knows when\ntime began and when it ends. The element of time alone can keep track of the creation, maintenance, and destruction of the physical world. This element of time is the physical cause of creation, therefore it is the essence of God. Kaal is considered to be the formless form of the God. Even modern people have explained the element of time in many ways. Some people accept it almost\nin the same way as it is described in Shrimad Bhagwat.\nFor example, in Hebrew literature,\ntime has been accepted in the form of God. It is said in it, \"God spoke to the fathers in the past\nthrough prophets\nsometimes and in many ways.\"\"From the perspective of philosophy, time is\ndifferentiated as the ultimate complete and real. The ultimate, perfect time is constant, and remains unaffected by the movement or slowness of material\nobjects. The calculation of time, astronomy and mathematics"
