          "textEn": "330\nChapter eleven\nCalculation of time from atom\nMaitreya says\nExtreme: many are the special ones, always united.\nAtom: is known, the confusion of the human mind is from that. 1>. Meaning of the word.\n\nMaitreya said--Maitreya said; Charama--Extreme; Sat-Prabha--Characteristic; Anekah--Innumerable; Anasuyata--Unmixed;\nSada--Always; Param-Anuha--Atom; Saha--He; Vijneya--Object of knowledge; Nrama--Of humans; Ekya--Oneness; Bhrama--Believed;\nYatah--From which. The infinite particle of physical expression, which is indivisible and not manifested in the form of a body, is called an atom. It always exists as an indivisible power, up to\nthat even after the dissolution (laya) of all forms. The physical body is the combination of such atoms,\nbut the common man understands it as a wrong deception. Meaning: The description of the atom in the Shrimad Bhagavatam is very similar to the modern science of atomic physics. Its further description is found in Kanada's atomism.\nEven in modern science, the atom is\nconsidered an infinitely indivisible particle, from which this universe is made. Shrimad Bhagavat is a complete textbook of knowledge,\nwhich includes atomicism as well. The eternal, time-bound, small, subtle form is an atom. That which is the nature of the substance, which is the state of being. The ultimate liberation is the supremely great and eternal. 2>. Meaning of the word\n\nSatata-- of an influential expression; Eva-- certainly; Pad-Arthasya- of physical objects; Swarupa- Avasthitasya- of the time of dissolution; Yat- that; Kaivalyam- oneness; Param- supreme; Mahan- unlimited; Avishah- of form\nNirantarah-- in an unceasing manner,\n\nAt the ultimate state of the expressed universe. When they create various bodies,\nliving in their own forms, they are called unlimited oneness (kavya). Certainly,\nthere are various bodies in physical forms, but the atom itself creates complete expression."
