          "textEn": "450\n\nMeaning: From this verse of Shrimad Bhagwat, it seems that the Sun acts as a source of light for all the worlds in the universe. This verse does not support that modern scientific theory, according to which there are many suns in each universe. It is understood that each universe\nhas only one sun, which fulfills the light of all the worlds. In the Bhagavad Gita, the moon is also called a\nNakshatra. There are many constellations, and when we see them shining at night, we can understand that they are reflecting light. Just as the moon's light is the reflection of sunlight,\nother worlds also reflect sunlight, and there are many such other worlds\nthat we cannot see with our eyes. From the demonic influence of the sons residing in Diti's womb,\ndarkness spread throughout the universe.\nDeva Oochu:\nTam etadvibho vettha samvigna yadvayam bhrusham.\nNa hyavyaktam bhagavatam kalenasapthavartmanah. 3. Meaning of the word:\n\nDevahoochu: The gods said; TH: — Andhakah: Darkness; Etat: This; Vibhoh: Hey great one; Vetthah: You know; Samvignah: Very much; Yat: Because; Vayam: We; Yom: Excessive; Na: Not; Nadimah: River; Hi: Because; Avyaktam: Unmanifest; Bhagavatam: Yours; Kalen: By time; Asprushtah: Untouched; Vartmanah: Whose path..\n\nThe fortunate gods said: O great one, just look at this darkness, which you know well, and which is causing us worry. Since the effect of time cannot touch you,\ntherefore, nothing is unrevealed before you. Note: Here, Brahma is called Vibh and Bhagwan. He is the Rajoguni incarnation of God in the physical world. As a representative, they are inseparable from God, therefore the effect of time does not affect them.\nThe effect of time manifests as past, present, and future. It cannot touch great men like Brahma and other\ngods. Sometimes gods and sages who have attained such\nachievements are called knowers of the three times.\nDevadeva Jagaddhatarlokanathshikhamane."
