          "textEn": "453\nIn other words, until a person gains control over their mind and senses, they cannot receive God's grace and boon. It is only possible when they are completely immersed in Krishna consciousness.\nThe person whose senses and mind are always engaged in the divine love and devotion of God,\ndoes not have the possibility of being engaged in material activities. Devotees of God are never defeated\nanywhere in the universe. It is said, 'Narayana-parah: all those who are devoted to Narayana, that is, the devotee of God, is never\nfrightened, whether he is sent to hell or sent to heaven.' (Bhagavat 6. 17. 28)\nYasya vacha prajah sarvam gavastantyeva aterah |\nSalutations to the one who is the cause of the destruction of the sacrifices. 8>. Meaning of the word:\n\nOf whom--whose; Vacha--by Vedic instructions; Prajaah--living beings; Sarvaah--all; Gaavah--garden; Tantya-with rope; Iva--like;\nYantitaah- directed; Haranti- meet, take; Bali- offering, worship material; Ayatta: under control; Tasyaai: to him; Mukhyaya- to the main person; Te- to you; TH: - Respectful greetings L\n\nUnder the universe, all living beings are operated according to Vedic orders in the same way as\na bull is operated by a rope tied to its nose (nose ring). No one can violate the rules specified in the Vedic texts. We respectfully bow to that supreme being,\nWho gave us the Vedas. Meaning: The Vedic texts are the rules for the Supreme Personality of Godhead, the Supreme Being. No person can violate the orders given in the Vedic texts, just as no one can violate the rules of a monarchy. Any living being who desires real benefit in life should perform actions according to the instructions of Vedic literature. In this world, bound beings who have come for physical sensual gratification are driven by the orders of Vedic\nliterature. Sensual gratification is like salt.\nTo make food\ntasty, some salt is added, but no one can take too much salt\nnor too little. In this physical world, the bound souls should use their senses according to the orders of Vedic literature, otherwise they will fall into a more painful life. No human or\ndeity can make rules like Vedic literature, because all Vedic regulations were made by the Supreme Being"
