          "textEn": "459\nOf; between; of the water; Anuvikasat--blooming; Madhu--jasmine; Madhavinam--of jasmine flowers; Gandhen--with fragrance; Khandit--broken; Dhiyah--mind; Api--even so; Anilam--slow wind; Kshipantah--while laughing; Kshiptah--been; Kshiptah--been; Kshiptah––while laughing..\n\nThe residents of Vaikuntha, along with their wives and loved ones, take flights in their planes\nand solemnly praise the character of God and the actions that are always free from all inauspicious\nqualities. While singing the praises of God, they mock even the presence of fragrant and honey-filled\nflowered Madhavi flowers. Meaning: This verse suggests that Vaikuntha Loka is full of all the beings. There are planes\nin which the residents of that place, sitting with their loved ones, wander in the spiritual sky.\nThere is a gentle breeze carrying the fragrance of blooming flowers, and this breeze is so excellent that it\ncarries the honey of flowers with it. But the residents of Vaikuntha are so interested in praising the Lord\nthat while uttering the glory of the Lord, they do not like the disturbance caused by such an excellent breeze. In other words, they are pure bhaktas. They consider the glory of God\nmore important than their own sense of satisfaction. In the Vaikuntha worlds, the question of sensual pleasure\ndoes not arise. Smelling the fragrance of a bloomed flower is definitely the best, but it is only for\nsatisfaction of the senses. Those who live in Vaikuntha give the highest priority to serving God, not their own\nsenses of pleasure. By serving God in divine love, one attains such divine bliss that\ncompared to it, sensual pleasure is considered negligible.\nParavatanyabhrutasarasachakravak-\nDatyuhaHanshukatitiribardinam Yaha.\nKolaahalo viramatechiramatramuchyai-\nBhujagadhiphe Harikathamiva Gayamane. 18. Meaning of the word\n\nParavat--Pigeon; Anyabhrut--Cuckoo; Saras--Sarus; Chakvak--Chakai Chakwa; Datyuh--Chatak; Hans--Hans; Shuk--Sparrow;\nTotara--