          "textEn": "463\n\nWhy? Because they are eager to gain God's favor by doing so. It is also said here that Vaikuntha lakshanas are flawless. Generally, Lakshmiji\ndoes not stay permanently in one place. His name is Chashnala, meaning, “He who is not steady.''\nTherefore, we see that a person who is very wealthy can become extremely poor. One more\nexample is of Ravana. Ravana took Lakshmi, that is, Sita, into his kingdom, but instead of being happy by Lakshmi's\ngrace, his family and the entire empire were destroyed. In this way, in Ravana's\nhouse, Lakshmi is restless, meaning unstable. People of Ravana's class only desire Lakshmi without her husband, Narayan.\nTherefore, because of Lakshmiji, they become restless. Materialistic people find fault with Lakshmiji.\nBut in Vaikuntha, Lakshmiji remains engaged in the service of God. Being the goddess of fortune,\neven Lakshmiji cannot remain happy without the grace of God.\nEven for Lakshmi Ji to be happy,\nGod's grace is needed; yet, even the supreme being Brahmaji in the material world seeks Lakshmi's grace for happiness.\nHis face remains longing for her grace. In the water of the Vapi tree, in the water of the dissolved mud, in the water of the nectar,\nIn the own house, adorned with Tulsi, the lord of the gods.\nHe worships his own face, the one who is always smiling,\nHe who is spoken of by the Lord, that is the best. 22). Meaning of the word\n\nVapishu- in the pond; Vidrum- made of mango; Tatasu- shore; Amal- transparent; Amritam- like nectar; Apsu- water; Preshya-\nAnvita- surrounded by maids; Nija- in one's own garden; Tulsibhih- with Tulsi; Isham- God; Abhyarchati- worships; Su- tilak- adorned face; Unnasam- crooked nose; Ikshya- seeing; Vaktaram- mouth; Uchisitam-\nHaving kissed; Bhagwata- by God; Iti- thus; Amat- thought; Ank- O Gods; Yat- whose beauty.\n.\n\nAfter offering basil leaves on the edges adorned with the trunks of divine reservoirs in their gardens,\nthey worship God. While worshiping God, they, with raised noses,\ncan see the reflection of their beautiful faces in the water, and it seems as if\nby kissing the face by God, they have become even more beautiful. Meaning: Generally, when a friend's husband kisses her, her face becomes more\nbeautiful, hey. Even in Vaikuntha, Lakshmi is as beautiful as one can imagine"
