          "textEn": "467\nThey are spiritual people. Brahmaji says here that the spiritual world is above the world of gods. A person can only enter the abode of God when desirable qualities are highly developed in them. Devotee.\nIn it, all virtues are developed. In Shrimadbhagavatam (5.18.12) it is said that one who is\nunited with Krishna consciousness is endowed with all the virtues of the gods. In the physical world,\nthe virtues of gods are highly praised, just as, as our experience is, the virtues of a gentleman\nare praised more than those of an ignorant person or someone living in a lower life. The qualities of the gods of the higher castes are superior to the qualities of the inhabitants of this earth. Here, Brahmaji assures that only those individuals can enter the Bhagavad Gita who\nhave developed desirable qualities. In the Chaitanya Charitamrita, the number of desirable qualities of a devotee is 26.\nTATE has gone.\nHere they are: He is very compassionate; he does not quarrel with anyone; he\nAccepts Krishna-bhava-nimrit as the highest goal of life; he has equal respect for all; no fault can be found in his\ncharacter; he is generous, gentle, and always clean from inside and out; he\ndoes not claim anything in this world; he is benevolent to all living beings; he remains calm\nand is completely surrendered to Krishna; he is selfless; he is humble, Always stable,\nremains, and he has conquered the functions of the senses; he does not eat more than is necessary for the sustenance of the body; he does not remain crazy behind material recognition; he respects others,\n(is) worthy of respect, but he does not remain hungry for his own respect (is) Amani); he is very serious, very compassionate,\nand cordial; he is full of poetry; he is skilled in all tasks, and remains silent during unnecessary discussions. Similarly, Shrimadudhavagvat (3.25.21) mentions the virtues of a saintly person.\nIt is said that a saintly person who is worthy of going to the abode of