ले भगवान्‌ ने अपराध करने वालों पर उतनी ही दयादृष्टि से देखा\nजितनी कि अपराध किये जाने वालों पर। भगवान्‌ की यह प्रवृत्ति दिव्यगुणों के अपार आगार होने के",
          "textEn": "484\nCan do in a way that we see other things. Favored by the smile of gratitude, with a desire-fulfilling abode,\nWith the play of affection, touching the lotus-like heart. Shyama, adorned with the radiance of separation, Shriya, like a jewel, beautifully situated in the midst of the self. 39). Meaning of the word\n\nKritsnaprasad-- blessing everyone; Su-mukham-- auspicious face; Spruhanya-- desirable; Dham-- shelter; Sneh-- affection;\nSneha-- affection; Avaloka-- looking; Kalaya-- by part; Hadi-- in the heart; Sansprushantam- touching; Shyame-- dark-complexioned;\nTowards God; Purthau-- wide; Urasi-- on the chest; Shobhitaya- adorned; Shriya-- by Lakshmi; Swaha-- heaven; Chuda-\nManim-- forehead; Su-bhagyayantam- expanding good fortune; Iva- like; Atma- God; Dhinnyam- abode.\n.\n\nGod is the abode of all bliss. His auspicious presence is for the blessings of everyone.\nAnd his loving smile and gaze touch the innermost of the heart. The beautiful\ncolor of God's body is dark, and his broad chest is the resting place of Lakshmiji, which is the entire\nthe divine that glorifies the entire spiritual world, which is the pinnacle of all\nheavenly realms. In this way,\nIt seemed as if God himself was expanding the beauty and good fortune of the spiritual world.\nHe was expanding. Meaning: When the Lord came, he was pleased with everyone; that is why it is said here that Kritsn\nPrasad-yumrukham. God knew that even the guilty gatekeeper was his pure devotee, although coincidentally he\ncommitted an offense at the feet of other devotees. In devotion, committing an offense against a devotee is extremely fatal.\nIt happens. That is why Shri Chaitanya Mahaprabhu has said that the offense committed against a devotee is like that intoxicated elephant\nthat runs here and there.\nWhen a mad elephant enters a garden, it crushes all the plants. Similarly, the offense committed at the feet of a pure devotee destroys his position in devotion.\nAs far as God is concerned, he was not in any humiliating posture, because he does not accept any offense committed by his\ndevout devotee. But a devotee should be very careful about committing offenses against the feet of another devotee. To have equanimity on all and to be especially inclined towards his devotees, the Lord looked upon those who committed offenses with the same compassion as those who were to be offended.\nAs much as those who were to be offended. This is the nature of God, being an infinite treasure of divine qualities"
