\nयद्यपि परब्रह्म कौ प्रथम अनुभूति निर्विशेष ब्रह्म है, किन्तु परमेश्वर के निर्विशेष तेज का अनुभव करने\nसे मनुष्य को तुष्ट नहीं हो जाना चाहिए । इँशोपनिषद् में भी भक्त प्रार्थना करता है कि उसकी आँखों से",
          "textEn": "495\nIf purified, then the senses can not only be controlled by passive actions, but they can be engaged in the divine service of God, as the four sages--Sanak, Sanatan, Sanandan, and Sanatkumar--\nhad desired. Therefore, Krishna consciousness is not a concept created by the mind. This method\nThe order has been given in the Bhagavad Gita (9.34)-- Manmadha bhav madbhaktau madyaji ma nasaskuru. Kumara Oochu:\nYou, who are hidden within, even though gone, are the wicked.\nEven now, you are the root of my eyes, the infinite. Yartyova Karnaviveren Guhagato Nah\nPitranuvarnitaraha Bhavadubhave. 46). Meaning of the word\n\nKumarahuuchu- Kumaro said; Yah- Who; Anatarhithah- unexpressed; Hadi- in the heart; Gatth- is seated; Api- although;\nDuratmanam- to the wicked; Tvam- you; Aday- today; Ev- certainly; Nah- ours; Nayan- root- of the eye;\nAnant- Hey Anant; Radhah- attained; Yarhi- when; Ev- certainly; Karn- through the ear; Guham- intellect;\nFrom-- has been received; Nah-- to us; Pitra-- by father; Anuvarna-- described; Raha:-- mystery; Bhavat-Uddhava:--\nFrom your manifestation..\n\nKumaro said: O dear Lord, you do not appear before the cunning, although you are in the hearts of everyone.\nBut as far as our relationship is, we see you before us\neven though you are infinite. The statements we have heard from our ears about you through our father Brahma,\nthey have now become truly realized through your merciful manifestation. Meaning: Here, a description of those so-called ARTE is given, who focus their minds on the formless or void,\nor meditate on it. This verse from Shrimad Bhagavat describes those individuals\nwho, hoping that they will be highly skilled yogis engaged in meditation, but who cannot find God\nseated within their hearts.\nSuch individuals are called Duratya here, which means\n'* Extremely crooked-hearted' or 'less intelligent person is the opposite of a great soul, whose\nmeaning is 'large-hearted'.' Such so-called yogis who, even while engaged in meditation, do not have a broad heart,\ncannot see the four-armed Narayana form, even if God resides in their hearts. Although the Supreme Brahman is the first experience, the unspecialized Brahman, but by experiencing the unspecialized radiance of God,\nman should not be satisfied. Even in the Bhagavad Gita, the devotee prays that from his eyes"
