          "textEn": "52\nThese are the best of the gods, the best of the gods, the best of the gods.\nThey are the best of the gods who are not obtained by other means.\nThey are the best of the gods, the best of the gods, the best of the gods.\nThe best of the gods, the best of the gods, the best of the gods, who are not obtained by other means. 10. Meaning of word\n\nYe- which is male; Me- mine; Tanu:--body; Dwija-Vārān- best among Brahmins; Duhṭiḥ- Cows; Madiya- Those related to me;\nBhūtani- Living beings; Alabdha- Without shelter; Cha- And; Bheda- By difference; Dṛkṣanti- See; Agha- Sin; Kṣānti- Destruction; Dasha- Ten; Nirnaya- Judgment; Hi- Because; Ahi- Snake; Manūrya- Angry; Tan- To those people;\nGrīdhra- Messengers like vultures; Rūṣa- From anger; Mama- Mine; Kuṣanti- To pierce; Adhidaṇḍa- One who oversees punishment; Yamarāja\nEan\n\nBrahmin, Cows and helpless creatures are my body.\nThose people whose decision-making power has been damaged due to their sins, they see them separately from me. They are like angry snakes.\nAnd they, like the jackals of Yamraj, the supervisors of sinful men, tear them apart in anger. Meaning: According to Brahma-Samhita, cows, Brahmins, women, children, and old men are helpless creatures. Among these five, Brahmins and cows are specifically mentioned in this verse, because God is always concerned about the welfare of Brahmins\nand cows and is devoted in this way. Therefore, God\nspecifically advises that no one should be jealous of these five, especially cows and Brahmins. In some parts of the Bhagavata, the word 'duhitrih' is used in place of 'kimidiin' (some kind of).\nIt is found. But the meaning comes out from both the words.\nGd stands for \"'cow' and\nShea: means \"cow!\". It may be, because the cow is considered the daughter of the Sun God ©. Just as\nchildren are taken care of by their parents, similarly, the care of women should be taken by the father, husband, or\nwise son. Those who are helpless should be taken care of by their guardians.\nIt should be known, otherwise they will be punished by Yamraj, because Yamraj is appointed by God to inspect the activities of sinful beings. Here, the comparison of Yamraj's assistants or messengers\nis given with vultures, and those who do not fulfill their duty to protect their protectors are compared to snakes. Vultures treat snakes very badly; similarly, messengers are negligent.\nThey treat guardians badly."
