          "textEn": "59\nAs a Karmayogi, a Jnanyogi, a Dhyanayogi, and a Bhaktiyogi. Karmajin, in particular, seek the grace of the gods.\nThe wise want to become one with the Supreme Brahman, and the yogis are satisfied by merely a partial glimpse of God (the Supreme Soul) and ultimately by attaining oneness with Him. But the devotees\nwant to attain the eternal presence of the complete Supreme Personality of Godhead and serve Him. This has already been\nproven that God is eternal, and those who constantly seek God's grace are also\neternal. Therefore, the meaning of yogis here is devotees.\nBy the grace of God, devotees can easily cross the ignorance of birth and death\nand attain the eternal abode of God. Therefore,\nGod does not need the grace of others, because no one is equal to Him, nor is anyone greater than Him.\nIn fact, for the successful realization of one's human purpose, everyone needs the grace of God.\n\nHe who, by becoming the manifestation of the divine,\nwithout any other means,\nwithout any other purpose, with his own head, with his own feet,\nwithout any other means, with his own head, with his own feet,\nwithout any other means, with his own head, with his own feet,\nwill attain the abode of the unattached. 20>. Meaning of the word\nYam- that which; Vai- certainly; Vibhuti- Lakshmi Ji; Upayati- stands in anticipation; Anuvelam- whenever; Anyaah- by others; Arthah- material comfort; Arthibhih- by those who desire; Swa- on one's own head; Dhritah- accepted; Pada- of feet; Renu- dust; Dhanyah- by the devotees; Arpitah- offered; Adghri- at your feet; Tulsi- Tulsi\nOf the group; new--innovative; price--on the garland; Dhamnah--with place; Lokam--place; Madhu-vrat-patih--of the king of the Bhauras; Iva--\nSimilar; Kama-yana--desiring to attain..\nThe dust of whose feet other people wear on their heads, there Lakshmi Devi stays in your\npre-determined service, because she remains eager to secure a place in the abode of the king of those bumblebees\nwho hangs on the fresh garland of basil leaves offered by a blessed devotee at your feet. Meaning: As has been described before, because Tulsi is offered at the lotus feet of God,\nTulsi has received all the best qualities. The simile used here is very good. Just as the king of Bharo\nremains hovering over the lotus petals offered at the lotus feet of God, similarly, Lakshmiji,"
