          "textEn": "52] Therefore, when God says that Lakshmiji does not leave them only by the unintentional grace of Brahmins,\nwe can understand that Lakshmiji is attracted by the opulence of God, not by the blessings of Brahmins.\nGod is not dependent on the grace of anyone for His pleasure; He is always self-contented. This statement of God that his authority is due to the blessings of Brahmins and Vaishnavas,\nis to give this lesson to other people that they should respect Brahmins and Vaishnavas who\nare devotees of God.\n\nDharmasya te bhagavatasthriyuga tribhih swaih\nPadhishcharacharamidam dwijadevatartham.\nNunam bhrutam tadabhighati rajastamashcha\nSattvena no varadaya tanuva nirasyah. 22). Meaning of the word\n\nOf Dharma--the embodiment of Dharma; Tene--yours; Bhagavat--of the Lord; Trayi-yuga--you who appear in the three ages; Tribhih--three;\nSvayam--own; Padi:--by the feet; Char-Acharam:--both moving and non-moving; Idam:--this universe; Dwija:--one who is born twice\nGod--God; for the meaning of 'Artham'; 'Nunam'- but; 'Bhrutham'- protected; 'Tat'- those feet; 'Abhighati'- destroying; 'Rajah'-\nRajoguna; 'Tamah'- Tamoguna; 'Cha'- likewise; 'Satvane'- of the Satguna; 'Naham'- to us; 'Var'- with kindness; 'Daya'- giving all blessings; 'Tanuva'- by one's own\ndivine form; 'Nirasya'- by chasing away; Oh Lord, You are the direct manifestation of Dharma. Therefore, you manifest yourself in all three eras, and in this\nway, you protect this universe in which moving and non-moving beings reside. You, being of pure essence,\nand by bestowing all blessings, may you drive away the Rajo and Tamo Gunas of the gods and twice-born. Meaning: In this verse, God is said to have appeared in the three eras--Satya, Dwapara, and Treta--of the three yugas. There is no mention here of their appearance in the fourth age or Kali Yuga.\nIt is described in Vedic literature that they come in the Kali Yuga as 99 incarnations, but do not appear\nas incarnations. But in other eras, God appears as an incarnation, that is why they are addressed as Triyuga\nby saying. Sridhar Swami describes Trayuga as follows: The word yuga means \"pair\" and tri means \"three\".\"God manifested in the form of three pairs or three pairs of opulence through his six opulences"
