          "textEn": "526\nAsmaasu--to us; va--or; yah--whoever; yogyah--appropriate; shviyatamu--to be given; sah--that; danda--punishment;\nYe--who; anagasau--sinless; vayam--we; ayudhamahi--has been ordained; kilbishena--from a curse..\n\nOh Lord, whatever punishment you want to give to these two innocent people or to us, we will accept it without\ndoubt. We know that we have cursed two innocent people. Meaning: Now the four Kumaras, the sages, Jaya and Vijaya, the curse given to the two gatekeepers,\nthey nullify it, because now they have realized that a person engaged in the service of God\ncannot be guilty in any situation. It is said that whoever has faith in the service of God,\nor whoever is truly engaged in divine love and devotion, possesses all the virtues of the gods. Therefore, a devotee can never be guilty. If it is ever found that he has made a mistake by chance or\ndue to some momentary arrangement, then it should not be taken seriously.\nHere,\nOn being cursed with victory and defeat, regret is being expressed. Now, the Kumaras are thinking of their situation\nas a form of Rajo and Tamo Gunas, and they are ready to accept any kind of punishment given by God. Generally, while dealing with devotees, we should not try to find fault. It is also confirmed in the Bhagavad Gita that the devotee who serves God with devotion, even if he is found to have made a grave mistake, should still be considered a saint. He may make a mistake due to his\npast habits, but because he is engaged in the service of his God,\nthat mistake should not be taken seriously.\nShri Bhagavan said:\nHaving attained this state of liberation from the gods and others,\nAnd having been bound by the yoga of beginninglessness, endlessness, and non-end,\nAgain, he should offer a repentance, quickly.\nThe Brahmins are the ones who are the cause of my curse. 26). Meaning of the word:\n\nShri-Bhagavan said--Bhagavan replied; Etau- these two gatekeepers; Sur- others-- demons; Gati- motion; Pratipadya- to be read;\nSadyah- quickly; Sarambh- with anger; Sambhrith- having become; Samadhi- concentration of mind; Anubaddh- attached; Yogau-\nBound by me; Bhuyah- sons; Sakasham- to me; Upayasyaatah- will return; Ashu- quickly; Yah- who; Vah- yours; Shapah- curse;\nMaya- by me; Ev- only; Nimitah- appointed; Tat- that; Aveet- know; Vipraah- O Brahmins..\nGod replied: O Brahmins, know that the punishment you have given them is"
