          "textEn": "53]\nMe, all are included in Bhakti Yoga. If someone somehow becomes attached to the Supreme God,\nthen he engages in devotion, as described in Shrimad Bhagavatam (10.29.15)--\nLust, anger, fear. The Gopis were attracted to Krishna through devotion in matters of work. Similarly, Kansa\nwas attached to devotion due to the fear of his death. In this way, devotion is so powerful\nthat by becoming an enemy of God and constantly thinking about Him, a person can be saved very quickly.\nIs. It is said-- Vishnu's devotees are remembered, the gods are the opposite of demons-- Vishnu's devotees are called gods,\nwhile the non-devotees are called demons. But devotion is so powerful that both gods and demons\ncan benefit from it if they constantly contemplate God. The basic principle of devotion is\nto constantly contemplate about God. In the Bhagavad Gita (18.65), God says -- Manmana bhavah\nMadbhaktaha- always contemplate on Me.\nWhether someone contemplates in any way, it makes no difference;\nContemplating God is the fundamental principle of devotion.\n\nIn the material world, there are various categories of sinful deeds, among which disrespecting a Brahmin or a Vaishnava\nis the most sinful. Here it is clearly stated that even from this grave sin, a person can be freed merely by contemplating Vishnu,\nnot in a favorable manner, but by doing so in anger. In this way,\nthose devotees who always contemplate Vishnu are also freed from all sinful deeds. Krishna bhava naamrata is the highest form of thinking. In this era, contemplate Lord Vishnu, Hare Krishna, Hare Krishna,\nKrishna Krishna, Hare Hare. Hare Rama, Hare Rama, Rama Rama, Hare Hare is done through Kirtan.\nFrom the stories of Bhagwat, it seems that if someone contemplates Krishna even as an enemy, then this special\nqualification--contemplation of Vishnu or Krishna--washes away all his sins.\nDwahastavadishyam Bhagavan Vimanashrenibhushanam.\nSarvatishayaya Lakshmya Jushtam Swam Dhinnyam Avishat