          "textEn": "29\nIt seems that Yogi is being attacked by death, but to keep serving God,\nhe overcomes many such attacks with His grace. Because God, with the strength of his\ncourage, remains independent, therefore devotees also, by his grace, live to serve him.\nThey remain alive. Again, the lord of the gods, driven by desire, was angry and struck with a loud thud. 96. Meaning of the word:\nAgain - then; Gadaam - mace; Swam - own; Aadaya - having taken; Bhramayantam - being wandering; Abhikshnaashah - repeatedly; Harih - Lord; Krodhah - angry; Sarambhat - in anger; Dashtah - bitten;\nhappened, chewing; Dachchadam - own ES L\n\nThen, Lord, showing his anger, rushed towards that demon, who was angry\n\nbecause of which he was chewing his lips. He again picked up his mace and repeatedly\nstarted rotating it. Tatsacha gadayaritim dakshinasya bhruvo prabhu.\nToday, with that gentle heart, the wise one spoke. 17). Meaning of the word:\nTata- Tavaah; Cha- Tatha; Gadaya- with his mace; Aaratim- to the enemy; Dakshinasyaam- to the right; Bhruvi- on the forehead;\nPrabhuh- by God; Aajaghne- struck; |: - God; §- but; Tam- mace; Saumya- gentle; Hey Bhadra Vidur;\nGadaya- with his mace; Kovidah- skillful; Ahanat- he saved himself. Then God struck the enemy's right eyebrow with his mace, but he\n\nwas skilled in the war of demons, therefore, O Bhadra Vidur, he saved himself with the swiftness of his mace. And with maces, with clubs, with clubs, Haryaksha, Hari, and others. Jigishaya sansarabdhavanyonyamabhijaghnatu. 18. Meaning:\nThus--in this manner; Gadabhyam--with his maces; Gurubhyam--with their heads; Haryakshah--the demon named Haryaksha (Hiranyaksha);\nHarih--Lord Hari; Ev--Indeed; Cha--and; Jigishaya--with the desire for victory; Su-sanrakshau--angry;\nAnyonyam-mutually; Abhijaghnatu-him; they struck."
