          "textEn": "40\nAh--He said; Ch--And; Ayudham--weapon; Adhatshva--acquire; Ghatasva--try; Tvamu--you;\nJigishasi--wanting to win; Iti--like this; Uktah--calling out; Sahah--Hiranyaksha; Tada--that; Bhuyah--again; Tadayan--attacking; Vyanadat--roaring; Bhrusham--fiercely..\n\nThen God said, \"' You pick up your weapon and if you are willing to win me, then\nagain try.\"Upon being challenged with these words, the Asura threw his mace at the Lord.\nAnd again, he roared loudly. Ta sa apatiti vikshya bhagavansamavasthitaah. Having obtained the earth, the earth is like Garuda. 19. Meaning of the word:\nTam--That mace;\nVikshya--Having seen; Bhagavan--Lord;\nSamavasthitah--Standing firmly; Jagraha--Having caught; Lilaaya--Simply; Praptaam--From there; Garutman--\nGaruda; Iva--Like; Pannagim--To the serpent.\n.\nWhen God saw the mace coming towards him, he stood firmly right there.\nAnd he effortlessly grabbed it in the same way that the king of birds, Garuda, would grab a snake.\nHe grabbed it. The great demon killed by the self-formed man. The one who is given by the sixteen-folded one, the radiant one, the one without a rival. 12). Meaning of the word:\nSelf-manifestation--one's own manliness or effort; defeated--became in vain; killed--destroyed; pride--arrogance; great demon--great;\nThe demon did not wish to take; Gadaam--mace; Diyamanam--given; Harina--by Hari; Vigat-\nPrabha--decreasing in brightness.\nThus, seeing his own efforts was in vain, that great demon became extremely ashamed\nand his radiance was lost. Now he was hesitant to accept the mace being returned by Shri Bhagavan. The one who holds the trident, the one who is fiercely burning with the fire of sacrifice,\nThe one who is steadfast in the yajna, the one who is the form of a Brahmin, the one who is the one who walks in the right path. 123. Shabdharam"
