          "textEn": "4]\nJagraha-- raised; trishikham-- with three points; shulam-- trident; jalat-- burning; jwalan-- fire;\nLolupam-- lapalapata; yagya- all sacrifices; dhrita-rupaya- in the form of Varaha; vipraya- to the Brahmin;\nAbhicharan- by misuse; yatha- as.Now he, like a blazing fire, brandished a trident and threw it at the enjoyers of all sacrifices, just as a person throws his penance on a sacred Brahmin\nout of malice.\n\nTadojasa Daityamahabhataarpitam\nChakasadantahksha Udiranadidhiti.\nChakren Chichcheda Nishatanemina\nHariyatha Taryapatatramujjitam. 14.\nMeaning of the word:\n\nThat- that trident; Ojasa- with all his might; Daitya- demon; Maha- great; Bhat- great warrior; Arpitamu- offered;\nThrown; Chakasat- shining; Antah- in the middle of the sky; Udirna- raised; Dijiti- light;\nChakren- with the Sudarshan Chakra; Chichhed- cut into pieces; Nishat- sharp; Nemina- edge (of a border); Harih- Indra;\nYatha- as; Tarya- of Garuda; Patram- wing; Uchchitam- sacrificed..\n\nThat supreme warrior, the Asura, threw his full force, that trident was rapidly\nshining in the sky. But Shri Bhagwan, with his sharp Sudarshan Chakra, cut off its pieces,\nCutting them into pieces, as if Indra had cut off Garuda's wing. Meaning: The context of Garuda and Indra who came here is as follows. Once, Garuda\nto rescue his mother Vinata from the clutches of the serpent Kaumaata and his stepmother Kadru,\nsnatched the vessel of nectar from the gods in heaven.\nWhen this was discovered, King Indra of heaven\nThrew his thunderbolt at Garuda. Although Garuda was invincible, to honor Indra's infallible weapon\nand because he was the vehicle of God himself, he dropped one of his wings\nwhich was shattered by the thunderbolt. The inhabitants of the higher realms are so sensitive that\neven in battle, they follow the rules of civility. Garuda wanted to express respect towards Indra,\nand he knew that something was bound to be destroyed by Indra's thunderbolt, therefore he\ngave away one of his wings."
