          "textEn": "42\nHaving struck with his own trunk, Hari struck with his own trunk repeatedly.\nHaving spread out his body, he became like a mountain.\nThe increasing anger, with a hard fist,\nHaving struck, he struck again. 95 Meaning of the word:\nVrkno-- upon being cut; Swa-shula-apna-- trident; Bha-maha-- in many sections; Arina-- by Sudarshan Chakra; Hare-ha--\nof Shri Bhagwan; Pratyatya-or increasing; Vistirnam-- wide; Ur-ha-- chest; Vibhuti-mat-- the goddess of wealth;\nAbode; Pravriddha-- grown; Roshah-- anger; Sah-ha-- Hiranyaksha; Kathor-- hard; Mushtina-with his fist; Nadan-\nroaring; Prahatya-- by striking; Antardhayat-- disappeared; Asurah-Asura L\nWhen his trident was shattered into pieces by the chakra of Shri Bhagwan, the Asura became extremely\nangry. Therefore, he lunged towards God and, making a loud roar, struck his broad\nchest, which had the mark of Srivatsa, with his hard fist. Then,\nhe disappeared. Meaning: The white hair is full of, which is on the chest of God and is a unique sign of being Shri Bhagwan.\nAll inhabitants of Vaikuntha Loka or Goloka Vrindavan\nare similar to Shri Bhagavan, therefore, through this Shrivatsa symbol, God is recognized among others.\nThus, the most important one, the one who is like Bhagavan, is like a goat.\nEven if a goat is hit by a nose, it is killed. 16). Meaning of the word\nTen--by Hiranyaksha; Ittham--in this way; Ahata-- struck; Kshatah--he who struck; Vidur--Oh Vidur; Bhagavan--Oh Lord;\nAdi-sukarah--the first pig; Na Akampat--not shaken; Manak--not even a little; Qu Api--anywhere; Sraja--\nFrom a garland of flowers; Hatah- struck; Iva- like; Sadah Dwipah--elephant. Hey Vidur, upon being struck by the Asura in this manner, the body of Lord Adi Varaha\nDid not move even a bit, as if struck by a garland of flowers on an elephant. Meaning: As it has been told, this Asura was previously a companion of God in Vaikuntha Loka.\nBut due to some reason, he became an Asura. He was fighting with God for his salvation.\nGod is giving the same joy as the blow inflicted by him in his divine body"
