          "textEn": "59\nBlessed is the fame, the foot, the long life,\nOf the lords of life, the warriors, the increase of valor,\nNarayana, the end, the unmoving, the one who listens. 38. Meaning of the word\n\nEtat- This narrative; Maha- Punyam- Punyaprada; Alam- Extreme; Pavitram- Pure; Dhanyam- Blessed; Yashas- Attainment of fame; Padam- Vessel, vessel; Ayuha- Longevity; Ashisham- Desires;\nPran- Life-giver of the limbs; Indriyanam- Of the organs of action; Yudhi- In the battlefield; Shaurya- Valor; Vardhanam- To increase;\nNarayanah- Lord Narayana; Ante- At the end of life; Gatiha- Refuge; Ank- Okay Shaunak; Shrunvatam- To listen. This supremely sacred narrative (character) is one that gives unique fame, wealth, glory, longevity, and\ndesirable fruits. In the battlefield, this is something that enhances the strength of the human life force and the organs of action. Hey Shaunak, whoever hears this in his final moments, he\ngoes to the supreme abode of God.\nMeaning: Devotees are generally attracted to the narratives of the Lord's pastimes.\nEven without performing penance or meditation, by merely listening to these pastimes, one attains many benefits, such as wealth, fame, longevity, and other desires of life. If someone, at the end of their life, listens to the complete Srimad Bhagavatam,\nfilled with the narratives of the Lord's pastimes,\nthey certainly attain the abode of the Lord. In this way, to the listeners,\neven as they live in this physical world, they also gain benefit in the end times. This is the ultimate pure benefit of devotion. The beginning of devotion is done by taking out time\nand listening to Shri Padbhagavat from the right person. Sri Chaitanya Mahaprabhu also praises the five\nsayings of devotion. This is the service of devotees of God, chanting the Hare Krishna mantra, listening to the Srimad Bhagavatam, worshiping the Shri Vigraha of God, and residing in pilgrimage sites.\nBy doing these five deeds,\na person can overcome the suffering of material life.\n\nThus, within the third canto of Srimad Bhagavat, the twenty-ninth chapter, entitled \"The Killing of Asura Hiranyaksha ^\"\nThe Bhakti Vedanta meaning is completed."
