          "textEn": "94\nare for making one capable for the practice of self-knowledge. But to attain real self-knowledge,\nother qualifications are required. In these, the worship of knowledge or God\nis mandatory. The word yoga is sometimes used to describe the physical postures (asanas) that are performed as a means to concentration of the mind. Generally, these physical actions that are adopted in the yoga method,\nignorant people consider them as the result of yoga,\nthey sit, but these are actually to concentrate the mind on God.\nFor the sake of economic\ngrowth, after creating humans, Brahma created the sages, so that they\ncould establish the ideal of self-realization.\nTebhyashchaikashah swasya dehasyamshamadajah |\nYatat samadhiyogardhitapavidyaviraktimat. 53. Meaning of the word:\n\nTebhyah--from them; Cha--and; Ekakshah--each one; Swasya--yours; Dehasya--of the body; Ansham--part, portion;\nAdat--provided; Ajah--unborn Brahma; Yat--that which; Tat--that; Samadhi- deep meditation; Yoga- concentration of mind;\nRiddhi- natural power; Tapah- penance; Vidya- knowledge; Virakti- detachment; Mat- joined. The unborn creator of the universe (Brahma) gave each of these sons a part of his body,\nwhich was endowed with deep contemplation, mental concentration, natural power,\npenance, worship, and the characteristics of detachment.\n\nMeaning: The word viraktimat in this verse means \"one endowed with dispassion\".\"\nBhutikvatikta is a lack of self-awareness.\nThose who remain engrossed in sensual pleasures,\nit is impossible for them to attain self-realization. It is said in the Bhagavad Gita that those who are attached to the pursuit of wealth and material pleasures, they cannot attain Yoga-Samadhi, that is,\nabsorption in Krishna-bhava-nimrita. Those who advertise that humans, in this\nlife, while enjoying material pleasures, can make spiritual progress, that is merely\nostentation. There are four elements of detachment--(1) to avoid unlawful worldly life,\n(2) to avoid meat,\n(3) to avoid intoxicating substances, and (4) to avoid gambling. These four elements are penance"
