्नत हैं, वे जन्म-जन्मान्तर श्रीभगवान्‌ के इस रूप का दर्शन करना चाहते हैं। वे कोई\nबनावटी योगी न थे । जो वास्तव में महान्‌ हैं, वे भगवान्‌ के नित्य रूप के दर्शन की ही कामना\nकरते हैं।",
          "textEn": "]07\nThe sage said-- The sage said; Jushtam is attained; Bat-- Ahah; Adya-- Now; Akhil- All; Satva--\nOf good qualities; Raho-- Which is the abode; Sansiddhyam- Complete success; Akshno-- Of both eyes; Tava-- Yours; Darshanat-- From seeing;\nNa-- By us; Yat- Whose; Darshanam- Seeing; Janmabhi-- By birth; Idya-- O venerable one;\nSadhidah-- Gradually attained; Ashasate-- Have great ambitions; Yoginah-- Yogis; Rudh- Yogis; Yoga- In yoga\nAttained\n\nKardam Muni said- O most venerable Lord, Your abode is the source of all existence.\nHaving attained your darshan (vision), my darshan is fulfilled. Great yogis repeatedly take birth\nand keep aspiring to have a vision of your divine form in deep meditation.\n\nMeaning: Here, Shri Bhagwan is called the abode of all virtues and all bliss.\nIs. Real happiness is not found until the quality of goodness is attained.\nTherefore, when someone serves God through their mind, speech, and action, they attain the state of perfection. Kardam Muni says, \"You are known by the name of virtue (Sattva),\nyou are the abode of all things, and now you have attained the perfection of seeing by looking at your own eyes.\"\nYou have attained it.\" Such statements are purely devotional. For a devotee, the significance of the senses is in serving God. The significance of the eyes lies in observing the beauty of God, the significance of the auditory sense lies in listening to the praises of the Lord, the significance of the tongue lies in tasting the offering.\nWhen all senses\nare engaged in the service of God, then this type of accomplishment is called Bhakti Yoga,\nwhich means engaging the senses, which are attached to materiality, in the service of God. When a person\nfree from all kinds of worldly attachments and fully\nattaches himself to the service of God, then his service is called Bhakti Yoga, which means engaging the senses, which are attached to materiality, in the service of God. Kardam Muni accepts that in Bhakti Yoga,\nto have the vision of God himself is the meaningfulness (siddhi) of the vision. Kardam Muni has not exaggerated the greatness of the vision,\nHe has not exaggerated the accomplishment. They present proof that those who are truly advanced in yoga, desire to see this form of Shri Bhagavan, from birth to birth. They were not any\nfake yogis. Those who are truly great desire only to see the eternal form of God."
