          "textEn": "09\nWhat is the ultimate goal? Therefore, they consider sensual gratification to be the highest goal of life. They\nStart giving this advice that along with the enjoyment of senses, they can wish for accomplishment by chanting some mantras and doing some asanas. Such persons are here called Hata-medhas,\nThat is, \"those whose minds have gone bad § .\" Such people desire material\nhappiness through yoga, perfection, or meditation. God has said in the Bhagavad Gita that those who worship the gods\ntheir intellect is corrupted. Similarly, here also, Kardam Muni says that whoever\ndesires material happiness through yoga practice, understand that his intellect has deteriorated\nand he is a great fool. One who is a good practitioner of yoga should worship Shri Bhagavan and\nbesides crossing the ocean of ignorance and attaining the sight of the lotus feet of God,\nshould not have any other desire.\nBut God is so merciful that even today, He gives such intellectually impaired people the boon of enjoying happiness through intercourse and sensual gratification by making them dogs, cats, and pigs. In the Bhagavad Gita, God has confirmed this boon, \"Whenever a man wants to obtain something from me,\nI grant him the desired boon.\"And I want to be a great helper.\nA cow of equal character, a house of wealth. Upeyivanmoolamshoshmoolam\nDurashayah Kamadughandhripsasya. 15). Meaning of the word\nAnd--in the same way; Sah-me--myself; Cha--also; Aham--in me; Parivodu-kamah--with the desire to marry; Saman-\nSheelam--a girl with a similar character; Griha-medh--in married life; Dhenum--Kamadhenu; Upayivan--came near; Moolam--root (of lotus); Ashesh--of every object; Moolam-source; Durashayah--with desire; Kam-dhug--\nOne who provides all desires; Anghripshyasya--your tree-like form.\nTherefore, I too, with the desire to marry a girl of the same nature,\nI have come to the refuge of your lotus feet, who can be proven like Kamadhenu in fulfilling my desires in my 