          "textEn": "[3\nLovers of the people, followers of the people,\nHaving heard, they are at your feet.\nEach other's virtues are the source of nectar,\nThe bodies of the emanations of nectar. 17). Meaning of the word\n\nLokan- worldly activities; cha-- and; lokan-followers of the world; pashun--\nanimal-like; cha-- and; hitva-- abandoning; shritaha— surrendered; te-- yours; charan-- lotus feet; atpatram-\numbrella; parasparam- from each other; tvam- yours; guna- of qualities; vada- through argument; sidhu- intoxicant; peeyush-\nfrom nectar; niryapta- extinguished; deha-dharma- these are the basic necessities of the body. Even then, those men who have abandoned the conventional worldliness and their animal-like followers,\nand those who, through mutual exchange of ideas, have partaken of the intoxicating nectar (Sudha) of your virtues and\ndeeds, have taken the umbrella of your lotus feet,\ncan be freed from the basic necessities of the physical body.\nMeaning: After describing the necessity of married life, Kardam Muni\nThey emphasize that for those who are engrossed in material sensual pleasures, marriage and other\nsocial functions are rigid (dhise-pithe) rules. The rules of animal life--eating, sleeping, mating, and\nprotecting--are actually the needs of the body, but the man who renounces these\nidiotic activities of this material world and engages himself in divine devotion to Krishna, he is freed from social\nidioms. A bound soul remains under the control of Maya or eternal time--past, present, and future--\nbut as soon as he engages himself in devotion to Krishna, he transcends the limitations of the past, future, and present, and becomes situated in the eternal activities of the soul. For the pleasures and enjoyments of material life, a person has to follow the Vedic commands,\nBut those who have accepted to worship God are not afraid of any kind of rules and regulations of this material world.\nSuch devotees do not care about the conventions of material activities;\nthey fearlessly accept that refuge which serves as an umbrella for the cycle of birth and death."
