          "textEn": "5\nN--No; Te--Yours; Aj--Among; Aks--Spindle; Bhrami--Rotating; Ayu--Life span, above; Esham--Of these;\nOf devotees; Trayodash--Thirteen; Aram--Loops, knots; Trayi--Six;\nNemi--Surroundings, rim; Anant--Infinite; Chhadi--Leaf, leaf; Yat--Which; Tri--Three; Nabhi--Navel; Karal--\nOf the source; With tremendous speed; Jagat--Universe; Achchhadi--Cutting; Dhavat--Running.. It has thirteen spokes (Are), 360 joints, six circumferences and on it are woven infinite leaves. Although its rotation reduces the lifespan of the entire creation,\nthis intensely fast cycle cannot touch the age of the devotees of God.\nIt cannot. Meaning: Devotees are not affected by the lifespan of time.\nIn the Bhagavad Gita,\nit is said that by doing a little devotion, a person is saved from even the greatest of troubles. The biggest\ntrouble is the soul's transmigration from one body to another, and the only way to avoid this is\nto worship God. It is said in Vedic literature-- Without the grace of God, one cannot cross the cycle of birth and death.\nWithout the grace of God, one cannot get rid of the cycle of birth and death. In the Bhagavad Gita,\nit is said that only by understanding the divine nature of God and His actions, His manifestation and disappearance,\ncan the cycle of death be stopped and man can return to God.\nTime is divided into several parts-- such as moment, clock, month, year, periods,\nseasons, etc. All the departments described in this verse are measured by the Vedic literature's Jyotirvidiya\nenumerations. There are six seasons in a year, and there is a four-month Chaturmasya. Three such Chaturmasya's make a year. According to Vedic astrology, there are thirteen\nmonths.\nThe thirteenth month is called Adhimas or Malamas, and it is added every three years.\nBut this period cannot touch the age of the devotees. In another verse it is said\nthat at the time of sunrise and sunset, the sun takes the life of all living beings, but those\nwho are engrossed in devotion, the sun cannot take their lives. Here, the comparison of time is done with a vast\ncycle, from which 360 are added, six circumferences in the form of seasons, and in the form of moments"
