          "textEn": "58\nYou will have to plead, because you need it, and it is possible that he may not treat you\nwell. Then you can lose your honor.The second characteristic of this incident is that even after becoming the self-created Manu emperor, he went to offer his worthy\ndaughter to a poor Brahmin. Kardam Muni had no household, he was living in the forest\nin a hut, but he was cultured. Therefore, while giving away one's daughter to a person,\ninstead of paying attention to property or other worldly matters,\nculture and virtue are given preference. I have come to listen to you, ready for the purpose of marriage.\nTherefore, I have come to you, to accept your proposal. 14.\nMeaning of the word.\n\nI--me; Tva--to you; Ashrunavam--to hear; Vidan--this wise man; Vivah-Artham--for the sake of marriage; Samudyatam--ready; Aatah--therefore; Tvam--you; Upakurvana-not taking a vow of lifelong celibacy; Prattam--met; Pratigrahana--please accept; Me--mine\\\n\nSvayambhuva Manu said-O wise one, I have heard that you are desirous of marriage. Please\nAccept the virgin girl (offered) by me in charity, because you\nHave not taken a vow of lifelong celibacy.\n\nMeaning: Being unmarried is the principle of celibacy. Brahmacharyas are of two types--one is\nNaishtik-Brahmachari, who observes lifelong celibacy, and the other is Upakurban-\nBrahmachari, who remains celibate until a certain age. For example, someone up to twenty-five years old\ncan enter married life by remaining celibate and with the permission of their guru. Brahmacharya.\nIt is the student life and the beginning of entry into the spiritual ashram.\nOnly a householder\ncan remain engaged in sensual gratification or worldly pleasures, not a celibate. Swayambhuva Manu asked Kardam\nto accept his daughter, therefore he prayed, because Kardam had not taken the vow of celibacy. They were interested in getting married, and they were getting a suitable bride from the royal family."
