          "textEn": "60\nWork: He is the lord of all men.\nSons are known in all the ways of marriage.\nWho should not be displeased with his daughters?\nHe himself shoots arrows like a hunter. 16. Meaning of the word\n\nWork:--Desire; He:--He; Bhuyat:--Being; Nar-Deva:--Hey King; Te:--Yours; ITE:--This; Putriyaah:--Daughter;\nSamagnyaa:--By the prescribed method; Pratitah:--Approved; Kah:--Who; Eva:--Indeed; Tena:By you; Tanayam:By the daughter;\nNa aadriyet:--Will not respect; Swayaam:By oneself; Eva:Indeed; Kanta:By the radiance of the body;\nKshipatim:--Will disrespect; Iva:Like; Shriyamu:Ornaments..\n\nYou fulfill your daughter's desire for marriage, which is accepted by the Vedas. Who\nwould not want to accept that? She is so beautiful that her physical radiance itself is surpassing the beauty of her\nornaments. Note: Kardama Muni wanted to marry Devahuti by the prescribed rituals.\nAccording to the Vedas,\nthe best method is to invite the groom to the bride's house and give the bride-\ngroom a dowry, along with jewelry, gold, furniture, and other household items\nin the form of dowry. Even today, this form of marriage is prevalent among upper-class Hindus, and\nin the scriptures, it is called a very religious act for the bride's father. To the suitable son-in-law,\ndonating one's daughter (Kanyadaan) is considered a virtuous act for a householder. In Yanusmriti,\nthere is mention of eight types of marriages, but now only one of these marriages, Brahma\nor Rajasik marriage, is prevalent. In this Kali Yuga, other types of marriages--such as those by love,\nby garland, by abduction--are forbidden. Earlier, Kshatriyas used to abduct the princess of another\nroyal family according to their wishes, then there was a war between that Kshatriya and the family members of the maiden,\nand if the abductor was victorious, then the maiden was given to him.\nEven Shri Krishna married Rukmini in this way, and their and some of their sons\nand grandsons also did the same. Shrikrishna's grandson abducted Duryodhana's daughter.\nBecause of which, a war broke out between the Kauravas and the Yadavas. Later, the Kaurava dynasty's elders made a compromise"
