          "textEn": "]68\n\nMaharani Shatarupa lovingly provided the bride and groom (daughter-in-law and son-in-law) with many valuable gifts, such as ornaments, clothes, and household items, according to the occasion.\n\nMeaning: Even today, in India, the practice of giving dowry along with the gift of a daughter is prevalent. To be given\nAccording to the father's status (position) of the bride. Parinih Mahadhanan ka\nMeans the dowry given to the groom at the time of marriage. Here, the meaning of 'Mahadhan' will be\nA dowry of valuable items suitable for a queen. 'Bhushavasah' means household items.\n'Ki' means 'of'. All kinds of things suitable for the wedding festival of an emperor's daughter were given to Kardam,\nthe sage, who was still celibate. Devahuti, adorned in the guise of a bride, with precious ornaments\nand clothes, off.\n\nThus, Kardam Muni's marriage was completed with a worthy wife, and along with it,\nall the household items were obtained.\nEven today, in the Vedic tradition of marriage, the bride's father\ngives such a dowry to the groom, to the extent that many poor families in India also spend\nhundreds of thousands of rupees in dowry. The practice of dowry is not illegal, as some people try to prove\n. Dowry is a gift given by the father to the daughter as a token of goodwill,\nwhich is mandatory. Where the father, due to being extremely poor, is unable to give dowry, there\nit is prescribed to give only fruits and flowers. As mentioned in the Bhagavad Gita, God is pleased with fruits and flowers.\nAnd also with flowers. Where there is economic hardship and in no other way\nThe amount of dowry cannot be collected, there fruits and flowers can be given to please the groom.\nHaving made his younger daughter the king, he was in agony.\nHis arms were intertwined, and his head was thrown down. 24). Shabdartham\n\nPratam--given as a donation; Duhitaram--to a daughter; Samrat--king (Manu); Sahakshaya--to a suitable person; Gata-vyathah--burden;\nFree from; IR--by kissing; Cha tatha; Bahubhyam--with both hands; Otkanthya-Unmadhit-Ashayah--extremely\nInterested and agitated TL"
