          "textEn": "77\nThe songs were sung by the three, and they were delighted. 28 ”. Meaning of the word:\n\nTam--that; Ayanantam-- having come; Abhipretva-- having understood; Brahmavartat-- from Brahmavartam; Prajaah-- his subjects;\nPatim-- to one's husband; Gita-sanstuti-vadiraih-- with songs, praises, and instrumental music; Pratyudiyuha-- came forward for welcome;\nPraharshitah-- extremely delighted..\n\nKnowing the arrival of the king, the people, with immense joy, came out of Brahmavarta.\nComing and returning, they honored their king with songs, hymns, and instrumental music.\nThey did so. Meaning: It is a custom that the citizens of a state's capital, upon returning from a journey,\naccompany their king. After the Kurukshetra war, there is a similar description of\nShri Krishna's return to Dwarka. On that occasion, citizens of all classes welcomed him at the city's entrance.\nThey welcomed him. In ancient times, capitals were surrounded by ramparts, and there were various gates for entry.\nEven today, there are many ancient gates in Delhi, and some other ancient cities have such gates\nwhere citizens would gather and welcome the king. Here also, the capital of Brahmavarta,\nThe citizens of Barhishmati, adorned beautifully, welcomed Emperor Swayambhuva Manu in many ways,\nWith various decorations and musical instruments. Barhishmati, the name, is complete in all wealth.\nHere, the hairs are adorned with arya. 29). Kushaha kashasta evasanshashchadhrithavarchasaha.\nRishayah yaaih parabhavya yagyaghnanyagyamijire. 30>. Shabdartham\n\nBharishmati -Bharishmati; Naam-- Namakah; Puri- Nagari; Sarva- Sampat- All kinds of wealth; Samanvita- Complete with;\nNyapat- Fell down; Yatra- Where; Romani- Child (A); Yanjnasya- Lord of the pig; FH- Body, Vidhunvata-\nUpon shaking; Kushah- Kusha (a grass); Kashah- Copper (a grass); Te- They; Ev- Indeed; Asan- Became;\nShashchat- Green- evergreen; Varchasah- Colour; Rishayah- Sages; Yaih- From whom; Parabhavya- Having def