          "textEn": "80\n\nMeaning: Every living being in this world is suffering from some kind of pain, whether\nit is related to the body or to the mind or to physical calamities. In this world,\neveryone suffers from extreme cold and heat, but whoever has taken refuge in the lotus feet of God within the Krishna consciousness,\nhas attained a divine state. He is not disturbed by any kind of suffering, whether it is suffering related to body and mind,\nor whether it is natural calamities of winter and summer. He's beyond this.\nHe who, upon being asked by the sages, explains the various righteous duties,\nAnd always does good to all beings, of all castes and creeds. 38. Meaning of the word\n\nYah- who; Pusth- page; Puchhene- when asked; Munibhishah- by the sages; Praah- spoke; Dharman- duty; Nana- various; Vrana- shelter; Cha- as; Sarva- all; Bhut- for all living beings; Hitah- beneficial; Sada- always lL\n\nUpon being asked by some sages, he (Swayambhuva Manu), having compassion on all living beings,\ngave the teaching of the common sacred duties of men and the Varna and Ashrama.\nThis is the description of the mind of the king.\nHaving described, listen to the description of his offspring. 39). Meaning of the word.\n\nEtat- This; te- you; adi- of the king; manoh- of the self-existent Manu; charitam- character; adhutam-\nWonderful; vivritam- described; varnayaniyasya- whose fame is worth describing; tat- his/her daughter; udayam- rise; shrunu- listen;\n\nI have described to you the wonderful character of the first emperor, the self-existent Manu. Their\nfame is worth describing. Now listen carefully to the description of the rise of his daughter Devahuti.\nListen. Thus, under the third book of Srimad Bhagavatam, \"Kardam Muni and Devahuti's marriage^\"\nThe twenty-second chapter, Bhakti Vedanta Tatparya, is completed."
