          "textEn": "]9\nMeaning: Devahuti expressed her happiness through the word 'kat,' because she knew that her\nhusband was in a very high divine position, in the refuge of Yogmaya. As it is said in the Bhagavad Gita,\nthat great souls do not remain under the control of material power. God has two powers--physical and\nspiritual. The souls are static powers. Under the static power, humans are subject to physical power or\nspiritual power (Yogamaya). Kardam Muni was a great soul, therefore he was under the control of Yogamaya,\nwhich means that he was directly related to God. Its characteristic is\nto remain engaged in Krishna consciousness or constant devotion. Even though this was known to Devahuti, she desired to have a son from the sage's\nphysical union (sambandh). Therefore, he reminded his parents of the promise he had made,\nremembering, \"I will remain until the time of Devahuti's conception.\"\" He reminded\nthat gaining offspring from a great man is very honorable for a virtuous woman.\nShe wanted to conceive.\nTherefore, a request was made for her. The word 'stri' means '*expansion*'.\"By the physical union of husband and wife, their qualities are expanded; the children born from good parents\nare only an expansion of their personal qualities. Kardama Muni and Devahuti both had spiritual\nlight, so Devahuti wanted from the beginning that she should conceive first, then she\nshould receive divine grace and divine love. The greatest desire of Kriya is that she should have a son\nas capable as her husband. Because she had Kardam Muni as her husband, therefore she\nwas desirous of a son through intercourse.\nThere, by the instruction of the Supreme Teacher,\nHe who is attached to the self, who is not attached to the self,\nWill be fulfilled, O mind, by the destruction of actions.\nBehold, O king, the nature of the self, which is like the nature of the self. 11). Meaning of the word\nTatra-- in that; Iti-krityam- what was to be done; Upashiksha- complete