          "textEn": "25\nBrahmahitrubhimastubhyam vimrgyah patayah samah. Kashchit samayam me vishvakaya tvayi pravrajite vanam. 52). Meaning of the word:\n\nBrahmanu--this Brahmin; Duhitribhih--by the daughters; Tubhyam--to you; Vimrugyah--having been searched for; Patayah--husband;\nSamah--suitable; Kashchit--someone; Syat--must be; Me--mine; Vishokaya--for consolation; Tvayi--yours;\nPravrajite--once gone; A--to the forest;\n\nO Brahmin, as far as your daughters are concerned, they themselves will find a suitable groom\nand go to their respective homes. But after you leave as a monk, who will give me\ncomfort? Meaning: It is said that the father becomes the son in another form. Therefore, father and son\nare considered different. A widow does not become a widow, because she has a representative of her husband.\nSimilarly, Devahuti indirectly asks Kardam Muni to leave a representative\nso that she can remain free from worries in his absence. The householder does not expect\nthat he will always be able to stay at home. By marrying off his sons and daughters, he can renounce worldly life by leaving his\nwife in the care of wise sons. The social tradition of Vedic\npractices is such. Devahuti is indirectly saying that from home,\nduring the period of absence, there should be at least one son who can free her from worries. This liberation means spiritual education. The meaning of this liberation is not material\nfacilities. Physical facilities will end with the body, but\nSpiritual education does not end, it will go along with the soul. The education of spirituality\nis necessary, but without obtaining a worthy son, how did Devahuti attain spiritual knowledge\n? It is the husband's duty to free her from debt.\nA wife, being devoted, serves her husband.\nAnd he becomes indebted, because no one accepts the service of their dependent until they give something in return. The guru never accepts the service of a disciple without giving spiritual education. This is the mutual exchange of love and duty.\nThus, Devahuti reminds Kardam Muni that he served with utmost devotion."
