          "textEn": "28\nFor example, Shri Krishna meets many souls--some consider him their enemy and\nsome as a means of sensory gratification. It is generally said that the Gopis were attached to Krishna for sensual pleasure,\neven then they were devotees of the best quality of God. But Kansa, Shishupala,\nDantavakra and other demons were enemies of Krishna. Whether they came in the form of enemies out of fear or for sensual pleasure,\nthey came in the company of Krishna as devotees, but everyone attained salvation. Of the company of God,\nThis is the result. If someone does not know who God is, then also the same result\nhappens. One also attains liberation from the company of a great saintly person, just as someone knowingly or\nunknowingly goes towards fire, then they receive warmth. Devahuti expressed her gratitude\nbecause she was associating with Kardam Muni only for the pleasure of Indra, but since\nthey were great men, she was sure that she would be freed by their blessings.\nNeither by action, nor by detachment, is it destroyed.\nEven if a living being dies for the sake of a pilgrimage, he is still dead. 56>. Meaning of the word.\n\nN--No; Ih--Here; Yat--Which; Karma--Action; Dharmaya--For the attainment of religious life; Na--No; Viragaya--For detachment;\nFor detachment; Kalpati--Takes; Na--No; Tirtha-Pad--The lotus feet of God; Sevayai--For service;\nJivan--Living; Api--Even though; Mrutah--Dead; Hi-Certainly; Sah--He. The man whose actions do not elevate religious life, nor does he attain detachment through religious\nrituals, and if a detached man does not attain devotion to Shri Bhagavan,\nhe should be considered dead even while alive. Meaning: Devahuti says that she was attached to her husband for the pleasure of the senses.\nFrom which liberation from material bonds cannot be obtained, therefore her life was in vain. Such a\ntask that by doing which, one cannot attain a religious life, that is futile.\nBy nature, every\nperson wants to do some work, and when doing that work leads to a religious life,\nand then detachment from it, and through detachment, devotion can be attained, then it should be understood that the work\nis accomplished. As it is said in the Bhagavad Gita, that by doing the work, ultimately devotion"
