          "textEn": "244\nTani--they; Ev--actually; Te--to you; Abhirupani--favorable; Rupani--forms; Bhagavan--Oh Lord; Tava--your; Yani Yani--who who; Cha--and; Rochante--seem good; Swa-janaanamu--to your devotees; Arupin--without;\nOf the formless, of\n\nOh Lord, although you have no physical form, you have your own infinite\nforms. They are truly your divine forms and those who delight your devotees.\nThey are. Meaning: The Brahma-Samhita says that God is one, but He has infinite\nforms. The non-dual, unmanifest, beginningless, and infinite form - God is of the form, but still, they have various\nforms. These various forms keep appearing according to the tastes of their many types of devotees.\nare.\nIt is said that once, Hanuman, the supreme devotee of Lord Ramachandra, said that Lakshmipati\nNarayan and Sitapati Ram are one and the same, and there is no difference between Sita and Lakshmi, but\nhe himself was more fond of the form of Lord Ram. Similarly, some devotees worship the original form of\nShri Krishna. When we say 'Krishna', we not only mention Krishna but also all the forms of God--Ram, Nrusimha, Varaha, Narayana, etc. This variety of divine forms exists together. It is also mentioned in Brahma-Samhita-- Rasadi murtishu... Nanavataram. They\nare already present in various forms, but none of these forms are material. Shridhar Swami\nhas commented that 'arupinah' means **formless**, in reality, without a physical form.\nIt is used for that. God has a form, otherwise why is it said here-- 'tanyev tebhirupani'\n'Rupani dhagavastav'-- your own forms are, but they are not physical.\nFrom your physical perspective,\nyou have no form, but from a spiritual, philosophical perspective, you are of various forms. Mayavadi\nphilosophers do not understand these divine forms of God, therefore, being disappointed, they tell God\nnirakar. But that is not the case; whenever there is a form, there is a person. In Vedic literature, God is often referred to as Purusha, which means '*first form,\nfirst enjoyer.\"The conclusion was that God has no form, yet there are various kinds"
