          "textEn": "256\nI will tell you, so that he too, by breaking the bonds of all desirable actions, can attain perfection and\nself-realization. In this way, she too will be freed from all material fears. Meaning: At the time of renunciation, Kardama Muni was very much concerned for his worthy wife Devahuti, therefore the worthy son promised that not only Kardam Muni would be freed from worldly bondage,\nbut Devahuti too would be freed from the teachings of her son. Here, an excellent example\nis presented--the husband leaves for self-realization by taking Sanyas and his\nrepresentative, his son, who is equally educated, stays at home for the salvation of his mother.\nA monk cannot take his wife with him; in the Vanaprastha Ashram, a man can keep his wife with him as a helper, but he cannot have intercourse with her.\nHe cannot But in the hermitage, he cannot keep his wife with him. Otherwise, Kardam\nAs a person would surely keep his wife with him, and in the practice of self-realization,\nhe would not have any obstacles.\n\nBut Kardam Muni followed the Vedic order, according to which, any person living a life of renunciation\ncannot have any kind of relationship with the Satiyas. But what happens to that\nwife, whom the husband abandons? She is handed over to the son, and the son\nvows that he will free her from bondage. Women do not take Sanyas (renunciation). In the modern era,\nmany fake and so-called spiritual organizations and committees also grant Sanyas to women,\nalthough there is no mention of women taking Sanyas in Vedic literature.\nOtherwise, if the law were to be enacted, Kardam Muni would take his wife along and give her also the sanyas (renunciation). Kintoki must stay home. His life has three stages--childhood\nIn which one depends on the father; in youth, one depends on one's husband; and in old age, wise\nson, as in Kapil's dependence. The progress of a woman in old age depends on her wise son"
