          "textEn": "267\nUnderstand, not to create any kind of imagined desire, only to maintain. Shaunak\nThe sage prayed to Sut Goswami that he had heard from his guru Shukdev Goswami about the divine plays that were revealed by God\nby the power of his inner power, he should tell them in the same form. God has no physical body, but by his supreme will,\nhe can assume any kind of body. This is possible through his inner power. Suta said,\nDipayanasakhasthavevam metreyo bhagavantaha. Prahēḍam viduram prita anvīkṣikyam prachoditaḥ. 4>. Meaning of the word.\n\nSuta said:-- Suta Goswami said; Draupayana-Sakha--friend of Vyasadeva; Tu--then; Evam--in this way; Maitreya--\nMaitreya; Bhagavan--venerable; Tatha--in this way; Praha--said; Idam--this; Viduram--from Vidura; Preehita--being pleased;\nAnvikshikyam--about divine knowledge; Prachoditah--upon being asked;..\nDivine.\nInspired and pleased by Vidur's curiosity about knowledge, Metreya said this way. Meaning: When the questioner is authentic and the speaker is also authoritative, then the question-answer session proceeds satisfactorily.\nIt goes on in a proper manner. Here, Maitreya is considered a powerful sage, therefore he is called Bhagwan. This word is not only used for God, but also for whoever is equivalent to God.\nIt is used for the one who is equally powerful. Matreya has been addressed as God\nbecause he was very advanced spiritually. They were friends of Dwaipayana Vyasa Deva,\nwho was the literary incarnation of God. Maitreya was very pleased with Vidur's questions, because these\nquestions were from an authentic, proven devotee. In this way, Maitreyi seemed enthusiastic in answering. When discussions on spiritual topics take place among devotees with similar mindset, then the questions\nand answers are ultimately fruitful and encouraging."
