          "textEn": "268\nMaitreya Ubacha\nPitarai prasthiteyaranyam matuh priyachikirshaya. In that, the sun, the moon, and the stars were in the sky. 500/-. Meaning of the word:\nMaitreya said—Maitreya said; Pitari—of the father; Prasthite— upon departing; Aranyam—for the forest; Matuh—\nto one's mother; Priya-chikarshaya—with the desire to please; Tasmin—that; Bindusare—in the Bindu Sarovar; Avatisit—\nremained; Bhagavan—God; Kapila—Kapila; Kil- undoubtedly L\nMaitreya said: When Kardama Muni went to the forest, Lord Kapila stayed on the banks of the Bindu Sarovar to please his mother\nDevahuti. Meaning: It is the duty of an adult son to, in the absence of his father, take on the burden of his mother\nand serve her with all his might, so that he does not feel the loss of his husband. It is also the duty of the husband that as soon as the son becomes wise and is able to take on the burden of his wife and household, he should renounce his home. This is the Vedic system of household life.\nA person should not remain constantly busy in household chores until death. He should leave home.\nHe should be given. Then the wise son should take on the responsibilities of family matters and his wife. Tamasikamkarnam Tattvamargagrahadarshanam.\nSwasutam Devahutyaha Dhatuha Samsarati Vachah. 6>. Shabdartham\nTam--to him (Kapil); Asinam--being; Akarmanam--free from action; Tatva--of the ultimate truth; Marga-agra-chamar--the foremost; Lakshya--goal; Darshanam--that which can be seen; Swa-sutam--one's own son; Devahuti: Devahuti; Aah--said; Dhatuh--of Brahma;\nSamsmarati--remembering; Vachah--word, word..\nKapil, capable of showing his mother Devahuti the ultimate goal of the supreme truth,\nwhen he was sitting in front of her leisurely, Devahuti remembered the words that Brahma had said to her,\nTherefore, she began to ask Kapil in this way.\nDevahuti said,\n\"By whom, being without knowledge, the blind one is blinded by the sight of Indra.\"\n\"By whom, by this possible one, th