          "textEn": "278\nFor the sake of liberation from bondage, for the sake of liberation, consider this. Attached to the senses, engrossed in bondage, or free from liberation. 15>. Meaning of the word\n\nChetah-- consciousness; Khalu- without doubt; Asya-- his; Bandhaya- for bondage; Muktaye- for liberation; Cha- and;\nAtmanah- of the soul; Matam- is believed; Guneshu- in the three qualities; Saktam- is attracted; Bandhaya- for bondage;\nRatam- is attached; Va- or else; Punasi- in the Supreme Soul; Muktaye- for liberation.\n\nThe consciousness of the soul, in which state it is attracted by the three qualities of nature,\nis called bondage. But when that same consciousness is attached to Shri Bhagavan,\nthen man is situated in the consciousness of liberation. Meaning: There is a difference between Krishna consciousness and Maya consciousness. In the senses, in this sense of illusion, of nature,\nthere is attachment to the three qualities, under which man sometimes works in the state of Satva, sometimes in Rajo, and sometimes in Tamo Guna.\nThese various qualitative actions, in which the main attachment remains for material enjoyment, due to the bound life of man, § | When that same consciousness or consciousness-filled\nPurushottam, Lord Krishna, is transferred, or when a man becomes a devotee of Krishna, then\nHe is on the path of liberation. I am, arising from pride, desire, and delusion, impure.\nWhen the mind is purified, it is without sorrow and happiness. 96. Meaning of the word:\n\nI-me; mine-mine; pride - from delusion; utthai-generated; kama-desire; lobh-greed; etc.-etc.; malai-filth; vitam-free; yada-when; man-mind; shuddham-pure; aduhkham-without sorrow;\nwithout sorrow; asukham-without happiness; samam-equanimity.\n\nWhen a person, with the false feeling of considering the body as \"I\" and the material objects as \"mine\",\nis completely freed from the vices of desires and greed, then his mind becomes pure. In that pure state, he transcends the so-called state of physical pleasure and pain.\nHe crosses over.\nMeaning: Lust and greed are the characteristics of the world. Every person always wants to have something. Here it is said that desire and greed, along with the body, are a wrong identification of oneself"
