वान्‌ हैं।\nउपनिषदों में भक्त को भगवान्‌ से प्रार्थना करता बताया गया है कि कृपया ब्रह्मज्योति को एक",
          "textEn": "282\nN--No; Yujyayan--being used; Bhaktya--with devotion; Bhagavati--Towards God; Akhil-Atmani--Of the entire self;\nParamatma--Supreme soul; Sahasha--Like; Asti--Is; Shivah--Blessing, auspicious; Panthah--Path; Yoginam--Of the yogis; Brahma--For the attainment of perfection..\n\nUntil any kind of Yogi is engaged in devotion to Shri Bhagavan,\nthen completeness cannot be achieved in self-realization, because devotion is\nthe only auspicious path. Meaning: Here it is clearly stated that without uniting devotion, knowledge and detachment are never\ncomplete. Nayujyamana means, \"without joining together\".\"When devotional service is performed,\nthe question arises, to whom should the service be offered? This devotion is to be offered only to Shri Bhagwan.\nBecause He is the Supreme Soul of everyone, and this is the only reliable path to self-realization or Brahma-\nrealization. The meaning of the word 'Brahmasiddhaye' is to understand oneself as separate from the material.\nTo understand oneself as Brahman.\nThe Vedic word is Aham Brahmasmi. The meaning of Brahmasiddhi is that a person understands that he is not a substance--he is a pure soul. Yogis are of many types,\nbut every Yogi remains engaged in self-realization or Brahma-realization. Here, this\nclearly mentions that until the complete Supreme Personality of Godhead is engaged in devotion,\nthen a person cannot reach the path of Brahmasiddhi. It is said at the beginning of the second chapter of Shrimad Bhagavat that when a person engages himself in the devotion of Vasudeva, then spiritual knowledge and detachment manifest spontaneously. As a result, the devotee does not have to make separate efforts for knowledge or detachment. Devotional service\nis so powerful in itself that through the service and devotion of man, everything is revealed.\nIt becomes manifest. Here, Shiva's path is said to be--this is the only auspicious path of self-realization. The path of devotion is the supreme secret means for the realization of Brahman.\nThis fact that through the auspicious path of devotion, the attainment of Brahma-Sakshatkar is achieved, this indicates that the so-called Brahma\nSakshatkar or FEAT is not a quick attainment of Brahma. There is God, beyond even the light of Brahma. In the Upanishads, the devotee is described as praying to God, asking that Brahmajyoti be given one"
