          "textEn": "For 290, there are many business, scientific, and other organizations, but there is not a single organization\nthat is helpful in being free from all physical associations (sanskar). If someone has reached such a state\nwhere he wants to be free from material impurities, he should seek the company of devotees\nwhere only devotion to Krishna is practiced. From this, man can be free from all physical\ncontact. Because the devotee is freed from all the faults of material association, therefore the sufferings of the world do not\naffect him. Although he seems to live in the physical world,\nthe world's\nafflictions cannot touch him. How is that even possible? It is a beautiful example regarding the functions of a cat. The cat takes her kittens by pressing them in her mouth, and when she kills the mice,\nshe takes that gift also in her mouth. In this way, both are taken away from the cat's mouth,\nbut their states are different.\nKittens experience happiness in the cat's mouth,\nbut when it takes the mouse, the mouse experiences the fear of death,\nit experiences. Similarly, those who are monks or those who are engaged in the divine service of God, in devotion to Krishna,\ndo not experience the impurities of material sufferings, but those who are not devotees of Krishna\nexperience worldly sufferings in reality. Therefore, man should\nabandon the company of materialistic men and seek the company of those engaged in devotion to Krishna. By this,\nhis spiritual progress will be possible. By his words and teachings, his worldly bonds will be cut.\nGo away. Those who are skilled in the study of stories,\nare filled with the nectar of heart and ears.\nThose who are satisfied with that,\nwill be in the state of being free from the cycle of birth and death.\nFaith, love, and devotion will be in order. 25. Meaning of the word:\n\nSatam- pure devotee; Prasangat- from the context; Mam- mine; Veerya- supernatural; Samvidaha- from discussion; Bhavanti- they become; Hat- to the heart; Karanam- to the ear; Ras- enjoyable; Kathah- story; Tat- that;"
