          "textEn": "296\nWithout accepting this, one cannot be freed from the cycle of this world. As far as the wise\nare concerned, they are interested in the path of knowledge, but if someone who practices restraint\nmay rise to the level of TRIAS, even then there remains a possibility of falling again in this world. Therefore, in reality, worldly existence does not end with the practice of knowledge. Similarly, in Hatha Yoga,\nwhose goal is to attain the indwelling Supreme Soul, it has been experienced that\nmany yogis like Vishwamitra also fall down. But the devotee, once reaching near God,\ndoes not return to this world again, as it is confirmed in the Bhagavad Gita. Yad gatva na tivarte manushya punah kadachinu na vaparte. Having abandoned the body, one does not regenerate.\nUpon abandoning this body, one does not have to take another body. The soul's existence does not end with Nirvana. The soul is eternally present. Therefore, Nirvana means to end one's world.\nWhich means to return to the abode of God.\nSometimes people ask how the soul falls from heaven to this world? The answer can be found here. Until a person reaches Vaikuntha Loka in direct contact with God,\nthe fear of falling down remains, whether it is through the realization of the formless Brahman\nor through the state of meditation in Samadhi. Another word in this verse is Bhagavat-kāṇḍha.\nThe word is also very important. Baan: The word Baan means 'bow' in the language. Bhakti Yoga is like an arrow,\nwhose target is Shri Bhagavan. The Bhakti Yoga method never tells anyone to go towards the formless Brahman energy,\nor towards the realization of the Supreme Soul. This arrow (arrow) is so sharp and\nfast that it pierces the fields of the formless Brahman and the indwelling Supreme Soul, directly\nreaching God.\nTadetamme vijanihim yathaham mandaadhirhare.\nSukham buddheya durdhabham yosha bhavadanugrahath. 30>. Shabdharam"
