          "textEn": "302\nThe description of the pastimes of God is considered by the Mayavadi people as stories, but in reality,\nThese are not stories, these are historical facts. Pure devotees accept the descriptions of the Lord's pastimes\nas the ultimate truth, not as mere stories. My words about the body are important.\nDevotees are very fond of praising the activities of God, but\nthe illusionists cannot even think about these activities. According to them, the ultimate truth is formless. What of karma without embodiment? The non-specialists take the activities described in the Srimad Bhagavat, Bhagavad Gita, and other Vedic\ntexts as fabricated stories, thus they unnecessarily\nexplain them in a wrong way. They have no awareness of the divine essence of God, and\nwith the intention of misleading the innocent public, they give misleading explanations.\nMayavad-\nThe activities of philosophy are extremely harmful to the public, that is why Lord Chaitanya warned us\nthat one should not listen to any branch by mistake from a Mayavadi; they will corrupt the entire process\nand the person who listens to them will never be able to walk on the path of devotion to attain supreme perfection\nor will be able to attain the path of devotion only after a long time. Kapil Muni has clearly stated that the activities of devotion are superior to liberation. This is called Pancham\nPurushartha. Generally, people engage in religious, economic, and sensual pursuits,\nand they act with the belief that they are going to become one with God,\n(liberation). But devotion is greater than all these activities. Therefore, the beginning of Shrimad Bhagavat\nstarts with this statement that in Bhagavat, all kinds of <uh> religiousness was eradicated.\nIn the Bhagwat, all those rituals have been boycotted, which are related to economic development and\nfor the satisfaction of the senses, or being frustrated by the satisfaction of the senses, related to the identity with God. Bhagwat is especially for those devotees who are constantly engaged in devotion to Krishna or in the activities of God,\nand they constantly sing the praises of these divine activities. Pure devotees\nWorship the divine activities of God residing in Vrindavan, Dwarka, and Mathura, because"
