          "textEn": "306\nThey are extremely attractive, therefore, when they see them in a decorated form in the temple, they become absorbed in the thoughts of God. This is the state of liberation. In other words, it has been confirmed here that\neven a low-class devotee is in a divine state compared to them, who try to attain liberation through contemplation or other means. Even those who are non-dualists like Shukdev Goswami and the four Kumaras\nbecame devotees, fascinated by the beauty of the idols in the temple, by the fragrance of the Tulsi offered to God.\nAlthough they were in a liberated state, instead of remaining non-dualists, they became devotees, fascinated by the beauty of God. Here, the word 'Vilas' is noteworthy. From 'Kilaas' comes the manifestation of the activities or divine plays of God.\nIn the functions of temple worship, it is certain that a person should not go to the temple to see God\nonly decorated, but he should listen to the reading of Shrimad Bhagavat Bhagavad Gita or such\nliterature, whose regular recitation takes place in the temple. In Vrindavan, this is the custom\nthat in every temple, the scriptures are recited. Even the lowly devotees,\nwho have neither literary knowledge nor the leisure to read the Bhagavata or the Bhagavad Gita,\nget the opportunity to listen to the divine plays of God. In this way, their minds\nare constantly immersed in the thoughts of God--in their forms, actions, and their divine nature.\nare. This state of Krishna consciousness is a state of liberation. Therefore, Lord Chaitanya, with devotion,\nto accomplish, proposed five methods--(1) Chanting the holy names of God--Hare\nKrishna Hare Krishna Krishna Krishna Hare Hare, Hare Rama Hare Rama Rama Rama Hare Hare.\n(2) Association with devotees,\nand to serve them as much as possible (3), listening to Shrimad Bhagavat,\nand to see the decorated temple and the deity; and if possible;\nto stay in a place like Vrindavan or Mathura. Only through these five can a devotee attain the ultimate perfected state. This is confirmed\nin the Bhagavad Gita and here in the Srimad Bhagavatam. All Vedic literature accepts\nthat even a lowly devotee can attain liberation."
