          "textEn": "330\nBecause of being covered, the spiritual form ends. Therefore, here, 'mumuhe'\nthe word has been used, which indicates that they have forgotten their spiritual form. This forgetfulness of the spiritual form is found in living beings, because they remain bound due to being covered by the power of nature. Another word has also been used, Gyan Guhya. Griha.\nMeans covering. Because the knowledge of small living beings remains covered, therefore they appear in many\nwombs. It is said in the seventh chapter of the first book of Shrimad Bhagwat,\n“Living beings remain fascinated by material power.\"It is also said in the Vedas that the eternal souls\nremain covered with various qualities, and those tri-colored beings are called red, white, and blue.\nThe color red is a symbol of Rajoguna, white is of Satoguna, and blue is a symbol of Tamoguna. These qualities of nature\nare related to physical power, as a result, within various qualities, living beings find different types of bodies.\nBecause they remain oblivious of their spiritual identity, they consider bodies to be the soul (swara). For a baddhaji, the muja refers to the body. This is called attachment. It is repeatedly said in the Kathopanishad that the Supreme Being, the Supreme Personality of Godhead, is never influenced by material nature. They are, in reality, bound souls or the most subtle parts of God, who\nare affected by physical nature and appear in various bodies under the control of physical qualities.\nThey are visible. And by the knowledge of the Supreme Being, the action is done by the nature of the man.\nIn actions, while performing, he considers himself as the Self. 6>. Meaning of the word\n\nEvam--in this way; Par--other; Abhidhayena--with recognition; Kartatvam--the accomplishment of action; Prakrutih--of nature;\nPuman--soul; Karmasu--while performing actions; Gunaih--through three qualities; Atmani--to oneself;\nManyate--believes;\n\nDue to his forgetfulness, the divine soul considers the influence of nature as its field of action\nHe sits and, inspired in this way, mistakenly considers himself the doer of his actions."
