          "textEn": "332\nAs it remains. Physical force that does whatever it can, the prisoner does. He has no own\nresponsibility, he remains only a witness to the action. But he is compelled to do so in the offense of not understanding his eternal relationship with Krishna. Therefore, Lord Krishna says in the Bhagavad Gita that Maya is so strong that it is indescribable.\nIt is. But if a being knows only this much that his natural state is to serve Krishna,\nand if he continues to act according to this rule, then no matter how bound he may be,\nthe effect of Maya is immediately removed. In the seventh chapter of the Bhagavad Gita, its clear\nstatement has been made. Whoever, due to being helpless, takes refuge in Krishna, Krishna takes their burden.\nAnd in this way, the effect of Maya (illusion) is erased. The soul is truly eternal, full of bliss and knowledge. But in the clutches of Maya,\nHe repeatedly suffers from birth, death, disease, and old age.\nTo deal with this\nworldly situation and to bring oneself to devotion to Krishna, one has to be industrious,\nbecause in this way, his long-term sufferings can be easily ended. In short, it can be said that the suffering that a living being experiences is due to its attachment to nature. Therefore, this attachment should be transferred from the inert substance to Shri Krishna.\nIt should be transferred. In the causality of action, the cause is known to be nature.\nIn the enjoyment of pleasures and pains, the enjoyer is the supreme being of nature. 8>. Meaning of word\n\nWorking body, cause, senses, the cause of the gods; cause-- cause; nature-- nature; knowers--\nKnowers understand; experiencer-- about experience; of happiness-- of happiness; of sorrow-- of sorrow; the soul--\nOf nature-- of nature; supreme-- divine\n\nThe body, senses, and the cause of the gods who are the supervisors of the senses of the bound soul, material\nnature is. It is known to the wise men.\nDivine by nature, such are the pleasures and 