          "textEn": "333\nMeaning: It is said in the Bhagavad Gita that when God incarnates in this world, then\nHe comes as a man through the illusion of the self. He is not bound by any external nature. They come of their own will and this is what can be called their ‘leela’. But here it is clearly\nsaid that a bound soul is bound by the three qualities of nature to assume a particular type of body\nand senses. He does not get this body according to his interest.\nHe does not get it. In other words, it can be said that a bound soul does not have the freedom to make independent choices; it has to accept some body according to its actions. But when physical reactions occur, as are experienced in pleasure and pain, then\nit should be understood that the cause of this is the self-soul. If the soul desires, it can transform this dull, bound life by accepting service to Krishna. The soul itself is the cause of its own sufferings,\nbut it can also be the cause of its eternal happiness.\nWhen he desires to be engaged in devotion to Krishna,\nthen by the spiritual power of God, he is given a suitable body, and\nwhen he wants to satisfy his senses, he is given a physical body. Therefore, accepting the spiritual body or the physical body depends on the will of the soul.\nBut once the body is accepted, one has to bear happiness or sorrow. The illusionist\ntheorist says that even by assuming the body of a pig, the soul enjoys its pastimes.\nHe enjoys. But this is not acceptable, because the word 'Leela' is used for the voluntary acceptance of enjoyment.\nTherefore, this explanation is extremely misleading. When sorrow is forced to be accepted,\nthen it is not 'Leela' (divine play). The plays of God and the acceptance of the fruits of karma by the bound soul\ndo not happen on the same ground. Devahuti said:\nThe nature of the Supreme Being is also the characteristic of the Supreme Being. The cause of the two is the sum of the two. 9>. Shabdharam"
