          "textEn": "339\nPrabhavam--effect; Paurusham--of God; Prahahu--it is said; Kalam--time; Eke--some; Yatah--from which;\nBhayam--fear; Ahankar--deluded by ego; Kartahu--of the soul; Prakritim--to nature; Eyushah--by touch;\nThe effect of Lord Purushottam is experienced in time, because this\nThe fear of death arises due to the ego of the deluded soul coming into contact with the material nature. Meaning: The fear of death in a living being arises from the ego that originates from the sense of self-awareness. Everyone is afraid of death, in reality, the soul does not die, but due to the self-realization of the body,\nthe fear of death arises. In Shrimadbhagavatam (11.2.27) it is said-- fear\nshould be avoided. The second is called the physical substance, that is beyond the soul. Substance\nis secondary manifestation of the soul, because substance is born from the soul. Just as the physical elements described here are produced by God or the Supreme Soul, similarly, the body is also produced by the soul.\nThat is why the body is called the second. When a human remains absorbed in the second manifestation of this second element or soul,\nthen he remains fearful of death. When he has complete faith\nthat he is not his body, then there is no question of fearing death because the soul\nnever dies. If the soul engages in spiritual action of devotion, then it becomes completely free from birth and death. His next state is complete liberation from the body. The time of fear of death\nis the effect, which is indicative of the effect of God. In other words, it is a time-busting. Time\nrepresents God, and it reminds us that we should take refuge in God. God speaks to every bound soul in the form of time. God says in the Bhagavad Gita\nthat if someone comes into my refuge, then there is no problem of birth and death. Therefore, we should consider time as God standing before us.\nIts detailed explanation is given in the next verse."
