          "textEn": "353\n\nIn this verse, it is clearly stated that both types of senses, namely the motor senses and the sensory senses, are born of the quality of Rajas. And because both of these types of senses require--\nlife force--, therefore this power also originates from Rajoguna. Therefore, we\ncan truly see that those who are extremely Rajasic (passionate) are rapidly progressing in material achievements as well.\nThey are progressing. In Vedic scriptures, it is prescribed that if someone encourages a person to acquire material\npossessions, then they should also encourage them for a lustful life. We find it easily that those who are attached to a lustful life are also moving forward from a material\npoint of view, because worldly life, that is, lustful life, is the motivator of the material\nprogress of civilization. For those who are eager to achieve spiritual progress, the existence of Rajoguna\nis almost non-existent. Only the Satogun prevails.\nIt is seen that those who\npractice devotion to Krishna are poor in material terms, but those who have eyes can see\nwho is greater. Although a devotee appears poor from the outside, Krishna Bhava Namrata\nA person who is attached to it is not actually poor. But one who is not interested in devotion to Krishna,\nand one who appears happy from the outside due to his material possessions, is truly poor. Those individuals who have physical consciousness are very intelligent in discovering new things for their physical comfort,\nbut they are not able to reach the soul and spiritual life. Therefore, if someone wants to move forward in physical life, then he\nwill have to descend to the level of pure desire, that is, pure desire of devotion. As Narad-\nIt is said in Pancharatri, when the senses are purified in Krishna consciousness and engaged in the service of God, then\nIt is called pure devotion.\nTamasach vikurvanaadhagavadriyachoditaat.\nShabdamatramabhuttasmannabhah shrotram tu shabdagam. 32). Shabdharam"
