          "textEn": "364\nBhavanam Brahmanah sthanam dharanam sad visheshanam.\nSarvasatvagunoddhēdaḥ pṛthivivṛttilakṣanam. 46). Shabdartham\n\nBhavanam, like the form of an idol; Brahmanah -- of the Supreme Brahman; Sthanam -- a place to reside; Dharanam -- to hold objects;\nSat-Vishishtam -- to separate the open sky; Sarva -- all; He -- of existence; Guna -- quality; Udbhedah --\nplace of manifestation; Prithvi -- of the earth; Vrutti-- of functions; Lakshanam-- characteristics;\n\nBy giving shape to the forms of the Supreme Brahman, by creating a place to reside, by making a vessel to hold water, the characteristics of the functions of the earth can be seen. In other words, the earth\nis the abode of all elements. Meaning: Earth, Sound, Sky, Air, Fire, Water - these are the different elements that can be seen in the earth.\nHere, another characteristic of the Earth is mentioned, and it is that from the Earth, various forms of God\nare manifested. This statement of Kapil confirms that the complete Supreme Being\nGod, that is, Brahma, has countless forms, which are described in the scriptures. Earth and its\nproducts-- such as stone, wood, and gems, by properly combining them, these forms of Brahma can be presented before us. When the form of Lord Krishna or Lord Vishnu is presented through a clay idol,\nthen it is not imaginary. The earth gives the form of God\nDescribed in the scriptures, it gives shape ©.\n\nIn the Brahma Samhita, the land of Lord Krishna, the Vaikunthadham, the uniqueness, and his\nDescription of his form playing the flute from his spiritual body is found. The mention of all these forms\nis found in the branches, and when they are presented in the same forms, they become venerable.\nThey are not imaginary, as the Mayavadi philosophy says. Sometimes the word 'bhavan'\nis interpreted as 'imagination', but that is not its meaning. Its\nreal meaning is to give shape according to Vedic descriptions. The earth is the ultimate transformation of all living beings\nand their qualities. The meaning of the special quality of the element of air is called that."
