          "textEn": "383\nremains, then he is liberated, but when he, with the feeling that he is satisfying the senses,\nputs himself into the actions of sensory gratification, then in reality he gets caught in the magic of nature.\nHe gets entangled. In the Bhagavad Gita, it is said that nature: acts according to the qualities of nature that man has acquired. The qualities of nature are indicated by the Guna. He\nremains subject to the qualities of nature, but due to delusion, he considers himself the doer. This delusional notion of being the doer\ncan only be removed by engaging in devotion according to the instructions of God or their authentic representative. In the Bhagavad Gita, Arjuna was taking responsibility for the death of his grandfather and guru in the war.\nBut when he acted according to Krishna's instructions, he was freed from the\nauthority of the action. He fought, but was freed from the fruits of fighting, although initially when he was non-violent, not ready to fight, then all the responsibility was on him.\nThis is the difference between liberation and\nbondage. Even if a living being is excellent and virtuous, yet he remains under the influence of the illusion of nature. Yet the devotee acts on the direction of God. In this way,\nHis actions may not seem to a common person to be of a very high order, but there is no\nresponsibility on the devotee. Then, the world is filled with the fear of 5 fears.\nDue to the faults of actions, it is mixed with good and bad. 3). Meaning of the word:\n\nBy that--from that; world--again and again birth and death; position--path, way; inevitably--being helpless; comes--experiences;\nUnrestrained--being dissatisfied; born--from the association of nature; due to karmic faults--due to wrong deeds; good--good; bad--bad; mixed--mixed; born--in various wombs\n\nTherefore, the bound soul, due to its association with the qualities of material nature, keeps changing higher and lower in various wombs.\nUntil he is freed from physical actions,\nhe has to accept this situation as a result of his sinful actions.\nHe has to remain. Meaning: here karmadoshaḥ means \"'deeds defiled by error\"’. This is good or"
