          "textEn": "394\n\nThe relationships will be stretched. Meaning: A bound soul, considering the body as itself, considers the body as 'me', and anything that is related to the body as 'mine'. In Sanskrit, it is called ego and affection, and this\nis the root cause of all bound lives. Man should consider objects as a combination of matter and soul.\nHe should differentiate between the nature of matter and the nature of soul, and\nhis true identity should be with the soul, not with matter. With this knowledge, he should\navoid delusional bodily consciousness.\nThe wise, situated at a distance, do not see others.\nThey see their own self as the sun, and their own self as the moon. 10 ||\n\nMeaning of the word\n\nNivritt-- separate; Buddhi-avasthanah-- states of physical consciousness; Durat-- distant; Anya-- other; Darshan: --Life\nKnowledge; available by obtaining ~; Atmana--with one's pure intellect; Atmanam--oneself; Chakshusha--with one's eyes; Iva--like; Arkam--Sun; Atma-Dak--Self-realized.\n\nMan should live in a divine state beyond the states of physical consciousness and\nshould remain detached from all other life-perceptions. Thus, freed from ego, he\nshould see himself in the same way as he sees the sun in the sky. Meaning: Consciousness in the physical life-state functions in three modes.\nWhen\nwe remain awake, consciousness functions in a specific way; when we remain asleep, it functions in another\nway, and when we are in deep sleep, consciousness functions in another manner.\nTo be filled with Krishna consciousness, a person has to transcend these states of consciousness.\nOur present consciousness should be free from all other life-awarenesses, in addition to the consciousness (devotion) of the Supreme Personality of Godhead, Krishna. This distance-filled-with-Krishna-consciousness is called\nIt means that when he attains complete Krishna consciousness (devotion), he sees nothing but Krishna.\nBesides Krishna, he sees nothing. It is said in Chaitanya-Charitamrita that a complete devotee sees many moving and non-moving objects,\nbut among all of them, he finds the power of Krishna to be active."
