          "textEn": "438\n\nAre attractive.\n\nMeaning: The act of meditating on the form of God from one's mind, and the act of chanting the praises and pastimes of God\nare equally equal. The only difference is that to listen to the divine plays and to stabilize the mind, the expectation of meditating on the form of God within the mind\nis simple, because in this era, whenever someone starts contemplating God, the mind\nbecomes distracted, and due to various distractions, there is a staleness in the method of seeing God within the mind. But when, while praising the divine plays of God,\nthe words are uttered, then we are bound to listen. This act of listening enters the mind,\nand the practice of yoga becomes self-evident. For example, if the description of going to the pasture with one's cows and friends for grazing by God\nis being read from the Bhagavatam,\nand if even a child hears it, then by listening to it, one can attain the benefit of meditating on the pastimes of God.\nListening is also included in the mind. Lord Chaitanya has recommended that in this Kali Yuga, man should always chant the Bhagavad Gita\nand listen to it. They also say that the liberated souls should engage in the glorification of God,\nso that other people can benefit from listening to it. For yoga,\nit is necessary to meditate on the divine plays of God, whether they are standing, walking, or lying down. That mind, which is attached to the object of its own nature, is situated in all its parts.\nSeeing that, the sage, united in one place, is attached to the Lord. 20>. Meaning of the word:\n\nIn that--in the form of God; Lavdha-padam--stable; Chittam--the mind; Sarva-sara--all essence; Avayah--in the limbs;\nSansthitam--stable; Vilakshya-antar--knowing the difference; Kuttha-ekastha-e--in one place; Sanjujyat---stabilize the mind;\nAngke hara-anga-e--of the body; Bhagavat-ah--of God; Muni-ah--a sage.While stabilizing one's mind in the eternal form of God, the Yogi should not think of al