          "textEn": "45]\nMean--of fish; Day--pair; Aashrayam--abode; Adhishipthu--one who is pleasing; Aban--lotus; Natram--eye;\nDhyayet--meditates; Manah--in mind; Mayam--made; Atandritah--alert; Ullasat--dancing; Bhru--forehead. Then the Yogi meditates on the beautiful face of God, which is adorned with curly hair,\nlotus-like eyes, and smiling eyebrows. Behind its beauty (chhata), a pair of lotus and swimming fish surrounded by bumblebees\nis defeated. Meaning: Here is an important statement-- Dhyāyēn manōyam. It is not a mental imagination.\nThe non-expert think that a yogi can imagine any form according to his wish,\nbut as it is said here, the yogi should meditate on the form whose experience\nthe devotees have had. Devotees never imagine any form of God. They are not satisfied with any\nimaginary object.\nGod has various divine forms; each devotee desires a particular\nform, and while worshiping that form, he engages in the service of God. This form of God is approved by the branch. As has been said before, of God,\nthere are eight types of forms. These can be prepared by devotees from clay, stone, wood, color, sand, etc.\nAccording to their means.\n\nInside the mind, the marking of the form of God is Manomayam. This marking is included in the eight different\ntypes of forms of God. The meditation on the real form of God can manifest in various ways, But from this, it should not be concluded that someone has to imagine that form.\nIn this verse, there are two similes. In the first simile, the face of God\nis compared to a lotus, then the black hair is compared to a bunch of bees circling around the lotus,\nand the eyes are compared to two fish swimming here and there.\nIn water,\na blooming lotus looks extremely beautiful if it is surrounded by chirping bumblebees and fish. The face of God is complete. Her beauty is shameful to the beauty of the lotus. His gaze is more auspicious, fierce.\nFor the pacification of the three enemies, the created, the uncreated."
