          "textEn": "472\nThe end of the decade. The Yogabhaskara of the Lord refers to all the yogic practices. Devahuti had already requested her glorious son to describe Bhakti Yoga.\nAnd God has described Bhakti Yoga as the ultimate yoga method. Bhakti Yoga is like sunlight for the salvation of those bound souls, whose ordinary condition\nis described here. They do not have eyes to see their own benefit. They do not know\nthat the goal of life is not to increase material needs, because the body cannot live for more than\na few years. The living are eternal, and their needs are eternal. If\na man is only engaged in fulfilling the needs of the body and ignores the eternal\nneeds of life, then he will be a part of that civilization whose progress\nputs living beings in the darkness of ignorance. While living in that dark area,\nHe does not get any kind of new consciousness, but he gradually gets tired.\nHe searches for many means to overcome this\nfatigue, but fails and remains disappointed.\nHe comes. In the struggle of life, the only way to overcome this fatigue is the path of devotion or\nthe path of Krishna consciousness. Maitreya Ubacha.\nThus spoke the mother, and the great sage, pleased, was moved.\nThen the best of kings spoke, and the compassionate one was pleased. 6>. Meaning of the word\n\nMaitreya said—Maitreya said; Thus—in this way; Mother—of his mother; Vachah—word; Shalakshnam—with a thousand qualities;\nRejoiced—welcoming; Great sage—Sage Kapila; Aababhase—spoke; Kuru-Shreshtha—the best among the Kurus, Vidura;\nPleased—pleased; Tamu—from him; With compassion—with kindness; Arditah—became enraged L\n\nShri Maitreya said: O best of Kurus, pleased by the words of your glorious mother,\nand moved by great compassion, the great sage Kapila spoke thus. Meaning: Lord Kapil was very pleased with his mother's prayer, because she\nwas not as concerned about her personal salvation as she was about the fallen bound souls"
