          "textEn": "477\nis: pure devotion is devoid of all material desires. AnyaBhilapita Shunyam. Personal\nor for material selfishness, this cannot be an excuse. Devotion-related actions should be beyond desire-driven actions\nand voluntary (experiential) philosophical thought. Pure devotion is beyond all\nmaterial qualities. Devotion under Tamo, Rajo, and Sattva Gunas can be divided into 81 categories. Devotional activities are divided into nine types--listening, chanting, remembrance,\nworship, adoration, service, and dedication, and these can again be divided into three-three qualitative categories.\nThus, listening can be divided under the Tamo, Rajo, and Satoguna. In this way, there are also three divisions of qualities. Three multiplied by nine equals twenty-seven, and when we multiply it by three again,\nwe get eighty-one. To reach the level of pure devotion, one has to cross such\nall mixed devotion as has been described in the following verses.\nBy the mere hearing of the divine sound, in my heart, all the inner chambers,\nThe mind's progress is like the stream of water in the forest. 11. The characteristics of devotion to the formless, the destroyer of the wicked,\nThe devotion that is free from the misunderstanding of what is not, is supreme. 12>. Meaning of the word\n\nMatt- mine; Gun- quality; Shruti- by listening; Matrena- only; Mayi- towards me; Sarva- in every; Guha- in the heart; Rahanta- remaining; Manah- movement; Gati- path; Avichinna- unbroken; Samudra- towards the sea; Yatha- as; Gaghka- of the Ganges; Ambhasah- of the water;\nLakshanam- characteristic; Prakatya- manifestation; Bhakti- yoga- of devotion; Nirgunasah- without qualities; Hi- without;\nNisandhah- without doubt; Udahatam- manifest; Ahaituki- without reason; Avyahitah- undisturbed; Ya- that; Bhakti: devotion; Purusha- the Supreme Being;\nUttame- towards the Supreme Being; Bhakti- yoga- of devotion; Lakshanam- characteristic; Prakatya- manifestation; Bhakti- yoga- of devotion;\n.