          "textEn": "498. Atha ma sarvabhuteshu bhutaatmanam kritalayam. Arjayadyaanmanabhyam maitrayabhinnechana chakshusha. 27). Meaning of the word\n\nAth--therefore; Mam Munchako; Sarva-bhuteshu--in all beings; Bhut-Atmanam--in all living beings, the self (Sv); Krit-\nAalayam--residing; Arhayet--should be worshiped; Daan-Maanabhyam--with donation and respect; Maitrya--with friendship\nFrom; Abhithena-Samanah; Chakshusha--seeing HL\n\nTherefore, along with charity and hospitality, one should worship me with a brotherly conduct and keeping a common\neye on everyone, because I reside in all beings in their\nform of soul. Meaning: People should not have this misconception that since God resides in the heart of every being,\ntherefore the individual soul is equal to Him. The equality of God and the soul\nis born in the minds of the non-resident. Here is a clear mention that the soul should be recognized only in relation to God.\nThe method of worshiping the soul is described here in this way: either by giving donations,\nor by freeing oneself from a separatist perspective, one should act with a sense of friendship. Sometimes,\nthese innocent souls mistakenly consider God as poor--which means\nGod Narayan has become poor. This is a paradox. God is full of all glories. They will accept living with a poor soul or even with an animal, but\nthis does not mean that they have become poor. Here, two Sanskrit words have come-- respect and charity. The best of mankind is indicative of charity.\nIt tells about the one who gives charity or shows compassion to someone inferior. We cannot consider God such a\nlowly person who depends on our charity. When we give charity, then always\nwe give to someone who is below us in physical or economic status. © | Charity is never given to a wealthy person. Similarly, it is indirectly said that respect or honor is given to the superior,\nwhile charity is given to the one below oneself."
