          "textEn": "500\nWill have to donate as Prasad. Many saints living in the forest do not maintain friendly relations with lions,\nbut they give them Prasad. Lions come, take Prasad, and leave, okay.\nJust like dogs do. The dog is not allowed inside the house according to Vedic practices. Because of their dirt, dogs and cats are not allowed to enter good homes, but\nthey are given such a lesson that they should stand outside the house. The kind householder brings the prasad from home,\nand gives it to them, they eat it and leave. We should treat the following creatures with kindness,\nbut it does not mean that we should treat them the same way we treat other humans. There should be a sense of equality, but there should be a difference in how this is practiced. Our discrimination, what kind of it is, its mention is given in the next six verses.\nIt is given. The first classification is done in inanimate and animate beings like stones.\nSometimes,\nit seems as if life manifests even in stone. It is known from experience that some hills and\nmountains increase. This is because of the presence of a spirit inside the stone. The second characteristic of the living state is the development of consciousness, and beyond that, the development of experience. In the Mahabharata,\nin the 'Mokshadharma Parva', it is said that trees have the ability to--see and smell. We have the experience of seeing trees. Sometimes, giant trees, during their growth,\nchange the direction of their growth to avoid disturbances. It means that a tree\ncan see, and according to the Mahabharata, a tree can also smell. This gives a hint of the development of the experience.\nThere, the knowers of touch are superior to those of taste.\nAmong them, the knowers of smell are the best, and among them, the knowers of sound are the best. 29). Shabdartham\n\nTatra-from them; Api-in addition to this; Syasham-from those who experience touch; Pravaraaha-the best; Rasa-\nVedinaha-those who experience taste;