          "textEn": "50]\n\nAmong the beings endowed with the faculty of sense (perception), those who have developed the sense of taste are superior to those who have developed the sense of touch. Those who are even better than these are\nthose who have developed the sense of smell, and those who are even better than these are those whose\nauditory sense is developed. Meaning: Although Western scholars believe that the establishment of evolution was first done by Darwin,\nbut anthropology is not new. The development process is from 5,000 years ago.\nKnown from before the written Bhagwat, oft. The words of Kapil Muni are proof. Who were present at the beginning of creation. This knowledge has been going on since the Vedic period, and all the development sequence is obtained in Vedic\nliterature; the principle of gradual development or archaeology is not new to scientists. Here it is said that the development process also takes place in trees, in different types of trees\nthe experience of touch is found.\nIt is said that fish are superior to trees,\nbecause they have developed taste buds. Bees are superior to fishes, in which somite\nhas developed and even superior to them is a snake in which the power of hearing has developed. In the darkness of night,\nthe snake can obtain its prey by hearing the alluring sound of the frog. The snake can understand\nthat, \"There is a frog\" and he catches it only by its sound. This\nexample is sometimes used for those individuals who utter the words for death\nare. A human can have a frog-like tongue that can produce words, but such a sound\nthen becomes an invocation of death. The best use of the tongue and sound is that Hare Krishna,\nHare Krishna, Krishna Krishna, Hare Hare, Hare Rama, Hare Rama, Ram Ram, Hare Hare, the chanting of the great mantra should be done.\nIt goes. From this, protection can be obtained from the hands of the cruel time.\nFrom the form of the ignorant, there are many feet, and from the best of four-legged creatures, there are two-legged ones. 30>. Shabdharam"
