          "textEn": "503\nIt will be until there is always confusion about who is doing the work for what purpose. The person\nwho can understand the ultimate truth is a Brahmin, and when such a Brahmin is ASH, then he can understand the purpose of the Vedas. The purpose of the Vedas is to understand the complete Brahman. The person who understands the ultimate truth\nin three states--as Brahman, the Supreme Soul, and God--and who understands the meaning of God, is considered the best among Brahmins or a Vaishnava. Knowing the doubt, he is better than the one who performs his own duty.\nHe is free, and he is the one who has attained the highest state of being. 32). Meaning of the word.\n\nMeaning-knower- of the Vedas, rather than; doubt-doubt; destroyer- destroyer; then- his\nneglect; superior- superior; self-action- one's assigned task; doer- doer; liberated- liberated; free from material association;\nThen- from that; superior- superior; non-doer- non-doer; dharma- devotion; for oneself- for oneself.\n.\n\nCompared to a Brahmin who understands the meaning of the Vedas, that man is superior, who resolves all doubts,\nand even superior to him is the one who diligently follows the Brahmin rules. Even better than that is a person free from all material impurities. Even better than that is\na pure devotee, who worships with selfless devotion. Meaning: YF Brahman is indicative of the person who has done a thorough\nanalytical study of the ultimate truth and who knows that the experience of the ultimate truth is done in three\ndifferent states as Brahya, Paramatma, and Bhagavan. If someone\nhas the ability to remove the doubts raised about the ultimate truth in addition to this knowledge,\nthen he is considered superior. It may also happen that a learned Brahmin-Vaishnava removes all\ndoubts, but if he does not follow the Vaishnava rules, then he does not remain seated on a high position. He should be free from all doubts and also be endowed with all Brahminic qualities.