          "textEn": "539\nHe acts and in this way keeps doing foolish and illegal deeds, then he is equal to that man\nwho grieves after losing all his property. What is the use of grieving over the loss of property? But as long as there is property nearby, its proper use\ncan be done to obtain eternal benefits. Here, it can be argued that when a person\nleaves the wealth earned through sinful deeds and dies, then along with their wealth, they also leave their sins. But here is a special mention that from the divine arrangement (daivenaasaditam)\nEven though he leaves his wealth, he takes its effect with him. If\nsomeone steals some money and is caught, and that money, according to the law of the state, even if\nhe returns it, he has to suffer punishment. Similarly, the money earned by crime, even if it is released here at the time of death,\nAccording to the divine system, it takes its fruit with it,\nTherefore, he has to suffer a hellish life.\nOnly by righteousness is he desirous of supporting his family.\nHe goes to the end, the poison of ignorance, the supreme, the state of darkness. 33). Meaning of the word\n\nKevalen-- only, Nire-- without; Hi-- certainly; Adharmen-- from unrighteous deeds; Kutumb-- family; Bharan-- to nurture;\nUtsukhah-- eager; Yati- goes; Jivah– person; Andh- darkness; Antham- darkness; Param- supreme;\nTamasah– of darkness; Padam- field\n\nTherefore, the person who, only by using vile means, strives to nurture his family and relatives,\nhe certainly goes to the deepest hell called Antham-Darkness. Meaning: In this verse, three words are very important. Kevalen ka matlab hai 'with bad means' and Adharmen ka matlab hai 'sinful or unrighteous!'. Or Kutumb-Bharan means\n“Parenting a family.''Undoubtedly, it is the duty of a family to support their household, but they should\nonly earn their livelihood through lawful means.\nIt is said in the Bhagavad Gita that God\ndivided the social system into four varnas or castes according to their qualities and deeds"
