          "textEn": "54]\nMeaning: Just as a prisoner is released after spending a miserable life in prison,\nsimilarly, the person who has previously committed impure and mischievous deeds\nis kept in hellish conditions, and after living a life of various kinds of hellish existence--such as\nliving the lives of lower animals like dogs, cats, and pigs, respectively, comes back to human form through the process of evolution. It is said in the Bhagavad Gita that a person engaged in yoga practice\nsometimes cannot complete it properly, and he falls down for some reason or the other,\nbut even then, it is certain that he will be a human being in the next life. It is said\nthat a person who deviates from the path of yoga is given the opportunity to be born in a very wealthy family or in a very\npious lineage. From this, it is understood that\na wealthy family is indicative of a large merchant family, because those who do business are\nextremely wealthy.\nThe person who remains engaged in self-realization or establishing a relationship with God,\nbut is unable to complete it, is allowed to be born in a wealthy family or in a\npious Brahmin clan. In both ways, he has the guarantee of being born in the next life, in human society. Therefore, it was concluded that one who does not want the life of hell like Tamik or\nAndhakamikh, should adopt the method of devotion to Krishna. Which is the first level of yoga, because if someone does not fully\nachieve Krishna-bhava-nimritta in this life, then in the next birth, his birth in the human family is certain. She couldn't be sent to hell. Being Krishna bhava is the purest life. This all\nprevents humans from going to hell by going towards hell and taking birth in the family of dogs or pigs. Thus, under the third canto of Srimad Bhagavatam, the thirty-first chapter, titled \"The Description of the Opposite Deeds by Lord Kapila,\" is completed with the meaning of Bhakti Vedanta."
