          "textEn": "55\nHe goes near, he does not come to take up a body again in this world, but to the abode of God\nwhere he never returns. He who is bound there, like a soul adorned with actions,\nBearing the support of the body, senses, and mind,\nHe who is pure, without distortion, and fully enlightened,\nI bow to the one who is situated in the heart, delighting in the mind. 13). Meaning of the word:\n\nYah- who; Tu- also; Atra- here; Baddhah- bound; Iva- like; Karmabhih- by actions; Avrit- covered;\nAtma- pure soul; Bhut- gross element; Indriya- senses; Aasya- mind; Mayim- with; Avalambya- falling;\nMaya- in illusion; Aste- remains; Vishuddham- completely pure; Avikaram- without change; Akhanda- unbroken; Bodham- knowledge;\nAnant- endowed with infinite knowledge; Atapyaman- afflicted; Hrudaye- in the heart; Avasitam- remaining; Namami- I bow. I, the pure soul, bound by my actions, due to the arrangement of Maya, at this time,\nI am lying in my mother's womb. I bow to those Gods who are here with me,\nbut who are unaffected and unchanging.\nThey are infinite, but are seen in the tormented heart. I respectfully greet them.\n\nMeaning: In the previous verse, it is said that the soul says, I take refuge in CH God.\nI take refuge.\"Therefore, naturally, the soul is a dependent servant of the Supreme Soul. The Supreme Soul and\nthe soul are seated in the same body, as the Upanishads declare. They are sitting as friends.\nOne is suffering and the other is completely separated from suffering. In this verse, Vishuddham Avikaram Akhanda-Bodham has come, which means that the Supreme Being\nalways stays away from all sins. Because of being a body, the living being is afflicted with impurity and suffers.\nBut this does not mean that because God is also with him, therefore God\nalso has a physical body. They are Avikaryam, that is, without change. They always live in one,\nBut unfortunately, the illusionist thinkers, due to their impure hearts, do not realize that God\ni