          "textEn": "Under 555\nHe is engaged in various kinds of work. We can see in this world that a living being\ndoes work with such power that for the satisfaction of the senses, in the so-called progress of material civilization,\nhis is an astonishing contribution. But in reality, he should know his position that he is\nthe eternal servant of God. When he attains complete knowledge, then he realizes that\nGod is supremely venerable and the living being is his eternal servant. Without this knowledge, he remains engaged in material activities. This is what is called ignorance or lack of knowledge.\nThat knowledge which is the supreme, which is the cause of all, that God-\nWho is eternal, unchanging, and follows the righteous path.\nThat which is the position of action of the living being,\nWe worship that. 16. Meaning of the word:\n\nGnanam- knowledge; Yat- that which; Etat- this; Adadhat- given; Katamah- besides the other; Sah- he; Devah- God;\nTr- of the three times; Sthir- in the stable and the moving; Anuvartita- remaining; Anshah- their partial\nSwarupah; Tam- that; Jiva- of the living beings; Karma- Padavim- path of action; Anuvartamanah- those who follow; Tapa- of the three kinds of suffering; Upashatamanaya- to be freed from; Vayam- we; Bhajem- let us worship; Varanam- take refuge..\n\nBesides the partial form of God, the omnipresent Supreme Soul, who else is directing all the\nand non-moving objects? They are present in these three states of time, past,\npresent, and future.\nTherefore, the bound soul is engaged in various actions by His command,\nand to be freed from the three afflictions of this bound life, we must take refuge in Him alone.\n\nMeaning: When the bound soul is eager to be freed from the clutches of Maya, then the Supreme Being, who resides in his\ninner self, gives him this knowledge, \"Come into My refuge.\"\"\nAs God says in the Bhagavad Gita, ^“ Abandon all other activities and take refuge in me.\" It is necessary to believe that the source of knowledge is the Supreme Being. In the Bhagavad Gita, its confirmation\nhappens. Matthaha smritigyatamopahanam cha. God says, \"By me alone, man attains true knowledge\nand memory, and forgetfulness is also obtained from me.\"\"Who is a man from a material point of view\""
