ा है, क्योकि वह अपनी मानवी बुद्धि का उपयोग आध्यात्मिक बोध के",
          "textEn": "557\nThe miserly one is always in distress. 97 Meaning of the word\n\nDehi- living being with body; Anya- other body; Vivare- in the stomach; Jathar- of the stomach; Agnina- from fire; Asruk-\nBlood; Vit- excrement; Mutra- of the urine; Koop- well; Patitah- fallen; Bhrush- strongly; Tapta- burnt;\nDeha- his body; Ichchh- wishing; Ith- from this place; Vivasitum- to go out; Ganayan-\nCounting; Swamasan- of the month; Nirvasayate- will be freed; Krupan- wise;\nBhagavan- O Lord; Kada- when; Nu- without doubt..\n\nFalling into the blood, feces, and urine pouches in his mother's womb, and in the agony of his mother's\ndigestive fire, the embodied being keeps counting months,\nand prays, O, *'Oh God, when will this unfortunate being be freed from this prison?'\nWill he be able to be freed from this prison?Meaning: here is a description of the uneven circumstances of life in the mother's womb.\nOn one side, the infant\nremains heated by the heat of the digestive fire, and on the other side, stool, urine, blood, etc., remain. After seven months,\nconsciousness comes into the infant, therefore he begins to realize his unbearable situation\nand he prays to God. Until he comes out, he keeps counting months.\nAnd he remains anxious to get out of that prison. The so-called civilized human life does not pay attention to this\nterrible situation, and sometimes, with the intention of sensual pleasure, he tries to kill it by contraceptive\nmethods or abortion. Such people, instead of being concerned about the terrible situation of the womb, remain in materialism,\nand misuse human life. The word 'Krupanadhiha' in this verse is important. The meaning of 'Shrih' is 'intelligence' and 'Krupan' means\n'stingy'. 'Baddhajeevan' is for those who are stingy in their intelligence or those who do not use their intelligence\n. In humans, intelligence remains developed, and this intelligence is to be used to escape the cycle of birth and death.\nHe who does not do this is a miser--he only keeps looking at it. The person who does not use his human intellect to escape from the cycle of birth and death, that is, the noose of Maya,\nis considered a miser. The antonym of miser is generous. A Brahmin is called generous because he uses his human intellect for spiritual understanding"
