          "textEn": "583\nIt is watered with water, so this seed grows and takes the form of a tree. In this, the\nflowers and fruits that grow, the living being can enjoy them even in this world. This is called the state of being Brahman. In this state of being, the living being is called materialistic. But when he\nbecomes free from all titles, becomes completely absorbed in Krishna consciousness, and remains engaged in service, then\nhe is called liberated. Until one attains the company of an authentic guru through the grace of God,\nthere remains no possibility of liberation from the cycle of birth and death in various wombs, and from wandering in various kinds of realms.\nLiving beings are subject to the control of their senses, which are made up of body, mind, and intellect.\nThat is their restraint; their death is inevitable. 44). Shabdartham\n\nJeevah-- Jee- nisandeeshan; Asya-- Usara; Anugah – Upayukta; Dehaah – Shareera; Bhutah – Sthulatatva; Indriyaah – Indriyas;\nManah – Manah; Mayah – Seenrupnee; Tat – Shareera Ka; Nirodah – Vinasha; Asya – Jeeva Ka; Maranam – Mruthya; Avirbhavah –\nPrakatya; Tu – Tab; Sambhavah – STL\nIn this way, according to the desires of actions, the soul is endowed with a suitable body, consisting of mind and senses. When the fruit of any action is exhausted, this end is called death.\nAnd when any fruit begins, that beginning is called birth.\n\nMeaning: From eternity, the soul has been constantly wandering in various wombs and realms. In the Bhagavad Gita, this process is explained. He who is deluded by the illusion of all beings, who is seated on the chariot of the body given by Maya,\nbeing subject to the control of Maya, each person, riding on the chariot of the body given by Maya, wanders throughout the universe.\nIn materialistic life, there remains a chain of karma and fruits. This is a long reel of karma and its fruits,\nand a lifetime is equivalent to a flash. When a child is born,\none should understand that his body is