          "textEn": "589\nChapter 32\nKarma-Bandhan\nKapil said,\nNow, those who are householders, who are in the house of darkness,\nand those who are driven by desire, who are driven by lust,\nand those who are driven by anger. 9>. Meaning of the word\n\nKapil said -- Lord Kapil said; Atha-- then; Yah- who; Griha- householder; Dharman- duties; Ev- certainly; Aavasan- while staying; Grihe- in the house; Kamam- desire; Artham- economic development; Cha- and; Dharman- religious rituals; Swan- of one's own; Dogdhi- enjoys; Bhuyah- again and again; Piparthi- makes complete;\nTan- them L\n\nLord said: A person living a household life, while performing religious rituals,\nkeeps getting material benefits, and in this way, he fulfills his desire for economic development and sensual gratification. He/She works in the same way again and again. Meaning: There are two types of householders-- TERE and GRIHASTHA.\nThe life goal of a householder\nremains indulgence, and the self-realization of a householder. Here, God is speaking about the householder\nor about that person who wants to remain in this world. His\ntask is to enjoy material benefits by performing religious rituals for economic development and\nultimately satisfy his senses. He doesn't want anything more than this. Such a person\nworks hard throughout his life to become rich, to live well, and to eat and drink. He can go to heaven in the next life by donating something for a virtuous deed,\nBut he does not want to stop the cycle of birth and death and does not want to see the end of the world's sufferings. Such a person is called a householder. A householder is a person who lives with his family, wife, children, and relatives,\nbut does not have any attachment to them.\nHe does not like to live a family life without becoming a saint or monk,\nbut his main objective is to attain self-realization or\nto reach the state of Krishna consciousness. But here, Kapildev tells about Grihamaidhi"
