          "textEn": "590\nare those whose goal is to live a materialistic, prosperous life, and whom they can attain through sacrifices, charity, and\ngood deeds. They occupy good positions, therefore they use the earnings of their good deeds\nand repeatedly engage in sensual pleasures. Prahmad\nThe Maharaj has said--Again and again, they like to chew the chewed food--They like to chew the chewed food again. Even while being prosperous, they repeatedly endure physical sufferings, but they do not want to abandon such a life.\nHe who is deluded by the desire for the Lord's Dharma, who is averse,\nHe who performs sacrifices, other than the gods, with faith and devotion. 2>. Meaning of the word:\n\nSaha--He; Cha api-Or also; Bhagavat-dharmat-with devotion; Kama-mudha-blind by desire; Para-mukha-with turned face; Yajate-worships; Kratubhih-by rituals; Devan-of gods; Pitran-of ancestors; Kaun-who;\nCha--and; Shraddhaya-with faith; Anvitah-united;\nSuch a person, due to excessive attachment to sensual pleasure, is devoid of devotion,\nis Therefore, even after performing many types of yagyas (sacrifices) and keeping big vows to please the gods and ancestors, they do not take interest in Krishna consciousness, that is, devotion. Meaning: The Bhagavad Gita (7.20) says that those who worship gods,\ntheir intellect remains corrupt-- Kagastaithlalanaah.\nThey remain excessively attracted to sensual pleasure,\ntherefore they worship the gods. Undoubtedly, it is prescribed in the Vedic scriptures that whoever desires wealth, health, or knowledge should worship the gods. Materialists.\nA person's demands are many, therefore the gods who satisfy his senses are also many. Those who\n“want to live a prosperous life, they generally worship gods or ancestors through offering of food.\nSuch people are devoid of Krishna consciousness and do not take interest in devotional service to God.\nThis type of so-called pure and religious person is the result of formlessism.\nThe Nirvisheshvadis believe that the ultimate truth has no form. Therefore, for one's own benefit, whatever form is imagined and worshipped. Therefore, \"Grihamedhi\""
