          "textEn": "59]\nOr the materialistic person says that they can worship any deity for the worship of God.\nThey can. Especially, among Hindus, those who are non-vegetarians, they give preference to the worship of Kali.\nBecause there is a provision for the sacrifice of a goat in front of the goddess. They say that\nwhether someone worships Goddess Kali or Lord Vishnu or any deity, the goal is the same. This is a first-class hypocrisy, and such people go astray. But still,\nthey like this vision. The Bhagavad Gita does not accept such hypocrisy. It clearly mentions that such rules are for deluded people. Here, this justice has been confirmed.\nAnd that is why the word 'Kamamudha' (engrossed in lust) has been used, which means one who has lost their senses\nor one who has become blind to sensual gratification. ©. Kamamudha people are devoid of Krishna consciousness and devotion,\nand remain blind to the strong desire for sensual gratification.\nThe worship of gods\nhas been done in both Bhagavad Gita and Bhagwat. He who is afflicted by impurity, who is devoted to his father,\nHaving gone to the moonless world, he will be reborn. 3). Meaning of the word:\n\nThat--to the gods and ancestors; with faith--with reverence; conquered--defeated; intellect--mind; of the ancestors; of the gods; vow--one's vow; man--individual; having gone--having gone; of the moon--of the moon; of the world--of the world;\nSipping the nectar of the moon--drinking the nectar of the moon; again--again; he returns--he returns.\n\nSuch materialistic people, attracted by the pleasure of Indra, and with devotion towards their ancestors and gods, can go to the lunar abode, where they drink the nectar of the moon,\nand then return to this world again. Meaning: The moon is considered a realm of heaven. © | Various Vedic sacrifices, as\nAs the worship of gods and ancestors is completed, a man goes to this realm. But no one here\ncan stay for a long time.\nAccording to the calculation of the gods, the duration of life on the moon is\nten thousand years. In the calculation of the time of the gods, one day (12 hours) is equal to six months of this world.\nUp to the moon, one cannot reach by any physical vehicle like Sutanik"
