          "textEn": "679\nIt is the radiance of the Supreme Being. Lord Krishna says in the Bhagavad Gita (14.27)--Brahmano hi\nPratishthaham, I am the basis of the imperishable, formless Brahman, which is imperishable and supreme bliss.\nZ| God is the ocean of all bliss, in which Brahmananda is included, therefore, one who has unwavering faith in God\nhas already realized the formless Brahman and the Supreme Soul.\nJust as the chariot is greatly dependent on separate horses,\nso too is the one who is dependent on various scriptures. 23). Meaning of the word:\n\nYatha--as; Indriyaih--from the senses; Prithak--separately; Dvarri--in different ways; Arthah--object; Bahu-guna--many qualities;\nAshrayah--being sheltered; Teh:--one; Nana--in different ways; Iyate--is experienced; Tadvat--like that;\nBhagavan--Lord; Shastra-varthasabhih--according to various scriptural commands;.\n\nThe same object is perceived differently by different senses due to its various qualities, in different\nways.\nSimilarly, God is one, but according to various scriptural commands,\nHe appears different. Meaning: It appears that by following the path of Gnanayoga, a person reaches the formless Brahman.\nHowever, by devotion in Krishna consciousness, his faith and devotion to God increase. But here it is said that Bhakti Yoga and Gnana Yoga both are to reach the same goal, God. Through the method of knowledge yoga, the same God appears formless. Just as\nthe same object appears differently to different senses, similarly, the same God appears formless through contemplation. A mountain looks like a cloud from a distance, and because of this,\nan unknown person may consider it a cloud, but in reality, it is not a cloud, it is a large-\nmountain. A human has to learn from evidence that the appearance of a cloud is not a cloud but\na mountain.\nIf someone moves forward little by little, instead of clouds, they start seeing hills and\ngreenery. When someone actually reaches the hill, they can see various\nobjects. The second example is of milk. When we look at milk, it\nlooks white, when we taste it, it tastes delicious. Cold on the touch, GI on"
