          "textEn": "620\nIt smells fragrant, and upon hearing it, it is understood to be milk. Upon experiencing milk through various senses,\nwe say that it is a white, delicious, fragrant object. It's actually milk. Similarly, those who want to find God through contemplation can reach His physical brilliance or\nthe formless Brahman, and those who want to find God through the practice of yoga can attain Him\nas the indwelling Supreme Soul. But those who want to reach God through devotion can directly have a vision of the Supreme Person. Ultimately, the Supreme Being is the destination of all methods. The fortunate man who, while following the rules of the scriptures,\nbecomes completely pure from all material impurities, he, considering God as everything, goes to his refuge. Just as the real taste of milk is taken from the eyes,\nnostrils, or ears, not from the tongue, similarly, man can attain the bliss of the ultimate truth\nonly through one path--devotion--.\nIn the Bhagavad Gita, its confirmation is as follows--\nBhaktiya Samadhijanaati-- If someone wants to know the ultimate truth completely, then he\nshould do devotion. Undoubtedly, no one can fully understand the ultimate truth. For a small\nliving being, this is not possible. But by being full of devotion, the soul can reach the peak of knowledge\nthrough other means, not otherwise. By following various paths of knowledge, a person can reach the formless essence of God. With God, identification or the knowledge of the formless Brahman gives divine bliss,\nbecause Brahman is infinite. That Brahman is eternal, infinite, blissful, and infinite. But beyond this infinite\nbliss, one can go. It is transcendental. Even crossing the infinite, the high position that is attained,\nis Krishna.\nThe honey and essence that is obtained through the exchange of devotion to Shri Krishna,\nis incomparable and even greater than divine bliss. Therefore, Prabodhananda Swami says,\nthat 'Kaivalya' or divine bliss is undoubtedly extremely great and is praised by many wise men,\nbut this divine bliss is like hell for the devotee who attains happiness through devotion to God"
