          "textEn": "628.\nMeaning: In the initial state, no one attains the highest state of devotion. Here, the meaning of 'bhakt' (devotee) refers to that person who, to become a devotee, does not hesitate to accept reformist methods.\nTo become a devotee of God, a person has to make a guru, and from that,\none has to inquire about the progress of devotion. Sixty-four are the ways to progress in devotion.\nThere are some of the devotional acts, such as serving the devotee, chanting the holy name according to the method,\nworshiping the idol, listening to Shrimad Bhagavat or Bhagavad Gita from a realized person, and living in such a holy place\nwhere there is no obstacle in devotion. He who does these five main tasks is a devotee. The duty of a human being is to respect the AUF of his Guru. He should not harbor hatred towards his fellow brothers.\nHe should not keep it. But if he is more enlightened and has increased devotion to Krishna, then he\nshould be considered equal to a guru, and one should be pleased to see such gurus progressing in Krishna consciousness.\nThe devotee should be very kind in giving the message of devotion to Krishna to the common people, because this is the only solution to get out of the noose of Maya. This is the real\npublic welfare, because this is the way to show compassion to those who need it. The word 'Shushreshabhirata' is indicative of a person who remains devotedly engaged in the service of the Guru. The Guru should be served in every way, and all facilities should be provided.\nThe devotee who does this is also eligible to receive these teachings. The word 'Bahirjat Viragaya' is indicative of the person who has attained detachment from internal and external material\ndesires. He is detached from activities that do not relate to devotion to Krishna,\nHe is detached, and at the same time, he should be internally averse to material life. Such a\nperson should be free from envy and should wish for the welfare of all beings--not only humans, but also their\nextra all beings--.\nShuchaye means inside and\nOutside, a pure person. To become pure from inside and outside, a person should chant the holy name of Lord Krishna or\nVishnu."
