          "textEn": "630\nChapter thirty-three\nKapila's activities\nMaitreya said\nAnd the one who is born from Kapila's speech\nThat Kardama's giver is Devahuti.\nThe one who is disturbed by delusion, bowing down to him\nThen, the one who is established in the knowledge of the essence, the land of accomplishment. 9>. Meaning of the word.\n\nMaitreya said—Maitreya said; Evam—in this way; Nishamya—after hearing; Kapilasya—to Kapila; Vachah—word;\nJanitri—mother; Sa-that—that; Kardamasya—to Kardama Muni; Dayita—beloved wife; Kil—named; Devahuti—Devahuti;\nVisrasta- having become; Mohapatla—the veil of delusion; Tam—that; Abhiprayamya—bowing down; Tushtavah—to be pleased;\nTattva—the essence; Vishayah—in relation to; Ankitah- the creator, the expounder; Siddhi- of liberation;\nBhumim—the background..\n\nShri Maitreya said: Thus, the mother of Lord Kapila and the wife of Kardama Muni,\nDevahuti, was freed from all ignorance related to devotion and divine knowledge.\nThey greeted those\nGod who is the proponent of Sankhya philosophy in the background of liberation, and\nthen pleased them with the following praise.\n\nMeaning: The philosophy that Lord Kapila propounded before his mother\nis the background of being situated on a spiritual level. The specific importance of this philosophy is proven-\nby being expressed here, which means the background of salvation. Lord Kapil\nunderstood the Sankhya philosophy that he propounded, and people bound by Maya, who are suffering in this world,\ncan get rid of the clutches of matter (inertia). Through this philosophical system,\neven a person situated in the physical world can be immediately liberated. This state is called Jeevan-yukti. Its meaning is that even while living in this physical body, a man becomes liberated.\nGod, it happened with Devahuti, the mother of Kapil, therefore, through his praises,\nHe satisfied God.\nWhoever understands the fundamental principles of Sankhya philosophy, attains devotion,\nand in this world, becomes absorbed in Krishna consciousness, that is, becomes liberated."
