          "textEn": "642\nHe who does, even if he is born in a Chandal's house, is freed from the cycle of birth and death. I bow to you, the supreme Brahman, the source of all beings.\nI am the manifestation of the self in the external flow.\nBy your own power, destroy the flow of bad qualities.\nI salute Vishnu, the Kapila, the womb of the Vedas. 8>. Meaning of the word.\n\nTam--That; Tvam--You; Aham--I; Brahma--Brahma; Param--Supreme; Pumamsa--To the Supreme God; Pratyaksha--Within; Sarotasi--Within the mind; Samvibhava--Became; Dhyan- meditated; Anubhuta- experienced; Swata- by oneself; Tejasa- by one's own power; Dhvasta- destroyed;\nGuna-flow- the influence of the qualities of nature; Vande- I salute; Vishnu- To Vishnu; Kapila- named; Vedagarbham- the abode of the Vedas..\n\nOh God, I believe that you, by the name of Kapil, are the Supreme Being, Lord Vishnu himself.\nThat is, the Supreme Brahman. All the sages, freed from the agitations of the senses and mind, contemplate only your\nthought, because only by your grace can a man be liberated from the bondage of worldly existence. At the time of destruction, all the Vedas find their place within you.\nMeaning: Kapil, the mother of Devahuti, without further elaborating on her prayer, briefly said that Lord Kapil is Vishnu, there is no one else, and since he himself is a true being, therefore,\nit is not possible for them to worship him only with praise. Their desire was that\nGod be pleased. Here, the word 'Pratyaksha' is important. The eight limbs of yoga practice are as follows--Yama, Niyama, Asana, Pranayama, Pratyahara, Dharana, Dhyana, and Samadhi. ' Pratyahara\" means\nto end the functions of the senses. The revelation of God by Devahuti,\nwhich is being said, is only possible when a person withdraws his senses from all actions. When\na person remains engaged in devotion, then there is no question of his senses being distracted here and there.\nIt does not arise. In such complete Krishna consciousness, a man can understand God in his true form.\n\nMaitreya said,\n\nThis is how the Lord is known, as the father of Kapila.\nHe is the mother of speech, and he is the father of speech. 9>."
