          "textEn": "65\nOne who has renounced the householder's life, free from the fear of separation from his wife. Even though you are known, O Gauri, you are the beloved of the world. 29). Shabdartham\n\nVabanam--To the forest; WER Patyau--When the husband leaves; Apatya-Viraha--From the separation of one's son; Atura--Extremely\nSad; Gnat-Tatva--Knowledge of truth; Api--Although; Abhut--Became; Nachhet Vatsay--When the calf died; Gauh--Cow;\nIva--Like; Vatsala-Snehila.\n\nDevahuti's husband had already renounced his home and taken Sanyas Ashram and\nthen her only son Kapil left home. Although they knew all the truths of life and death,\nand although their hearts were free from all kinds of impurities, but they were so saddened by the loss of their\nson, as a cow is saddened when a calf dies. Meaning: The wife of a Kheer who is away from home or has become a renunciant should not be very sad, because her husband's representative, her son, stays with her. Of the scriptures,\nit is said-- Aatyaiiv putra jayate, the son represents the body of the husband.\nIf a woman has\nan adult son, then she is not a widow. When Kapil Muni was with them, Devahuti was more\nnot distressed, but as soon as they departed, she became extremely sorrowful. They were not saddened by\ntheir worldly relationship with Kardam Muni, but rather towards God,\nbecause of their steadfast love, they were saddened off.\n\nHere, an example is given that Devahuti became like that cow who lost her calf. Separated from its mother, the cow writhes in pain day and night. Similarly, Devahuti, the sorrowful one,\noff and to her friends and relatives, she prays this: *'Bring my son home so that I\ncan stay alive, otherwise I will die.'\"This deep affection towards God, although manifested in the form of one's own son-\nlove, is beneficial from a spiritual perspective. From having attachment towards one's son,\na person is bound to live in this world, but that same attachment,\nwhen directed towards God, takes him to Vaikuntha, in the company of God.\nEvery woman, by making herself worthy like Devahuti, attains God as her son"
