          "textEn": "2\nAfter that, in five chapters, the character of Maharaj Dhruva has been described. Then in the next eleven chapters,\nthe character of King Prithu and in the other eight chapters, the activities of the Pracheta kings have been described. As it is said in the first verse of this chapter, Swayambhuva Manu had three daughters, whose names were Akuti, Devahuti, and Prasuti. Among these three daughters, the account of Devahuti has already been given by her husband Kardam Muni\nand along with their son Kapil Muni. In this chapter, the first daughter Akuti\nwill be described in particular. Svayambhuva Manu was the son of Brahma. Brahma had many other sons,\nbut Manu is specially mentioned first because he was a great devotee of God. In this verse, the word 'Cha' has also come, which indicates that these three daughters of Swayambhuva Manu\nIn addition, there were two sons as well. The king, who is pleased with the sky, is also pleased with the mother.\nHaving taken refuge in the son of Dharma, having approved of the hundred forms. 2>. Meaning of the word:\n\nAakutim-- Aakuti; Ruchay-- Supremely auspicious; Pradat-- Gave; Api-- Although; Bhratru-matim-- A daughter who is like a brother;\nNripah-- King; Putrika-- To accept a son obtained from a daughter; Dharmam-- Religious duty; Ashritya-- Having taken refuge; Shatarupa--\nBy the wife of Swayambhuva Manu; Anumoditah-- Accepted..\n\nAakuti had two brothers, yet King Swayambhuva Manu married Prajapati Ruchi on this condition\nthat the son born to her would be returned to Manu as a son. He\ndid so on the advice of his wife, Shatrupa. Meaning: Sometimes an unmarried person gives his daughter to her husband with the condition that he returns the grandson to whom he has adopted as a son and makes him the heir to his property.\nThis is called Putrika-Dharma, which means that even if a man does not have a son with his wife,\nhe can obtain a son through religious rituals. But here we see a strange thing with M