          "textEn": "3\nPurushottam Bhagwan will be born, therefore, even with two sons, he wanted to make the special son born from Akuti\nhis own son, because he wanted Shri Bhagwan as his son and grandson. Manu\nare the law-makers of mankind, and since they themselves have observed the rules of the son, therefore we can accept\nthat mankind can also adopt this system. Therefore, even while having a son,\nif someone wants to take one son of their daughter, then with this condition, they can donate their daughter. This is the opinion of Srila Gopala Goswami. Prajapati, that God, desired this.\nMithunam Brahmavarchasvi, in the supreme Samadhi. 3). Shabdartham\n\nPrajapati: --One who is entrusted with the responsibility of producing offspring; Sah: --He; Bhagavan: --The Supreme Lord; Ruchi: --The supremely auspicious inclination;\nTET —From him; Ajijanat:--Born; Mithunam: --A pair, a couple; Brahma-Varchasvi: --Lord of the spiritual realm;\nParmen: --The most powerful; Samadhinah: --In Samadhi.\nAppointed in his Brahmana as the supreme powerful and father of souls,\n( Prajapati ) Ruchi gave birth to a son and a daughter from the womb of his wife Akuti. Meaning: The word 'Brahmavarchasi' is extremely meaningful. ©. Ruchi was a Brahmin, and he diligently followed the duties of Brahmakaryas. As mentioned in the Bhagavad Gita, the qualities of Brahma are as follows--control of the senses, control of the mind, internal and external cleanliness, progress in spiritual and material knowledge, simplicity, truthfulness,\ndevotion to Shri Bhagavan, etc. There are many qualities by which a Brahmavadi personality is manifested, and it is believed\nthat Ruchi was a strong follower of Brahmavadi principles. Therefore, they are especially\ncalled Brahmavarchasi. One who is born in a Brahmin family but does not act like a Brahmin is called Brahmin-like in the Vedic language, and he is counted among the Shudras and Siyos.\nIn this\nway, we see from the Bhagwat that Ved Vyas composed the Mahabharata especially for the sri-shud-bah-\nbondhu. Khi, Shudra, and Brahma-Bandhu, these three classes are called ignorant, they are not able to study the Vedas, because the Vedas are for those who have attained Brahma-yoga. This\nrestriction is based on merit, not caste. Without the qualification of Brahma"
