          "textEn": "9\nPurnamasuta virajam vishwagam cha parantapa.\nDevakulyam hareh padashauchadyaabhutasaridivah. 94. Meaning of the word\n\nPurnima- Purnima ne; Asut- produced; Virajam- son named Viraj; Vishvagam- also named Vishvag; Param- Tap- destroyer of enemies; Devakulyam- daughter named Devakulya; Hare- of Shri Bhagavan; Pad- Shauchat- washed feet; Jal- from water; Ya- she; Abhut- happened; Sarit- river; Divah- under the banks of Ganga, divine water..\n\nThese two saints named Vidur, Kashyap, and Purnima, from whom three saints of Purnima were born.\nWhose names were Viraj, Vishchag, and Devkulya. Among these three, Devakula was the water that washed away the truth of Shri Bhagwan,\nwhich later turned into the Ganges of Swargaloka. Note: Kashyap and Purnima, here the description is of the descendants of Purnima. Their detailed\ndescription will be given in the sixth book.\nHere it is known that Devkulya is the main deity of the Ganges River,\nwho descends from heaven to this world and is considered sacred, because she touches the lotus feet of Shri Bhagwan\nHari. Atreya, the son of Palyanasuya, the son of Suyashas, the son of Suyashas.\nDattam, Durvasam, Soma, the lord of Brahman, the one who is always present. 15. Meaning of the word\n\nAtre--of Atri Muni; Pali--wife; Anasuya--Anasuya; Rin--three; Jagne- originated; Su-Yashasah- very famous;\nSutan- to the sons; Dattam- Dattatreya; Durvasasam- Durvasa; Soma- Soma (Lord of the Moon); Atma- the Supreme Soul; Isha- Shivji;\nBrahma- Brahmaji; Sambhavan- incarnations..\n\nAtri Muni's wife, Anasuya, gave birth to three famous sons. These are--Soma, Dattatreya and\nDurvasa, who are the incarnations of Lord Brahma, Vishnu and Shiva, OF | Soma of Brahma, Dattatreya, Lord\nVishnu and Durvasa were the representatives of Shiva. Meaning: In this verse, the words at-p-insi-bra-sambhavan have come.\nThe meaning of 'Atma' is the Supreme Soul or\nVishnu; 'I' means Shiva, and 'Brahma' means the four-faced Brahma. Anasuya