          "textEn": "0. Another meaning of 'Atp' can also be considered, which is as follows: That element which is in every soul\nis the Supreme Soul or the soul of all, that appeared as Dattatreya because here the word 'Asht'\nis used. In the Bhagavad Gita, every soul (jiva) is said to be a part of Shri Bhagavan or the Supreme Soul,\nTherefore, why not assume that Dattatreya was also one of those parts? Here, Shiva and Brahma\nare also considered a part, therefore, why not consider all of them as ordinary beings? Its answer is this.\nThat the manifestation of Vishnu and ordinary souls is definitely a part of God, and none of them\nare equal to them, but there are many categories in these parts. In the Varaha Purana, in a very beautiful way,\nit has been told that some parts are self-contained and some are diverse parts. The various parts are called living beings, and the self-existent\ncome into the category of Vishnu. There are many categories, even in the differential range of living beings.\nIts explanation\nhas been given in Vishnu Purana, where it is clearly stated that the separate parts of God are covered by Maya. Such individual parts that can wander anywhere in God's creation are called omnipresent\nand endure the sufferings of existence. They, being subjected to various qualities, according to their respective actions,\nAccording to that, they are freed from the veil of ignorance. For example, a person of sattva quality has to endure less suffering,\nand a person of tamo quality has to endure more. But pure Krishna consciousness (devotion) is the birthright of every soul because it is a part of God. Divine consciousness is also a part, and in the proportion that\nthis consciousness is free from physical impurities, accordingly, the souls take different places. In the Vedanta Sutra, the souls of different types are compared to lamps of different brightness.\nFor example, some electric bulbs have one thousand candle power, while some have five hundred, these few\nhave fifty, but all these bulbs emit light. Although light exists in all these bulbs,\nthe degree of light in them varies. Similarly, there is a classification of Brahma. In the various forms of Vishnu, the expansion of Vishnu's essence is like lamps, Shivji is also like a lamp.\nBut a hundred times the light is only Shri Krishna. In the Vishnu principle, 94; in the Shiva principle, 84; Brahma"
