          "textEn": "3\nAfter that, the whole world merges into Him: Yato va imati bhutani (Taittiriya Upanishad 3.1.1).\nFrom Vedic mantras, we gain knowledge about Shri Bhagavan; as a result, Atri Muni, while pleading for a son equal to Shri Bhagavan,\nwithout knowing what his name was, focused his attention\non him. This type of devotion to God, in which even the name of God is not known,\nis described in the Bhagavad Gita, in which God says that four types of virtuous souls come to me to ask for desired fruits. Atri Muni wanted a son like God; therefore, they cannot be considered pure devotees.\nBecause they had a desire, which was material. Although they wanted a son like Shri Bhagavan, but this\ndesire was material, because they did not want Shri Bhagavan but wanted a child like him.\nIf\nthey had desired Shri Bhagwan as their offspring, they would have remained free from this material desire, because then\nthey would have desired the supreme truth, but desiring a son like them, this material desire\nwould have been off. Therefore, the count of Atri Muni cannot be done among the pure devotees.\nThe burning three worlds are controlled by the life force, the fire. Nirgaten munermmurdhnah sami kshya prabhavastrayah. 21). Meaning of the word:\n\nTapyamanam - while doing penance; Trimbhuvanam - the three worlds; Pranayam - practice of controlling breath; Edhasa - fuel; Agnina - from fire; Nirgatena - coming out; Munehah - of a sage; Murdhah - head; Sikhriya - by looking; Prabhavah - three - the three great gods\n(Brahma, Vishnu and Maheshwar).\n\nWhen Atri Muni was doing deep penance, due to his Pranayama, a blazing fire emerged from his head\nwhich was seen by the three main gods of the three worlds. Meaning: According to Srila Gopala Goswami, the fire of pranayama is mental satisfaction.\nThat fire\nwas seen by the Supreme Lord Vishnu, and through them, Brahma and Shiva also saw it. Atri Muni, through his\npranayama, focused his attention on the Supreme Being, th