          "textEn": "35\nThe wise, the ascetics, and the Brahmins, the fire,\nThe virtuous, the austerities, and the ignorant, not out of envy. 9>. Meaning of the word\n\nShruyatam-- Listen; Brahma-rishayah-- These are the Brahma sages; Me-- To me; Saha-devah-- These are the gods; Saha-agnayah-- These are the fire gods; Sadhunam-- Of the virtuous; Fad:— While speaking; Vrittam-- Behavior, conduct; T— Not; Annanat-- From ignorance; Na cha-- Not so; Matasarath-- From envy.\n\nO all present sages, Brahmins, and Agni Deva, listen carefully because I am speaking about etiquette. I am not saying this out of ignorance or jealousy. Meaning: Speaking against Shiva, Daksha, by saying this, wanted to pacify the assembly very cleverly,\nthat he is going to speak about the conduct (etiquette) of a gentleman, because this would cause disturbance in the assembly,\nand people might get upset, because they did not want any\nindecent man to be insulted. In other words, he knew that he was speaking against Shiva,\nwho was pure in conduct.\nAs far as envy and malice are concerned, he already harbored hatred towards Shivji,\ntherefore he did not let his hatred be revealed. Although he was speaking in such a way as if he did not know anything,\nbut by saying that he was not speaking out of any enmity, he wanted to hide his words. This one is the TREAT of the guardians of the worlds, without fault.\nHe is the one who is stained by the path of good conduct, by whose path he is stunned. 10>. Meaning of the word\n\nAyam-- this (Shiva); tu- but; lokapalanam-- protector of the world; yashah-- famous; ghnah- destroyer; nirvatapah-\nfrets; sadhahi- by the virtuous; acharitah- followed; panthah- path; yena- by whom; stabdhena- without conduct;\ndushtah- wicked; lajjah- shameful..\n\nShiva has mixed the names and fame of the Lokpalakas in the dust and has\ncontaminated the path of good conduct. Due to being timid, he does not know how to behave.\nAMET\n\nMeaning: Daksha wanted to show all the saintly men gathered in that assembly that even being a god"
