          "textEn": "37\nGrihitva--by taking; Mrig-shav--like a deer cub; Akshya--with eyes; Pani-m--hand; Markat--monkey;\nLochana--with eyes; Pratyutthan--getting up from one's seat; Abhi--respectful salutation; Arha--like a vessel; Mu-with; Api--also; Akruta--not done; Yogya--appropriate; Sammana--respect.,\n\nEven though his eyes are like a monkey's, he has married my maiden with eyes like deer.\nHe has done. Even then, he did not welcome me by getting up, nor did he greet me with sweet words.\nHe considered it appropriate. Luktakriyasuchaye manine bhinsetve. Even if unwilling, he should protect the girl and the Shudra. 13. Meaning of the word\n\nLupt-kriya-- without observing etiquette; Ashuchi-- impure; Manine-- arrogant; Bhinna-setve-- breaking all watered;\nKarke-- unwillingly; Api-- although; Adam-- given; Balam-- one's daughter; Shudray-- to Shudra; Iva--\nLike; Ushtim-giram- the message of the Vedas.\n.\n\nI had no desire to offer my daughter to this person who violated all the rules of etiquette.\nDue to not following the desired procedures, this impure\nOh, but I was bound to offer my daughter in the same way as a\nShudra has to be taught the Vedas. Meaning: It is forbidden to teach the Vedas to a Shudra because he is not worthy of listening to such teachings due to his impure habits. Until one is endowed with Brahmin-like qualities, one cannot read the Vedas.\nThe restriction is the same as that a student of Vidhi is not allowed to enter the Vidhi school until he has passed the lower classes. In the eyes of Daksha, Shiva\nwas of impure conduct and was not worthy of marrying his daughter, because his daughter\nSati was extremely knowledgeable, beautiful, and virtuous.\nIn this context, the word 'bhinsetve' has been used, which\nmeans such a man who, without following Vedic rules, has broken all kinds of regulations. In other words, according to Daksha, the marriage of his daughter with Shiva was entirely\ninappropriate."
