          "textEn": "38\nIn the ghosts, in the darkness, surrounded by ghostly beings,\nWith a body like a wild elephant, with a smiling face, with a hairy body,\nWith ashes on the pyre, with a bath, with the ornaments of a ghost,\nAnd with the posture of a ghost. 94. Shivapadshayo hyashivo matto mattajanapriyah. Husband: Of the luminaries of the Supreme Being. 15>. Meaning of the word\n\nPret-aavaseshu--in the cremation ground; TY—terrible; Pratayah—by the dead; Bhut-ganaih--by the ghosts; Vrutah—being surrounded; Atati--\nwandering; Unmatta-vat--like a madman; Nagnah—naked; Vyupta-kesha--with disheveled hair; Hasan--laughing; Rudan--\nlamenting; Krita-snana--bathing; --Bathing (as in applying); Preta--shroud of corpses; Srak--garland;\nNri-asthi-bhushanah--Ornaments made of dead men's bones; Shiva-apad-nisa--Shiva, the auspicious one; Hi--\nbecause; Ashivah--inauspicious); Matthah--distressed; Mattah-jan-priya--beloved of the disturbed; Patih--husband, master;\nPramatha-nathanaam--lords of the wandering wives; Tamah-matra-atmata-atmanam--of all these Tamoguna; of all.\n.\n\nHe lives in dirty places like cremation grounds, and ghosts and spirits live with him. He remains naked like a madman, sometimes laughs, and sometimes shouts, and the ashes of cremation ground\nare wrapped around his entire body. He does not even bathe properly. He adorns his body with a garland of skulls and bones. Therefore, he is Shiva only by name, otherwise he is extremely\nmad and an inauspicious being. He is only dear to the Tamasic and intoxicated people and is the leader of them. Meaning: Those who do not bathe regularly are considered to be in the company of ghosts and mad people. Shivji seemed like this, but his name Shiv is very appropriate, because he is very compassionate towards such people\nwho are in the deep darkness of Tamoguna - such unclean drunkards who\ndo not even bathe regularly.\nLord Shiva is so merciful that he provides refuge to such beings\nand gradually takes them up to spiritual consciousness. Although it is difficult to lift such people to the level of spiritual knowledge,\nLord Shiva himself takes responsibility for this, that is why,\nas it is said in the Vedas, Shivji is all auspicious. In this way, with their company, such fallen\nsouls can also rise up. It has sometimes been observed that the offering to a great man is for the benefit of fallen souls,\nnot for their personal gain. In this creation of God, there are many kinds of creatures.\nHmm. Some of them are endowed with Satoguna, while some are endowed with Rajoguna and Tamoguna. Lord Vishnu takes the burden of such\npeople, who are Krishna-conscious, advanced Vaishnavas. God Brahma Sakam Karma"
