          "textEn": "42\nNandishwaro Roshakshayadushitah.\nDashay Visasarj Darunam.\nYe Chanvamodamstadavachyam Dwijah. 20>. Meaning of the word\n\nKnowing--Knowing; Shapam--curse; Girish--of Shiva; Anuga-Agrahinah--one of the chief associates; Nandishwarah--Nandishwar; Rosh--Anger; Kashaya--Red; Dushitah--Tainted; Dakshayah--To Daksha; Shapam-Curse; Visasarj--Giving; Darunam--Harsh; Ye--Those who;\nChach--And; Anvamodan--Bearing; Tat-Avachyatam--Giving a curse to Shiva; Dwijah--Brahmin; L\nKnowing that Lord Shiva has been cursed, Nandishwar, the chief associate of Shiva,\nbecame extremely angry. His eyes turned red, and he, along with Daksha and all the Brahmins present there,\nwho had endured Shiva being cursed by Daksha with bitter words,\nprepared to give a curse. Meaning: Some newly initiated Vaishnavas and Shivas have been having a long-standing disagreement; they always\nkeep clashing.\nWhen Daksha cursed Shiva with bitter words, some Brahmins present there\nmust have enjoyed it because some Brahmins do not like Shiva very much. They do this because they do not understand the state of Shiva. Nandishwar was affected by this curse, but he did not imitate Lord Shiva present there. If Shivaji wanted, he could have cursed Daksha in the same way.\nHe could have, but he remained calm. But his follower Nandishwar could not bear it. Undoubtedly,\nbecause of being a follower, it was appropriate that he did not tolerate the slander of his master, but he\nshould not have cursed the Brahmins present there. Because of this, the whole matter got so entangled that those who\nwere not more powerful forgot their positions, and in this way, curses and counter-curses continued in that assembly. The meaning is that the physical world is so unstable that Nandishwar, Daksha, and\nmany Brahmins were not unaffected by the atmosphere of anger.\nO Lord, having manifested this mortal world,\nHe is ignorant, separate, and averse to the essence. 29). Lexicon"
