          "textEn": "43\nYah--He who is (the) Daksha--This mortal--Of this perishable body--Uddishya--In the context--Bhagavati--To Shiva--Apritrudhhi--Those who are not joyful--\nDruhyati--One who is hateful; Agyah--One with little intelligence; Prithak-drishti--Dualistic perception; Tatvat--From divine knowledge; Vimukh--Without;\nBhavet--Will become;\nDhruva--Firm;\nDhruva--Firm;\nDhruva--Firm;\nDhruva--Firm;\nDhruva--Firm;\nDhruva--Firm;\nDhruva--Firm;\nDhruva--Firm;\nDhruva--Firm;\nDhruva--Firm;\nDhruva--Firm;\nDhruva--Firm;\nDhruva--Firm;\nDhruva--Firm;\nDhru.\n\nWhoever, considering Daksha as the best man, has disrespected Lord Shiva,\nHe is of little intelligence, and due to his foolishness, he will become devoid of divine knowledge. Meaning: Nandishwar's first curse was that whoever is supporting Daksha, out of ignorance, considers his body as everything, and since Daksha did not have divine knowledge, therefore, by supporting him,\nhe too will become devoid of divine knowledge. Nandishwar said that Daksha, like other materialistic people,\nhas considered his body as everything and is trying to attain all physical comforts.\nHe has excessive attachment to his body, his wife, children, home, and other such things that are different from the soul.\nHe has excessive attachment. Therefore, Nandishwar's curse was that whoever supports Daksha will be divine\ndevoid of self-knowledge, and in this way, he will also be averse to the knowledge of the Supreme Personality of Godhead. In houses, in rituals, and in worldly pleasures,\nHe who is engaged in the system of action, is deluded by the Vedas. 22). Meaning of the word:\n\nIn houses - in household life; in deceitful - in deceitful religiousness; Teh: - by being attached; by desire for material pleasures; Karma-Tantram: - action-desire; Vitnute: - does; Veda-vad: - from the explanation of the Vedas; Vipanna-dhi: - destroyed-wit;..\n\nIn which deceitful religious household life, a person remains attached to material pleasures\nAnd at the same time, is attracted to the futile interpretation of the Vedas, in this, all his intellect is lost\nAnd he becomes completely engrossed in desire-driven actions.\nMeaning: Those who consider their physical existence as everything, they remain attached to the actions done with desire, as described in the Vedas. For example, it is mentioned in the Vedas that one who observes the Chaturmasya Vrat will\nhave eternal happiness in heaven. It is said in the Bhagavad Gita that this ornate language of the Vedas\nmostly attracts those men who consider themselves to be embodied. For such people,\nHeavenly bliss is all happiness; they do not know that beyond this, there is the world of the self or the divine abode.\nAnd one can go there. Thus, they are deprived of divine knowledge. Such people's family life"
