          "textEn": "55\nThe destruction of the yajna did not happen because of it. The sages kept on doing their work. In other words, it can be assumed\nthat even if someone does not worship gods like Shiva and Brahma, they can still please God. In the Bhagavad Gita (7.20) it is also confirmed - Kamartheertha-jnanaha.\nPrapadyantevyadevataha. Those who are motivated by work and desire go to the gods to benefit. Nasti buddhi in the Bhagavad Gita: There is a subtle use of the word, which means, \"*Those persons\nwho have lost their wisdom or intellect.\"Only such people care about the gods and want to go to them and take advantage of them. Undoubtedly, it does not mean that we should not respect the gods.\nBut there is no need to worship them. An honest person is loyal to the government,\nbut he does not need to bribe government employees. Bribery is illegal.\nIf a government employee does not give a bribe, it does not mean that he does not respect him.\nNo. Similarly, the person who is absorbed in the divine love and devotion of God, he neither needs to worship any deity,\nnor does he have any tendency to disrespect any deity in any way. It is said elsewhere in the Bhagavad Gita (9.23)-- Yadapi yadvadevatah bhakta yajante shraddhayanvitaah.\nBhagavan says that if someone worships the gods, then he also worships me, but he\nworships without proper means, that is, without following the prescribed rituals. The rule is that God\nshould be worshipped.\nThe worship of deities, SI: God can be worshiped, but this\nwill not be said in a prescribed manner. By worshiping God, all deities are automatically worshiped,\nbecause they are parts of the supreme being. If someone waters the root of a tree, then all parts of the tree--such as\nleaves and branches--are automatically satisfied. Similarly, if someone provides food to the stomach, then\nall parts of the body--hands, feet, fingers--receive food. In this way, by worshiping Shri Bhagwan,\nall the gods can be pleased, but by worshiping all the gods, the worship of God\nis not complete. The worship of deities is forbidden and violates the classical rules. In this dark age, it is impossible to perform a divine sacrifice. Therefore, for this age, in the Shrimad Bhagavatam"
