          "textEn": "63\nThere--there; Svasu--one's own sisters; Me--mine; Nanu--certainly; Bhartha-sammita--with their respective husbands; Matri-svasu--mothers; Drakshye-I will see; Chir-Utkantha-Mana--longing for a long time;\nMaha-Rishibhih--by great sages; Unniyamanam-lifted up; Cha-Tatha; Mrud-Hey Shiva; Adhvar-Yagya; Dhwajam-flag..\n\nThere, my sisters, aunts, and other dear relatives will be gathered; therefore, if I go there,\nI will meet all of them, and also I will be able to see the flying flags and the yagya (sacrificial fire) being performed by the sages. Hey dear, that's why I am eager to go. Meaning: As has been said before, tension remained between the father-in-law and the son-in-law for a long time.\nTherefore, Sati could not go to her father's place for a long time, often. That is why she was very eager to go to her father's\nhome (mother's house), especially on such an occasion when all her\nsisters, her brothers-in-law, and her aunts would have gathered there. As it is natural for every woman, she too wanted to wear a veil like her other sisters and go with her husband. Undoubtedly, she did not want to go alone. You are the wonder, the unborn, the mother, the cause, the three qualities. Although I am a Yogi, I know the essence of that.\nI see the world as a treasure for the poor. 11. Meaning of the word:\n\nTvayi-- in you; Etat-- that; Aashcharyam-- astonishing; Aaj-- this Shiva; Atma-mayaya-- from the external power of God;\nVinyastam-- created; Bhaati-- appears; Guna-traya-atmakam-- due to the interaction of the three qualities of nature; Tathapi- even so; Aham-- I; Yoshit- woman; Atatatva-vit- unaware of truth; Cha-- and; Tene-- yours; Dina- poor, distressed;\nDitiksha- wishes to see; Bhav- Hey Shiva; May- my (respectful); Bhav- birth-place..\n\nThis visible world is the inner interaction of the three Gunas or the wonderful creation of the external power of God.\nIs. You're well aware of this reality. But I am a helpless woman, and as you\nknow, I am unaware of the truth. Therefore, I once again want to see my birthplace. Meaning: Dakshayani Sati knew very well that her husband, Shivji, was not very interested in the material world's\nglitter and glamour, which is produced by the interaction of the three qualities of nature. Therefore, she has addressed her husband as 'Aja,' which means, one who is beyond the bonds of birth and death,\nor one who has experienced their eternal state. He said, \"In you this"
