          "textEn": "67\nIt maintains balance. Therefore, here this question can be raised that why was a liberated man like Shiva so unhappy with Daksha's\nwords? The answer has been given by Srila Viswanath Chakravarti Thakur. Lord Shiva\nis self-controlled, but since he has been entrusted with the burden of Tamoguna, therefore, he sometimes remains affected by the pleasures and sorrows of this material world. The difference between the pleasures and\nsorrows of this material world and the spiritual world is that in the spiritual world, his influence is beyond the three Gunas. Therefore, in the supreme world, someone\nmay be sad, but the expression of this so-called sorrow (suffering) is always blissful. For example, once when Lord Krishna was a child, Mother Yashoda tapped him on the head, and he began to cry. Although tears fell from his eyes,\nthis should not be considered the effect of the quality of darkness, because this event is from divine bliss\ncompletely off. Krishna was performing various types of divine plays. Sometimes it seemed that they were tormenting the Gopis,\nbut such behavior was imbued with divine bliss.\nIn the physical and spiritual world, this is the difference. In the spiritual world, everything is pure, but in the physical world, it appears distorted. Because everything in the spiritual world is complete (param), therefore, in happiness and sorrow, besides joy,\nthere is no other experience, but in the physical world, every object is contaminated by the qualities of nature, therefore,\nthe experience of sorrow and happiness continues. That is why, even being a self-realized man, Shiva became sad\nbecause of bearing the burden of the quality of darkness. Shri Bhagavan said:\nThat which is augmented by you is the best of augmentations.\nEven uninvited, they fear among relatives.\nThey are not produced by anything.\nThey are not superior to the self. 16). Meaning of the word\nShri-Bhagavan said-- God said; Tvaya-- by you; Uditam-- said; Sh