          "textEn": "69\nEven a snake wearing a gem is terrifying because it is a snake. The serpent is jealous of other creatures of nature,\neven if they are not innocent. When a snake bites another creature, then it\nis not necessary that the creature is guilty. The habit of snakes is to bite innocent creatures. In this way,\nthough Daksha was rich in all kinds of material possessions, he was proud of his wealth and\nhe was jealous, therefore all these qualities had been corrupted. Therefore, sometimes having such property\nbecomes an obstacle for a person progressing in self-awareness or Krishna consciousness.\nWhile praising Shri Krishna, Float called him Akinchan Gochar, which means all\nThose who are devoid of wealth and possessions, easily attain. To progress in Krishna's nectar,\nthe absence of material possessions is beneficial in a way-- a person who is conscious of their eternal relationship with Shri Bhagavan\ncan take advantage of their material possessions, such as knowledge, beauty, and nobility,\nfor the service of God, and then such wealth becomes praiseworthy, O. In other words,\nuntil someone is filled with devotion to Krishna, all their wealth remains zero, but when\nthis zero becomes attached to God, its value increases tenfold. Until the zero is attached to God,\nthe zero remains a zero, no matter how many zeros one keeps, its value will remain zero. Until a person's wealth is invested in devotion to Krishna, it creates a stir and\ndrives the collector down.\nOf the luminaries, dependent on their own relatives,\nAnd situated in the house, without being known.\nThey, having approached, are turned away by the crooked-minded.\nBy the arrogant, angry-eyed ones. 18>. Meaning of the word\nN--No; Etahshanam--in this manner; Sw-jan--related to; Vyakshepaya--depending on; Grihan--in the house; Pratiyat--\nshould go; Anavasthit--disturbed; Atmanam--mind; Ye--which; Aryagatan--guest; Vakra-dhiya--with disrespect;\nAbhi-chakshate--while looking; Aropit-bh