          "textEn": "88\nIf attraction towards Vishnu is not developed through these actions, then all the effort is meaningless. He who has generated love for Vishnu,\nshould develop love and respect for Vishnu's devotees as well. Shiva is considered as the first Vaishnava among the Vaishnava sampradhaya. Vaishnavas as Shambhava. Therefore, when Sati saw that\nher father was performing a great yagya (sacrifice), but she had no respect for the supreme devotee Shiva in her heart,\nshe became extremely angry. This is correct, if Vishnu or Vaishnav is insulted, then a person should\nbe angry. Lord Chaitanya Mahaprabhu, who taught the lesson of non-violence, humility, and modesty,\nwhen he heard that Jagai and Madhai had insulted Nityananda, he became extremely angry and wanted to\nkill them with his life. When a Vishnu or Vaishnava is insulted, a man must be angry. Narottamadas Thakur said-- Anger is a devotee-destroyer.\nWe have anger, and this anger can become a great virtue,\nif its use is made on that person who is an enemy of God or his devotees. If\nsomeone is attacking a Vishnu or Vaishnav, then this should not be tolerated. Sati's anger towards her father\nwas not objectionable, because although he was her father, he was the best Vaishnav\nand was insulting. Therefore, Sati's anger towards her father was praiseworthy. Devyuvaacha\nNa yasya lokaistishayanah priya-\ntathaapriyo dehabhrutam priyatmanah.\nThose who are free from attachment and aversion,\nWho are always engaged in the creation, what is the use of opposition? 99. Meaning of the word\nDevi Ubacha--The Goddess said; Na-not; Yasya-whose; Loke-in this world; Asti-is; Atishay-excessive; Pratidwandi-rival; N-rival; Priyah-beloved; Tasmin-of that; Shiva-to Shiva; Samasta-all; Atmani-in the self; Dehabhrataam-in the body; Priya-beloved; Atmanah-of the self; Atyant-extremely; Prati-against; Vaira-rival; Mukta-free; Vairak-rivalless; Rite-extra; Bhavantam-your; Katamah-which; Prati-against; Prati-against; Priyah-beloved; Tasmin-of that; Shiva-to Shiva; Samasta-all; Atmani-in the self; Dehabhrataam-in the body; Priya-beloved; Atmanah-of the self; Atyant-.\nDevi Sati said: Lord Shiva is very dear to all living beings. She has no rivals. Neither is anyone very dear to them, nor is anyone their enemy. Besides you, who else is there who will hate such a universal soul, who is free from all animosity? Meaning: The Bhagavad-gita (9.29) says: I am the support of all beings"
