          "textEn": "90\n\nEven among extraordinary great men, there are categories, and the best man is one who, considering someone's insignificant\nspecialty, enhances that quality. Lord Shiva is also called Ashutosh, because\nHe becomes pleased immediately and gives the biggest blessing to anyone. For example, once a\ndevotee asked Shivji for this boon that whomever's head he touches, that person's head should separate from the torso. Shivji agreed. Although the boon asked by the devotee was not praiseworthy, because\nhe wanted to kill his enemy, but Lord Shiva, considering the devotion and worship of the devotee as his\nquality, granted him the boon. Thus, Shiva accepted his vice as a virtue. But Sati,\ncondemning her father, said, ** You are completely the opposite of this. Although Shiva has many qualities,\nand there is not a single fault in them, but you have considered them inferior and extracted their faults.\nBecause of considering their qualities as faults, you have become extremely fallen, not becoming a great soul.\nMan becomes great only when he accepts the qualities of others, but you have made yourself the most fallen by considering the qualities of others\nas bad.This is the wonder that is always there.\nIt is not inferior to the greatness of the self-realized.\nIt is like the great feet of the great men,\nIt is the radiance that destroys the radiance. 13. Meaning of the word\n\nN-- No; Aashcharyamu-- astonishing; Etat-- this; Yat-- that; Asatsu-- evil; Sarvada-- always; Mahat-- insulting; Atma-vadishu-- those who consider the body as the self; Sa-inhyam-- this; Maha-purusha-- of great men;\nPad-pansudih-- from the dust of the feet; Nirasta-tejahsu-- diminished radiance; Tat-- that; Ev-- certainly; Shobhanam-- most excellent,\nThose who have considered this mortal body as the self, It is not surprising for them to criticize great men. For materialistic men, such jealousy is quite alright\nYes, because this is how their downfall happens. They attai