          "textEn": "28\nThen, the all-pervading one, the radiant one, said to the skilled one.\nFor the protection of that, she was not born there, but she was made to be adorned. 4>. Meaning of the word\nTat- events described by gods and others; Akanhyam- by listening; Vibhuh- by Brahma; Praah- answered; Teejayasi- great man;\nKrut- Agasi- offense committed; Kshemay- for one's own well-being; Tat- that way; Sa- that; Bhuyat- not; T- not good;\nPrayen- generally; Bubhushtam- desire to live; Rahi- to live..\nWhen Brahma heard everything from the gods and the members participating in the Yagya,\nhe replied, \"If you insult a great man and disrespect his lotus feet,\nyou can never be happy by performing Yagya.\" You cannot attain happiness in this way.\nYou cannot.\nMeaning: Brahma told the gods that although Daksha wanted to enjoy the fruits of desire-driven Yagya-karma,\nhe could not enjoy it when someone insults a great man like Shiva.\nThis\nSo it was good for Daksha that he was killed in the war, because if he had lived, he would repeatedly\ninsult the lotus feet of the great men. According to Manu's rules, if a person commits murder,\nthen the punishment is in his interest, because if that does not happen, then by committing murders repeatedly,\nhe will remain in bondage for many births in the future. Therefore, the murderer deserves the royal punishment. Those who are extremely sinful, if they are killed by the grace of God, then it is good for them.\nIt is. In other words, Brahmaji told the Devtas that Daksha's death was good.\nTherefore, may you be free from the suffering caused by actions.\nThose who are divided by the burden of worldly existence,\nMay they be blessed with a pure mind.\nMay they quickly accept the offering of the feet. 5. Meaning of the word\nAthapi--even then; Yuyam--you all; Krut-kilbishah--by committing sin; Bhavam--to Shiva; Ye--you all; Barishah--of the Yagya\nKa; Bhag-bhajam praapya bhag; Paraduh - separated from; Prasadadhvam - may you all be pleased; Parishuddh-chetasa - without\nany hesitation; Kshipra-prasadam - immediately; Kinda - taking refuge in the lotus feet.\nYou people, by depriving Shiva of receiving the Yagya-bhag, have deprived him, therefore all of you are his"
