न्‌ की",
          "textEn": "54\n\nDoes not display his valor.\n\nMeaning: It is said that the ornament of a ascetic is forgiveness. In the spiritual history of the world,\nthere are many such examples when many saintly men were unnecessarily afflicted, but even being capable,\nthey did not react. For example, Parikshit Maharaj was unnecessarily cursed by a Brahmin boy,\nand although the boy's father expressed regret, Maharaj Parikshit\naccepted the curse and agreed to die within a week according to the wishes of the Brahmin boy. Maharaj Parikshit was an emperor and was full of physical and spiritual powers, but because of respect and compassion towards the Brahmin\ncaste, he did not stop the actions of the Brahmin boy, but rather accepted to die within seven days.\nBecause Lord Krishna wanted that Parikshit Maharaj should accept this punishment\nso that the teaching of Shrimadhudagvat should be revealed in the world, as a result Parikshit Maharaj was advised\nthat he should not take any revenge. A Vaishnava is tolerant for the benefit of others. When he does not display his\ncourage, it does not indicate that he lacks power, but rather\nit shows that he is tolerant for the welfare of the entire human society. May the Supreme Being, through me,\nMay the one who is always eager, be pleased.\nBy killing him, you are self-controlled,\nYou are desirous of bestowing grace, O Lord. 49>. Meaning of the word\n\nBhavan-- you; Tu-- but; Punasah-- man; Parasya-- supreme; Mayaya-- by material power; Durantaya-- of excessive power;\nIY-- unaffected); Mati-- intellect; Samasta-daksha-- one who sees or knows everything; Taya-- by that Maya; Hata-\nAtmasu-- in the heart, deluded; Anukarmaschitah- those whose hearts are attracted by desire-driven actions; Anugraham- grace; Katum- to do; Kartum- to make\nTake; in this context; desirous of; pray; Lord.\n\nOh Lord, you are never deluded by the enchanting influence of the Supreme Being's illusion. Therefore, you\nare omniscient, and those who are deluded by the same Maya and are excessively engrossed in desire-driven actions, be\nmerciful and compassionate towards them.\n\nMeaning: A Vaishnava is never deluded by the influence of external powers, because he is of God"
