          "textEn": "59\nChapter seven\nTo perform the Yagya by Daksha\nMaitreya said\nBy renouncing, by not listening, one becomes satisfied. Abhyadhayi Mahabaho Prahasya Shruyatamiti. 9>. Meaning of the word.\n\nMaitreya:--Maitreya said; Uvacha--said; Iti--thus; Ajena--by Brahm; Anunitena- calmed down; Bhavena--by Shiva;\nBeing fully satisfied; Abhyadhayi--said; Maha-baho--Oh Vidura; Prahasya--laughing; Shrutayam- listen; Iti--this.\nThus..\n\nMaitreya Muni said: O mighty-armed Vidura, Shiva, pleased by the words of Lord Brahma,\nresponded to their prayer in this way.\nMahadeva said,\nI do not describe the children of Prajapati, nor do I contemplate them.\nI hold the punishment of the gods who are enveloped in the divine illusion. 2>. Meaning of the word:\n\nMahadevah--Shiva; Uvacha--said; Na- no; Agham--sin; Praja-Ishah--Oh Lord of the people; Balanam--of the children; Varnyey- I treat; Abhiguhya- I consider; Dev-Maya--Of the external power of God; Abhiguhyatam--by them;\nDandah-stick; Tatra-there; Dhrutah-used; Maya-by me. Because these gods are as ignorant as children, therefore I am not seriously considering their offenses. I punished them only to bring them to the path, huh? Meaning: Punishment is of two types ©. One is that which the victor gives to his enemy, and the other\nis that which the father gives to his son. There is a world of difference between the two types of these bones.\nLord Shiva is naturally a Vaishnav, and therefore he is called Ashutosh. He is always pleased.\nTherefore, he was not even slightly angry like an enemy. They are not enemies of anyone, but rather, they are well-wishers of everyone. Whenever they punish someone, they do it in the same way that a father punishes his son.\nPunishes. Shivji is like a father, because he does not take the offenses of any soul, especially of the gods,\nseriously."
