          "textEn": "60\nMay the head of the one who is burnt by the fire of the sun be burnt, and may the head of the friend be burnt, and may the share of the friend be burnt. 3). Shabdartham\nPrajapatih -- of Prajapati Daksha; Dagdhasirshah -- one whose head has been burnt to ashes; Bhavatu -- let it be; Aja-mukham -- of a goat; Mukham -- face; Shirah-head; Mitrasya- of a friend; Chakshusha- with eyes; Ikshet- see; Bhagam- part; Swam- own; Barishah- of a Yagya\nKa; Bhagah- part; Bhag..\nShiva further said: Since Daksha's head has already been burnt to ashes, therefore he will\nget the head of a goat. The deity Bhag will be able to see his share of the Yagya from the eyes of Mitra. Pusha, may it be offered to the sacrificer, may it be eaten by the pounded offering.\nThe gods, all-pervading, offer this remnant. 4>. Shabdarman\nPusha-- Pusha; Tu-- but; Yajamanasya-- of the sacrificer; Dadhi:-- with teeth; Jakshatu-- chew; Pisht-bhuk-pisht-bhuk-- food of flour;\nDevaah-- Gods; Prakritah-- created; Sarva-agnah-- all knower; Ye-which; Me-to me; Uchchhrashanam-the offering; Dadu: given.\n.\nPushadev will chew with the teeth of his disciples, and if he wants alone, he will have to be satisfied by eating a dough made of sattu. But those deities who have accepted to give my share of the Yagya,\nwill recover from all kinds of injuries.\nMeaning: Pushadev had to depend on his disciple to chew, otherwise he would get to eat a dough of gram flour. In this way, his punishment continued. He could not use his teeth for eating, because he laughed at Shivji and made fun of him by showing his teeth.\nHe made fun of them. In other words, it was better to not let him have teeth because of using teeth against Shiva.\nThose who are with arms and horses, with hands and horses,\nMay they be the sacrifices of the gods, and may the ears of the gods be filled with the fragrance of the sacred ash. 5>. Shabdartham\nBahubhyam--with two arms; Aschinoh--of the Ashini Kumara; JOT:—of Pusha; Hastabhyam--with two hands; Krit-bahvah--those who desire; Bhavantu-hon; Adhwaryavah--priests; Cha--and; Anya-anya; Bast-Shamshruh-goat's beard; Bhrigu-\nBhrigu; Bhavet-hon-his."
