          "textEn": "64 With a mind, he was adorned, and with a head, he was decorated with Rudra's mark.\nHe was asleep, and with his forehead, he was adorned with the sharp edge. 9>. Shabdartham\n\nSandhiamane- upon being completed; Shirasi- from the head; Dakshah- the king Daksha; Rudra- seen by Rudra; Sadyah- immediately;\nSupte- sleeping; Iva- like; Uttasthau- having gone to sleep; Dadrase- seen; Cha- also; Agrathah- in front; Mrudam- to Shiva..\n\nWhen an animal's head was placed on Daksha's body, Daksha immediately regained consciousness, and\nAs soon as he woke up from sleep, he saw his own Shiva standing. Meaning: Here, this example is given that Daksha woke up, stood up, as if he was awakened from a deep sleep.\nWoke up. It is also called supta evotsthau in Sanskrit. This means that upon waking from sleep,\na person immediately remembers what they have to do.\nDaksha was killed and his\nhead was cut off and burned. His body was lying dead, but by the grace of Shiva, as soon as his\nbody was joined with the head of a goat, Daksha regained consciousness. This indicates that consciousness\nis also individual. In reality, when Daksha's goat's head was attached, Daksha received another body,\nbut because consciousness is individual, he received the same consciousness as before, although his body changed. In this way, the physical structure has no concern with the development of consciousness. Consciousness is taken with the soul\nto the afterlife. In Vedic history, there are many examples of this, such as Maharaj\nBharat, who, after giving up his king's body, received the body of a deer, but his consciousness remained as before. They knew that in their previous life, they were King Bharat, and their body was transferred into the body of a deer,\nbecause at the time of death, they were absorbed in the contemplation of a deer.\nEven with the body of a deer,\ntheir consciousness was as good as that of King Bharat. God, this system is so\nexcellent that if someone's consciousness transforms into Krishna consciousness, there is no doubt that in the next\nlife, even if he attains a different kind of body, he will be a great devotee of Krishna."
