्चित: |",
          "textEn": "77\n\nDaksha, while addressing God, said--Oh Lord, you are beyond all imaginations. You are the supreme consciousness, free from all material illusions. Although you\nappear to be situated in illusion, but you are divine. You are free from physical impurities, because you\nare supremely independent. Ritvij Oochu:\nThat essence, not by our own power, is destroyed by the curse of Rudra.\nThe wise, engaged in action, are devoted to the Lord. This is the characteristic of religion, which is known as the three-fold.\nKnowing this, the divine order is established. 27). Meaning of the word:\n\nRitvijah -- priests; Ouchu -- to begin; Tatvam -- truth; Na -- not; Te- your; Vayam- we all; Ananjana- without any impurity;\nFree from any physical impurity; Rudra- of Shiva; Shapat- from a curse; Karmani- in action; Avagraha- being excessively attached; Dhihyah- of such intellect; Bhagavan- O Lord; Vidamah- know; Dharma- Dharma; Upalakshanam- symbolic; Idam- this; Trich- three;\nVrit- three divisions of knowledge, Vedatrayi; Adharva- that; Akhyam- of name; Gnitam- known; Yat- that; Artham- purpose\n\nTherefore,\nLord Vishnu is addressed here as Anjan (devoid of blemishes). In the Bhagavad Gita (2.42),\nShri Krishna has thus condemned the ignorant Vedic scholars--\n\nYamimam pushpitam kachcha pravadantya vipashchitah."
