          "textEn": "96. Actually, Brahma, Shiva, Indra, and\nFrom the moon to the rulers, presidents, ministers, presidents, and kings of the lower realms, there are many deities.\nAnd anyone can consider them as God, but this is the false ego of material life. In reality,\nVishnu is the Supreme God, but even above him is Shri Krishna, because Vishnu is a part of him. In this verse, its\ndirective is given by the word 'anshanshah', which means a fraction of a fraction. In Chaitanyacharitamrita, there are many verses\nthat explain how the parts of God expand further into parts. As described in the Shrimad Bhagavatam, Vishnu and the individual soul both have countless forms. Forms of Vishnu\nAre called Swangra, and the various parts of the soul. Brahma and Indra, like other gods, have been given high positions due to their good deeds and\npenance, but Vishnu or Krishna are the masters of all.\nIt is said in the Chaitanya Charitamrita-- Ekala Hitha Krishna Aar San Shrutya./ This means that alone,\nShri Krishna is God, and all others, even the elements of Vishnu and the souls, are His servants. Baldev\nare the immediate extension of Krishna. They too remain engaged in the service of Krishna, and ordinary souls\ndo serve. Naturally, everyone is born to serve Krishna. Here,\nthere is an acceptance from the Gandharvas that although gods may consider themselves supreme, in reality, they are not like that. The real excellence is of Krishna. Krishnaastu bhagavan swayam--This is a verse from Shrimad Bhagavatam. Krishna is the only supreme being. Therefore, in the worship of Krishna alone, the worship of all parts is included,\nJust as by irrigating the root of a tree, its branches, leaves, and flowers all get irrigated. Vidyadhara, speak up!\nIn this body, which is the abode of my own purpose,\nhaving made it mine, I am deluded by my own intellect.\nEven if you are attached to worldly pleasures, do not be attached to self-delusion.\nDo not be deluded by the nectar of my story