          "textEn": "206\n\nA person with normal intelligence does not consider the head and other parts of the body separately. Similarly, my devotees do not believe that there is any difference between the omnipresent Lord Vishnu and any object or any living being.\nThey do not believe. Meaning: when a part of the body is ailing, the whole body tends to take care of that part. Similarly, the devotee's compassion for all living beings reveals the devotee's oneness. The Bhagavad Gita (5.18) says-- Panditah samadarshinah, meaning those who are learned, they see every bound soul\nwith equanimity. Devotees are compassionate towards every bound soul, therefore they are called Aryaka-buddhi.\nIt is said. Because devotees are scholars and know that the soul is a different part of God, therefore they teach every\nperson the lesson of devotion to Krishna so that they can live happily. If any part of the body becomes diseased,\nthen the entire attention of the body remains focused on that side.\nSimilarly, devotees, of any such person,\nwho has forgotten Krishna and, as a result, is lost in material consciousness, pay attention. The devotee's\nequanimity lies in this that it helps all the souls to go to the abode of God. He who sees the three qualities of existence, not as separate. He who is in all beings, to them he attains the supreme peace. 54>. Meaning of the word:\n\nOf the three--of the three; of one--of the same nature; who--who; does not see--does not see; certainly--certainly; separate--separate; all-being-soul--of the soul of all beings, of the Supreme Being of Om; know--know; clever--clever;:--He; peace--peace;\nAttains..\n\nGod further said: The man who does not consider Brahma, Vishnu, Shiva, or the souls as separate from the Supreme Brahman,\nand who knows Brahman, only he truly attains peace, not others.\n\nMeaning: In this verse, two words are extremely important. Trayanam is indicative of 'three', in which\nBrahma, Shiva and Vishnu are included.\nBhedam means separation.\" They are three, therefore separate,\nBut at the same time, those three are one. This is a vision of unity and separation together, which is called Achintya-\nBhairabhairab-Tattva. An example is given in the Brahma Samhita that milk and alcohol are the same, together, one\nand different--both are milk, but the alcohol has changed. To achieve true peace, man"
