          "textEn": "207\nIt is necessary that every object and every soul, in which Brahma and Shiva are also included, is distinct from God.\nMeaning. No one is independent. Each of us is a projection of the divine. From this, unity in diversity\nis proven. Although there are multiple avatars, they belong to Vishnu. Every object is the expansion of Vishnu's\npower. Maitreya Ubacha\nAnd the one ordained by the Lord, the lord of Prajapati, Hari.\nHaving made a sacrifice with a vow, he performed it with the gods as witnesses. 55 Meaning of the word\n\nMaitreya:--Maitreya; Uvacha--said; Evam--in this way; Bhagavata--by the Lord; Adhishtaaha--being given; Prajapati-\nPatiha--the head of all Prajapatis; Harim--of Hari; Architva--worshiping; Kratuna--from the Yagya festival; Svain--own; Devan-\nOf the gods; Ubhayatha-separately; Ayajat-worshiped; Ka.\n.\n\nMaitreya Muni said: Having received the order from God in this way, the chief Daksha worshiped Lord Vishnu.\nAfter worshiping him according to the prescribed method for the Yagya festival,\nhe also worshiped Brahma and Shiva separately.\n\nMeaning: Everything should be offered to Lord Vishnu, and his Prasad should be distributed among all the\ngods. Even today, this tradition continues in the Jagannath temple in Puri. Main\nAround the Jagannath Temple, there are many temples of deities, and first of all, the prasad that is offered to Jagannath Ji\nis distributed among all the deities. The worship of 'Bhagalin' is done with the blessings of Vishnu, and in the famous Shiva temple of Bhubaneswar, the blessings of Vishnu or Jagannath Ji are offered to the idol of Shiva. This is the Vaishnava rule. Vaishnavas do not mock even a common creature, even a female serpent,\nbut respect everyone according to their position.\nBut this offering\nis offered with Shri Bhagwan Krishna or Vishnu as the center. The devotee who has become extremely elevated\nsees the relationship of Shri Krishna with every object, he does not see any object independent of Krishna. This is the vision of oneness."
