          "textEn": "208\nRudram cha swane bhage hupadhavat samahitah\nKarmanodavasanena somapanitaranapi. Udavasyasahasvatvigbhih sasnavabhrutham tatah. 56. Meaning of the word.\n\nRudram-Shiva; cha--and; swan--own; bhagen--from a part; hi--because; upadhavat--by worship; samahitah--being absorbed;\nkarmanam--by action; udavasanen- by the act of finishing; somapan-with drink; others-others; api-also; udavasya-\nhaving finished; saha- together; ritvigbhih- with the priests; sasnau- having bathed; avabhrutham- without bathing; tatah-thereafter.,\n\nDaksha, in every way, respectfully worshiped Shiva with the remaining part of the Yagya. After the completion of the Yagya rituals, he satisfied all the other gods and the other people gathered there. Then, along with the priests, after completing all these duties, he took a bath and\nHe was completely satisfied. Meaning: Rudra was worshipped in due manner by the remnants of the yajna.\nYagya is Vishnu, and whatever\nprasad is offered to Vishnu, it is given to everyone, even to Shiva. In this context, the tika of Shridhar Swami is also given to all the gods and\nothers, the remains of the Swain Thagen-Yagya. Therefore, by the power of his own self, he attained the three worlds.\nHaving given his mind to righteousness, he went to the divine abode. 57 Meaning of the word:\n\nTo that (Daksha),--to that (Daksha); Api--he; Anubhavena--through the worship of God; Svain--of oneself; Eva--certainly; Avapta-radhase--having attained perfection; Dharma--in dharma; Eva--certainly; Matim--intellect; Datva--giving; Tridashah--the three; Te-they; Divam--to the heavens; Yayuha-went away..\n\nIn this way, by performing the Yagya rituals and worshiping Vishnu, Daksha was completely on the path of religiousness.\nHe was situated. Along with this, all the gods present in the Yagya blessed him that\n'Be devoted to Dharma' and then they left.\nMeaning: Although Daksha was quite arrogant, he waited for the blessings of the gods.\nOff. Thus, the Yagya (sacrificial ritual) conducted by Daksha was completed peacefully and harmoniously."
