          "textEn": "20\nThis is the Lord, Shambho, skilled in action, the tree of the earth.\nHaving heard from the Bhagavata, I, Uddhava, have risen from the Brihaspati. 60>. Shabdartham\n\nEtat- This; Bhagavatah- Of Bhagavan; Shambhooh- Of Shambhu (Shiva); Karma- Katha; Daksha- Adhvara- Dhuh: The one who destroyed Daksha's\nYajna; Shrutam- Heard; Bhagavataat- From Bhagavata; Shishyat- From disciple; Uddhavat- From Uddhava; Me- By me;\nBrihaspatisha- To Brihaspati..\n\nMaitreya said: O Vidura, I had heard this story of the destruction by Shiva of the Daksha Yagya by the supreme devotee and disciple of Brihaspati, Uddhava.\n\nThis sacred, supremely performed act,\nWhich is the nectar of the waters of the sacrifice of the sinful Kauravas,\nWhich is never heard by men,\nIs washed away by the devotion of the Kauravas. 61). Meaning of the word:\n\nIdam-- This; Pavitram-- Pure; Param-- Supreme; Isha-chestitam-- Divine plays of God; Yashasam-- Fame; Ayusham-- Long life; Jeevakalam; Agh-og-marshanam-- Destruction of sins; Yah-- Who; Nityada- always; Akarnya- unheard; Narah- man;\nListen to this anthem; Dhunoti--washes; Agham--material impurity; Kaurav--these Kuruvanshi; Bhakti-bhataat--with devotion and faith.\nWith\n\nMaitreya Muni said in the end: O Kurunandana, if anyone listens to this story of Daksha-Yagya, which is conducted by Lord Vishnu, with faith and devotion, and then narrates it again, then he will surely\nbecome pure from all the impurities of this world. Thus, under the fourth canto of Srimad Bhagavatam, the seventh chapter, titled \"The Completion of the Yagya by Daksha,\"\nthe meaning of the conclusion of devotion is fulfilled."
