          "textEn": "222\nwas remaining. And Manu, the great father of God,\nWith this great honor, I offer you.\nMay you be free from evil desires,\nMay you be happy, divine, and attain liberation. 29>. Shabdartham\nTatha-- in the same way; Manuh-- the self-existent Manu; Vah-- yours; Bhagavan— venerable; Pitamahah– grandfather (father); Cham– those who; Eka-\nMatya- through devout service; Puru- great; Dakshinah- through charity; Makkhah– by performing yagya; Ita– by worshiping; Abhipheta- attained;\nDurvapam- difficult to obtain; Anyatha- by any other means; Bhomam- material; Sukham- happiness; Divyam- natural; Ath– thereafter;\nApavargyam- liberation..\nSuniti told her son: Your father, Swayambhuva Manu, along with donations and alms, performed great\ngreat yagyas (sacrifices) and with unwavering faith and devotion, he pleased God through worship.\nHe pleased.\nIn this way, they achieved great success in attaining material happiness and later liberation.\nWhich is impossible to attain by worshiping the gods.\nMeaning: The measurement of success in life is done by material happiness in this birth and attainment of salvation in the next birth. Such success is only possible because of God's grace. Here, the word 'ekamatya' used\nmeans to focus the mind on God with unwavering devotion. Such unwavering worship of God\nhas been expressed in the Bhagavad Gita as a devotee. “Which is unattainable from any other Kharot, this also\nIt is said here, \"Daai\". Here, \"Anya Kharot\" means the worship of gods. Here, this is also\nspecial mention that Manu's glory was due to his unwavering faith in the divine service of God. He who,\nfor the attainment of material pleasures, diverts his mind in the worship of other deities, he\nis considered unintelligent.\nEven if someone desires material pleasures, they can worship God with unwavering devotion, and one who is desirous of liberation can also achieve their life goal by worshiping them. You are the refuge of the child, the selfless protector,\nThe one who is pleasing to the seekers of liberation, the one who is the means of food for the deer."
