ट पर सात वन हैं, जो इस प्रकार",
          "textEn": "24]\nIt is a famous forest and there, be pure. By going there, a man reaches closer to the Lord residing in Vrindavan. Meaning: Narad Muni and Dhruva Maharaj's mother, Suniti, both advised Dhruva Maharaj to worship Lord Kau. Now Narad Muni is specifically instructing that, O Lord, how this\nworship will soon bear fruit. They advise Dhuv Maharaj to go to the banks of the Yamuna, where there is a forest named Madhuvan, and meditate and chant. For rapid progress in spiritual life, a devotee receives special benefits from pilgrimage sites. Although Lord Krishna resides everywhere, going to these holy places makes it easier to reach Him.\nBecause great sages live in such places. Lord Shri Krishna\nsays that wherever his devotees sing the praises of his divine pastimes, he resides\nthere. There are many pilgrimage sites in India.\nOf these, Badrinarayan, Dwarka, Rameshwaram and Jagannath Puri are the most prominent. These four shrines are called the Char Dham. Dham means such a place where immediate contact with God\ncan be established. To go to Badrinarayan, one has to go via Haridwar ©. Similarly, other pilgrimage sites\nare, such as Prayag (Allahabad) and Mathura, and among all these, the most important is Vrindavan. Until someone\nis not very elevated in spiritual life, they are advised to stay in such places and practice devotion.\nIt is recommended. But a true devotee like Narad Muni, who is always engaged in preaching work, can serve God anywhere. Sometimes Narad even goes to hell.\nHe is not affected by the hellish conditions, because he remains busy in the most responsible work of devotion. According to Narad Muni,\nVrindavan, which is located in the Vrindavan area of Mathura district, is the most sacred place. Today,\nmany saintly men stay there and worship God.\nIn the Vrindavan area, there are twelve forests, and Madhuvan is one of them. Pilgrims from all over India gather and\nvisit these twelve forests. Five of these forests are located 