          "textEn": "242\nare--Madhuvan, Talvan, Kumudvan, Bahulavan, Kamyavan, Khadirvan and Vrindavan. In these twelve forests,\nthere are various ghats, whose names are as follows--(1) Avimukta, (2) Adhirudh, (3) Grihya-Tirtha,\n(4) Prayag-Tirtha, (5) Kankhal, (6) Tindak-Tirtha, (7) Surya-Tirtha, (8) Varaswami, (9) Dhruva-\nGhat (where there are many fruits and flowering trees). This place is famous because here, Dhruva Maharaj performed penance on a high platform), (10) Rishi-Tirtha, (11) Moksha-Tirtha, (12) Buddha-Tirtha, (13) Gokarna,\n(14) Krishna-Ganga, (15) Vaikuntha, (16) Asi-Kund, (17) Chatushamudrika Koop, (18) Akrura-Tirtha\n(When Krishna and Balaram were going to Mathura from Akrura's chariot, everyone bathed in this ghat),\n(19) Yakshanik-Vipra-Sthan, (Ro) Kunja-Koop, (21) Raga-Sthal, (22) Manch-Sthal, (23) The place of wrestling and (24) the Dashashwamedha.\nAfter bathing, in the water of the Kalinda river, in the Shiva lake.\nHaving performed the prescribed rituals, he sat in the desired posture. 43). Shabdharam\nHaving bathed--by bathing; Anusvānam--three times; Tasmin--in that; Kalindyāh--in the Kalindi River (Yamuna); Salilē--in water;\nShive--auspicious; Kṛtvā--by doing; Uchitāni--appropriate; Nivāsan--by sitting; Atmanah--yourself; Kalitā-āsanah--asana\n` Narada Muni gave the teaching: O child, you should bathe three times daily in the water of the Yamuna River or in the water of Kalindi,\nbecause this water is auspicious, pure, and clean. After bathing, perform the necessary rituals of Ashtanga Yoga,\nand then sit in a calm posture on your seat. Meaning: It seems that Dhuv Maharaj was already told to practice the eightfold yoga method. This method is explained in our book Bhagavad Gita Yatharup, in chapter six, verses 11 to 15 of the title Sankhya Yoga.\nIn AT Yoga, the mind is stabilized\nand then focused as Vishnu, as described in the next verses.\nHere it is\nclearly stated that Ashtanga Yoga is not any physical exercise, but rather an exercise to concentrate the mind\nwith the form of Vishnu. Before sitting in the asana, as it is said in the Bhagavad Gita, a person should cleanse himself daily\nwith clean or pure water, three times a day. Yamuna's water"
