          "textEn": "255\nFood should be regular (sit-bhuku) for the devotee; such a devotee is the best of\nqualities. He should not pursue any particular food to satisfy his tongue's taste. One should be content by whatever offering is made to God's grace. By the self-manifested character of the self-manifested, with the mind of one's own.\nThe best verse is to be done, with the heart of one's own. 57 Meaning of the word:\n\nSelf-will--by their (God's) supreme will; Avatar--incarnation; Charitai:-- activities; Achintya-akalyaniya--incomprehensible; Nij-\nMaya--by one's own power; Karishyati--does; Uttam-Shlokah--God; Tat--that; Dhyayet--should meditate;\nHridayam-gamam--extremely attractive.\n\nO Dhruva, besides chanting the mantra three times daily and worshiping the idol of Shri Vigraha, you should also meditate on the divine works of the various incarnations of God, which are manifested by their supreme will\nand personal power.\nMeaning: Devotion is specified by the method of listening, chanting, remembering, worshiping, serving, and considering the form as everything.\nDedication, etc. Here, Dhruva Maharaj is advised to not only meditate on the form of God,\nbut also contemplate on the divine pastimes of his various incarnations. The Mayavadi philosophers\nconsider the incarnations of God to be of the level of ordinary souls. It's a terrible mistake. The incarnation of God\nis not bound to act according to the physical laws of nature. Here, the word 'Swechha' (voluntary) has been used to indicate that God manifests from His supreme will. The living being bound by God is bound by the laws of nature according to his actions.\nHe is bound to accept a special body. But when God appears, then those\nthe physical laws of nature cannot be forcefully imposed. They appear as desired by their inner power.\nThis is the difference.\nThe bound soul accepts a special type of body, such as a pig's body, given by the superior power of its actions and material nature. But when Krishna appears in the pig incarnation,\nhe is not a pig in the form of an ordinary animal. Krishna appears in the form of an extremely cosmic boar incarnation,\nwhich cannot be compared to an ordinary pig. His absence and disappearance"
