          "textEn": "264\nTatra- thereafter; Abhisikta- after bathing; Prayatah- carefully; Tamu- that; Upavyah- by fasting; Vibhavareem- with auspiciousness;\nNight; Samahithah- with full attention; Paryacharat- of worship; Rishi- by Narada Rishi; Aadeshena- by order; Purusham- of man..\n\nHere, Dhruva Maharaj, after reaching Madhuvan, bathed in the Yamuna River and that night, with utmost\ndevotion, he fasted. Thereafter, they became engrossed in the worship of God, as described by Narada Muni. Meaning: The importance of this specific verse is that Dhruva Maharaj acted according to the method told by his Guru Maharishi Narad. Srila Vishwanath Chakravarti also has the same teaching that if we\nwant to succeed in returning to the abode of God, then we should act seriously according to the orders of the Guru. It is the only way to success.\nOne should not have even a little worry about attaining Siddhi,\nbecause one who follows the orders of the Guru certainly attains Siddhi. Our only concern should be how to fulfill the Guru's orders. Guru\nIs skilled in giving special orders to each of his disciples, and if the disciple transforms that order into action,\nthen that is the path to success. Triratrante, Triratrante, Kapitthabadarashanah. By self-observation, one should worship Hari. 72 Meaning of the word\n\nThree- three); night-after-night; three- three; night-after-night-- after the night; Kapittha-badar- berries and plums; Ashanah- to eat\nhaving eaten; Atmavritti- to take care of the body; Anusaran-- necessary or minimum; Masam- one month; Ninye- gone;\nArchayan- to worship; Harim- Lord Ka L\n\nDhruva Maharaj, in the first month, for the protection of his body (Nirvaha), only every third day\nate only caterers and plums, and in this way, he continued to advance the worship of God.\nMeaning: Kapittha is a fruit, which is called Kayatha in Hindi. Monkeys eat its fruit mostly,\nHumans eat it less. But Dhruva Maharaj accepted to consume such fruits not for the satisfaction of taste but for the sustenance of the body. The body needs food, but the devotee needs\nthat the tongue should not eat for satisfaction. The Bhagavad Gita commands that to keep the body healthy"
