          "textEn": "268\nNo one is bigger than him. In the Bhagavad-gita too, the Supreme Being says: I am the support of Brahman.\" Many men,\nEspecially the mystic philosophers, consider Brahma to be the greatest all-pervading object, but from this verse\nand from other Vedic literature like the Bhagavad Gita, it is revealed that the refuge of Brahma is God, just as\nthe sun is the refuge of light, the sphere of the sun. Therefore, Srila Jiva Goswami says that the divine form of God is the seed of all greatness, therefore he is Parabrahma. Since Parabrahma was present in the heart of Dhruva Maharaj, therefore he became greater than the greatest. Therefore, from their weight, all the three worlds and the Vaikuntha world\nAll things started to shake.\n\nSahasrata-katva is the ultimate transformation of all the universes, in which all souls are present. Brahma is the refuge of this great-element. In this context, it is said that the Supreme Being is the lord of both the Primal Being and the individual being.\nIt is.\nThe meaning of 'Pradhan' is subtle matter, such as the sky; and the meaning of 'Purush' is the souls that have become trapped in the subtle physical\nworld; these can also be called 'Para Prakriti' and 'Apara Prakriti,' as it is said in the Bhagavad Gita. Krishna is the controller of both these natures, therefore he is the master of the Pradhapa and the Purusha. Even in Vedic mantras, the Supreme Being is referred to as 'antah-pravesah shasta'. This indicates that Shri Bhagwan\nenters every object and has control over every object. From the Brahma Samhita\n(5.65) also confirms this. The internal atom's movement is not only in the universe,\nbut also in every atom. In the Bhagavad Gita (10.42), Krishna also says -- Vishtabhyahayidam\nKritsnam. God enters into every object and regulates it. In his heart, with the constant companionship of the Supreme Being,\nDhruva Maharaj became like Brahma, and in this way, he became the heaviest, causing the entire universe to tremble.\nIn conclusion, a person who always meditates on the divine form of Krishna in his heart\ncan amaze the entire world with his actions. This is the perfection of yoga, as it is\nproclaimed in the Bhagavad Gita (6.47)-- Among all yogis, the devotee is the best,\nbecause he constantly contemplates Shri Krishna in his heart and remains engaged in divine love and devotion. The ordinary yogi is able to perform astonishing physical feats, called ashta siddhi, but the Lord"
