          "textEn": "289\nTiryaku--animals; Nag--trees; Dwija--birds; Siree-ripu-creature; Dev--gods; Daitya--demons; Matya--etc. by; Prakatam--pervaded; Sat-asat-visesham--manifest and unmanifest wombs; Rupam--form; Sthavistham--stable;\nSthavistham--stable; Vaishch-visesham--exalted by material power; Anekamu--many; Karanam--cause; Na--not; Ata--from this; Param--divine; Param--that supreme; Veddhi--I know; Na--not; Yatra-where; Vadah--different arguments..\n\nOh Lord, oh supremely unborn, I know that the various wombs of the souls, such as animals,\nbirds, crawling creatures, deities, and humans, are spread throughout the universe, which are produced from the entire material power. I also know that sometimes they exist in manifest form, and sometimes in unmanifest form,\nBut I have never seen such a supreme form as I am seeing now. Now there is no need to create any\nprinciple.\nMeaning: In the Bhagavad Gita, God says that He is pervading the entire universe, but even though every\nobject depends on Him, He still remains separate. The same thought was expressed here by Dhruva Maharaj. They say that before seeing the divine form of God, they were only familiar with the fee of physical forms,\nwhose number is eighty-four lakhs. The fact is that until devotion to God is not done,\nthe ultimate form of God cannot be understood. This is also confirmed in the Bhagavad Gita (18.55). By devotion, one knows Him; beyond devotion, through no other means can the supreme truth, the Supreme God, be properly known. Here, Dhruva Maharaj compares the initial state of his knowledge with the perfection of knowledge in the presence of God. The work of the soul is to serve. Until he comes to the state of knowing God,\nhe remains engaged in the service of various types of trees, reptiles, animals, humans, gods,\netc.\nThe common experience is that if a human serves a dog, then\nif someone serves plants and vines, if someone serves gods, then someone serves humanity\nor their office, b