          "textEn": "292\nIn the form of, you keep track of all the works of the universe, even then you remain separate and\nare the enjoyers of all sacrifices. Meaning: The argument of atheists against the omnipotence of God is that if God appears and disappears,\nand sleeps and wakes, then what is the difference between the soul and God? Here, Dhuv\nMaharaj is carefully explaining the difference between the existence of the soul and God. He is explaining this kind of\ndifference--God is eternally liberated. Whenever they appear in this physical world, they\nnever get bound by the three qualities of nature. Therefore, they are called Tridev, which means the lords of the three qualities. In the Bhagavad Gita (7.14) it is said--Daivi hyasha gunamayi mama saya duratyaya--All\nliving beings are bound by the three Gunas (qualities) of nature. The external power of God is very strong, but\nGod, the master of the three qualities, is always free from the reaction of these qualities.\nTherefore, they are\npure, as it is said in the Ishopanishad. The sin of the physical world has no effect on God.\nIt does not affect. Therefore, in the Bhagavad Gita, Shri Krishna says that those who are foolish and cunning, they consider me an ordinary human\nbecause they do not know my supreme emotion. Para Bhavam means that they always remain on the Divapada. They are not affected by physical impurities. The other difference between God and the soul is that the soul always remains in darkness. Even though\nHe is situated in the quality of goodness, yet he remains unaware of many things. But with God\nIt is not so. They are the ones who know about the past, present, and future, and whatever happens in the heart of every living being.\nThey are the ones who know that. The Bhagavad Gita confirms this (Vedaham Samatitati 2). God is not a part of the soul.\nThey are not parts; they are the letters of the Supreme Being, while the souls are their different parts.\nThe soul is bound to appear in 