          "textEn": "293\nWe salute the first-born Govind, who has no beginning, while this material creation\nhas a beginning. Vedanta says--Janmadyasya yatah--every object is originated from God, but their birth\ndoes not happen. They are endowed with all six opulences, are unique, are the masters of nature, their intellect is not fragmented in any\nsituation, and even being the caretaker of the entire creation, they remain separate. As it is said in the Vedas (Katha Upatrisadh 2.2.13)-- Titiyo nityanam chetan shretanamanam. God\nis the ultimate caretaker. The soul is the instrument for their service through Yagya because they are the legitimate enjoyers of the fruits of all Yagyas.\nTherefore, every person should engage in their devotion with their life, wealth, intellect, and words. This is the original natural state of living beings. The comparison of the ordinary soul to sleep\nCannot be compared to the Lord's sleep in the causal ocean. In any situation, the comparison of the soul to the supreme\nman cannot be compared.\nBecause the Mayavadi philosophers cannot compromise, they reach the conclusion of nihilism or\nNirgunvad. Those who are opposed to Yasmin, they fall down continuously.\nThe knowledge-giving, various-powerful, and unmanifest origin.\nThe one who is the cause of all beings, the beginning, the first,\nI am the only one without change, the cause of all existence. 16). Meaning of the word\nYasmin- in which; Viparita-gatayah- of opposite nature; Hi- certainly; Anisham- always; Patanti- appear; Vidya-\nAadih- knowledge and ignorance etc.; Various-- Different; Shaktayah-- Powers; Anupuvyat– Constant; Tat– That; Brahma– Brahman;\nVishcha-bhavam– The cause of physical creation; Ekam– One; Anantam– Infinite; Adya– First; Ananda-mattam– Only\nAnandamayah; Avikaram– Unchanged; Aham– I; Prapadye – I bow\nOh Lord, in the manifestation of the formless nature of Brahman, there are always two opposing elements—Knowledge and\nIgnorance. Your various powers are constantly manifested, but the formless Brahman, which is indivisible,\netc., unchanged, infinite, and blissful, is the cause of the physical world.\nBecause you are the same\nNirguna Brahma, therefore I respectfully greet you. Meaning: The Brahma-Samhita states that the radiance of the divine body of Govinda, who is without beginning or end, is the source of all existence. This boundless luminous sphere contains countless universes, each of various kinds, populated by countless realms. Yadhyan"
