ंग में विश्वनाथ चक्रवर्ती ठाकुर ने एक उदाहरण दिया",
          "textEn": "294\nThe supreme being is the root cause of all causes, but their formless light, which is called Brahma, is the immediate cause of the material\nworld. Therefore, Dhruva Maharaj bowed to the formless form of God. He who\nexperiences the formless form, enjoys the imperishable Brahmananda, which here\nis called Atmap-Ananda. Srila Vishwanath Chakravarti Thakur says that this formless form or the form of Brahma is for those individuals\nwho are extremely knowledgeable, but do not understand the individual forms or the diversity of the spiritual world. Such devotees are called Gyan-Mishra devotees, meaning that their devotion is mixed with experiential knowledge.\nIt remains mixed. Since the understanding of the formless Brahman is a partial knowledge of the ultimate truth, therefore, Dhruva Maharaj respectfully\nobeisances. It is said that the formless Brahman is the ultimate reality, the distant realization of the ultimate truth. Although from the outside it appears devoid of divine power,\nin reality, within knowledge and ignorance, many powers remain active in it.\nIt is because of these powers that knowledge and ignorance constantly manifest. In the Isha Upanishad, there is a very beautiful description of knowledge and ignorance. It is said that sometimes, due to ignorance, a person completely considers the ultimate truth to be without attributes,\nbut in reality, with the development of devotion, the understanding of the without-attribute and the with-attribute\ncontinues to develop. The more our devotion develops, the more we reach the ultimate truth.\nWhereas, in the beginning, due to the greater distance, it appears to be formless. Generally, people, being under the control of ignorance or Maya, are neither endowed with knowledge nor with devotion. But when a man becomes wise, then that knowledge is mixed. He reaches the level of a devotee, that is,\nhis love is mixed with knowledge. When he moves forward a little, he realizes that the ultimate truth\nis a man endowed with many powers. Only a learned devotee can understand God and His creative power.\nAs soon as he accepts the creative power of the ultimate truth, then all six\nunderstandings of Shri Bhagwan also become clear. Those devotees who have progressed even further and have attained complete knowledge, they\ncan receive the divine bliss of God. In this context, Vishwanath Chakravarti Thakur gave an example"
