          "textEn": "303\n\nMeaning: This verse suggests that Lord Vishnu gave Dhruva Maharaj his own abode.\nGiven. Their abode is described in the Bhagavad Gita (15.6)-- yad gatva na nivartante taddhama param sama. Even so, by the service of Vishnu's lotus feet, which is the cause of fulfillment.\nHaving attained the liberation from the cycle of birth and death, do not approach the city. 27). Meaning of the word\n\nSah-- He (or she); Api-- Although; Sankalpa-jam-- Desired fruit; Vishnoha-- Of Vishnu; Pada-seva- by serving the lotus feet;\nUpasamitam- attained; Prapya- having attained; Sankalya- of one's firm resolve; Nirvana- satisfaction; Na- not; Atipritah-\nExtremely pleased; Abhyagat- returned; Puram- to one's home.\n\nBy worshiping the lotus feet of the Lord, having obtained the desired fruit of one's resolve,\nBhadra Maharaj was not very pleased. Then he went back to his home. Meaning: According to the teachings of Narad Muni, by worshiping the lotus feet of God with devotion, Dhuv\nMaharaj attained the desired fruit.\nHis wish was that he would surpass his father, grandfather, and great-grandfather\nand attain a more respected position, and although this was a childish resolution, because Dhruva Maharaj was a\nsmall child, Lord Vishnu became so merciful that he fulfilled his wish. Dhruva\nMaharaj wanted such a high abode that no one else in his family had ever attained.\nHo. Therefore, they were granted that world in which God himself resides, and thus\ntheir resolution was fulfilled. Even then, when Dhruva Maharaj was returning home, he was not very happy, because\nAlthough with pure devotion, he did not ask for anything from God, but he had presented his request\ndue to his childish nature. In this way, although God fulfilled their desires, but\nthey were not more pleased than this, but they felt ashamed that whatever they asked from God\nthey should not have asked for.\nVidura said,\nThat supreme position, which is rare, is attained by the one who worships the destroyer of Maya.\nHaving attained this accomplished goal, even in a single birth"
