          "textEn": "3\nThe supreme Vaishnava Narada Muni gave initiation that they should chant 'Om Namo Bhagavate Vasudevaya'. This mantra is of Vishnu.\nThis mantra is, because the one who practices chanting this mantra attains Vishnuloka. Dhruva Maharaj\nis saddened that even after being initiated into the Vishnu mantra by a Vaishnava, he was desiring material gain. This was the second reason for his remorse. Although they received the fruit of the Vishnu mantra from the unconditional grace of God,\nthey are regretting that while practicing devotion, they foolishly\ntried for material gain. In other words, each of us who is engaged in Krishna devotion\nshould be completely free from material desires, otherwise, like Dhruva Maharaj,\nwe too will have to repent.\nThose who are deceived by gods, who are pleased by the taste of alcohol,\nWho accepts the truth of Narada's words, the best of the wicked. 32). Shabdartham\n\nMatih- Buddhi; Vidushita- corrupted, Kalmash- afflicted; Devaih- by the gods; Patadhidhih- by the one who falls; Asadishnidhih-\nBy the intolerant; Yah- I who; Narad- of Narada Muni; Vachah- of the teachings; Tat- that; N- not, Agrahayam- accepted; Kriya- action; Sat- truth; Na- not, Agrahayam- accepted;\nDone; Asat- Tamah- the most wicked..\n\nSince all the gods, who are situated in the higher realms, will have to come down again, therefore they\nare all gateways to attaining my Vishnuloka through devotion. These intolerant gods\nhave destroyed my intellect, and this is the only reason why I could not accept the blessings\nof Narad Muni's teachings! Meaning: Vedic literature mentions many instances where when a man performs rigorous penance,\nthe gods become disturbed and wonder if someone else might take their prominent place in heaven.\nThey know that their place in heaven is temporary, as it is said in the ninth\nchapter of the Bhagavad Gita (kshine gud martyalokam vishanti), all the gods residing in heaven,\nreturn to this earth when their meri