          "textEn": "374\n\nPrayed again for! Meaning: Sometimes it happens that a devotee who is absorbed in the love of God desires some material gain in return for his service. This is not the right way to do Bhakti. Undoubtedly, due to ignorance, a devotee sometimes does such a thing, but Dhruva Maharaj is regretting his such\nbehavior. Swarajya yachato modhyayanmano me bhikshito bata. By the merit of the deeds of God, the obstacles are removed. 25. Shabdartham.\n\nSwarajyam--their devotion; yachchhatat--from God, who was willing to give; maudhyat--from foolishness; manah--prosperity; me--by me; dvitiya--by the second; bhikshitah--asked for; bata--Oh; Ishwarat--from the great emperor; kshinah--diminished; punyena--by good deeds; phalinah--fruited; karanah--by the factory; kshanah--by the moment; adhanah--poor man.\n.\n\nCompletely due to my foolishness and lack of meritorious deeds, I desired material name, fame,\nand prosperity, although God had provided me with his personal service. My situation is like that\nof a poor person, who, when asked to ask for a valuable item, due to ignorance, asked for only a few rice grains. Meaning: In this verse, the word 'Swarajya' (self-rule), which means complete freedom, is extremely\nimportant. A bound soul does not even realize what complete freedom is. Complete freedom means one's own\nis the natural state. The real freedom of the soul, a part of God, is that it always\nremains dependent on God, just as a child plays independently under the care of parents. The meaning of being liberated by the living being is not that it keeps clashing with the obstacles created by Maya,\nbut that it goes to the refuge of Shri Krishna.\nIn the physical world, every individual wants to be completely free from the obstacles created by Maya.\nBy enduring, one wants to be completely independent. It is said to be a struggle for existence. The real\nfreedom is to be reunited in the service of God. Whoever goes to Vekunthlok, Golok, Vrindavan,\nHe serves God with a free spirit. This is complete freedom. Contrary to this, there is physical\nownership, which we mistakenly consider freedom. Many political leaders established freedom"
