          "textEn": "322\nSunitirsya Janani, more dear than life, son. The hidden boat, the ocean, is filled with its touch. 49). Meaning of the word:\n\nSuniti: Suniti, Dhuv Kaasali Mata; Asya: His; Janani: Mother; Praanebhyah: More than the life air; Api: Also;\nPriyam: Beloved; Sutam: To the son; Upaguhya: Hugging the throat; Jahau: Gave up; Adhim: All sorrow; Tat: His body;\nSparsham: Touching; Nirrita: Satisfied.\n\nThe real mother of Dhruva Maharaj, Suniti, embraced her son's tender body,\nbecause he was dearer to her than her own life. In this way, she forgot all the physical sorrow,\nbecause she was very happy. With breasts, from the nipples, with tears, with eyes, with water, Shiva. Then, with those who were anointed, the heroic heroes, for a long time. 50>. Meaning of the word\n\nPayah--milk; Dridhah--strong; Stanabhyam--from both breasts; Susravah--started flowing; Netra-jihah--from the eyes; Salilah--through tears; Shivahi--auspicious;\nTada--at that time; Abhisichyamanabhyam--after bathing; Veera--Oh Vidur; Veera-suvah--of the mother who gave birth to Veera; Muhah--continuously.\nHey Vidur, Suniti was a brave mother. His tears, along with the stream of milk flowing from his breasts,\njoined together and soaked the essence of Dhruva Maharaj's body. This was the most auspicious characteristic. Meaning: When the gods are established, they are bathed with milk, yogurt, and water.\nAnd this ritual is called Abhishek Utsav. ©. In this verse, there is a special mention that the tears flowing from Suniti's\neyes were all-beneficial. The auspiciousness of this consecration festival performed by his beloved mother\nindicated that in the near future, Dhruva Maharaj would receive his father's\nthrone. The story of Dhruva Maharaj's renunciation is that his father forbade him from sitting in his lap, so Dhruva made a firm resolution that until he receives his father's throne\nhe will not come home. Now, this consecration festival performed by his mother indicated that he\nis going to take the place of Maharaj Uttanpad.\nThe word Veera Suvaa in this verse is also important, which means \"heroic mother\" and this"
