          "textEn": "345\nMunay Oochuha\nOttanapada Bhagavastava Sharudhvna\nDevah Kshinotvavanataritihara Vipakshan. Having spoken of the Yamuna, having crossed the darkness,\nThe world is difficult to cross, it is a difficult obstacle, death. 30. Meaning of the word\n\nMunayah uchu: The sages said; Auttanapada: son of Utthanapada; Bhagavan: God; Tava: Yours; Shara: Dhanva: Shar\nThe one who holds the bow named; Devah: God; Kshinoatu: to kill; Avanat: of the surrendered; Aarti: suffering;\nHarah: to take away; Vipakshan: opponents; Yat: of which; Namadheyam: holy name; Abhidhayah: to take; Nishamyah: hearing; Cha: also; Adhah: quickly; Lokah: people; Anjasah: completely; Tarati: crosses; Durlabham: difficult; Adgah: O Dhuv;\nMrityu: to death.. i The sufferings of the devotees.\n\nAll the sages said: O son of Uttanapada, O Yd, the destroyer of the sufferings of your devotees.\nMay TET, the Lord, destroy your terrible enemies.\nThe holy name of God, of God,\nis equally powerful, therefore, by merely chanting and listening to the holy name of God, many people\ncan be protected from terrible death. In this way, the devotee is saved. Meaning: When Dhruva Maharaj was very distressed in his mind by the illusory powers of the Yakshas, at that very moment,\ngreat sages went to him. The devotee has always been protected by God. From them,\nwith their inspiration, they had come to encourage and reassure Dhruva Maharaj that they have no fear,\nbecause they are completely surrendered to God. If, by the grace of God, a devotee, at the time of death, only chants their\nholy name, Hare Krishna, Hare Krishna, Krishna Krishna, Hare Hare, Hare Rama, Hare Rama, Rama Rama, Hare Hare,\nof the six-limbed mantra, then he crosses the ocean of life and enters Vaikuntha. He does not have to be trapped in the cycle of birth and death.\nBecause by chanting the holy name of God, one can cross the ocean of death,\ntherefore, Dhuv Maharaj was certainly able to cross the illusion of the Yakshas, from which at that time his mind\nwas getting distracted, to the extent of being able to cross. Thus, under the four-part Skandha of Srimad Bhagavat, the Bhakti Vedanta meaning of the tenth chapter named \"The War of Dhruva Maharaj with the Yakshas\"\nwas completed."
