          "textEn": "350\n\nWent to give. Meaning: Dhruva Maharaj attacked Yakshas's Puri Alkapuri because\none of those Yakshas had killed his brother. In reality, all the residents were not responsible for the killing of their brother Uttam, because the killing was done by only one Yaksha on) but by the Yakshas\nDhruva Maharaj took serious action after his brother was killed. The war had been declared and\nthe battle was going on. Even in today's time, sometimes it happens, because of one person's mistake,\ndue to which sometimes the entire state is attacked. The father of mankind and the law-maker\nManu has not permitted such collective raids. Therefore, they wanted their grandson not to kill the Yakshas,\nbecause they were not criminals.\nManuruvacha\nAlam Vatsatiroheshen Tamodarayen Pappmana\nYena Punyajananatanavadhisvatmanaagasah. 7/. Meaning of the word\n\nManu said-- Manu said; Alam-- it happened a lot, just; Vatsa-- my child; Atiroshena-- with extreme anger; Tamah-darrena--\nThe path of Anjan; Pampmana-- sinner; Yena- by whom; Punyajanan-- Yakshagana; Etan- these all; Avadhih- killed by you;\nTvam- you; Anagasah- innocent.. It is not good to be angry in vain -- this is the path to a hellish life. Now you are going beyond your limits by killing Yakshas, because they are not really\ncriminals. Meaning: In this verse, the word Atiroshen means, 'out of unnecessary anger'.\"When Dhruva Maharaj\ncrossed the limits of righteous anger, his great-grandfather Swayambhuva Manu came there immediately to save him from committing sinful deeds.\nFrom this, we can understand that killing is not bad, but when it is done in vain,\nor when a person without fault is killed, then such killing opens the path to hell.\nBecause of being a great devotee, Dhruva Maharaj was saved from such sinful deeds. The Kshatriya was permitted to kill to maintain peace and order in the kingdom;"
