          "textEn": "357\nare done and then by mating, they increase in more organisms. This is the law of creation, sustenance, and destruction. In this method, no one should be disturbed by the waves of physical nature. Dhuv Maharaj was being advised indirectly not to be saddened by the death of his brother,\nbecause our relationship with the body is entirely material. The real self, that is, the soul, is neither destroyed\nnor killed by anyone. And it creates creation, preservation, and restraint. The Supreme Self, who is beyond qualities and enemies. 96. Meaning of the word:\n\nEvam--in this way;--Pravartate--becomes;--Sargah--creation;--Sthitih--maintenance;--Sanyamah--restraint;--Pralayah--destruction;--Ev--certainly;--Cha--and;--Tatha--likewise;--Gunah--of qualities;--Vyatikaraat--from mutual action;--Rajah--king;--Maya--by Maya;--Param-Atmanah--of the Supreme Soul.\n.\n\nManu further said: O King Dhruva, by the enchanting material power of God and by the mutual action of the three qualities of material nature, creation, sustenance, and destruction continue to happen. Meaning: First, it originates from the five elements of nature, then it is maintained by the other actions of qualities. When a child is born, the parents immediately arrange for his upbringing.\nThey do. This tendency to raise offspring is not only found in human society, but also in animal societies. Tigers also take care of their cubs, although they have a predatory instinct to eat other animals.\nThere is a natural instinct. The origin, sustenance, and destruction of qualities are inevitable through their mutual interaction. But we should know that all this happens under the supervision of God. In this way,\neverything is happening. Creation is the work of Rajoguna, nurturing is of Satoguna, and destruction is of Tamoguna.\nWe\ncan see that the one who is virtuous lives longer than the one who is passionate or ignorant.\nLiving longer. This means that one who becomes virtuous goes to the higher realm, where\ntheir lifespan increases. Urdhva Gacchanti Satvastah-- great sages, saints, and ascetics, who\nare virtuous, attain the higher realm. Those who are beyond qualities are also virtuous, and they\nAttain eternal life in Vaikuntha."
