          "textEn": "370\nAll the gods, including Brahma, are working under his control, just as a rope is put in the nose,\nthe bull is controlled by its master. Meaning: To declare oneself independent of the supreme regulator is a physical disease. In reality, when we forget the supreme\nregulator and want to establish control over the physical nature, then our physical\nexistence begins. In this world, every individual is making every effort to become the ultimate regulator,\nwhether it is personal, at the national level, social, or in other forms. Dhruva Maharaj\nwas forbidden by his grandfather to wage war because he was worried that Dhruva, due to his personal\nambition, was determined to destroy the entire Yaksha community in battle. Therefore, in this verse,\nby explaining the state of Swayambhuva Manu, the supreme controller, they also want to erase the final part of Dhruva's false desire. The word matyum agatam here is meaningful, which means, \"death and immortality\".\n\"\nIn the Bhagavad Gita, the Lord says, \"I am the supreme time, which takes away everything from the demons.\".\"The task of the demons\nis to remain engaged in life's struggles by becoming the master of nature.\" The demons keep dying repeatedly.\nAnd they remain badly entangled in the material world. Death for the demons, but for the gods,\nthere is nectar, that is, eternal life. Those devotees who constantly serve God, they already\nhave attained immortality, because whatever they do in this life, they will continue to do in the next births as well. They will receive divine bodies, instead of just physical bodies. Unlike demons, they do not have to change their physical bodies. Therefore, God is both death and immortality at the same time. They are for the Asuras\ndeath and for the devotees immortality § . They are the ultimate destination of each and every one, because they are the cause of all causes. Dhuv Maharaj received this advice that without keeping any personal aspirations, one should go to the refuge of God.\nOne might argue why Gods are worshipped? Here, this\nanswer has been given that only ignorant men worship the gods. The gods themselves accept sacrifices with the intention of pleasing God."
