          "textEn": "380\nIncluded. For example, a tree is a form, and when water is poured into the roots of a tree, then\nother forms--leaves, branches, flowers, and fruits--also automatically get watered. Here, this perception of the illusionists, that the ultimate truth is everything, therefore it is formless, becomes nullified. On the contrary, it is confirmed\nthat it is the form of the ultimate truth (it is Sagun) yet it is omnipresent. Nothing is independent of it. I worship Him, who is the cause of the creation of the world, the first cause, the destroyer of the world.\nUnited, separated, with power, with qualities, by the self-illusion. 6. Meaning of the word:\n\nBhajasva-- Serve; Bhajaniya-- Worthy of worship; Adhriim-- Those whose feet are lotus-like; Abhayav-- For salvation from the world;\nBhava-chidham-- That which cuts the knot of material bondage; Yuktam-- Attached; Virahitam-- Separate, detached; Shaktya-- His power; Gunamachya- endowed with three qualities; Atma-mayaya-- With one's unimaginable power;\nTherefore, you fully engage yourself in the devotion to God because He alone\nThey can liberate us from worldly bonds. Even while being attached to their physical power, they\nremain detached from its activities. It is only by the unimaginable power of God that every event happens in this physical world. Meaning: Here, it is specifically mentioned from the previous verse that Dhruva Maharaj should be engaged in devotion. Devotion can never be the formless aspect of God. Whenever the word 'bhajasva' (worship)\ncomes, then there should be a servant, service, and the one being served. God is worthy of service, and the servant is worthy of service.\nAnd the activities done to please Him are service. The second thing that is said in this verse is,\nThat only God should be served, not anyone else. This is confirmed in the Bhagavad Gita.\n(Mamakam Sharanam Braj)\nThere is no need to serve the gods. They are like the hands and feet of God. When service is offered to God, even their hands and feet become worthy of service.\nThere is no need to serve separately. As it is said in Bhagavad Gita (12.7)-- Teshamaham sayuddharta mrityukhansarasagarat. Its meaning is that to take the side of the devotee, God\ngives such instructions from within him that ultimately he gets freed from the bondage of worldly existence. Of God"
