          "textEn": "382\nA devotee is eligible to accept any blessing given by Gods. Like this,\nfor blessings, he does not have to worship the gods separately. Kubera is the lord of the gods and\nhe himself is asking Dhruva Maharaj to grant a desired boon. Therefore, Srila Bilvamangal Thakur said\nthat those who remain engaged in the service of God, for them, all material blessings remain waiting\nequal to the maids. At the door of the devotee, at any time, the goddess of liberation, liberation, or even more than that,\nkeeps waiting to grant. Therefore, being a devotee is a respectable position. Through the divine love and devotion of God, a person can effortlessly attain all the blessings of the world. Kubera said to Dhruva,\nMaharaj, he has heard that Dhruva always stays in Samadhi or keeps contemplating the lotus feet of God.\nHe keeps thinking. In other words, they knew that Dhruva Maharaj did not desire any object in the three physical worlds.\nIn other words, they knew that.\nThey knew that they would not ask for anything other than to keep remembering the lotus feet of God. Maitreya Ubacha\nSa Rajarajena Varaya Chodito\nDhuvo Mahabhagavato Mahamatih\nHarau Sa Vavre'achalitam Smriti Yaya\nTaratyayalaneena Duratyayam Tamah. 8 ”. Meaning of the word:\nMaitreya said--Maitreya Rishi said; Sah-byah--He; Raj-raj-raj--King of Kings (Kubera); Varay--Blessing fag;\nChoditah--Upon asking; Dhruva--Jd Maharaj; Maha-bhagwatah--First class devotee; Maha-matih--Most intelligent or\nVicharvahanah--Thoughtful; Harau--To God; Sah-byah--He; Vatre- asked; Achalitam- steady, unbroken; Smritim- memory; Yaya- from whom;\nTarati- crosses; Aylen- without difficulty; Duratyayam- impossible, difficult; A0: - Darkness, ignorance.\n.\nMaharishi Metreya further said: O Vidur, when Yaksharaja Kuber asked Dhruva Maharaj to ask for a boon,\nthen that supreme scholar, pure devotee, intelligent, and thoughtful king Dhruva made this request\nthat their unwavering faith and memory in God remain, because in this way man can easily cross the ocean of ignorance,\nalthough crossing it is extremely difficult for others. Meaning: According to the skilled followers of Vedic rituals, there are many types of boons"
